__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"ASPECT_TERMS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t=[{id:'aspects',term:'Aspect',category:'aspect',aliases:['aspects','aspected'],simple:'An aspect is a meaningful angle between two planets, like 90\xb0 or 120\xb0. It shows how those two parts of you talk to each other: easily, tensely, or as one.',detailed:{sky:'Measured along the ecliptic, the angle between two longitudes. The major aspects divide the circle by 1, 2, 3, 4 and 6; minor ones by 5, 8 and 12. Allowed slack either side is the orb.',history:'Ptolemy (Tetrabiblos, second century) explained the majors through musical ratios and through which signs "see" each other: signs of the same element trine, of the same modality square or oppose. Kepler added the quintile and others in the 1600s.',traditions:'Hellenistic astrologers (the Greek-speaking astrologers of the ancient Mediterranean, where Western astrology began) often counted aspects by whole sign (any planet in Aries sees any in Leo by trine). Later astrologers measured by degree with orbs. Modern astrology reads "hard" aspects (square, opposition) as tension that drives growth and "soft" ones (trine, sextile) as ease that can be taken for granted.',craft:'Aspects in the current sky are used for timing: a Moon trine Venus is a sweet moment for love work; Mars square Saturn a poor one for starting fights. Aspects in your own chart show which planetary allies work well together for you.',care:'No aspect is simply good or bad. Many of the most capable people have charts full of squares.'},related:['orb','conjunction','square','trine','applying-separating','aspect-patterns']},{id:'orb',term:'Orb',category:'aspect',aliases:['orbs'],simple:'The orb is how far from exact an aspect can be and still count. A square at 87\xb0 is 3\xb0 from exact: within a 7\xb0 orb, so it counts.',detailed:{sky:'An allowance in degrees either side of the exact angle. The tighter the orb, the stronger the aspect is usually read.',history:'Medieval astrologers gave each planet its own "moiety" of light (the Sun 15\xb0, the Moon 12\xb0, Saturn and Jupiter 9\xb0, Mars 8\xb0, Venus and Mercury 7\xb0), and two planets were in aspect when within half the sum. Modern astrologers give each aspect an orb instead.',traditions:'This app\'s defaults: conjunction and opposition 8\xb0, trine and square 7\xb0, sextile 5\xb0, quincunx 3\xb0, semisextile, semisquare and sesquiquadrate 2\xb0, quintile and biquintile 1.5\xb0, with 2\xb0 more when the Sun or Moon is involved and no more than 3\xb0 for points like the nodes. Transits use tighter orbs (about 1.5\u20132\xb0). You can change all of these in the chart\'s settings.',craft:'For timing a working by a sky aspect, aim for the hours before it is exact (applying), when its energy is building.',care:'Orbs are a convention, not a law; different apps disagreeing about an aspect usually means different orbs.'},related:['aspects','applying-separating']},{id:'applying-separating',term:'Applying and separating',category:'aspect',aliases:['applying','separating'],simple:'An applying aspect is still getting closer to exact; a separating one has passed and is moving apart. Applying aspects are read as building, separating as fading.',detailed:{sky:'Depends on the planets\' speeds: the faster one moves toward or away from the exact angle.',history:'Central to horary astrology (answering questions by the chart of the moment), where an applying aspect shows something about to happen.',traditions:'In transits, an applying aspect is "coming", a separating one "has been".',craft:'Begin workings while a helpful aspect is applying; release while a difficult one separates.',care:'With a retrograde planet involved, an aspect can apply, separate and apply again.'},related:['aspects','orb','retrograde']},{id:'conjunction',term:'Conjunction',category:'aspect',symbol:'\u260c',aliases:['conjunct','conjoins','joins'],simple:'A conjunction is two planets together (0\xb0 apart). Their energies blend and act as one, for better and worse.',detailed:{sky:'The two longitudes coincide. A New Moon is a Sun\u2013Moon conjunction; the Great Conjunction of Jupiter and Saturn comes every twenty years.',history:'Not counted by Ptolemy as an aspect, but always the strongest contact. Great Conjunctions were used to time eras and dynasties by medieval Persian and Arabic astrologers.',traditions:'The nature depends on the planets: Venus and Jupiter together are sweet; Mars and Saturn together are hard. Modern astrology reads fusion and emphasis.',craft:'A conjunction in the sky concentrates one theme; work with it, not against it.',care:'A planet conjunct the Sun within about 8\xb0 is "combust" in traditional astrology, its light swallowed; within half a degree it is "cazimi", in the heart of the Sun, strengthened.'},related:['aspects','stellium','lunation']},{id:'opposition',term:'Opposition',category:'aspect',symbol:'\u260d',aliases:['opposite','opposes','opposed'],simple:'An opposition is two planets across the circle (180\xb0): a pull between two sides that asks for balance, often met through other people.',detailed:{sky:'The planets sit in opposite signs. A Full Moon is a Sun\u2013Moon opposition; outer planets are brightest and retrograde at opposition to the Sun.',history:'Ptolemy counted it a hard aspect, by the ratio 1:2 (the octave).',traditions:'Traditional: enmity, separation, open conflict. Modern: awareness through contrast, projection onto others, the need to integrate two needs.',craft:'Full-moon energy: illumination, culmination, release. Good for seeing clearly and for balancing workings.',care:'An opposition is a seesaw, not a war: both ends are you.'},related:['aspects','t-square','grand-cross']},{id:'trine',term:'Trine',category:'aspect',symbol:'\u25b3',aliases:['trines','trined'],simple:'A trine is two planets 120\xb0 apart, usually in signs of the same element. It is easy flow and natural talent.',detailed:{sky:'A third of the circle; four signs apart.',history:'Ptolemy\'s harmonious aspect, by the ratio 2:3 (the fifth).',traditions:'Traditional: friendship and help. Modern: gifts, ease and support, which can be taken for granted or left unused.',craft:'A sky trine is a kind window for workings in that part of life.',care:'Too many trines can mean too little push; ease is a resource, not a result.'},related:['aspects','grand-trine','elements']},{id:'square',term:'Square',category:'aspect',symbol:'\u25a1',aliases:['squares','squared'],simple:'A square is two planets 90\xb0 apart: friction and challenge that pushes you to act and build.',detailed:{sky:'A quarter of the circle; three signs apart, usually in signs of the same modality. The quarter moons are Sun\u2013Moon squares.',history:'Ptolemy\'s inharmonious aspect, by the ratio 3:4 (the fourth).',traditions:'Traditional: obstruction and strife. Modern: dynamic tension, growth through effort, the engine of achievement.',craft:'First-quarter energy: push through. Squares in the sky are for effort and courage, not for gentle beginnings.',care:'A square in your chart isn\'t a flaw; it\'s where you do your strongest work.'},related:['aspects','t-square','grand-cross','modalities']},{id:'sextile',term:'Sextile',category:'aspect',symbol:'\u26b9',aliases:['sextiles'],simple:'A sextile is two planets 60\xb0 apart: an opportunity that helps if you take it.',detailed:{sky:'A sixth of the circle; two signs apart, usually fire with air or earth with water.',history:'Ptolemy\'s lesser harmonious aspect, by the ratio 3:5.',traditions:'Traditional: mild friendship. Modern: opportunity, cooperation, skill that grows with use.',craft:'A small door opens; make the first move.',care:'Sextiles are easy to miss; they need you to notice them.'},related:['aspects','yod','kite']},{id:'quincunx',term:'Quincunx',category:'aspect',symbol:'\u26bb',aliases:['inconjunct'],simple:'A quincunx (150\xb0) joins two signs with nothing in common: an awkward fit that asks for constant small adjustments.',detailed:{sky:'Five signs apart; the signs share neither element, modality nor polarity.',history:'Ptolemy\'s signs that were "unconnected" (aversion). Kepler and later astrologers made it an aspect.',traditions:'Modern: strain, adjustment, health matters, things that don\'t fit and must be reworked.',craft:'Work in small adjustments rather than big gestures.',care:'Read gently and with a tight orb.'},related:['yod','aspects']},{id:'semisextile',term:'Semisextile',category:'aspect',symbol:'\u26ba',simple:'A semisextile (30\xb0) links neighbouring signs: a slight, sometimes uneasy nudge toward growth.',detailed:{sky:'One sign apart.',history:'A Keplerian minor aspect.',traditions:'Modern: small opportunities, awkward neighbours, building on what came before.',craft:'Minor in timing; little used for workings.',care:'Minor aspects add colour, not plot.'},related:['aspects']},{id:'semisquare',term:'Semisquare',category:'aspect',symbol:'\u2220',aliases:['octile'],simple:'A semisquare (45\xb0) is half a square: minor friction, irritation that prompts action.',detailed:{sky:'An eighth of the circle.',history:'Added by Kepler; used heavily by Uranian and cosmobiology astrologers.',traditions:'Modern: tension, impatience, a spur.',craft:'Minor; notice it in timing for quarrels and haste.',care:'Minor aspects add colour, not plot.'},related:['square','sesquiquadrate']},{id:'sesquiquadrate',term:'Sesquiquadrate',category:'aspect',symbol:'\u26bc',aliases:['sesquisquare'],simple:'A sesquiquadrate (135\xb0) is a square and a half: minor tension, often felt as agitation.',detailed:{sky:'Three eighths of the circle.',history:'Keplerian minor aspect.',traditions:'Modern: disruption, restlessness, effort.',craft:'Minor; little used for timing.',care:'Minor aspects add colour, not plot.'},related:['semisquare','square']},{id:'quintile',term:'Quintile',category:'aspect',symbol:'Q',simple:'A quintile (72\xb0) divides the circle by five. It is read as creative gift and a particular kind of talent.',detailed:{sky:'A fifth of the circle.',history:'Introduced by Kepler, inspired by the pentagon; developed by John Addey\'s harmonics.',traditions:'Modern: creativity, style, skill and a personal signature.',craft:'Pentacle and five-fold symbolism make quintiles a favourite of some witches for creative magic.',care:'Very sensitive to orb; this app allows 1.5\xb0.'},related:['biquintile','aspects']},{id:'biquintile',term:'Biquintile',category:'aspect',symbol:'bQ',simple:'A biquintile (144\xb0) is two fifths of the circle: like the quintile, a creative talent.',detailed:{sky:'Two fifths of the circle.',history:'Kepler\'s, like the quintile.',traditions:'Modern: creativity and unusual gifts.',craft:'As the quintile.',care:'Very sensitive to orb.'},related:['quintile']},{id:'aspect-patterns',term:'Aspect pattern',category:'pattern',aliases:['aspect patterns','configurations'],simple:'An aspect pattern is a shape three or more planets make together, like a triangle or a cross. It shows a theme running through a chart.',detailed:{sky:'Several aspects linking the same planets into a figure.',history:'Named and popularised in the twentieth century (Marc Edmund Jones\'s chart shapes, then grand trines, T-squares and yods in modern textbooks).',traditions:'Modern astrology reads them as major themes; traditional astrology doesn\'t use them as such.',craft:'A pattern\'s planets make a natural set of allies: their days and correspondences can be worked together.',care:'Different orbs make patterns appear and vanish; read them as emphasis, not fate.'},related:['grand-trine','t-square','grand-cross','yod','kite','stellium']},{id:'grand-trine',term:'Grand trine',category:'pattern',aliases:['grand trines'],simple:'A grand trine is three planets each 120\xb0 apart, a triangle usually in one element: a circuit of easy talent and support.',detailed:{sky:'Three trines closing a triangle.',history:'A modern pattern name.',traditions:'Read as a gift in that element (fire inspiration, earth practicality, air ideas, water feeling) that may need effort to use rather than coast on.',craft:'Element magic in the trine\'s element tends to come easily to you.',care:'Ease can become a closed loop; look for an opposition or square that gives it somewhere to go (see Kite).'},related:['trine','kite','elements']},{id:'t-square',term:'T-square',category:'pattern',aliases:['T-squares','T square'],simple:'A T-square is two planets in opposition, both squared by a third. The third, the apex, is where the tension is released and the work gets done.',detailed:{sky:'An opposition and two squares to a single point.',history:'A modern pattern name.',traditions:'Read as drive, pressure and achievement; the empty point opposite the apex is often read as what would bring balance.',craft:'The apex planet\'s day and hour are good moments to work on the T-square\'s themes.',care:'Pressure is energy; it needs channelling, not fixing.'},related:['square','opposition','grand-cross','modalities']},{id:'grand-cross',term:'Grand cross',category:'pattern',aliases:['grand square'],simple:'A grand cross is four planets in a square, two oppositions crossing: great tension and great strength, held in balance.',detailed:{sky:'Two oppositions and four squares, usually in one modality.',history:'A modern pattern name.',traditions:'Read as a life pulled four ways, and as resilience and capacity once it is owned.',craft:'Four-quarter magic, the cross of the elements and calling all four directions suit it.',care:'Rare; read with care and without alarm.'},related:['t-square','square','opposition']},{id:'yod',term:'Yod',category:'pattern',aliases:['Finger of God','Finger of Fate'],simple:'A yod is two planets in sextile, both 150\xb0 from a third: a long, pointing triangle. The point planet is read as a special task or calling.',detailed:{sky:'A sextile and two quincunxes.',history:'Named by twentieth-century astrologers; the "Finger of God" name dramatised it.',traditions:'Read as adjustment, a sense of mission and an unusual path.',craft:'The apex planet is a natural focus for devotional or vocational work.',care:'The dramatic nickname oversells it; it is a pattern of adjustment more than of fate.'},related:['quincunx','sextile']},{id:'kite',term:'Kite',category:'pattern',simple:'A kite is a grand trine with a fourth planet opposite one corner, sextile the other two: talent with a direction to fly in.',detailed:{sky:'A grand trine, an opposition and two sextiles.',history:'A modern pattern name.',traditions:'Read as gifts put to purpose; the opposing planet (the tail) gives the grand trine a focus.',craft:'The tail planet suggests what to aim your easy gifts at.',care:'As with all patterns, orbs decide whether it appears.'},related:['grand-trine','opposition']},{id:'stellium',term:'Stellium',category:'pattern',aliases:['stelliums'],simple:'A stellium is three or more planets in one sign or one house: a strong emphasis on that sign or part of life.',detailed:{sky:'A cluster of planets in one thirty-degree sign or one house.',history:'A modern term (from Latin stella, star); older astrologers spoke of a sign "full of planets".',traditions:'Read as focus, intensity and a theme the person returns to. Some astrologers ask for four or more; this app shows three or more planets from the Sun to Pluto.',craft:'The stellium\'s sign or house is a natural centre for your practice.',care:'Outer-planet stelliums are shared by everyone born that year; they mean more when personal planets join.'},related:['conjunction','aspect-patterns']}]},893,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"BODY_TERMS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t=[{id:'sun',term:'Sun',category:'planet',symbol:'\u2609',aliases:['the Sun','Sun sign'],simple:'The Sun is your core self: your will, vitality and the light you live by. Your "star sign" is simply the sign the Sun was in when you were born.',detailed:{sky:'The Sun is a star, not a planet, but astrology calls all seven visible lights "planets" (Greek plan\u0113t\u0113s, wanderers). Seen from Earth it travels the whole zodiac once a year along the ecliptic, about a degree a day, which is why your Sun sign is tied to your birthday.',history:'Babylonian sky-watchers tracked the Sun\'s path against the stars by at least the eighth century BCE; the Greeks gave it the twelve thirty-degree signs we use. In the charts of Hellenistic astrology (the astrology of the ancient Greek-speaking world, where Western astrology began) the Sun was the light of the day, head of the "sect" of day planets (with Jupiter and Saturn).',traditions:'Traditional astrology reads the Sun for life force, honour, fathers, rulers and public standing. Modern psychological astrology reads it as the conscious self and the path of becoming. Vedic astrology leads with the Moon and the rising sign rather than the Sun, which is one reason a Vedic reading can feel so different.',craft:'The Sun rules Sunday and the first hour after sunrise on that day. Its workings: success, confidence, health, recognition, blessing new ventures, and lifting heavy moods. Correspondences commonly given: gold, yellow and orange, frankincense, bay, calendula, sunflower, citrine, carnelian, sunstone. Deities with solar ties include Ra, Helios, Sol, Apollo, Sunna (S\xf3l), Surya, Amaterasu and Saul\u0117.',care:'Sun-sign columns describe a twelfth of humanity at once; your chart is far more than your Sun. And "solar" is not "masculine" in any fixed way: several traditions, Norse, Baltic and Shinto among them, hold the Sun as a goddess.'},related:['moon','ascendant','planetary-days','leo','solar-return'],links:[{kind:'planet-guide',planet:'Sun'}]},{id:'moon',term:'Moon',category:'planet',symbol:'\u263d',aliases:['the Moon','Moon sign'],simple:'The Moon is your inner life: feelings, needs, habits and what makes you feel safe. It changes sign every two and a half days, so it says a lot about the mood of a day.',detailed:{sky:'The Moon circles Earth every 27.3 days against the stars (sidereal month) and returns to the same phase every 29.5 days (synodic month). It moves about 13\xb0 a day, fast enough that a birth time a few hours out can shift its degree noticeably, and occasionally its sign.',history:'Lunar calendars are among humanity\'s oldest timekeeping; Babylonian omen texts (En\u016bma Anu Enlil) read the Moon long before the zodiac was fixed. In Hellenistic astrology (the astrology of the ancient Greek-speaking world, where Western astrology began) the Moon was the light of the night and ruled the body, fortune and the mother.',traditions:'Traditional readers look to the Moon for the body, the mother, the common people and daily changes. Modern astrology reads it as emotional needs, memory and instinct. Vedic astrology gives it pride of place: your "rashi" is usually your Moon sign, and the 27 lunar mansions (nakshatras) are counted from it.',craft:'The Moon rules Monday and the first hour after sunrise on that day, and the whole of lunar timing: waxing to draw in, waning to release, full to charge and reveal, dark and new to rest and plant. Its sign colours a working too. Correspondences: silver, white, moonstone, selenite, jasmine, mugwort, willow, water and mirrors. Lunar deities include Selene, Luna, Artemis and Diana (in later tradition), M\xe1ni, Khonsu, Nanna, Chang\'e, Tsukuyomi and M\u0117nulis.',care:'Feeling the Moon is personal; not everyone feels a full moon, and that is fine. Without a birth time your Moon sign may be uncertain if it changed sign that day; the chart will say so.'},related:['sun','lunation','void-of-course','cancer','moon-phases','progressions'],links:[{kind:'planet-guide',planet:'Moon'},{kind:'moon-phase',phase:'full',label:'The Full Moon'}]},{id:'mercury',term:'Mercury',category:'planet',symbol:'\u263f',simple:'Mercury is how you think, speak, learn and move about: the messenger. Its sign shows your mind\'s style; its retrogrades are famous for tangled messages.',detailed:{sky:'The innermost planet, Mercury is never more than about 28\xb0 from the Sun, so it is always in the Sun\'s sign or a neighbouring one. It turns retrograde three or four times a year for about three weeks each time.',history:'Babylonians knew it as Nab\xfb\'s star, the scribe god; the Greeks as Hermes\' star (Stilb\u014dn, "the gleaming"); the Romans named it Mercury. In Hellenistic astrology (the astrology of the ancient Greek-speaking world, where Western astrology began) it was neither day nor night but took the sect of whatever planet it was nearest, as changeable as its god.',traditions:'Traditionally Mercury rules speech, writing, trade, calculation, siblings, young people and thieves. Modern astrology reads perception, learning style and how you process information. Vedic Budha rules intellect, commerce and humour.',craft:'Wednesday (Mercredi, Woden\'s day) and Mercury hours suit communication, study, writing, exams, contracts, travel, divination and quick business. Correspondences: quicksilver (never handle mercury; use the symbol), mixed colours and yellow, lavender, mint, fennel, fluorite, agate, sodalite. Deities: Hermes, Mercury, Thoth, Nab\xfb, Odin (through the weekday), Ogma, and Budha in the Navagraha.',care:'Mercury retrograde is a time to re-check, not to fear. Contracts still get signed and phones still work; read the small print twice.'},related:['gemini','virgo','retrograde','shadow-period','planetary-hours'],links:[{kind:'planet-guide',planet:'Mercury'}]},{id:'venus',term:'Venus',category:'planet',symbol:'\u2640',simple:'Venus is love, pleasure, beauty and what you value: how you attract, enjoy and relate. Its sign shows your style of affection and taste.',detailed:{sky:'Venus is the brightest planet, the morning or evening star, never more than about 47\xb0 from the Sun. Its cycle traces a five-petalled pattern over eight years, and it turns retrograde roughly every nineteen months for about six weeks.',history:'Babylonian Inanna/Ishtar was the planet herself, descending into the underworld as Venus vanished in the Sun\'s glare. The Greeks named it for Aphrodite, the Romans for Venus. In Hellenistic astrology (the astrology of the ancient Greek-speaking world, where Western astrology began) Venus was the lesser benefic of the night sect.',traditions:'Traditionally Venus rules love, marriage, women, art, adornment, pleasure and peace. Modern astrology reads relating, values, self-worth and money. Vedic Shukra is teacher of the asuras and rules comforts, arts and marriage.',craft:'Friday (Freya\'s or Frigg\'s day; vendredi, Venus\' day) and Venus hours suit love, friendship, beauty, art, self-worth, reconciliation and drawing money through pleasant means. Correspondences: copper, green and pink, rose, vervain, yarrow, rose quartz, emerald, jade. Deities: Aphrodite, Venus, Inanna, Ishtar, Astarte, Freyja, Frigg, Hathor, Lakshmi, Oshun (in Lucum\xed, approached through initiated practice) and Shukra.',care:'Love workings aimed at a particular person cross into their free will; most witches work for love in general, or for themselves. Venus retrograde is a reflective time, not a curse on romance.'},related:['taurus','libra','planetary-days','dignity'],links:[{kind:'planet-guide',planet:'Venus'},{kind:'library',name:'Aphrodite'}]},{id:'mars',term:'Mars',category:'planet',symbol:'\u2642',simple:'Mars is your drive, courage, anger and desire: how you go after what you want and how you defend yourself.',detailed:{sky:'The red planet takes about two years to go round the zodiac, lingering about six weeks in a sign, longer in a retrograde year. It turns retrograde about every 26 months for some ten weeks, when it is closest and brightest.',history:'Babylonians linked it to Nergal, god of plague and war; the Greeks to Ares (Pyroeis, "fiery"); the Romans to Mars, also a guardian of fields. Hellenistic astrology (the astrology of the ancient Greek-speaking world, where Western astrology began) made Mars the greater malefic of the night sect: hot, sharp and separating.',traditions:'Traditionally Mars rules soldiers, surgeons, smiths, fevers, cuts, fire and strife. Modern astrology reads assertion, sexuality, energy and how anger moves. Vedic Mangala rules courage, land and brothers.',craft:'Tuesday (Tiw\'s day; mardi, Mars\' day) and Mars hours suit courage, protection, defence, cutting cords, banishing, physical energy and competition. Correspondences: iron, red, dragon\'s blood, nettle, black pepper, garlic, basil, garnet, hematite, bloodstone. Deities: Ares, Mars, Nergal, Tyr and Tiw, Thor, Sekhmet, Mangala, Hachiman, Perun.',care:'Mars workings are strong medicine; aim them at situations and your own courage rather than at people. "Malefic" means hard to bear, not evil.'},related:['aries','scorpio','planetary-hours','retrograde'],links:[{kind:'planet-guide',planet:'Mars'}]},{id:'jupiter',term:'Jupiter',category:'planet',symbol:'\u2643',simple:'Jupiter is growth, luck, generosity and faith: where life opens and you are inclined to trust. It spends about a year in each sign.',detailed:{sky:'The largest planet circles the zodiac in about twelve years, so it spends roughly a year in each sign, and turns retrograde for about four months each year. Its twelve-year return marks ages 12, 24, 36, 48 and so on.',history:'Babylonian Marduk, king of the gods, was Jupiter; the Greeks named it for Zeus, the Romans for Jupiter. Hellenistic astrology (the astrology of the ancient Greek-speaking world, where Western astrology began) called it the greater benefic of the day sect.',traditions:'Traditionally Jupiter rules law, religion, wealth, judges, priests and good fortune. Modern astrology reads meaning, expansion, optimism and excess. Vedic Guru (Brihaspati) is the gods\' teacher, the great benefic of wisdom and children.',craft:'Thursday (Thor\'s day; jeudi, Jupiter\'s day) and Jupiter hours suit luck, abundance, growth, legal matters, learning, generosity and spiritual expansion. Correspondences: tin, royal blue and purple, clove, sage, nutmeg, star anise, amethyst, lapis lazuli. Deities: Zeus, Jupiter, Marduk, Thor and Thunor, Perun, Taranis, Brihaspati.',care:'Jupiter enlarges whatever it touches, including mistakes. A "lucky" transit still needs you to show up.'},related:['sagittarius','pisces','returns','planetary-days'],links:[{kind:'planet-guide',planet:'Jupiter'}]},{id:'saturn',term:'Saturn',category:'planet',symbol:'\u2644',simple:'Saturn is time, limits, structure and responsibility: where life asks for patience and rewards real work. It spends about two and a half years in each sign.',detailed:{sky:'The slowest planet visible to the eye, Saturn takes about 29.5 years to go round, about two and a half years per sign, with a retrograde of some four and a half months each year. The Saturn return falls around ages 29, 58 and 87.',history:'Babylonians saw Ninurta and called it "the steady one"; the Greeks named it Kronos, the Romans Saturn, god of sowing and the golden age. In Hellenistic astrology (the astrology of the ancient Greek-speaking world, where Western astrology began) Saturn was the greater malefic of the day sect, the outermost boundary of the visible cosmos.',traditions:'Traditionally Saturn rules old age, ancestors, land, lead, illness, delay and sorrow, and also patience and endurance. Modern astrology reads structure, maturity, fear, mastery and commitment. Vedic Shani is feared and honoured as the slow teacher of karma.',craft:'Saturday and Saturn hours suit binding, banishing, boundaries, endings, protection of the home, ancestor work, discipline and long-term goals. Correspondences: lead (handle never; use the symbol), black and deep indigo, myrrh, patchouli, cypress, comfrey, mullein, obsidian, jet, onyx, black tourmaline. Deities: Kronos, Saturn, Ninurta, Shani, and in some modern practice Hekate and the Cailleach as keepers of thresholds and old age.',care:'Saturn is the planet people fear most and often thank later. Its transits ask for structure, not punishment.'},related:['capricorn','aquarius','returns','transit'],links:[{kind:'planet-guide',planet:'Saturn'}]},{id:'uranus',term:'Uranus',category:'planet',symbol:'\u2645',simple:'Uranus is sudden change, freedom and invention: where you break from the expected. It spends about seven years in a sign, so it marks generations as much as people.',detailed:{sky:'Found by William Herschel in 1781, the first planet discovered by telescope. It takes about 84 years to go round, roughly seven per sign, and is retrograde about five months a year.',history:'Its discovery in the age of revolutions (American, French, industrial) shaped how astrologers read it. It was named for Ouranos, the Greek sky; modern astrology gave it rulership of Aquarius alongside Saturn.',traditions:'Traditional astrology, built on the seven visible planets, doesn\'t use it, though many traditional astrologers add it as a modern note. Modern astrology reads awakening, rebellion, originality, technology and shocks. Its square at about 21 and opposition at about 42 are counted among life\'s turning points.',craft:'Uranus has no weekday or planetary hour. Witches call on it for breaking habits and bindings, sudden insight, freedom, community and innovation. Correspondences in modern practice: electric blue, aquamarine, labradorite, storms and lightning.',care:'Outer-planet meanings are young (about two centuries) and generational; read them with lighter hands than the seven.'},related:['aquarius','rulership','transit']},{id:'neptune',term:'Neptune',category:'planet',symbol:'\u2646',simple:'Neptune is dreams, imagination, compassion and illusion: where the edges blur, for inspiration or for confusion.',detailed:{sky:'Neptune was found in 1846 by mathematics before telescope: Le Verrier and Adams predicted it from wobbles in Uranus\'s path. It takes about 165 years to go round, about fourteen per sign.',history:'Named for the Roman sea god, its discovery came with the rise of spiritualism, anaesthesia and photography, and astrologers came to read it through them. Modern astrology gave it rulership of Pisces alongside Jupiter.',traditions:'Not used in traditional astrology. Modern astrology reads mysticism, art, empathy, addiction, deception and longing for something beyond. Its sign describes generations.',craft:'No weekday or hour. Witches call on Neptune for dreamwork, trance, glamour and veiling, psychic opening, and compassion. Correspondences in modern practice: sea water, mist, sea-green and violet, amethyst, aquamarine, poppy imagery.',care:'Neptune\'s gift for dissolving can dissolve good sense too: ground well before and after trance and dream work.'},related:['pisces','rulership','transit']},{id:'pluto',term:'Pluto',category:'planet',symbol:'\u2647',simple:'Pluto is power, death and rebirth: where life asks you to let something end so something deeper can begin.',detailed:{sky:'Found by Clyde Tombaugh in 1930 and reclassified as a dwarf planet in 2006; astrologers kept it. Its oval orbit takes 248 years, spending from about 12 to 32 years in a sign.',history:'Named for the Roman lord of the underworld (and the abundance under the earth), discovered in the decade of depth psychology\'s spread and, later, nuclear fission, which coloured its reading. Modern astrology gave it rulership of Scorpio alongside Mars.',traditions:'Not used in traditional astrology. Modern and psychological astrology read transformation, compulsion, power, taboo, survival and the unconscious. Evolutionary astrology builds a whole method around it and the Moon\'s nodes.',craft:'No weekday or hour. Witches call on Pluto for shadow work, deep release, reclaiming power, and work with the dead. Correspondences in modern practice: black and deep red, obsidian, smoky quartz, pomegranate. Deities: Hades and Pluto, Persephone, Ereshkigal, Hel.',care:'Pluto is often written about in dramatic language; most Pluto transits are slow and inward. Shadow work is best done gently and, when old wounds open, alongside real support.'},related:['scorpio','transit','rulership'],links:[{kind:'library',name:'Hades'}]},{id:'chiron',term:'Chiron',category:'point',symbol:'\u26b7',simple:'Chiron is the wound that teaches: a tender place in you that, worked with, becomes a gift for helping others.',detailed:{sky:'Chiron is a small icy body found in 1977, orbiting between Saturn and Uranus every 50 years on a very oval path, so it spends anywhere from under two to about eight years in a sign. The app\'s positions come from a JPL Horizons table.',history:'Named for the wise centaur of Greek myth who taught Asclepius, Achilles and Jason, and who, wounded by an arrow he could not heal, gave up his immortality. Astrologers, notably Barbara Hand Clow and Melanie Reinhart, built its meaning from that story.',traditions:'Not used in traditional astrology. Modern astrology reads woundedness, healing, mentoring and bridging. The Chiron return around age 50 is read as a time of making peace with old hurts.',craft:'Chiron suits healing work, especially for others, herbalism, and turning pain into teaching. Associated in modern practice with Asclepius and with healing herbs.',care:'A "Chiron wound" is a story to reflect with, not a diagnosis. Real hurts deserve real care.'},related:['transit','returns'],links:[{kind:'library',name:'Asclepius'}]},{id:'north-node',term:'North Node',category:'point',symbol:'\u260a',aliases:['North Node of the Moon','True Node','Mean Node','Rahu','lunar nodes','nodes'],simple:'The North Node is where the Moon\'s path crosses the Sun\'s going north. In a chart it is read as a direction of growth, the unfamiliar that is good for you.',detailed:{sky:'The Moon\'s orbit is tilted about 5\xb0 to the ecliptic. The two places it crosses are the nodes, always exactly opposite. They slide backwards through the zodiac, round in 18.6 years, about a year and a half in each sign. Eclipses can only happen near them. The "mean" node is the smoothed average; the "true" node wobbles with the Moon\'s real orbit and sometimes moves forward for a while. The app uses the true node unless you choose the mean in Settings.',history:'Known in Babylon because of eclipses. Indian astrology personified them as Rahu (head) and Ketu (tail) of a demon who swallowed the nectar of immortality and swallows the Sun and Moon in eclipses. Medieval Western astrologers called them the Dragon\'s Head and Tail.',traditions:'Medieval astrology treated the North Node as increasing whatever it touched, the South as diminishing. Modern and evolutionary astrology read the North as purpose, growth and the soul\'s direction. In Vedic astrology Rahu is hunger, obsession and the foreign.',craft:'Some witches time beginnings near the North Node\'s contacts and releases near the South\'s. Eclipse seasons, when the Sun nears the nodes, are often kept as times of watching rather than casting.',care:'"Destiny" language is common around the nodes. Read them as an invitation, not an assignment.'},related:['south-node','eclipse','saros']},{id:'south-node',term:'South Node',category:'point',symbol:'\u260b',aliases:['South Node of the Moon','Ketu'],simple:'The South Node, opposite the North, is read as what comes easily from the past: skills and habits to draw on, and to grow beyond.',detailed:{sky:'Where the Moon\'s orbit crosses the ecliptic going south; always exactly opposite the North Node.',history:'The Dragon\'s Tail of medieval astrology; Ketu in Indian astrology, the headless body of the demon.',traditions:'Medieval readers took it as loss and diminishment. Evolutionary astrology reads past patterns, even past lives. Vedic Ketu is detachment, spirituality and release.',craft:'Suits release, cord cutting, ancestor work and working with old skills.',care:'Past-life readings are a way of telling stories about patterns; hold them lightly.'},related:['north-node','eclipse']},{id:'lilith',term:'Black Moon Lilith',category:'point',symbol:'\u26b8',aliases:['Lilith','Black Moon','mean Lilith','lunar apogee'],simple:'Black Moon Lilith marks the point where the Moon is farthest from Earth. It is read as the wild, refused and unapologetic part of you.',detailed:{sky:'Not a body at all: it is the apogee, the far end of the Moon\'s oval orbit, an empty point that circles the zodiac in about nine years. The app uses the mean apogee (the smoothed one), the version most astrologers use.',history:'Named in the twentieth century for Lilith of Jewish folklore, Adam\'s first wife who would not submit, and through her for older Mesopotamian night spirits (lil\u012btu). The astrological point was popularised by French and American astrologers from the 1930s on.',traditions:'Not used in traditional astrology. Modern astrology reads exile, raw desire, rage at injustice, sexuality without shame and the parts of us that were pushed out. Feminist astrology has embraced it.',craft:'Shadow work, reclaiming, and sovereignty work. Some witches who work with the figure of Lilith connect her to this point; the Library has her entry.',care:'There are several "Liliths" (mean, true or oscillating, and an asteroid). Don\'t be surprised if other apps give a different degree. Approaching Lilith the figure is a matter of her own lore, not only astrology.'},related:['moon','north-node'],links:[{kind:'library',name:'Lilith'}]},{id:'part-of-fortune',term:'Part of Fortune',category:'point',symbol:'\u2297',aliases:['Lot of Fortune','Fortuna point'],simple:'The Part of Fortune is a calculated point that shows where luck, comfort and the body\'s well-being tend to flow for you.',detailed:{sky:'Not a body: it sits as far from your Ascendant as the Moon is from the Sun. By day: Ascendant + Moon \u2212 Sun; by night, the Sun and Moon swap: Ascendant + Sun \u2212 Moon. The app chooses by whether the Sun was above the horizon. It needs a birth time.',history:'The most important of the "lots" of the ancient astrologers, used by Vettius Valens and others for fortune, the body and timing. Medieval Arabic astrologers multiplied the lots into hundreds; the Part of Fortune is the one that lasted.',traditions:'Ancient astrologers used it as a second Ascendant for material life. Many modern astrologers use the day formula for everyone; this app follows the older day/night rule.',craft:'Its sign and house suggest where prosperity work may take root; Fortuna is the Roman goddess of luck.',care:'It changes quickly with birth time (as fast as the Ascendant), so an uncertain time makes it uncertain.'},related:['ascendant','sect','lots'],links:[{kind:'library',name:'Fortuna'}]},{id:'ascendant',term:'Ascendant',category:'angle',symbol:'AC',aliases:['rising sign','ASC','the rising','rising'],simple:'Your Ascendant, or rising sign, is the sign that was rising on the eastern horizon when you were born. It shows how you meet the world and how it first meets you.',detailed:{sky:'As the Earth turns, the whole zodiac rises over the eastern horizon once a day, a new sign about every two hours (faster or slower by latitude). The exact degree rising at birth is the Ascendant. It needs an accurate time and place: four minutes of clock time move it about a degree.',history:'Hellenistic astrologers (the Greek-speaking astrologers of the ancient Mediterranean, where Western astrology began) called it the h\u014droskopos, the "hour-marker", which is where our word horoscope comes from. It was the first house and the place of life itself.',traditions:'Traditional astrology reads the Ascendant and its ruler (the chart ruler) for the body, temperament and the course of life. Modern astrology reads the persona, first impressions and approach to life. Vedic astrology centres the chart on the Ascendant (lagna).',craft:'The ruler of your rising sign is your chart ruler: its day, hours, colours and correspondences are a natural place to build a personal practice. Your rising sign\'s element can suggest which element you call first.',care:'Your rising sign is only as good as your birth time. If the time is a guess, so is the Ascendant.'},related:['descendant','chart-ruler','house-1','house-systems'],links:[{kind:'tool',tool:'chart',label:'Your chart'}]},{id:'descendant',term:'Descendant',category:'angle',symbol:'DC',aliases:['DSC'],simple:'The Descendant is the point setting in the west, opposite your Ascendant: the start of the seventh house, of partnership and what you seek in others.',detailed:{sky:'The ecliptic degree on the western horizon, always exactly opposite the Ascendant.',history:'Hellenistic astrologers (the Greek-speaking astrologers of the ancient Mediterranean, where Western astrology began) called it the "setting place", linked with marriage, partners and open enemies.',traditions:'Traditionally the angle of marriage, partnership, contracts, lawsuits and open enemies, and, as the place where planets set, of old age and the close of life. Modern astrology reads the qualities we look for, or project, in close partners: the Descendant\'s sign is often a fair picture of the people we are drawn to and the parts of ourselves we meet through them. A planet close to the Descendant tends to show strongly in your relationships and in the partners you choose.',craft:'Partnership and relationship workings can be tuned to the Descendant\'s sign and its ruler: work on that planet\'s day and in its hours, use its colours and correspondences in handfasting, reconciliation or contract magic, and use the full Moon (Sun and Moon facing each other across the sky, as partners do) for blessing a bond. Work on how you relate rather than on another person\'s will.',care:'Like the Ascendant it needs a reliable birth time.'},related:['ascendant','house-7','synastry']},{id:'midheaven',term:'Midheaven',category:'angle',symbol:'MC',aliases:['MC','Medium Coeli'],simple:'The Midheaven is the highest point of the zodiac at your birth: it is read for vocation, reputation and the life you build in public.',detailed:{sky:'The ecliptic degree crossing the meridian (due south in the northern hemisphere, due north in the southern) at the moment of birth. It is not the point overhead, and in most house systems it begins the tenth house.',history:'Latin medium coeli, "middle of the sky". In Hellenistic astrology (the astrology of the ancient Greek-speaking world, where Western astrology began) it was the most powerful place for a planet to be "on top" of the chart.',traditions:'Traditionally action, honour, profession and the king, and the strongest angle after the Ascendant. Medieval and Renaissance astrologers also read it for the mother (Lilly) and for any public authority. Modern astrology reads calling, ambition, reputation and public image, and the path that gives a life its direction. Planets close to the Midheaven tend to describe your work and how you are seen, and often rise into view as life goes on.',craft:'Career and recognition workings can be tuned to its sign and ruler: work on the ruler\'s day and in its hours, or at noon, when the Sun stands at its own midheaven. A written aim kept on the altar, a sigil for your calling, Bay leaves for success and a candle in the Midheaven sign\'s colour suit it, and transits to the Midheaven are good times to begin or rededicate your work.',care:'Needs a birth time; it moves a degree in about four minutes.'},related:['imum-coeli','house-10','house-systems']},{id:'imum-coeli',term:'IC',category:'angle',symbol:'IC',aliases:['Imum Coeli','Nadir'],simple:'The IC, opposite the Midheaven, is the lowest point of the chart: roots, home, family and the private self.',detailed:{sky:'The degree crossing the meridian below the horizon, exactly opposite the Midheaven.',history:'Latin imum coeli, "bottom of the sky"; the "subterraneous" place of the ancient astrologers, of parents, land and endings.',traditions:'Traditionally home, land, buildings, the father (in some authors the mother), hidden things and the end of matters: how a life, or any question, finally turns out. Modern astrology reads emotional foundations, ancestry, family patterns and the private self, and often the second half of life. Planets close to the IC tend to show at home and in the family, and in what you return to when everything else is taken away.',craft:'Home blessing, hearth and ancestor work can be tuned to its sign and ruler: an ancestor shelf, offerings to the land you live on, a protection charm buried by the door, and house blessings at the full Moon or when the Moon crosses your IC each month. Dark Moon and Samhain suit work with the dead.',care:'Needs a birth time.'},related:['midheaven','house-4']}]},894,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"CONCEPT_TERMS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t=[{id:'natal-chart',term:'Birth chart',category:'basics',aliases:['natal chart','birth charts','natal','horoscope'],simple:'A birth chart is a map of the sky at the moment and place you were born: where the Sun, Moon and planets were, and which signs were rising and overhead.',detailed:{sky:'Drawn as a wheel: the zodiac around the edge, the horizon across the middle (Ascendant on the left, Descendant on the right), the meridian up and down (Midheaven at the top, IC at the bottom), the houses as wedges, and lines for the aspects between planets.',history:'The oldest surviving personal horoscopes are Babylonian, from about 410 BCE. Hellenistic astrologers in Egypt (the Greek-speaking astrologers of the ancient Mediterranean, from the first century BCE onward) built the system of signs, houses and aspects that Western astrology still uses.',traditions:'Traditional astrology reads a chart for the shape of a life: health, wealth, relationships, timing. Modern psychological astrology reads it as a picture of the psyche. Vedic (Jyotisha) astrology uses the sidereal zodiac and its own techniques. This app shows the tropical chart by default, with sidereal as a choice.',craft:'Many witches use their chart to find personal allies: the chart ruler and strong planets point to days, hours, colours, herbs, stones and deities that suit them. The natal altar suggestion gathers these together.',care:'A chart is a language for reflection, not a verdict. Nothing in it decides who you are or what you will do.'},related:['zodiac','houses','aspects','ascendant','big-three'],links:[{kind:'tool',tool:'chart',label:'Your chart'}]},{id:'hellenistic-astrology',term:'Hellenistic astrology',category:'basics',aliases:['Hellenistic astrologers','Hellenistic astrologer','Hellenistic tradition','Hellenistic period','Hellenistic'],simple:'Hellenistic astrology means the astrology of a historical period: the Greek-speaking astrologers of the late ancient Mediterranean, from about the first century BCE, where Western astrology as we know it began. It is a name for a time and a body of texts, not a religion, and has nothing to do with Hellenic (Greek pagan) religious practice.',detailed:{history:'Historians call the centuries after Alexander the Great (who died in 323 BCE) the Hellenistic period, when Greek was the common language from Egypt to Persia. In that world, especially in Ptolemaic and Roman Egypt, astrologers joined Babylonian sky-omens and Egyptian star lore with Greek philosophy and mathematics, and built the horoscope: signs, houses, aspects and planetary rulers. Astrologers use "Hellenistic astrology" loosely for this tradition from about the first century BCE to around the seventh century CE. Its main surviving authors include Dorotheus of Sidon, Manilius (who wrote in Latin), Ptolemy, Vettius Valens, Firmicus Maternus (also Latin), Paulus Alexandrinus and Rhetorius.',traditions:'Its methods passed into Persian, Arabic and Indian astrology and then into medieval and Renaissance Europe. Since the 1990s, translation projects have made the Greek texts widely available, and many modern astrologers now practise "Hellenistic" or "traditional" astrology: whole sign houses, sect, lots such as the Part of Fortune, and time-lord techniques.',craft:'When this app says "Hellenistic astrologers" it means these ancient authors and their techniques, whatever gods they or their clients honoured (they wrote for a mixed Graeco-Roman and Egyptian world). If you follow a Hellenic path, the planetary gods and the old house names may still be meaningful to you, but the astrology itself belongs to everyone who studies it.',care:'Hellenistic (of the period after Alexander) and Hellenic (Greek, and in the craft the revived Greek religion, Hellenismos) are easy to confuse. The first is a date range for astrology; the second is a living religious path.'},related:['natal-chart','zodiac','houses','whole-sign','sect']},{id:'zodiac',term:'Zodiac',category:'basics',aliases:['the zodiac','zodiac sign','zodiac signs','star sign','signs'],simple:'The zodiac is the band of sky the Sun, Moon and planets travel through, divided into twelve signs of 30\xb0 each, from Aries to Pisces.',detailed:{sky:'Centred on the ecliptic, the Sun\'s yearly path, and about 8\xb0 wide either side, where the Moon and planets always appear. The signs are equal 30\xb0 divisions, not the constellations of the same names, which are of unequal sizes.',history:'Babylonian astronomers divided the ecliptic into twelve equal parts by about 500 BCE; the Greeks named it z\u014didiakos kyklos, the "circle of little animals".',traditions:'The tropical zodiac (Western) starts the signs at the spring equinox; the sidereal zodiac (Vedic and some Western astrologers) ties them to the stars. They are now about 24\xb0 apart.',craft:'The signs are a calendar of the year\'s moods (Aries season, Scorpio season) and a way to colour any working by the Moon\'s sign.',care:'Your "sign" in a magazine is only your Sun sign; you have all twelve somewhere in your chart.'},related:['ecliptic','tropical','sidereal','aries']},{id:'ecliptic',term:'Ecliptic',category:'basics',simple:'The ecliptic is the Sun\'s apparent yearly path across the sky. Positions in astrology are measured along it, in degrees from 0\xb0 Aries.',detailed:{sky:'The plane of Earth\'s orbit projected onto the sky, tilted about 23.4\xb0 to the celestial equator (the obliquity). Eclipses happen only when the Moon is on or near it, hence the name.',history:'Known to Babylonian astronomers; the Greeks measured its tilt.',traditions:'Every longitude in a chart, a planet\'s degree, is measured along the ecliptic.',craft:'Not used directly, but every timing tool rests on it.',care:'Latitude above or below the ecliptic is ignored in most charts.'},related:['zodiac','eclipse']},{id:'degree',term:'Degree',category:'basics',aliases:['degrees'],simple:'Each sign has 30 degrees (0\xb0 to 29\xb0). "Venus at 14\xb0 Leo" means Venus is a little under halfway through Leo.',detailed:{sky:'A degree is 1/360 of the circle; each is split into 60 minutes of arc (\u2032). The Sun moves about one degree a day, the Moon about thirteen.',history:'The 360-degree circle comes from Babylonian base-sixty mathematics.',traditions:'Some astrologers read 0\xb0 as raw beginnings and 29\xb0 (the "anaretic" degree) as urgency to complete; the Sabian symbols give each degree an image.',craft:'An exact degree can be marked on an altar or used to time a return.',care:'Degrees shift with birth time for the fast points (the Moon and angles).'},related:['zodiac','decans']},{id:'big-three',term:'Big three',category:'basics',aliases:['sun, moon and rising'],simple:'Your "big three" are your Sun, Moon and rising signs: core self, inner life and outer manner. Together they are a good first sketch of a chart.',detailed:{traditions:'A modern shorthand. Traditional astrologers would add the chart ruler and the planets on the angles.',craft:'Some witches keep a stone or herb for each of the three on their altar.',care:'Without a birth time you have a "big two".'},related:['sun','moon','ascendant']},{id:'chart-ruler',term:'Chart ruler',category:'basics',aliases:['ruler of the chart','chart rulers'],simple:'Your chart ruler is the planet that rules your rising sign. It is read as a guide for your whole chart, and makes a natural planetary ally.',detailed:{sky:'Found from the Ascendant\'s sign: Aries rising, Mars; Taurus rising, Venus; and so on.',history:'The ancient astrologers\u2019 "lord of the hour-marker", watched closely for the course of a life.',traditions:'Traditional astrologers use the seven classical rulers (Scorpio rising, Mars). Modern astrologers often use Pluto, Uranus and Neptune for Scorpio, Aquarius and Pisces; this app shows both.',craft:'Your chart ruler\'s day, hours, metals, colours, herbs, stones and deities make a personal set of correspondences; the app gathers them for you.',care:'Needs a birth time.'},related:['ascendant','rulership']},{id:'houses',term:'Houses',category:'basics',aliases:['house'],simple:'The houses are twelve parts of life (self, money, home, love, work and so on) laid over the sky around you at birth. A planet\'s house shows where in life it acts.',detailed:{sky:'The signs come from the Sun\'s path; the houses come from the Earth\'s daily turn, so they depend on the exact time and place of birth. The first house starts at the Ascendant and they run counter-clockwise below the horizon.',history:'Hellenistic astrologers (the Greek-speaking astrologers of the ancient Mediterranean, where Western astrology began) used "places" counted by whole signs from the rising sign; quadrant systems such as Porphyry and later Placidus divided the sky by angles and time.',traditions:'Angular houses (1, 4, 7, 10) are strongest; succedent (2, 5, 8, 11) build; cadent (3, 6, 9, 12) change and learn.',craft:'A house suggests which part of life a working belongs to, and which planets in your chart are your allies there.',care:'Without a birth time there are no true houses; the app can show a "solar chart" instead, clearly marked.'},related:['house-systems','house-1','cusp']},{id:'cusp',term:'Cusp',category:'basics',aliases:['cusps','house cusp','born on the cusp'],simple:'A cusp is a starting line: the beginning of a house, or the border between two signs. "Born on the cusp" means the Sun was near the edge of a sign.',detailed:{sky:'House cusps are calculated points; sign cusps are fixed at every 30\xb0.',history:'House cusps became central with quadrant house systems in the medieval period.',traditions:'A planet within a few degrees before a house cusp is often read in the next house. A "cusp" Sun is in one sign only: the exact degree decides which.',craft:'The sign on a house cusp, and its ruler, colours that part of life even when no planet is there.',care:'If you were "born on the cusp", your chart settles which sign it is, given your birth time.'},related:['houses','house-systems']},{id:'decans',term:'Decans',category:'basics',aliases:['decan','faces'],simple:'Each sign splits into three decans of 10\xb0, each with its own sub-ruler and flavour.',detailed:{sky:'Thirty-six ten-degree slices of the zodiac.',history:'The decans began as 36 Egyptian star groups that rose in turn through the night and marked ten-day weeks; the ancient Greek-speaking astrologers folded them into the signs. Later magic (the Picatrix, Agrippa) gave each an image.',traditions:'The "faces" rule them by the Chaldean order starting with Mars at 0\xb0 Aries. A modern method rules them by the three signs of the same element. The app shows both.',craft:'Decan images and their rulers are used in talismanic magic and in tarot: each minor arcana card from 2 to 10 is assigned a decan in the Golden Dawn system.',care:'A small refinement; the sign and house matter far more.'},related:['degree','chaldean-order']},{id:'sect',term:'Sect',category:'basics',aliases:['day chart','night chart','diurnal','nocturnal'],simple:'Sect is whether you were born by day (the Sun above the horizon) or by night. Old astrologers read some planets as kinder in a day chart and others by night.',detailed:{sky:'A day chart has the Sun in houses 7 to 12; a night chart in houses 1 to 6.',history:'A cornerstone of Hellenistic astrology (the astrology of the ancient Greek-speaking world, where Western astrology began), revived by modern traditional astrologers.',traditions:'Day sect: the Sun, Jupiter and Saturn; night sect: the Moon, Venus and Mars. A malefic of the chart\'s own sect is gentler (Saturn by day, Mars by night).',craft:'Some witches favour their sect\'s light: day-born working by the Sun, night-born by the Moon.',care:'Needs a birth time.'},related:['part-of-fortune','lots']},{id:'lots',term:'Lots',category:'basics',aliases:['Arabic parts'],simple:'Lots, or "parts", are points calculated from three others, like the Part of Fortune. Each answers a particular question in a chart.',detailed:{history:'Hellenistic astrologers (the Greek-speaking astrologers of the ancient Mediterranean, where Western astrology began) used a handful (Fortune, Spirit, Eros, Necessity); medieval Arabic astrologers listed hundreds, hence "Arabic parts".',traditions:'This app shows the Part of Fortune.',care:'Lots move as fast as the Ascendant; they need an accurate birth time.'},related:['part-of-fortune','sect']},{id:'elements',term:'Elements',category:'element',aliases:['element','triplicity','triplicities'],simple:'Each sign belongs to one of four elements: fire (spirit, drive), earth (body, practice), air (mind, connection) and water (feeling, intuition).',detailed:{sky:'The signs cycle fire, earth, air, water from Aries; the three signs of one element sit 120\xb0 apart, in trine.',history:'The four elements come from Empedocles (fifth century BCE); astrologers paired them with the signs, called "triplicities".',traditions:'Traditional astrology gives each triplicity day and night rulers. Modern astrology reads the balance of elements as temperament: a chart heavy in water, light in air, and so on.',craft:'The same four elements as the circle\'s quarters. Your dominant element can suggest where you feel at home in ritual; a missing one, what to invite.',care:'A "missing" element is not a lack: people often seek it in others or develop it with care.'},related:['fire','earth','air','water','modalities']},{id:'fire',term:'Fire',category:'element',aliases:['fire signs','fire sign'],simple:'The fire signs, Aries, Leo and Sagittarius, are spirited, warm, bold and inspired.',detailed:{traditions:'Hot and dry in the old physics; the choleric temperament. Modern: enthusiasm, will, creativity, impatience.',craft:'Candle magic, courage, passion, transformation, banishing by burning. South in many traditions (north in some southern-hemisphere practice).',care:'Fire needs fuel and tending; it burns out without rest.'},related:['aries','leo','sagittarius','elements']},{id:'earth',term:'Earth',category:'element',aliases:['earth signs','earth sign'],simple:'The earth signs, Taurus, Virgo and Capricorn, are practical, steady, sensual and grounded.',detailed:{traditions:'Cold and dry; the melancholic temperament. Modern: realism, patience, the body, material life.',craft:'Grounding, money and prosperity, herbs and stones, burying spells, home and land. North in many traditions.',care:'Earth can settle into ruts; it grows by being turned.'},related:['taurus','virgo','capricorn','elements']},{id:'air',term:'Air',category:'element',aliases:['air signs','air sign'],simple:'The air signs, Gemini, Libra and Aquarius, are thoughtful, social, curious and communicative.',detailed:{traditions:'Hot and moist; the sanguine temperament. Modern: ideas, language, relationship, detachment.',craft:'Word and breath magic, incense and smoke, divination, communication, travel. East in many traditions.',care:'Air can live in the head; it needs earth to land its ideas.'},related:['gemini','libra','aquarius','elements']},{id:'water',term:'Water',category:'element',aliases:['water signs','water sign'],simple:'The water signs, Cancer, Scorpio and Pisces, are feeling, intuitive, caring and deep.',detailed:{traditions:'Cold and moist; the phlegmatic temperament. Modern: emotion, empathy, memory, the unconscious.',craft:'Moon water, cleansing baths, scrying, dreamwork, healing and love. West in many traditions.',care:'Water takes the shape of its container; boundaries help it keep its own.'},related:['cancer','scorpio','pisces','elements']},{id:'modalities',term:'Modalities',category:'element',aliases:['modality','quadruplicities'],simple:'Each sign is cardinal (starts things), fixed (keeps them going) or mutable (adapts and changes them).',detailed:{sky:'Cardinal signs begin each season at the equinoxes and solstices; fixed signs hold the season\'s middle; mutable signs bring its end. Signs of one modality are 90\xb0 apart.',history:'Ptolemy\'s "tropical, solid and bicorporeal" signs.',traditions:'Modern astrology reads the balance of modalities as how you act: initiating, sustaining or adapting.',craft:'Cardinal times for beginnings, fixed for holding and protecting, mutable for change and release.',care:'Most charts mix all three.'},related:['cardinal','fixed','mutable','square']},{id:'cardinal',term:'Cardinal',category:'element',aliases:['cardinal signs'],simple:'Cardinal signs (Aries, Cancer, Libra, Capricorn) start the seasons: initiative, leadership and beginnings.',detailed:{traditions:'The "movable" signs of traditional astrology, quick to begin and to change.',craft:'The Sun\'s entry into a cardinal sign is an equinox or solstice: four great turning points of the wheel.',care:'Starting is easy; seeing through needs help from the fixed signs.'},related:['modalities','aries','cancer','libra','capricorn']},{id:'fixed',term:'Fixed',category:'element',aliases:['fixed signs'],simple:'Fixed signs (Taurus, Leo, Scorpio, Aquarius) hold the middle of each season: steady, loyal and determined.',detailed:{traditions:'The "solid" signs. The four fixed signs match the four living creatures (bull, lion, eagle, angel) of Ezekiel and the evangelists, and the cross-quarter festivals fall in them.',craft:'Protection, binding, long-term spells, and the cross-quarter sabbats: Beltane, Lughnasadh, Samhain, Imbolc.',care:'Steadiness can become stubbornness.'},related:['modalities','taurus','leo','scorpio','aquarius']},{id:'mutable',term:'Mutable',category:'element',aliases:['mutable signs'],simple:'Mutable signs (Gemini, Virgo, Sagittarius, Pisces) end each season: flexible, adaptable and in motion.',detailed:{traditions:'The "bicorporeal" (double-bodied) signs: twins, two fish, centaur, and the maiden with her sheaf.',craft:'Change, release, travel, learning and adapting spells.',care:'Flexibility can scatter; mutable signs grow with a centre.'},related:['modalities','gemini','virgo','sagittarius','pisces']},{id:'polarity',term:'Polarity',category:'element',aliases:['yin and yang','masculine and feminine signs','positive and negative signs'],simple:'The signs alternate between outward, active (fire and air) and inward, receptive (earth and water) polarity.',detailed:{history:'Old texts call them masculine and feminine, or diurnal and nocturnal; modern writers often prefer yang and yin, or active and receptive.',traditions:'Read as a lean toward expressing or taking in. This app uses yang and yin.',craft:'Projective and receptive tools and gestures in ritual mirror the same idea.',care:'These are labels for kinds of energy, not for people\'s genders.'},related:['elements']},{id:'rulership',term:'Rulership',category:'dignity',aliases:['ruled by','traditional ruler','modern ruler','rulerships'],simple:'Each sign has a ruling planet, its "host": Mars rules Aries, Venus rules Taurus, and so on. A planet in a sign it rules is at home.',detailed:{sky:'The traditional scheme follows the planets\' speeds outward from the lights: the Sun and Moon rule Leo and Cancer, then Mercury, Venus, Mars, Jupiter and Saturn each rule a pair, symmetrically.',history:'Set out in the ancient Greek astrological sources and by Ptolemy. After Uranus, Neptune and Pluto were found, modern astrologers gave them Aquarius, Pisces and Scorpio.',traditions:'Traditional: Aries and Scorpio Mars; Taurus and Libra Venus; Gemini and Virgo Mercury; Cancer the Moon; Leo the Sun; Sagittarius and Pisces Jupiter; Capricorn and Aquarius Saturn. Modern adds Scorpio Pluto, Aquarius Uranus, Pisces Neptune. The app shows both.',craft:'A sign\'s ruler gives its correspondences: a working timed to the Moon in Libra may borrow Venus\'s herbs and day.',care:'Neither scheme is "wrong"; they answer different questions.'},related:['dignity','chart-ruler','domicile']},{id:'dignity',term:'Dignity',category:'dignity',aliases:['dignities','essential dignity','debility','peregrine'],simple:'Dignity is how comfortable a planet is in a sign: at home (domicile), honoured (exaltation), away from home (detriment) or humbled (fall).',detailed:{history:'The ancient and medieval system of essential dignities, which also includes the finer triplicity, term and face rulers.',traditions:'Traditional astrology reads dignity as a planet\'s means to act well; a planet in detriment or fall must work harder or in a roundabout way. Modern astrology uses it more loosely, as comfort. A planet with none of the four is "peregrine", a wanderer.',craft:'Some witches time planetary talismans for when the planet is dignified (Venus in Taurus or Libra, Jupiter in Sagittarius or Pisces).',care:'A planet in fall in your chart isn\'t weak in you; it often means a skill hard-won.'},related:['domicile','exaltation','detriment','fall','rulership']},{id:'domicile',term:'Domicile',category:'dignity',aliases:['in domicile','at home','in its own sign'],simple:'A planet in domicile is in the sign it rules: at home and able to act fully in its own way.',detailed:{traditions:'The strongest essential dignity. Mars in Aries or Scorpio, Venus in Taurus or Libra, the Moon in Cancer, the Sun in Leo, and so on.',craft:'Planetary magic is often timed for when the planet is in its own sign.',care:'Comfort can mean less growth, not more virtue.'},related:['dignity','rulership','detriment']},{id:'exaltation',term:'Exaltation',category:'dignity',aliases:['exalted'],simple:'A planet in exaltation is an honoured guest: at its best, admired, sometimes a little idealised.',detailed:{history:'Possibly from Babylonian "secret houses" of the planets. Ptolemy lists: the Sun in Aries, the Moon in Taurus, Mercury in Virgo, Venus in Pisces, Mars in Capricorn, Jupiter in Cancer, Saturn in Libra.',traditions:'Traditional astrology counts it the second strongest dignity. The outer planets have no agreed exaltation.',craft:'Exaltation degrees (the Sun at 19\xb0 Aries, the Moon at 3\xb0 Taurus, Venus at 27\xb0 Pisces and others) are favoured for talismans in the old texts.',care:'Exaltation is a kind of pedestal; it can be lonely up there.'},related:['dignity','fall']},{id:'detriment',term:'Detriment',category:'dignity',aliases:['in detriment','exile'],simple:'A planet in detriment is in the sign opposite its home: away from its comforts, so it works in unfamiliar ways.',detailed:{traditions:'Venus in Aries or Scorpio, Mars in Taurus or Libra, the Moon in Capricorn, the Sun in Aquarius, Saturn in Cancer or Leo, and so on. Traditional astrology reads difficulty; modern astrology, unconventional expression.',craft:'Avoid timing a planet\'s magic while it is in detriment, if you can choose.',care:'Not a defect in a person; often a source of originality.'},related:['dignity','domicile']},{id:'fall',term:'Fall',category:'dignity',aliases:['in fall'],simple:'A planet in fall is in the sign opposite its exaltation: humbled, unseen or underestimated.',detailed:{traditions:'The Sun in Libra, the Moon in Scorpio, Mercury in Pisces, Venus in Virgo, Mars in Cancer, Jupiter in Capricorn, Saturn in Aries.',craft:'As with detriment, a poor time to call on that planet if you can wait.',care:'Again, a story of effort, not of failing.'},related:['dignity','exaltation']},{id:'solstice-equinox',term:'Solstices and equinoxes',category:'basics',aliases:['solstice','solstices','equinox','equinoxes','quarter days'],simple:'The solstices and equinoxes are the four moments the Sun enters the cardinal signs: Aries and Libra at the equinoxes, Cancer and Capricorn at the solstices. They are the quarter points of the Wheel of the Year.',detailed:{sky:'At the equinoxes the Sun crosses the celestial equator and day and night are nearly equal everywhere; at the solstices it reaches its farthest north or south and the days are longest or shortest. In the tropical zodiac they fall at exactly 0\xb0 Aries, Cancer, Libra and Capricorn.',history:'Marked by monuments from Newgrange to Stonehenge to Chich\xe9n Itz\xe1; the four "quarter days" of the year in many calendars.',traditions:'Tropical astrology is defined by them; mundane astrologers cast charts for each "ingress" to forecast the season.',craft:'Ostara, Litha, Mabon and Yule in the modern Wheel of the Year (reversed in the southern hemisphere\'s Wheel): balance at the equinoxes, the height and the return of the light at the solstices.',care:'The exact moment varies by a day either way from year to year and by time zone.'},related:['tropical','cardinal','sun','ingress']},{id:'retrograde',term:'Retrograde',category:'motion',symbol:'\u211e',aliases:['retrogrades','retrograde motion','Rx','goes retrograde','turns retrograde'],simple:'A planet is retrograde when it seems to move backwards through the zodiac for a while. Astrologers read it as a time to review, redo and turn inward.',detailed:{sky:'An illusion of perspective: as Earth overtakes an outer planet (or an inner planet overtakes Earth), it appears to slide backwards against the stars before moving on. The Sun and Moon never go retrograde. Mercury does it three or four times a year, Venus every 19 months, Mars every 26 months, and Jupiter to Pluto every year for four to five months.',history:'Puzzled ancient astronomers; Ptolemy explained it with epicycles, Copernicus with a moving Earth. Astrologers long read retrograde planets as weakened or acting in reverse.',traditions:'Traditional: a debility, the planet "unable to keep its promises". Modern: internalisation, reflection, revisiting. Natal retrogrades are read as an inward, individual way with that planet.',craft:'Retrogrades suit re- words: review, revise, return, reconnect, release. Many witches avoid launching big new workings under Mercury retrograde, and use it for divination about the past, ancestor work and finishing old projects.',care:'Fear around retrogrades is mostly unearned. Nothing stops working; you are asked to pay attention.'},related:['station','shadow-period','mercury']},{id:'station',term:'Station',category:'motion',aliases:['stations','stationary','stations retrograde','stations direct'],simple:'A station is when a planet seems to stand still before turning retrograde or direct again. Its energy is read as concentrated there.',detailed:{sky:'At the turning point the planet\'s apparent speed falls to zero for a few hours (Mercury) to days (outer planets).',history:'Traditional astrologers read a planet "stationing direct" as gaining strength, "stationing retrograde" as faltering.',traditions:'A station on a natal planet or angle is felt strongly; modern astrologers watch the degree of each station.',craft:'The days of a station are potent and slow; many witches rest rather than cast on them, and work after.',care:'A few days, not a crisis.'},related:['retrograde','shadow-period']},{id:'shadow-period',term:'Shadow period',category:'motion',aliases:['pre-shadow','post-shadow','retrograde shadow'],simple:'The shadow is the stretch of zodiac a planet covers three times during a retrograde. Before the retrograde (pre-shadow) its themes stir; after it (post-shadow) they settle.',detailed:{sky:'The pre-shadow begins when the planet first reaches the degree where it will later station direct; the post-shadow ends when it passes the degree where it stationed retrograde. For Mercury, about two weeks either side.',history:'A modern refinement, popular since the late twentieth century.',traditions:'Read as the build-up and integration of the retrograde\'s lessons.',craft:'In the pre-shadow, back up and check; in the post-shadow, move forward with what you revised.',care:'With shadows included, Mercury is in some phase of retrograde for much of the year; keep it in proportion.'},related:['retrograde','station']},{id:'ingress',term:'Ingress',category:'motion',aliases:['ingresses'],simple:'An ingress is a planet entering a new sign. The Sun\'s ingress into Aries, the spring equinox, begins the astrological year.',detailed:{sky:'The moment a body\'s longitude crosses a multiple of 30\xb0.',history:'Mundane astrologers cast charts for the Sun\'s ingresses into the cardinal signs to forecast the season.',traditions:'A planet just entering a sign is read as new to it, finding its feet.',craft:'The Sun\'s ingresses mark the solstices and equinoxes; the Moon\'s ingress ends its void of course.',care:'Planets near the end of a sign may move back and forth across it in a retrograde.'},related:['void-of-course','zodiac']},{id:'lunation',term:'Lunation',category:'moon',aliases:['lunations','lunar cycle','synodic month'],simple:'A lunation is one full cycle of the Moon\'s phases, new to new, about 29.5 days. "Lunation" also names a new or full moon itself.',detailed:{sky:'The Moon\'s cycle relative to the Sun: new moon (conjunction), first quarter (square), full moon (opposition), last quarter (square).',history:'Lunar months are the basis of the Hebrew, Islamic, Chinese and ancient Greek calendars, among many others.',traditions:'Astrologers cast charts for each new and full moon (lunation charts) for the month ahead; the sign and house of a new moon in your chart show where something is beginning.',craft:'Waxing to draw in, full to charge and reveal, waning to release, dark and new to rest and plant. The sign the lunation falls in colours the work.',care:'The Moon\'s phase is the same everywhere on Earth at once, but the date can differ by time zone.'},related:['moon-phases','new-moon','full-moon','eclipse'],links:[{kind:'moon-phase',phase:'new',label:'The New Moon'},{kind:'moon-phase',phase:'full',label:'The Full Moon'}]},{id:'moon-phases',term:'Moon phases',category:'moon',aliases:['phase','phases','waxing','waning','Waxing Crescent','Waxing Gibbous','Waning Gibbous','Waning Crescent','dark moon','balsamic'],simple:'The Moon waxes (grows) from new to full and wanes (shrinks) from full to new. Each phase has its traditional kind of work.',detailed:{sky:'The lit part of the Moon we see depends on its angle from the Sun: 0\xb0 new, 90\xb0 first quarter, 180\xb0 full, 270\xb0 last quarter. Crescent and gibbous fill the gaps.',history:'Dane Rudhyar named eight phases for the soul\'s cycle, including the balsamic phase before the new moon; folk practice has always planted and harvested by them.',traditions:'A natal "lunar phase" (the angle of your Moon from your Sun) is read by some astrologers as temperament; progressions use a progressed lunar phase over about thirty years.',craft:'Waxing crescent: hopes and first steps. First quarter: action. Waxing gibbous: refining. Full: power and gratitude. Waning gibbous: sharing and thanks. Last quarter: release. Waning crescent and dark: banishing, rest, reflection.',care:'Phases are guides, not rules. Work when you need to.'},related:['lunation','new-moon','full-moon']},{id:'new-moon',term:'New Moon',category:'moon',aliases:['new moons'],simple:'The New Moon is the Sun and Moon together: the dark start of a lunar cycle, for intentions and planting seeds.',detailed:{sky:'The exact Sun\u2013Moon conjunction; the Moon is invisible, lost in the Sun\'s glare, for about a day either side. The first thin crescent appears a day or two later.',history:'Many calendars begin the month at the first visible crescent rather than the astronomical new moon.',traditions:'The sign and house of each new moon in your chart are read as where something begins.',craft:'Intentions, new workings, seed spells, rest. Some keep the dark moon itself for banishing and the new crescent for beginnings.',care:'If a new moon is also a solar eclipse, many practitioners watch rather than cast.'},related:['lunation','solar-eclipse','conjunction'],links:[{kind:'moon-phase',phase:'new',label:'The New Moon page'}]},{id:'full-moon',term:'Full Moon',category:'moon',aliases:['full moons'],simple:'The Full Moon is the Moon opposite the Sun, fully lit: a peak of power for charging, celebrating, divining and giving thanks.',detailed:{sky:'The exact Sun\u2013Moon opposition. The Moon rises near sunset and sets near sunrise.',history:'Esbats, gatherings at the full moon, are central to modern Wicca; each month\'s full moon has folk names (Wolf, Harvest, Hunter\'s).',traditions:'Read as culmination and revelation in the sign it falls in.',craft:'Charging tools and moon water, esbats, divination, gratitude, and releasing at its peak.',care:'If it is also a lunar eclipse, the energy is read as stronger and stranger.'},related:['lunation','lunar-eclipse','opposition'],links:[{kind:'moon-phase',phase:'full',label:'The Full Moon page'}]},{id:'quarter-moons',term:'Quarter moons',category:'moon',aliases:['First Quarter','Last Quarter','quarter moon','half moon'],simple:'At the quarter moons half the Moon is lit. The first quarter (waxing) is for action; the last quarter (waning) for release.',detailed:{sky:'The Sun\u2013Moon squares: 90\xb0 (first quarter) and 270\xb0 (last quarter).',traditions:'Read as crisis points in the lunar cycle: of action and of reorientation.',craft:'First quarter: push through obstacles. Last quarter: clear, break habits, give away.',care:'A natural point to check in on a month-long working.'},related:['moon-phases','square'],links:[{kind:'moon-phase',phase:'firstQuarter',label:'The First Quarter page'},{kind:'moon-phase',phase:'lastQuarter',label:'The Last Quarter page'}]},{id:'void-of-course',term:'Void of course',category:'moon',aliases:['void-of-course','void of course Moon','void-of-course Moon','VoC','Moon void of course'],simple:'The Moon is "void of course" from its last major aspect in a sign until it enters the next sign. Traditionally it is a time to rest and finish rather than start.',detailed:{sky:'Every couple of days, for a few minutes to more than a day. The app counts the Moon\'s conjunctions, sextiles, squares, trines and oppositions to the Sun and planets through Pluto, as most modern ephemerides do.',history:'From medieval horary astrology (William Lilly, 1647): matters begun while the Moon is void "come to nothing". Modern popularity owes much to Al Morrison in the 1970s.',traditions:'Some astrologers use only the seven classical planets, giving longer void periods; others make exceptions for the Moon in Taurus, Cancer, Sagittarius and Pisces.',craft:'Rest, meditation, reflection, finishing, cleaning and journaling. Many witches don\'t begin new workings while the Moon is void, but use it for open-ended spiritual work.',care:'Life doesn\'t stop; if you must start something, start it.'},related:['moon','aspects','ingress'],links:[{kind:'tool',tool:'hours',label:'Today\'s void of course'}]},{id:'eclipse',term:'Eclipse',category:'eclipse',aliases:['eclipses','eclipse season','eclipse seasons'],simple:'An eclipse is a new or full moon close to the Moon\'s nodes, when the Sun, Moon and Earth line up and one throws a shadow on another. Astrologers read eclipses as powerful turning points.',detailed:{sky:'Because the Moon\'s orbit is tilted, the three line up only when a new or full moon falls near a node. That happens in two "eclipse seasons" a year, about six months apart, each with two or three eclipses.',history:'Babylonian astronomers predicted eclipses with the Saros cycle; eclipses were omens for kings across the ancient world.',traditions:'Read as sudden beginnings (solar) and endings or revelations (lunar), with effects unfolding over months. Eclipses on your natal planets or angles are watched closely.',craft:'Many witches rest and observe during an eclipse rather than casting, and work with its themes after. Others use lunar eclipses for powerful release.',care:'Never look at a solar eclipse without proper eclipse glasses. And eclipses are not dangerous to people, pets or plants; old fears are just that.'},related:['solar-eclipse','lunar-eclipse','north-node','saros']},{id:'solar-eclipse',term:'Solar eclipse',category:'eclipse',aliases:['solar eclipses','total solar eclipse','annular eclipse','partial solar eclipse','hybrid eclipse'],simple:'A solar eclipse is a new moon passing in front of the Sun. It is read as a supercharged new moon: a sudden new beginning.',detailed:{sky:'Total when the Moon covers the Sun, annular when it is too far away and leaves a ring, partial when it only clips it; hybrid ones change along their path. Only seen from a narrow track on Earth.',history:'Eclipses that darkened the day were omens of kings\' deaths; Thales is said to have predicted one in 585 BCE.',traditions:'Read as beginnings, often unplanned, in the sign and house where they fall.',craft:'Watch, journal and set intentions afterwards; many avoid casting at the moment itself.',care:'Eye safety first: proper eclipse viewers only.'},related:['eclipse','new-moon']},{id:'lunar-eclipse',term:'Lunar eclipse',category:'eclipse',aliases:['lunar eclipses','total lunar eclipse','partial lunar eclipse','penumbral eclipse','blood moon'],simple:'A lunar eclipse is a full moon passing through Earth\'s shadow. It is read as a supercharged full moon: endings, revelations and release.',detailed:{sky:'Total when the Moon is wholly in the umbra (often turning copper-red, the "blood moon"), partial when part of it is, penumbral when it only dims. Visible from the whole night side of Earth.',history:'Read as omens everywhere; Columbus used one predicted by an almanac in 1504.',traditions:'Read as culminations and things coming to light, in the sign and house where they fall.',craft:'Powerful for release and shadow work; safe to watch with the naked eye.',care:'A penumbral eclipse is very subtle; it may not be noticeable at all.'},related:['eclipse','full-moon']},{id:'saros',term:'Saros',category:'eclipse',aliases:['Saros cycle','Saros series'],simple:'The Saros is a cycle of about 18 years and 11 days after which a very similar eclipse happens again.',detailed:{sky:'223 synodic months nearly equal 242 draconic months and 239 anomalistic months, so the Sun, Moon and node line up again in almost the same way. Each eclipse belongs to a Saros series that runs for over a thousand years.',history:'Used by Babylonian astronomers to predict eclipses; the name was given by Edmond Halley in 1691.',traditions:'Some astrologers read an eclipse as echoing the one 18 years before it in the same series.',craft:'A journal kept across a Saros shows the echo.',care:'Echoes, not repeats.'},related:['eclipse','north-node']},{id:'planetary-days',term:'Planetary days',category:'timing',aliases:['planetary day','day ruler','ruler of the day'],simple:'Each day of the week belongs to one of the seven classical planets: Sunday the Sun, Monday the Moon, Tuesday Mars, Wednesday Mercury, Thursday Jupiter, Friday Venus, Saturday Saturn.',detailed:{sky:'Not something in the sky: a scheme that follows from the planetary hours. The planet that rules the first hour after sunrise rules the day.',history:'The seven-day planetary week spread through the Roman Empire by the first century; the names survive in Romance languages (lunes, martes, mercredi, jeudi, vendredi) and, through Norse and Anglo-Saxon gods, in English (Tiw, Woden, Thor, Frigg).',traditions:'Late ancient (Graeco-Egyptian), medieval and Renaissance magic (the Heptameron, Agrippa, the Key of Solomon) timed work by day and hour.',craft:'Choose the day whose planet suits the working: Venus\'s Friday for love, Saturn\'s Saturday for banishing and protection. Today\'s correspondences card shows the day\'s planet.',care:'A day is a helpful start; the planetary hour can refine it, and life sometimes chooses the day for you.'},related:['planetary-hours','chaldean-order'],links:[{kind:'tool',tool:'hours',label:'Planetary days and hours'}]},{id:'planetary-hours',term:'Planetary hours',category:'timing',aliases:['planetary hour'],simple:'Day and night are each split into twelve "hours", long in summer days and short in winter ones, each ruled in turn by a planet. Witches time spells by them.',detailed:{sky:'From sunrise to sunset is divided into twelve equal parts, and from sunset to the next sunrise into twelve more, so the hours stretch and shrink with the seasons and your latitude. They need your place: the app uses your real local sunrise and sunset.',history:'Ancient in origin (from the Greek-speaking world of the Roman era), used across the Roman world; the order of rulers follows the Chaldean order. Renaissance grimoires give tables for every day.',traditions:'The first hour of the day belongs to the day\'s planet, then the others follow in Chaldean order: Saturn, Jupiter, Mars, Sun, Venus, Mercury, Moon, round and round.',craft:'Time a working to the hour of the planet that suits it, ideally on that planet\'s day: a Venus hour on a Friday for love, a Jupiter hour on a Thursday for luck. The planner and reminders can find the next one.',care:'Near the poles, in summer and winter the Sun may not rise or set, and the hours don\'t exist.'},related:['planetary-days','chaldean-order'],links:[{kind:'tool',tool:'hours',label:'Planetary hours where you are'}]},{id:'chaldean-order',term:'Chaldean order',category:'timing',aliases:['Chaldean'],simple:'The Chaldean order lists the seven classical planets from slowest to fastest: Saturn, Jupiter, Mars, Sun, Venus, Mercury, Moon. Planetary hours cycle through it.',detailed:{sky:'Their apparent speeds against the stars: Saturn slowest (29 years), the Moon fastest (27 days).',history:'Named for the Chaldeans (Babylonian astronomers) by Greek and Roman writers. Run twenty-four hours a day through this order, and each day begins with the planet three places on: Sun, Moon, Mars, Mercury, Jupiter, Venus, Saturn, which is the order of our week.',traditions:'Also gives the ruler of each decan (the "faces") and the order of the spheres in medieval cosmology.',craft:'The order behind every planetary hour and the seven-day week.',care:'A beautiful piece of old arithmetic, not a law of the sky.'},related:['planetary-hours','planetary-days','decans']}]},895,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"HOUSE_TERMS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return n}});var s=r(d[0]);const t=['first','second','third','fourth','fifth','sixth','seventh','eighth','ninth','tenth','eleventh','twelfth'],o={angular:'An angular house (1, 4, 7, 10): the strongest places, where planets act most visibly.',succedent:'A succedent house (2, 5, 8, 11): the houses that follow the angles, of resources and what is built up.',cadent:'A cadent house (3, 6, 9, 12): the houses that "fall away" from the angles, of learning, adjusting and change.'},n=s.HOUSE_MEANINGS.map(s=>({id:`house-${s.house}`,term:`${t[s.house-1][0].toUpperCase()}${t[s.house-1].slice(1)} house`,category:'house',symbol:String(s.house),aliases:[`${s.house}${1===s.house?'st':2===s.house?'nd':3===s.house?'rd':'th'} house`,`house ${s.house}`],simple:s.simple,detailed:{sky:`${o[s.kind]} Its natural sign, the one that would sit on it if Aries were rising, is ${s.naturalSign}. Where its cusp falls depends on the house system (see House systems).`,traditions:s.detailed,craft:s.craft,care:`Keywords: ${s.keywords.join(', ')}. A house without planets is not empty of meaning: read its cusp's sign and where that sign's ruler sits.`},related:['houses','house-systems',1===s.house?'ascendant':4===s.house?'imum-coeli':7===s.house?'descendant':10===s.house?'midheaven':'cusp']}))},896,[897]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"ordinalHouse",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return n}}),Object.defineProperty(e,"HELLENISTIC_ASTROLOGERS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}}),Object.defineProperty(e,"HOUSE_KIND_TEXT",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return s}}),Object.defineProperty(e,"HOUSE_MEANINGS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return l}}),Object.defineProperty(e,"houseAllies",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return u}});const o=['first','second','third','fourth','fifth','sixth','seventh','eighth','ninth','tenth','eleventh','twelfth'],n=n=>`${o[n-1]} house`,t='the Hellenistic astrologers (the Greek-speaking astrologers of the late ancient Mediterranean, from about the first century BCE, where Western astrology began)',s={angular:'This is an angular house, one of the four that begin at the angles of the chart (the Ascendant, the IC, the Descendant and the Midheaven). Traditional astrologers counted the angles the strongest places in a chart: planets here act soonest and most visibly, and what they promise tends to happen in the open, where other people can see it. Modern astrologers link the angular houses with the cardinal signs and with initiative. A planet within a few degrees of the angle itself is one of the loudest voices in the whole chart.',succedent:'This is a succedent house, one of the four that follow (succeed) the angles. Traditionally these were places of middling strength: what an angle begins, the house after it gathers, holds and builds on, which is why so many succedent houses are about resources of one kind or another. Modern astrologers link them with the fixed signs and with staying power. Planets here tend to work steadily and over time, through what accumulates, rather than through sudden events.',cadent:'This is a cadent house, one of the four that fall away (from the Latin cadere, to fall) from the angles. Traditional astrologers counted these the weakest places, where planets struggle to act out in the world and work in the background instead. Modern astrologers link them with the mutable signs and read them as houses of learning, adjustment and preparation. Some also point to Michel Gauquelin\'s twentieth-century statistical studies, which found planets just past the rising and culminating points (so in the twelfth and ninth houses) unusually often in the charts of eminent people, though those results are still argued over. Read planets here as quietly influential rather than simply weak.'},h='Traditionally this is also one of the four houses (the second, sixth, eighth and twelfth) that make no classical aspect to the Ascendant. Older astrologers said such places "cannot see" the native, so their matters can feel harder to steer directly.',l=[{house:1,name:'The house of self',keywords:['body','appearance','temperament','beginnings','vitality'],kind:'angular',naturalSign:'Aries',joy:'Mercury',simple:'The first house is you: your body, your way of meeting the world, and how life begins for you. It starts at the Ascendant.',detailed:[`In traditional astrology the first house is the h\u014droskopos, the "hour-marker", which ${t} also called the helm of the chart: the place that steers everything else. It shows life itself, the body and its health, the temperament, the face and manner, and the general direction a life takes. It is the joy of Mercury, the house where Mercury was said to rejoice, because the first breath is where the mind and the body first meet the world. Medieval astrologers such as Abu Ma'shar and, later, William Lilly read it for the native's life, stature and complexion, and for the start of any matter.`,'Modern astrology reads the first house as the self-image and the persona: the part of you people meet first, your instinctive way of starting things, your physical energy and how you take up space. Psychological astrologers see it as the mask that is also a real face, the style you have grown into because it worked.','In a life, the first house shows how you arrive: whether you walk into a room quietly or announce yourself, how quickly you act, how robust or sensitive your body is, and how you handle new beginnings of any kind, from new jobs to new places. Because it starts at the Ascendant, which moves a degree about every four minutes, it is the most personal house in the chart and the one most dependent on an accurate birth time.',s.angular,'The sign on the cusp is your rising sign, and its ruler is the ruler of the whole chart, so the first house is read twice over: through the rising sign\'s manner and through wherever that ruler sits. Planets in the first house are hard to hide. They show in the body and the face, in first impressions and in how you meet challenges: Mars here often gives a quick, bold manner, Venus a pleasing one, Saturn a reserved and serious one, the Moon a changeable and responsive one. A planet close to the Ascendant itself is especially strong and may describe you better than your Sun sign does.'].join('\n\n'),magic:'Planets here are part of your personal power: lean on their days, hours and correspondences in any working for yourself. Self-blessing, glamour and protection of the body begin here.',craft:'The first house is the house of workings for yourself: self-blessing, glamour, protection of the body, health and vitality spells, and any rite of new beginning or new identity, such as a dedication, a name taken in the craft, or a new chapter of life. Time them by your chart ruler: its day of the week and its planetary hours (see the Hours tool) are your strongest personal times, and the days when the Moon passes over your Ascendant each month are good for small acts of self-care and renewal. A rising-sign altar is a good start: a candle in your rising sign\'s colour, a stone for the chart ruler, and an object that stands for your body and your face to the world. Rosemary for clarity and protection, Basil and Carnelian for courage in beginnings, and Clear Quartz as a general amplifier all suit first-house work, and a simple offering of water and bread to the start of each new year of your life (your birthday, or your solar return) honours it.'},{house:2,name:'The house of resources',keywords:['money','possessions','values','self-worth','livelihood'],kind:'succedent',naturalSign:'Taurus',simple:'The second house is what you have and what you value: money, belongings, skills you earn by, and your sense of worth.',detailed:[`${t[0].toUpperCase()}${t.slice(1)} called the second house the "gate of Hades" in some texts, as the house that rises next after the Ascendant (it is still below the horizon, under the earth). Its main meaning was livelihood: the native's possessions, the means of living, and how wealth comes and goes. Medieval and Renaissance astrologers kept this, reading the second house for riches, movable goods (what you can carry, as opposed to land, which belongs to the fourth), loans and the profit of any undertaking. In horary astrology it shows the querent's money and things they own.`,'Modern astrology widens it from money to values: what you consider worth having, your sense of your own worth, your talents and earning skills, and your relationship with comfort, the body\'s pleasures and security. Many astrologers read it as the house of what you can rely on, whether that is savings, a trade or simply a steady sense of being enough.','In a life, the second house shows how you earn and spend, whether money comes easily or by effort, what you hold on to and what you let go, and what you are prepared to work for. It is also where many people meet their questions about self-worth: whether they charge enough, give themselves permission to enjoy what they have, or feel safe only when they own more.',s.succedent,h,'The sign on the cusp describes your style with resources: a fire sign spends and earns boldly, an earth sign steadily, an air sign through ideas and trade, a water sign through feeling and care. The cusp ruler shows where your money comes from, by its house: in the tenth through career, in the seventh through partners or clients, in the sixth through daily work. Planets here colour the means: Venus through art, beauty or pleasant work, Mars through effort and enterprise, Mercury through words and trade, Jupiter through generosity and growth, Saturn through patience and slow building. With no planets here, read the cusp sign and its ruler: an empty second house is not a sign of poverty.'].join('\n\n'),magic:'Prosperity work, money jars and abundance spells. A planet here suggests the means: Venus through art or pleasant work, Mars through effort, Mercury through words and trade.',craft:'Second-house magic is prosperity and security work: money jars and bowls, abundance grids, candle spells for steady income, blessings for the tools you earn by, and spells for self-worth, which often unlock the rest. Work while the Moon is waxing for increase and while it wanes to clear debt or a scarcity habit; Thursdays (Jupiter) for growth and Fridays (Venus, who rules the natural second sign, Taurus) for comfort and value; and the hours of your second-house ruler, found with the Hours tool. Correspondences: Basil, Cinnamon, Mint and Patchouli among herbs; Citrine, Pyrite and Green Aventurine among stones; green and gold candles; a coin from your first wage or a recent payment on the altar. A small regular offering, such as the first coin of each month set aside, or bread and honey left out with thanks, keeps the house fed. Be honest about what you ask for: the old texts link this house with the means of living, not wealth for its own sake.'},{house:3,name:'The house of communication',keywords:['siblings','neighbours','short journeys','learning','speech','writing'],kind:'cadent',naturalSign:'Gemini',joy:'Moon',simple:'The third house is your near world: siblings, neighbours, daily travel, early learning, and how you speak and write.',detailed:[`${t[0].toUpperCase()}${t.slice(1)} called the third house the house of the Goddess (thea), and it is the joy of the Moon, who rejoices here. As well as brothers, sisters, relatives and friends, it was linked with rites, religious duties and omens: the everyday, close-to-home side of religion, as opposed to the great temples and oracles of its opposite, the ninth house of the God. Medieval and Renaissance astrologers such as Lilly read it for brethren, sisters, kindred, neighbours, short journeys and inland travel, letters, rumours and messengers.`,'Modern astrology keeps siblings, neighbours and short trips and adds the everyday mind: how you think, learn, speak, write and listen, your early schooling, your local community, and the habits of communication you grew up with. It is the house of the commute, the group chat, the corner shop and the notebook.','In a life, the third house shows how you take in information and pass it on, how you got on with siblings and cousins (and how you still do), how much you move around your own area, and whether learning came easily in childhood. It describes the texture of ordinary days: errands, messages, small conversations and the people you see without planning to.',s.cadent,'The sign on the cusp describes your way with words and the near world: Gemini or Virgo quick and curious, Taurus slow and sure, Scorpio probing, Sagittarius blunt and broad. Its ruler, by sign and house, shows where your thinking most likes to go. Planets here colour the mind and the neighbourhood: Mercury here is at home and talkative, the Moon (in her joy) gives a feeling, intuitive mind and close ties with siblings, Mars sharp speech and quick travel, Saturn careful, serious study and sometimes a distant or burdened sibling bond, Jupiter a love of learning and fortunate short journeys. An empty third house still speaks through its cusp and ruler.'].join('\n\n'),magic:'Word magic, letter spells, petition writing, travel protection and divination by signs and omens.',craft:'The third house is the house of word magic and small daily omens: petitions and letter spells, sigils, written charms carried in a pocket, blessings for a car or a bike or a daily route, spells for study and exams, and reconciliation with a sibling or neighbour. As the Moon\'s joy and the old house of the Goddess, it also suits everyday devotion and simple omen-reading: noticing birds, overheard words and signs on a walk, keeping an omen diary, or a short tea-leaf reading at the kitchen table. Wednesdays and Mercury hours suit it, as do Mondays and Moon hours for its omen side; the waxing Moon helps learning and messages to land. Correspondences: Lavender, Mint, Peppermint and Fennel; Fluorite for study, Blue Lace Agate and Sodalite for clear speech; yellow candles, good paper and coloured ink. Offer a written thank-you, or a coin at a local crossroads, to the spirits of your own streets.'},{house:4,name:'The house of home and roots',keywords:['home','family','ancestors','land','endings','private life'],kind:'angular',naturalSign:'Cancer',simple:'The fourth house is home and roots: family, ancestry, where you come from and the private self. It begins at the IC, the bottom of the chart.',detailed:[`${t[0].toUpperCase()}${t.slice(1)} called the fourth house the subterraneous place (hypogeion), the angle under the earth. It shows the parents (the father especially in many old texts), land, houses and buildings, inheritance of property, things hidden underground including buried treasure, and the end of matters: how a life, or any undertaking, finally turns out. Lilly read it for the father, lands, houses, towns, fields and gardens, mines and the grave, and the end of anything.`,'Modern astrology reads the fourth house as emotional foundations: the home you grew up in and the one you make, family patterns, ancestry and heritage, your private life and the self you are when nobody is watching. Many astrologers read it for the nurturing parent, while others keep the older reading of the father; it is best read for whichever parent most shaped your sense of home.','In a life, the fourth house shows what home means to you and where you feel rooted, how you relate to your family and its history, your relationship with land and place, and how you end things and grow old. Because it is the lowest point of the chart, it is also the base everything else stands on: an unsettled fourth house is often felt as restlessness that no outer success settles.',s.angular,'The sign on the IC, the cusp of the fourth house, describes the atmosphere of home: Cancer or Taurus warm and settled, Capricorn structured and dutiful, Aries lively and sometimes combative, Aquarius unconventional, Pisces porous and dreamy. Its ruler, by sign and house, shows where you find your roots, which may be far from your family home. Planets here colour home and family: the Moon at home here and deeply attached, Saturn duty and sometimes a hard or distant parent, Jupiter a generous home and often property, Mars a lively or quarrelsome household, Venus a love of a beautiful home. A planet near the IC itself often shows in the second half of life.'].join('\n\n'),magic:'Hearth and home blessing, house protection, ancestor altars, land spirits and burying spells. A planet here shows what your home asks of you.',craft:'Fourth-house magic is hearth, land and ancestors: blessing and protecting a home, house cleansing, an ancestor altar with photographs, names or keepsakes, offerings to the spirits of the land you live on, and spells that are buried or kept under the threshold or in the garden. Mondays and Moon hours suit it (the Moon rules Cancer, its natural sign), as do dark and new Moons for ancestor and endings work and the full Moon for blessing a house. Correspondences: Rosemary and Bay (laurel) by the door, Hyssop and Sage for cleansing, Juniper for protection, Moonstone and Selenite for the home, Jet and Smoky Quartz for ancestors and grounding, and Salt for the threshold. Offer water, bread, a little of each family meal or a candle on an anniversary to your dead, and something from the kitchen to the house itself. If your family history is painful, the fourth house also holds the home and kin you choose: tend those too.'},{house:5,name:'The house of pleasure and creation',keywords:['children','creativity','play','romance','joy','risk'],kind:'succedent',naturalSign:'Leo',joy:'Venus',simple:'The fifth house is joy: creativity, play, romance, children and whatever you make for the love of it.',detailed:[`${t[0].toUpperCase()}${t.slice(1)} called the fifth house Good Fortune (agath\u0113 tych\u0113), and it is the joy of Venus, who rejoices here. It was the house of children, pleasure, feasting, gifts and the good things life brings. Medieval and Renaissance astrologers read it for children and pregnancy, pleasure, entertainments, taverns and banquets, gifts, and, in horary, messengers and ambassadors. It was one of the good houses, easy on the planets placed there.`,'Modern astrology reads the fifth house as self-expression and creativity: art, performance, play, hobbies, games, love affairs and romance, children and the inner child, and what you do purely because it delights you. It is also the house of taking a chance: speculation, gambling and risks taken for the thrill of it.','In a life, the fifth house shows how you play, what you create and how freely you share it, your experience of children (your own, other people\'s, or creative work as offspring), and the tone of your romances, especially the early, courting stage of love. A strong fifth house often shows a person who needs a creative outlet to stay well.',s.succedent,'The sign on the cusp describes how you take your pleasure and make things: Leo or Aries boldly and visibly, Taurus through the senses and handwork, Virgo through craft and detail, Pisces through dream and music. Its ruler, by house, shows where joy and creativity are drawn: in the ninth through travel and study, in the eleventh through friends and groups. Planets here colour play, love and children: Venus (in her joy) brings charm, art and happy romance, the Sun creative pride and a need to shine, Jupiter generosity and often children or students, Mars passionate, competitive play, Saturn serious creative discipline and sometimes delayed or careful parenthood. An empty fifth house is read through its cusp and ruler, not as an absence of joy.'].join('\n\n'),magic:'Creative magic, art as offering, fertility (of any kind), love that delights, and glamour for joy rather than gain.',craft:'Fifth-house magic is creative and joyful: art made as an offering, music, dance and song in ritual, fertility magic (for children, or for any project you want to bring to life), love spells that ask for delight rather than control, glamour for pleasure, and blessings for children in your care. It is the right house to remember that ritual can be fun. Sundays and Sun hours suit it (the Sun rules Leo, its natural sign), and Fridays and Venus hours for its love and pleasure side; the waxing and full Moon favour creation and celebration, and Beltane and the height of summer are its festivals. Correspondences: Sunflower, Calendula, Rose and Orange; Carnelian and Sunstone for creative fire, Rose Quartz for love, Citrine for joy; gold and orange candles. Offer what you made: a song, a painting, a cake shared with friends. A feast is one of the oldest offerings there is.'},{house:6,name:'The house of work and health',keywords:['daily work','health','routine','service','animals','illness'],kind:'cadent',naturalSign:'Virgo',joy:'Mars',simple:'The sixth house is daily life: work and routines, health and illness, service, and the care of animals.',detailed:[`${t[0].toUpperCase()}${t.slice(1)} called the sixth house Bad Fortune (kak\u0113 tych\u0113), and it is the joy of Mars, who rejoices in this house of toil and trouble. It showed illness and injury, enemies of lower standing, slaves and servants, and hard labour. Medieval and Renaissance astrologers read it for sickness and its causes, servants and employees, small animals (sheep, goats, pets) and the work of tenants and labourers. It was counted a difficult place, weak for the planets there.`,'Modern astrology has softened it into the house of daily life: work routines and conditions, habits, health and the body\'s upkeep, diet and exercise, service to others, colleagues and employees, and pets and working animals. Many astrologers read it as the house where we learn skills by repetition and improve things one small step at a time.','In a life, the sixth house shows how you work day to day, how you get on with colleagues, how your health tends to go and what wears you down, and the kind of routines that keep you well. It is also where people meet the limits of the body and learn to care for it, and where service, given freely or as a job, shapes the self.',s.cadent,h,'The sign on the cusp describes your working style and the body\'s needs: Virgo careful and analytical, Capricorn disciplined, Leo proud of good work, Pisces adaptable but in need of rest and boundaries. Its ruler, by sign and house, shows where your daily work leads and what supports your health. Planets here colour work and health: Mars (in his joy) gives energy for hard work and sometimes fevers or strains, Saturn steady endurance and chronic issues to manage, Venus pleasant working conditions, Mercury mental work and nervous strain, the Moon a changeable routine and close bonds with animals. Treat what the chart says about health as a prompt for care, never as a diagnosis.'].join('\n\n'),magic:'Healing work (beside proper care), herbalism, cleansing, kitchen and household magic, familiars and working animals, and spells for steady work.',craft:'Sixth-house magic is practical and daily: healing work alongside proper medical care, herbalism and kitchen magic, cleansing the body and the house, blessings for a workplace or work tools, spells for steady work and good colleagues, and care for familiars, pets and working animals. Small daily rites belong here too: a morning blessing, a cleansing wash, a tidy altar kept clean. Wednesdays and Mercury hours suit it (Mercury rules Virgo, its natural sign), and Tuesdays and Mars hours for its effort and vigour; the waning Moon helps release illness and bad habits, the waxing Moon builds health and new routines. Correspondences: Thyme, Rosemary, Chamomile, Lemon Balm, Nettle and Calendula among herbs; Bloodstone, Fluorite, Moss Agate and Amethyst among stones; green and blue candles. A healing broth or a warm cup offered and then drunk is a sixth-house working in itself. Never let magic replace a doctor or a vet: this house is about tending the body faithfully.'},{house:7,name:'The house of partnership',keywords:['marriage','partners','contracts','open enemies','others'],kind:'angular',naturalSign:'Libra',simple:'The seventh house is the people you face one to one: partners, spouses, close collaborators, and open rivals. It begins at the Descendant.',detailed:[`${t[0].toUpperCase()}${t.slice(1)} called the seventh house the setting place (dysis), the angle where planets go down in the west. It showed marriage and the spouse, partnerships, lawsuits and open enemies, and, as the place where the light sets, old age and the approach of death. Medieval and Renaissance astrologers read it for marriage, love and public enemies, partners in business, contracts and lawsuits, and in horary for thieves, fugitives and "the other party" in any question.`,'Modern astrology reads the seventh house as committed relationship: marriage and long partnership, business partners, close collaborators, agents and clients, and rivals met face to face. Psychological astrologers add projection: the qualities we look for, or disown and then find, in other people. It is the house of "you" as the first is the house of "I".','In a life, the seventh house shows what you seek in a partner and what you attract, how you behave in close one-to-one relationships, how you handle contracts and agreements, and how you deal with open conflict. It often describes the partner themselves, or the kind of person who keeps arriving in your life until you have learnt what they bring.',s.angular,'The sign on the cusp, your Descendant, is always opposite your rising sign: what you meet in others balances the way you meet the world. Aries rising brings Libra on the Descendant, a search for harmony in partners; Leo rising brings Aquarius, a need for independent, unusual partners. Its ruler, by sign and house, shows where you meet partners and how the relationship lives: in the tenth through work, in the ninth abroad or through study. Planets here colour partnership: Venus brings ease and affection, Jupiter generous or fortunate partners, Saturn commitment that is serious, sometimes late, and built to last, Mars passion and conflict, Uranus a need for freedom inside a bond. An empty seventh house does not mean no partner.'].join('\n\n'),magic:'Partnership blessings, handfasting, contract spells, reconciliation, and work on how you relate.',craft:'Seventh-house magic is the magic of two: blessings for a partnership, handfastings and anniversaries, sweetening jars and honey spells for goodwill, reconciliation work, spells to bring a fair contract or a good business partner, and protection in a dispute. Ethical care matters most here: work on the relationship and on yourself, not on another person\'s will, and use the house\'s protective side for honest defence rather than harm. Fridays and Venus hours suit it (Venus rules Libra, its natural sign), the full Moon (Sun and Moon opposite, as partners face each other) for blessing a bond, and the waning Moon for ending a partnership kindly. Correspondences: Rose, Jasmine, Lavender and Vanilla; Rose Quartz, Rhodonite and Emerald; paired candles, two of everything on the altar, pink and green. Share an offering: a cake or a cup made and taken together, or two flowers left side by side.'},{house:8,name:'The house of death and shared things',keywords:['death','inheritance','debts','shared resources','intimacy','the occult'],kind:'succedent',naturalSign:'Scorpio',simple:'The eighth house is what is shared and what ends: other people\'s money, debts and inheritance, intimacy, death and transformation.',detailed:[`${t[0].toUpperCase()}${t.slice(1)} called the eighth house the idle place (argos topos), the house that follows the setting angle, and read it as the beginning of death: the manner and quality of death, inheritance, and the spouse's goods and dowry. Medieval and Renaissance astrologers read it for death and its kind, wills and legacies, the partner's money, fear and anguish, and debts owed by or to others. It was one of the hard houses, weak and inactive for the planets placed there.`,'Modern astrology reads the eighth house as merging and transformation: shared money (a partner\'s income, loans, taxes, investments, inheritance), sexuality and deep intimacy, crisis, psychology, the occult and hidden knowledge, and death and rebirth in every sense. Many astrologers see it as the house where we let others in, and where control has to be given up.','In a life, the eighth house shows how you handle money that is not wholly yours, how you deal with debt, trust and power in close bonds, your experience of loss and grief, your relationship with sex and vulnerability, and your draw to the hidden and the occult. It is often where the biggest inner changes of a life take place, usually after an ending.',s.succedent,h,'The sign on the cusp describes how you meet shared things and endings: Scorpio or Capricorn guarded and serious, Aries or Sagittarius brave and quick to move on, Taurus slow to let go, Pisces open and sometimes boundary-less. Its ruler, by sign and house, shows where shared resources and transformation come from: in the seventh through a partner, in the tenth through career. Planets here colour intimacy and change: Pluto or Mars intensity and power struggles that transform, Jupiter inheritance or generous support, Saturn debts and a cautious, careful approach to trust, Venus money or affection through others, the Moon deep emotional bonds and a feeling for the dead. Nothing in this house predicts the timing or manner of anyone\'s death: modern ethical practice does not read it that way.'].join('\n\n'),magic:'Shadow work, death and ancestor work, rites of passage, debt and inheritance matters, and deep transformation.',craft:'Eighth-house magic goes deep: shadow work and journalling, grief rites and letting go of the dead, ancestor and death work, rites of passage, spells to clear debt or settle an inheritance fairly, protection in intimacy and power, and the study of the occult itself. Rebirth work, such as a symbolic burial and rising, belongs here. Tuesdays and Mars hours suit it (Mars is the traditional ruler of Scorpio, its natural sign), the dark and waning Moon for endings, release and talking with the dead, and Samhain and the turn of autumn are its season. Correspondences: Mugwort, Wormwood, Myrrh, Yew (to be honoured, not handled: it is poisonous) and Cypress among plants; Obsidian, Jet, Smoky Quartz and Garnet among stones; black and deep red candles. Offer a lit candle and a glass of water to your dead, and give back something real when you ask for help through a hard change. Take care with heavy work: ground afterwards, and do not work alone on grief that needs a person beside you.'},{house:9,name:'The house of faith and far travel',keywords:['religion','philosophy','higher learning','long journeys','dreams','divination'],kind:'cadent',naturalSign:'Sagittarius',joy:'Sun',simple:'The ninth house is the far horizon: faith, philosophy, higher learning, long journeys and the search for meaning.',detailed:[`${t[0].toUpperCase()}${t.slice(1)} called the ninth house the house of the God (theos), and it is the joy of the Sun, who rejoices here. It showed religion and the gods, oracles and divination, dreams, travel abroad, foreign lands and wisdom. It is the counterpart of the third house of the Goddess: the great temples and far journeys, where the third holds the local shrine and the daily errand. Medieval and Renaissance astrologers read it for religion, the church and clergy, long voyages, learning and books, dreams and visions, and the spouse's kin.`,'Modern astrology reads the ninth house as belief and meaning: philosophy and worldview, religion and spirituality, higher education and universities, law, publishing and teaching, long-distance travel and foreign cultures, and the search for a bigger picture. It is where curiosity becomes a quest.','In a life, the ninth house shows what you believe and how you came to believe it, how you learn once school is over, your experience of travel and of people from other places, and the teachers and teachings that shape you. It often shows how a person relates to a spiritual path, and whether they lead or follow on it.',s.cadent,'The sign on the cusp describes how you seek meaning: Sagittarius or Leo with enthusiasm and faith, Virgo through careful study, Scorpio through mysteries, Aquarius through ideas and reform, Cancer through family religion or tradition. Its ruler, by sign and house, shows where your path to meaning leads. Planets here colour faith and travel: the Sun (in his joy) gives a strong, often public, sense of purpose, Jupiter a love of learning and fortunate journeys, the Moon a life lived partly abroad or in dream, Saturn serious study and a hard-won faith, Mercury the scholar and writer, Neptune mysticism and the visionary. An empty ninth house still describes your beliefs, through its cusp and ruler.'].join('\n\n'),magic:'Devotion and deity work, divination and dream oracles, pilgrimage, study of the craft, and travel blessings.',craft:'Ninth-house magic is devotion and divination: deity work and a devotional altar, prayers and hymns, oracles and dream incubation (asking for a dream and recording it), tarot and other divination, pilgrimage to a sacred place, blessings for long journeys and for study, and the study of the craft and its history. Thursdays and Jupiter hours suit it (Jupiter rules Sagittarius, its natural sign), and Sundays and Sun hours honour the Sun in his joy; the full Moon is good for oracles and the waxing Moon for study and travel. Correspondences: Bay (laurel, the leaf of oracles), Frankincense, Sage and Star Anise; Lapis Lazuli, Sodalite, Turquoise and Amethyst; purple, royal blue and gold candles. Offer incense, a reading of a hymn or poem, or a stargazing vigil. When you take up practices from other cultures here, learn how they are taught and respect the closed and living traditions.'},{house:10,name:'The house of vocation',keywords:['career','reputation','public life','achievement','authority'],kind:'angular',naturalSign:'Capricorn',simple:'The tenth house is your public life: vocation, reputation and what you are known for. In most systems it begins at the Midheaven.',detailed:[`${t[0].toUpperCase()}${t.slice(1)} called the tenth house the midheaven place (mesouran\u0113ma), the highest of the angles, and counted it the strongest place in the chart after the Ascendant: a planet here was "upon" or over-seeing all the others. It showed action and profession, honour and rank, and those who rule. Medieval and Renaissance astrologers read it for kings and magistrates, honour, preferment, the trade or profession, and, in Lilly, the mother.`,'Modern astrology reads the tenth house as career and calling: ambition, achievement, public image and reputation, the people in authority over you and the authority you come to hold, and the parent who most shaped your sense of duty and the world\'s expectations. It is what you do in the world and what the world knows you for.','In a life, the tenth house shows the shape of your working life, the kind of recognition you seek, how you deal with bosses and responsibility, and the mark you hope to leave. It is not only paid work: caring, creating, campaigning and running a coven are all tenth-house callings. Planets here often rise into view as life goes on.',s.angular,'The sign on the cusp is, in most house systems, the sign of your Midheaven, and describes how you go about your public life: Capricorn ambitious and enduring, Aries pioneering, Libra diplomatic, Pisces artistic or compassionate. (In whole sign houses the tenth is simply the tenth sign from the rising sign, and the Midheaven can fall in the ninth or eleventh.) Its ruler, by sign and house, shows where your calling leads. Planets here are public: the Sun gives a wish to lead and be seen, Jupiter honours and opportunity, Saturn slow, solid achievement and sometimes the weight of responsibility, Mars ambition and competition, Venus success through charm and art, the Moon a public life with ups and downs. An empty tenth house is read through the Midheaven and its ruler.'].join('\n\n'),magic:'Career and recognition work, job spells, reputation and protection in public, and long-term goals.',craft:'Tenth-house magic is career and reputation work: spells for a job or promotion, success and recognition, protection of your good name, blessings for a business or a public project, and rites for long-term goals that will take years. A vision board, a written ten-year aim kept on the altar, or a sigil for your calling all suit it. Saturdays and Saturn hours suit its slow building (Saturn rules Capricorn, its natural sign), Sundays and Sun hours its wish to shine, and noon, when the Sun is at its own midheaven, is the house\'s hour of the day; work with the waxing Moon for growth and the full Moon for recognition. Correspondences: Bay (laurel, the victor\'s leaf), Cinnamon, Cedar and Oak; Tiger Eye, Pyrite, Onyx and Garnet; gold, black and dark green candles. Offer the first fruit of a success: a gift, a donation or a share of your first earnings from new work.'},{house:11,name:'The house of friends and hopes',keywords:['friends','groups','allies','hopes','benefactors','community'],kind:'succedent',naturalSign:'Aquarius',joy:'Jupiter',simple:'The eleventh house is your people and your hopes: friends, allies, groups, covens and causes, and the future you wish for.',detailed:[`${t[0].toUpperCase()}${t.slice(1)} called the eleventh house the Good Spirit (agathos daim\u014dn), and it is the joy of Jupiter, who rejoices here. It showed friends, patrons and benefactors, gifts and favours, and hopes for the future, and it was counted one of the best houses of the chart. Medieval and Renaissance astrologers read it for friends, hope, trust and comfort, the favour of the powerful, the king's counsellors, and the profit of the tenth house's work.`,'Modern astrology reads the eleventh house as community: friends, networks and groups, clubs, societies and covens, causes and collective work, ideals and hopes for the future, and the gifts that come through other people. It is where the self joins something bigger than one life.','In a life, the eleventh house shows what kind of friend you are and what kind of friends you find, how you belong to groups, the causes that move you, the help that comes to you from unexpected people, and your wishes and plans for the future. A strong eleventh house is often found in people who do their best work alongside others.',s.succedent,'The sign on the cusp describes your style of friendship and group life: Aquarius or Gemini sociable and idea-driven, Capricorn a few loyal, long friendships, Cancer friends who become family, Leo a warm circle with you at its heart. Its ruler, by sign and house, shows where friends and hopes come from: in the ninth through study or faith, in the sixth through work. Planets here colour community: Jupiter (in his joy) brings generous friends and fortunate allies, Venus warm and affectionate friendships, Saturn few but lasting friends and responsibility in groups, Mars activism and sometimes quarrels, Uranus unusual and changing circles. An empty eleventh house is read through its cusp and ruler.'].join('\n\n'),magic:'Coven and group work, wishing magic, friendship spells and community blessings.',craft:'Eleventh-house magic is group and wishing magic: coven work and group rituals, blessings for a circle, friendship spells, wishing magic for the future, community blessings and spells for a cause, and charms that ask for help to come through good people. A wish written on a bay leaf and burned, a shared candle lit at the same hour by friends in different places, or a coven cord with a knot for each member all suit it. Thursdays and Jupiter hours honour Jupiter in his joy, and Saturdays and Saturn hours suit its traditional ruler (Saturn rules Aquarius, its natural sign); the waxing Moon carries wishes, and the full Moon is the classic time for group rites. Correspondences: Bay, Star Anise and Lavender; Amethyst, Aquamarine, Turquoise and Clear Quartz; blue and turquoise candles. Offer to the group itself: food brought to share, time given to a cause, or thanks spoken aloud for the people who have helped you.'},{house:12,name:'The house of the hidden',keywords:['solitude','secrets','retreat','sorrow','the unconscious','spirituality'],kind:'cadent',naturalSign:'Pisces',joy:'Saturn',simple:'The twelfth house is what is hidden: solitude, retreat, secrets, sorrows, dreams and the life of the spirit.',detailed:[`${t[0].toUpperCase()}${t.slice(1)} called the twelfth house the Bad Spirit (kakos daim\u014dn), and it is the joy of Saturn, who rejoices in this house of solitude and confinement. It showed enemies, especially hidden ones, captivity, grief and suffering, and losses. Medieval and Renaissance astrologers read it for secret enemies, imprisonment, sorrow, self-undoing, witches (Lilly lists them here), large animals such as horses and cattle, and hospitals and places of confinement.`,'Modern astrology reads the twelfth house as the hidden life: the unconscious, dreams, solitude and retreat, spirituality and mysticism, compassion and self-sacrifice, institutions such as hospitals, prisons, monasteries and retreats, and what is kept secret, from others or from yourself. Many astrologers see it as the house of endings before a new beginning, since it ends just as the first house begins.','In a life, the twelfth house shows what you need to withdraw for, how you deal with what you cannot control, your inner and dream life, the patterns that undo you until you see them, and the quiet ways you give to others. It is often strong in the charts of artists, healers, mystics and people who work behind the scenes.',s.cadent,h,'The sign on the cusp describes how you retreat and what lies hidden: Pisces or Cancer a deep, dreamy inner life, Virgo worry turned inward, Aries anger kept unspoken, Sagittarius a private faith. It is also the sign just above your rising sign, so it is close to the surface even when hidden. Its ruler, by sign and house, shows where the hidden comes to light. Planets here work out of sight: Saturn (in his joy) can give real strength in solitude and discipline in spiritual work, Venus a secret love or quiet kindness, the Moon a sensitive, psychic and private nature, Mars hidden anger or courage, Jupiter a protective presence often called a guardian angel, Neptune mystic gifts. A planet within a few degrees of the Ascendant, though in the twelfth, often acts as if it were in the first.'].join('\n\n'),magic:'Solitary practice, meditation, dreamwork, hidden and protective magic, retreat, and the spirit world.',craft:'Twelfth-house magic is quiet and inward: solitary practice and retreat, meditation, dreamwork and dream pillows, a dream journal, contact with spirits and guides, hidden and protective magic (charms kept secret, wards against unseen harm), spells to break a self-undoing pattern, and compassion work for those in hospitals, prisons or other confinement. Silence and darkness are its tools. Saturdays and Saturn hours honour Saturn in his joy, and Thursdays and Jupiter hours its traditional ruler (Jupiter rules Pisces, its natural sign); the dark Moon and the last days of the waning Moon suit it best, as do the days before your birthday, when your solar year is ending. Correspondences: Mugwort and Lavender for dreams, Myrrh and Sandalwood for spirit work, Amethyst, Labradorite, Moonstone and Black Tourmaline among stones; deep blue, violet and black candles. Offer silence: an hour unplugged, a candle left to burn while you sit with it, or a quiet act of kindness that no one will ever know was yours.'}],u=(o,n)=>[...new Set([n(o.naturalSign),...o.joy?[o.joy]:[]])]},897,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SIGN_TERMS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t=[{name:'Aries',symbol:'\u2648\ufe0e',simple:'Aries, the Ram, is the first sign: fire and beginnings. Brave, direct, impatient, it meets life head-first.',sky:'The Sun is in tropical Aries from the March equinox (about 20 March) to about 20 April. The sign begins at the spring equinox point; because of precession the constellation Aries now lies mostly behind the sign of Taurus.',history:'Aries opened the zodiac because the Sun stood among its stars at the spring equinox in Greek times. Its ram is linked with the golden fleece of Greek myth and with the ram-headed Amun.',traditions:'Cardinal fire, yang, ruled by Mars, the Sun exalted here, Venus in detriment, Saturn in fall. It rules the head and face. Modern readers: initiative, courage, self-assertion and the urge to be first.',craft:'The Moon in Aries suits courage, new starts, protection and clearing obstacles. Aries season is a classic time to begin new workings and cleanse for spring. Colours: red; stones: carnelian, bloodstone; herbs: nettle, black pepper.',care:'Aries energy starts better than it finishes; build in a plan for the middle.',related:['mars','fire','cardinal','house-1']},{name:'Taurus',symbol:'\u2649\ufe0e',simple:'Taurus, the Bull, is earth at its most settled: steady, sensual, patient, loyal and fond of good things that last.',sky:'The Sun is in Taurus from about 20 April to 21 May. The Pleiades and the red star Aldebaran sit in the constellation, now behind the sign of Gemini.',history:'The bull of heaven appears in the Epic of Gilgamesh; in Greek myth Zeus became a white bull to carry off Europa. Taurus held the spring equinox some 4,000 to 6,000 years ago.',traditions:'Fixed earth, yin, ruled by Venus, the Moon exalted here, Mars and Pluto in detriment. It rules the throat and neck. Modern readers: stability, the body, resources, comfort and stubbornness.',craft:'The Moon in Taurus suits money and prosperity, home blessings, gardening, slow steady spells and self-care. Beltane falls in Taurus season. Colours: green, rose; stones: rose quartz, emerald; herbs: rose, thyme, vanilla.',care:'What Taurus gains it keeps; that can mean holding on past time.',related:['venus','earth','fixed','house-2']},{name:'Gemini',symbol:'\u264a\ufe0e',simple:'Gemini, the Twins, is restless air: curious, talkative, quick, playful and interested in everything at once.',sky:'The Sun is in Gemini from about 21 May to 21 June. The bright twin stars Castor and Pollux mark the constellation.',history:'Castor and Pollux (the Dioscuri), one mortal and one divine, shared their brother\'s fate between Olympus and the underworld; sailors prayed to them in storms.',traditions:'Mutable air, yang, ruled by Mercury, Jupiter in detriment. It rules the arms, hands and lungs. Modern readers: communication, learning, siblings, wit and duality.',craft:'The Moon in Gemini suits communication, study, writing, divination and quick errands. Colours: yellow, mixed colours; stones: agate, citrine; herbs: lavender, mint, fennel.',care:'Gemini\'s breadth can scatter; pick one thread to follow.',related:['mercury','air','mutable','house-3']},{name:'Cancer',symbol:'\u264b\ufe0e',simple:'Cancer, the Crab, is cardinal water: tender, protective, home-loving and moody as the tides, soft inside a strong shell.',sky:'The Sun is in Cancer from the June solstice (about 21 June) to about 22 July. The faint constellation holds the Beehive cluster (Praesepe).',history:'The crab Hera sent against Heracles. Cancer held the summer solstice in classical times, which is why the northern tropic is the Tropic of Cancer.',traditions:'Cardinal water, yin, ruled by the Moon, Jupiter exalted here, Saturn in detriment, Mars in fall. It rules the breasts and stomach. Modern readers: home, family, nurture, memory and belonging.',craft:'The Moon in Cancer (its own sign) suits home and family blessings, emotional healing, dreamwork, kitchen witchery and ancestor offerings. Litha begins Cancer season. Colours: silver, white; stones: moonstone, pearl; herbs: lemon balm, jasmine, willow.',care:'Cancer\'s care can tip into worry; nurture yourself too.',related:['moon','water','cardinal','house-4']},{name:'Leo',symbol:'\u264c\ufe0e',simple:'Leo, the Lion, is fixed fire: warm, proud, generous, creative and made to be seen.',sky:'The Sun is in Leo from about 22 July to 23 August. The royal star Regulus, "the little king", sits near the ecliptic in the constellation.',history:'The Nemean lion of Heracles\' first labour. In Egypt the Nile flood came as the Sun entered Leo, and lion-headed gods (Sekhmet among them) guarded the season.',traditions:'Fixed fire, yang, ruled by the Sun, Saturn and Uranus in detriment. It rules the heart and spine. Modern readers: self-expression, play, children, romance, leadership and pride.',craft:'The Moon in Leo suits confidence, creativity, joy, glamour and courage. Lughnasadh falls in Leo season. Colours: gold, orange; stones: sunstone, citrine, tiger eye; herbs: calendula, sunflower, bay.',care:'Leo shines brightest when it isn\'t asking to be told it does.',related:['sun','fire','fixed','house-5']},{name:'Virgo',symbol:'\u264d\ufe0e',simple:'Virgo, the Maiden, is mutable earth: careful, skilled, helpful and discerning, always refining.',sky:'The Sun is in Virgo from about 23 August to the September equinox (about 22 September). The bright star Spica, "the ear of wheat", lies in the constellation.',history:'The maiden with a sheaf of wheat: Demeter or Persephone, Astraea who left the earth last at the end of the golden age, and in some readings Isis.',traditions:'Mutable earth, yin, ruled by Mercury and exalted Mercury, Jupiter and Neptune in detriment, Venus in fall. It rules the digestion. Modern readers: service, health, craft, analysis and daily routine.',craft:'The Moon in Virgo suits health, cleansing, herbcraft, organising and practical magic. Mabon closes Virgo season. Colours: green, brown, navy; stones: amazonite, moss agate; herbs: fennel, lavender, valerian.',care:'Virgo\'s eye for flaws is a gift for craft and a weight on the self; turn it gently.',related:['mercury','earth','mutable','house-6']},{name:'Libra',symbol:'\u264e\ufe0e',simple:'Libra, the Scales, is cardinal air: gracious, fair, sociable and drawn to beauty and balance.',sky:'The Sun is in Libra from the September equinox (about 22 September) to about 23 October, when day and night are near equal.',history:'The only sign not a living being. Once the claws of the Scorpion, the scales were named by the Romans and linked to justice and Astraea; the equinox balance gave the image.',traditions:'Cardinal air, yang, ruled by Venus, Saturn exalted here, Mars in detriment, the Sun in fall. It rules the kidneys and lower back. Modern readers: relationship, fairness, diplomacy, art and indecision.',craft:'The Moon in Libra suits love and friendship, harmony, justice, beauty and partnership. Mabon opens Libra season. Colours: pink, pale blue; stones: rose quartz, lapis; herbs: rose, violet, thyme.',care:'Keeping the peace isn\'t the same as making it.',related:['venus','air','cardinal','house-7']},{name:'Scorpio',symbol:'\u264f\ufe0e',simple:'Scorpio, the Scorpion, is fixed water: deep, private, intense and loyal, unafraid of what others look away from.',sky:'The Sun is in Scorpio from about 23 October to 22 November. The red star Antares, "rival of Mars", is the scorpion\'s heart.',history:'The scorpion that killed Orion, set opposite him in the sky so they never rise together. Samhain falls in Scorpio season, as does the Day of the Dead, both times of the thinning veil in modern practice.',traditions:'Fixed water, yin, ruled by Mars (traditional) and Pluto (modern), Venus in detriment, the Moon in fall. It rules the genitals. Modern readers: intimacy, power, transformation, secrets, shared resources and regeneration.',craft:'The Moon in Scorpio suits shadow work, banishing, transformation, protection and psychic work. Colours: black, deep red; stones: obsidian, garnet, smoky quartz; herbs: wormwood, basil, pomegranate.',care:'Scorpio\'s intensity heals best with trust and air; not every secret needs keeping.',related:['mars','pluto','water','fixed','house-8']},{name:'Sagittarius',symbol:'\u2650\ufe0e',simple:'Sagittarius, the Archer, is mutable fire: free, honest, hopeful and hungry for meaning, travel and truth.',sky:'The Sun is in Sagittarius from about 22 November to the December solstice (about 21 December). The centre of our galaxy lies in the constellation\'s direction.',history:'A centaur archer, sometimes Chiron, sometimes Crotus. Babylonian images showed a winged centaur god, Pabilsag.',traditions:'Mutable fire, yang, ruled by Jupiter, Mercury in detriment. It rules the hips and thighs. Modern readers: philosophy, faith, higher learning, travel, teaching and restlessness.',craft:'The Moon in Sagittarius suits luck, travel, learning, expansion and seeking truth. Colours: purple, royal blue; stones: turquoise, lapis, amethyst; herbs: sage, clove, star anise.',care:'Sagittarius\'s honesty is a gift when kind; aim the arrow carefully.',related:['jupiter','fire','mutable','house-9']},{name:'Capricorn',symbol:'\u2651\ufe0e',simple:'Capricorn, the Sea-Goat, is cardinal earth: serious, ambitious, patient and responsible, climbing steadily.',sky:'The Sun is in Capricorn from the December solstice (about 21 December) to about 20 January. The southern tropic is the Tropic of Capricorn for the solstice once held here.',history:'A goat with a fish\'s tail, the Babylonian emblem of Ea (Enki), god of fresh water and wisdom; in Greek myth Pan or Amalthea.',traditions:'Cardinal earth, yin, ruled by Saturn, Mars exalted here, the Moon in detriment, Jupiter in fall. It rules the knees, bones and skin. Modern readers: career, discipline, authority, tradition and endurance.',craft:'The Moon in Capricorn suits career, structure, long-term goals, boundaries and binding. Yule opens Capricorn season. Colours: black, brown, deep green; stones: onyx, jet, garnet; herbs: comfrey, cypress, patchouli.',care:'Capricorn can carry everyone; let something be carried for you.',related:['saturn','earth','cardinal','house-10']},{name:'Aquarius',symbol:'\u2652\ufe0e',simple:'Aquarius, the Water-Bearer, is fixed air: independent, inventive, idealistic and devoted to friends and causes.',sky:'The Sun is in Aquarius from about 20 January to 19 February. The constellation lies in a region the Babylonians called "the Sea".',history:'Ganymede, cupbearer to the gods, or the Babylonian figure pouring the rivers. Imbolc falls in Aquarius season.',traditions:'Fixed air, yang, ruled by Saturn (traditional) and Uranus (modern), the Sun in detriment. It rules the ankles and circulation. Modern readers: community, ideals, originality, detachment and the future.',craft:'The Moon in Aquarius suits friendship, community, breaking habits, innovation and freedom. Colours: electric blue, silver; stones: amethyst, aquamarine, labradorite; herbs: lavender, mint.',care:'Loving humanity is easier than loving the person in front of you; Aquarius learns both.',related:['saturn','uranus','air','fixed','house-11']},{name:'Pisces',symbol:'\u2653\ufe0e',simple:'Pisces, the Fishes, is mutable water: dreamy, compassionate, intuitive and porous to the world\'s feeling.',sky:'The Sun is in Pisces from about 19 February to the March equinox (about 20 March). The spring equinox point now sits in the constellation Pisces, slowly drifting toward Aquarius.',history:'Two fish tied by a cord: Aphrodite and Eros, turned to fish to escape the monster Typhon. The drift of the equinox through Pisces gave rise to talk of the "Age of Pisces" and the coming "Age of Aquarius".',traditions:'Mutable water, yin, ruled by Jupiter (traditional) and Neptune (modern), Venus exalted here, Mercury in detriment and fall. It rules the feet. Modern readers: imagination, empathy, spirituality, surrender and escapism.',craft:'The Moon in Pisces suits intuition, dreams, meditation, divination and spiritual healing. Colours: sea-green, lavender; stones: amethyst, aquamarine; herbs: mugwort, jasmine, willow.',care:'Pisces feels everything; good boundaries and grounding keep its gifts its own.',related:['jupiter','neptune','water','mutable','house-12']}].map(t=>({id:t.name.toLowerCase(),term:t.name,category:'sign',symbol:t.symbol,simple:t.simple,detailed:{sky:t.sky,history:t.history,traditions:t.traditions,craft:t.craft,care:t.care},related:t.related}))},898,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"TECHNIQUE_TERMS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t=[{id:'house-systems',term:'House systems',category:'house-system',aliases:['house system'],simple:'A house system is the method used to divide the sky into the twelve houses. Different methods put the house lines in different places, which is why two apps can disagree.',detailed:{sky:'Every system agrees on the Ascendant (and nearly all on the Midheaven) but divides the space between them differently: by equal stretches of the zodiac, by time, or by space on the sky. Far from the equator they disagree most.',history:'Hellenistic astrologers (the Greek-speaking astrologers of the ancient Mediterranean, where Western astrology began) mostly used whole-sign houses. Quadrant systems came in late antiquity (Porphyry) and the medieval period (Alcabitius, Regiomontanus); Placidus, published in the 1600s, became standard in the 1900s because its tables were cheap and everywhere.',traditions:'This app offers Placidus (the default, most common in modern Western astrology), Whole Sign (the oldest, popular with traditional astrologers), Equal (simple and works everywhere), and uses Porphyry where Placidus cannot be drawn.',craft:'If a planet sits near a house line, try another system and see which reading rings true for you.',care:'No system is proven "right"; each answers the question in its own way.'},related:['placidus','whole-sign','equal-house','porphyry','houses']},{id:'placidus',term:'Placidus',category:'house-system',simple:'Placidus divides each quarter of the sky by time: a house line is where a degree has travelled a third, or two thirds, of the way between horizon and meridian. It is the most-used modern system.',detailed:{sky:'Each degree takes a certain time to rise from the horizon to the meridian (its "semi-arc"). Placidus cusps are the degrees one-third and two-thirds through those journeys. Houses can be very unequal in size, and near and inside the polar circles some degrees never rise or set, so the method breaks down. The app then falls back to Porphyry and says so.',history:'Named for the seventeenth-century monk Placidus de Titis; spread through cheap printed tables of houses in the nineteenth and twentieth centuries.',traditions:'Standard in modern psychological astrology; used for progressions and timing by many.',craft:'The default for most charts you will meet elsewhere, so it helps when comparing.',care:'At high latitudes (Scandinavia, northern Canada, Alaska) Placidus houses distort; consider Whole Sign or Equal there.'},related:['house-systems','porphyry']},{id:'whole-sign',term:'Whole Sign houses',category:'house-system',aliases:['Whole Sign','whole-sign houses'],simple:'In Whole Sign houses, your rising sign is the whole first house, the next sign the second, and so on: each house is exactly one sign.',detailed:{sky:'The Ascendant marks the rising sign but not the house line; the Midheaven can fall in the 9th, 10th or 11th house.',history:'The original method of the ancient Greek-speaking astrologers, still standard in Vedic astrology, revived in the West from the 1990s by translators of the old texts.',traditions:'Favoured by traditional astrologers, including those who practise the ancient methods; simple and clean, and never breaks down near the poles.',craft:'Each house has one sign and one ruler, which makes correspondences easy to read.',care:'Planets can change house compared with Placidus; that\'s expected.'},related:['house-systems','ascendant']},{id:'equal-house',term:'Equal houses',category:'house-system',aliases:['Equal','equal house','equal house system'],simple:'Equal houses are 30\xb0 each, counted from the exact degree of your Ascendant.',detailed:{sky:'The first house begins at the Ascendant\'s degree, the second 30\xb0 later, and so on. The Midheaven floats free of the tenth house line.',history:'Ancient (described by Ptolemy-era writers) and favoured by some British astrologers in the twentieth century.',traditions:'Simple, works at any latitude.',craft:'A good choice for anyone born far north or south.',care:'The Midheaven still matters even when it isn\'t a house line.'},related:['house-systems']},{id:'porphyry',term:'Porphyry',category:'house-system',simple:'Porphyry houses divide each quarter between the angles into three equal parts. The app uses them where Placidus can\'t be drawn.',detailed:{sky:'Takes the arc from the Ascendant to the IC (and each other quarter) and splits it in three.',history:'Named for the third-century philosopher Porphyry, though older.',traditions:'A simple quadrant system that works everywhere the Ascendant does.',care:'Shown in place of Placidus near the poles; the chart says when.'},related:['house-systems','placidus']},{id:'tropical',term:'Tropical zodiac',category:'zodiac',aliases:['tropical'],simple:'The tropical zodiac starts Aries at the spring equinox, so the signs follow the seasons. It is the one Western astrology uses and the app\'s default.',detailed:{sky:'0\xb0 Aries is where the Sun crosses the celestial equator going north each March. Because Earth\'s axis wobbles (precession), that point drifts against the stars by about a degree every 72 years.',history:'Fixed by ancient Greek astronomers and Ptolemy, when the equinox point and the stars of Aries roughly coincided.',traditions:'Western astrology: the signs as seasons of the year (Cancer begins at the June solstice, Capricorn at the December one).',craft:'The signs line up with the Wheel of the Year: equinoxes and solstices at 0\xb0 of the cardinal signs, cross-quarters in the fixed signs.',care:'In the southern hemisphere the seasons are reversed while the tropical signs aren\'t; many southern astrologers use them anyway, as the signs follow the equinoxes, not the weather.'},related:['sidereal','zodiac','precession']},{id:'sidereal',term:'Sidereal zodiac',category:'zodiac',aliases:['sidereal','Jyotish','Vedic'],simple:'The sidereal zodiac keeps the signs lined up with the stars. Today it sits about 24\xb0 behind the tropical, so many people\'s sidereal Sun sign is the one before their tropical sign.',detailed:{sky:'Signs are measured from a fixed star point (in the Lahiri system, opposite the star Spica). The difference from the tropical zodiac, the ayanamsa, grows by about 50 seconds of arc a year.',history:'Babylonian astronomy was sidereal; Indian astrology kept the sidereal zodiac, and a Western sidereal school (Cyril Fagan, from the 1940s) revived it.',traditions:'Vedic astrology (Jyotisha) is sidereal, with its own techniques (nakshatras, dashas, divisional charts) that this app does not attempt. Switching the zodiac here shifts the signs only.',craft:'If sidereal signs ring truer for you, use them; the planets\' natures and the houses don\'t change.',care:'A sidereal sign isn\'t your "real" sign and a tropical one isn\'t fake; they are two measures for two traditions.'},related:['tropical','ayanamsa','lahiri']},{id:'ayanamsa',term:'Ayanamsa',category:'zodiac',aliases:['ayanamsha'],simple:'The ayanamsa is the gap between the tropical and sidereal zodiacs, about 24\xb0 today.',detailed:{sky:'It grows with precession, about 1\xb0 every 72 years. Different sidereal schools set its starting point differently, by up to a degree or so.',history:'Sanskrit for "portion of movement".',traditions:'Lahiri is the official ayanamsa in India; Fagan-Bradley is common in Western sidereal astrology. The app uses Lahiri.',care:'A planet near the start or end of a sign may change sign between ayanamsas.'},related:['sidereal','lahiri','precession']},{id:'lahiri',term:'Lahiri ayanamsa',category:'zodiac',aliases:['Lahiri','Chitrapaksha'],simple:'Lahiri is the most widely used ayanamsa: it puts the star Spica at 0\xb0 Libra in the sidereal zodiac.',detailed:{history:'Adopted by the Indian government\'s Calendar Reform Committee in 1955, named for N. C. Lahiri.',traditions:'Standard in Vedic astrology; about 23\xb051\u2032 at the start of 2000.',care:'Other apps may use other ayanamsas; small differences are normal.'},related:['ayanamsa','sidereal']},{id:'precession',term:'Precession',category:'zodiac',aliases:['precession of the equinoxes','astrological age','Age of Aquarius'],simple:'Precession is the slow wobble of Earth\'s axis that makes the equinox drift back through the constellations, one full turn in about 25,800 years.',detailed:{sky:'The axis traces a cone like a spinning top; the equinox point slides about 50\u2033 a year westward along the ecliptic.',history:'Discovered by Hipparchus in the second century BCE. The drift of the equinox through constellations gives the "astrological ages" (Pisces, Aquarius).',traditions:'The reason tropical and sidereal zodiacs differ.',care:'Nobody agrees when the Age of Aquarius begins; the constellations have no fixed edges.'},related:['tropical','sidereal']},{id:'transit',term:'Transit',category:'technique',aliases:['transits','transiting'],simple:'A transit is where a planet is now, compared with your birth chart. When today\'s Saturn crosses your natal Moon, that is a Saturn transit to your Moon.',detailed:{sky:'Today\'s positions laid over the natal wheel. Fast planets (Moon, Sun, Mercury, Venus, Mars) make transits that last hours to days; slow ones (Jupiter to Pluto) weeks to months, often three times over with retrogrades.',history:'Used since antiquity alongside other timing methods; the main forecasting tool of modern astrology.',traditions:'Outer-planet transits to the Sun, Moon, angles and chart ruler are read as life\'s seasons; inner-planet transits as the weather of the week.',craft:'Transits suggest timing: a Jupiter transit to your Venus is a kind time for love and abundance work; a Saturn transit for building boundaries. The app shows the strongest ones and a line on Today.',care:'A transit describes a season, not an outcome. What you do with it is yours.'},related:['aspects','progressions','returns'],links:[{kind:'tool',tool:'chart',label:'Your transits'}]},{id:'progressions',term:'Progressions',category:'technique',aliases:['secondary progressions','progressed','progressed Moon','progressed chart','solar arc'],simple:'Secondary progressions take the sky one day after your birth to stand for your first year, two days for your second, and so on. They show slow inner growth; the progressed Moon is the clock.',detailed:{sky:'A "day for a year": at age 30, the progressed planets are where they were 30 days after birth. The progressed Moon moves about a degree a month, changing sign every two and a half years and going round in about 27. The progressed Sun moves about a degree a year. This app moves the angles by "solar arc" (the distance the progressed Sun has travelled).',history:'Rooted in Ptolemy\'s and medieval directions; the day-for-a-year method was popularised by Placidus and by twentieth-century astrologers.',traditions:'Read for inner development: the progressed Moon\'s sign as the emotional season of those years, the progressed lunar phase (new, full, balsamic) as a thirty-year cycle, and planets changing sign or direction as turning points.',craft:'The progressed Moon\'s sign suggests the correspondences of your current chapter; mark its sign changes as personal turning points.',care:'Progressions describe unfolding, not events.'},related:['transit','moon-phases','returns']},{id:'returns',term:'Returns',category:'technique',aliases:['return','planetary return','Saturn return','Jupiter return'],simple:'A return is when a planet comes back to exactly where it was when you were born. A chart cast for that moment is read for the cycle ahead.',detailed:{sky:'The Sun returns every year (near your birthday), the Moon every 27.3 days, Jupiter about every 12 years, Saturn about every 29.5.',history:'Solar returns were used by medieval Persian and Arabic astrologers for the year ahead.',traditions:'The Saturn return around 29 is read as coming of age; the second around 58 as a second maturity.',craft:'Birthdays are a natural time for a personal ritual; the exact solar return moment, to the minute, is a favourite time to cast for the year.',care:'A return chart colours a year; it doesn\'t script it.'},related:['solar-return','lunar-return','saturn','jupiter']},{id:'solar-return',term:'Solar return',category:'technique',aliases:['solar returns','birthday chart'],simple:'Your solar return is the moment each year when the Sun comes back to its exact birth degree, on or within a day of your birthday. Its chart is read for the year ahead.',detailed:{sky:'The Sun\'s birth longitude is found again, to the second. The return chart is cast for where you are at that moment, so the houses change with the place.',history:'A staple of medieval Persian and Arabic astrology (Abu Ma\'shar wrote a whole book on revolutions of the years).',traditions:'The return\'s rising sign and the house the Sun falls in are read as the year\'s focus.',craft:'Light a candle at the exact moment, set intentions for the year, or make a birthday talisman.',care:'Some people travel for a "better" return chart; reflection works anywhere.'},related:['returns','lunar-return']},{id:'lunar-return',term:'Lunar return',category:'technique',aliases:['lunar returns'],simple:'Your lunar return is each month\'s moment when the Moon comes back to its birth degree: a personal new month.',detailed:{sky:'Every 27.3 days, the Moon reaches your natal Moon\'s longitude again.',traditions:'Read for the month\'s emotional tone.',craft:'A monthly check-in, moon water or a self-care ritual at your lunar return.',care:'A small chart for a small season.'},related:['returns','moon']},{id:'synastry',term:'Synastry',category:'technique',aliases:['compatibility','chart comparison'],simple:'Synastry compares two people\'s charts: the aspects between one person\'s planets and the other\'s, and where each person\'s planets fall in the other\'s houses.',detailed:{sky:'Inter-aspects between two natal charts, with the usual orbs a little tightened, and "house overlays".',history:'Ptolemy wrote of comparing charts for marriage and friendship; modern synastry grew with psychological astrology.',traditions:'Sun\u2013Moon, Venus\u2013Mars and contacts to the Ascendant are read as attraction and ease; Saturn contacts as commitment and weight. No single aspect makes or breaks a bond.',craft:'Partnership, friendship and coven workings can draw on the planets the two charts share.',care:'Synastry describes dynamics, not destiny, and only with charts people have given you to use. This app only compares charts you have entered yourself.'},related:['aspects','descendant','house-7'],links:[{kind:'tool',tool:'charts',label:'Your charts'}]}]},899,[]);
