__d(function(g,r,i,a,_m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"MARSEILLE_MEANINGS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return c}});const t=(t,o,n,s={})=>({...s.title?{title:s.title}:{},picture:t,upright:{keywords:o[0],text:o[1]},reversed:{keywords:n[0],text:n[1]},...s.note?{note:s.note}:{}}),o={'00-the-fool':t('A wanderer in a jester\u2019s cap and motley strides along with a bundle on a stick over his shoulder and a staff in his other hand. An animal tugs at his breeches from behind, and they have torn.',['freedom, the urge to go, unreason','Le Mat, the madman or the fool: the one who belongs nowhere and goes anyway. He is energy without a plan, a freedom that pays no mind to rules, and the push to move on. Go, but know that something is snapping at your heels.'],['aimlessness, being chased, a journey stalled','The going has no direction, or something keeps tearing at you until you turn and deal with it. Look at what follows you. Then choose a road on purpose.'],{title:'Le Mat',note:'Unnumbered, like the old Fool of the Italian games, and not the first card: older French readers put him at the end or outside the trumps. There is no cliff in the Marseille picture; the danger is the animal at his back. In Etteilla\u2019s and later French cartomancy he reads as folly, thoughtlessness or extravagance; the freer reading is modern.'}),'01-the-magician':t('A young conjuror stands at a table set with cups, dice, a knife, coins and a bag, a little wand in one hand and a ball or coin in the other. His wide hat curls into a figure of eight.',['a beginning, skill, a quick hand','Le Bateleur is the conjuror at the fair: the one who starts the show with whatever lies on his table. A beginning, a new skill, wit and a little daring. You have more to work with than you think; begin.'],['sleight of hand, a false start, inexperience','The trick shows, or the hand is not yet quick enough. Watch for smooth talk, your own or someone else\u2019s. Practise before you perform.'],{title:'I \xb7 Le Bateleur',note:'Bateleur means a street performer or juggler, not a magician; the card was renamed the Magus by 19th-century occultists such as \xc9liphas L\xe9vi. Some Marseille readers still read a hint of the trickster in him.'}),'02-the-high-priestess':t('A woman in a papal tiara sits before a hanging veil with a great book open in her lap. She wears a nun\u2019s wimple under the crown.',['knowledge kept, study, quiet authority','La Papesse, the she-pope, keeps the book open and the door closed. Learning, patience and a knowing that takes its time; a woman of judgement, or your own wisdom gathering. Read, wait and keep your counsel.'],['closedness, rigid belief, knowledge withheld','The book is shut, or held too tightly to the chest. Coldness, secrecy or a rule followed without thought. Let something new be written in.'],{title:'II \xb7 La Papesse',note:'The she-pope is likely older than the legend she is often tied to: a female pope in the tradition of Pope Joan, or a figure of Faith or the Church. Waite made her the High Priestess with a scroll; here it is an open book.'}),'03-the-empress':t('A crowned woman sits facing us on a throne whose back rises like wings, a sceptre in one hand and a shield with an eagle in the other.',['growth, eloquence, creative force','L\u2019Imp\xe9ratrice is creation that pours out: ideas, words, things growing. She is intelligence and abundance at their most fertile, and a woman of influence. Let what you\u2019ve begun bud and flower.'],['scattered growth, vanity, ideas without root','Too much comes out at once, or nothing will grow. Talk outruns action; beauty is admired but not tended. Choose one thing and feed it.'],{title:'III \xb7 L\u2019Imp\xe9ratrice',note:'French cartomancers read her for intelligence, charm and fruitfulness. The garden of wheat and the Venus sign are Waite\u2019s and Smith\u2019s additions, not hers.'}),'04-the-emperor':t('A bearded emperor sits in profile on a low throne, legs crossed, a sceptre raised. His eagle shield rests at his feet.',['power, stability, protection','L\u2019Empereur has made his place in the world and keeps it. Order, material security, a father or a patron, a word that holds. Build on solid ground and stand by what you\u2019ve built.'],['domineering, stuck, power misused','The throne becomes a fortress, or the ground under it gives. Control where care would do better, or no one at the helm. Loosen your grip without letting go of the reins.'],{title:'IIII \xb7 L\u2019Empereur'}),'05-the-hierophant':t('The pope sits between two pillars with a triple cross, his hand raised in blessing. Two smaller figures kneel before him with their backs to us, one in a broad round hat.',['teaching, counsel, a bridge between','Le Pape is the one who passes knowledge on: a teacher, a counsellor, a tradition that helps. He stands between heaven and the people kneeling. Ask for good advice, or give it.'],['dogma, bad advice, a closed door','The teacher stops listening, or the rule no longer fits the person. Beware counsel that serves the giver. Seek your own understanding.'],{title:'V \xb7 Le Pape'}),'06-the-lovers':t('A young man stands between two women, one older with a hand on his shoulder, one younger. Above them a winged Cupid in a blazing sun aims his arrow.',['choice, affection, the heart pulled two ways','L\u2019Amoureux is a young man who must choose: between two loves, two paths, or the family and a new bond. It is the card of feeling, of attraction and of the choice the heart has to make. Let Cupid\u2019s arrow fall where it will, then commit.'],['indecision, a wrong choice, divided loyalties','He can\u2019t choose, or chooses to please everyone. Feelings tangled, or a choice made for the wrong reasons. Be honest with yourself about what you want.'],{title:'VI \xb7 L\u2019Amoureux',note:'Not Waite\u2019s Adam and Eve under an angel: the Marseille card shows a choice between two women, often read as virtue and vice, or a mother and a bride. Older French cartomancy reads it as a choice, a love affair or a marriage.'}),'07-the-chariot':t('A crowned young man in armour, with crescent faces on his shoulders, rides a canopied chariot. His two horses face forward but seem to step in different directions.',['triumph, progress, going out into the world','Le Chariot is success in motion: the victor riding home, or the young man setting out. Action, travel, a goal reached through effort. Hold the reins and go.'],['scattered effort, a stall, pride before a fall','The horses pull apart, or the triumph is for show. Drive without direction. Gather your forces before you set off again.'],{title:'VII \xb7 Le Chariot'}),'08-strength':t('A woman in a wide hat, its brim curved like the Bateleur\u2019s, holds open a lion\u2019s jaws with her bare hands, calmly and without strain.',['courage, mastery, gentle force','La Force is strength that doesn\u2019t need a weapon: the instinct tamed and turned to good use. Courage, patience, a will that persuades rather than forces. You are equal to it.'],['force without control, weakness, fear','The lion pulls free, or the hand won\u2019t reach out. Anger, impatience, or a doubt in your own strength. Breathe, and go gently.'],{title:'XI \xb7 La Force',note:'In the Marseille this is XI, after the Wheel; the Golden Dawn and Waite moved it to VIII. Your lists keep it in Waite\u2019s place, but the card carries Conver\u2019s number.'}),'09-the-hermit':t('An old man in a hooded cloak leans on his staff and holds up a lantern, half hidden in its folds, as he walks.',['prudence, the wisdom of age, a search','L\u2019Hermite walks slowly and lights the way a step at a time. Experience, care, time taken, a guide or a teacher. The answer comes with patience and looking closely.'],['isolation, slowness, a lamp hidden','Withdrawal becomes loneliness, or caution becomes delay. The wisdom is there but kept under the cloak. Share the light you have.'],{title:'VIIII \xb7 L\u2019Hermite',note:'In earlier Italian decks the old man held an hourglass and was called Time; the lantern came later, and with it the hermit. Something of Father Time remains in the reading: things ripen slowly.'}),'10-wheel-of-fortune':t('A wheel on a crank turns with no hand on it. A creature climbs up one side and another sinks down the other, while a crowned, winged beast with a sword sits on top.',['change, fortune, a turn of events','La Roue de Fortune turns, and what was down comes up. A change of luck, a new cycle, events that move by themselves. Take the moment while it\u2019s yours.'],['a downturn, stagnation, waiting for the turn','The wheel sticks, or turns against you for now. What goes down comes up again. Hold steady until it moves.'],{title:'X \xb7 La Roue de Fortune',note:'The old figure of Fortune\u2019s wheel, with those rising, ruling and falling; the Marseille shows them as half-animals. The sphinx and Hebrew letters of Waite\u2019s card are not here.'}),'11-justice':t('A crowned woman sits facing us, a sword upright in one hand and scales in the other.',['balance, fairness, a decision','La Justice weighs and then decides. Fairness, order, the right measure, legal or official matters. Weigh it honestly, then act.'],['imbalance, bias, a hard judgement','The scales are tilted, by someone else or by you. Severity, or a decision put off. Look for what\u2019s fair, not just what\u2019s strict.'],{title:'VIII \xb7 La Justice',note:'VIII in the Marseille, as in most French decks before the Golden Dawn swapped it with Strength. Your lists keep it in Waite\u2019s place, but the card carries Conver\u2019s number.'}),'12-the-hanged-man':t('A young man hangs upside down by one foot from a bar between two lopped trees, his other leg crossed behind and his hands behind his back. His face is calm.',['suspension, waiting, another view','Le Pendu has stopped: tied, but not suffering. A pause, a sacrifice made willingly, a time to see the world turned over. Don\u2019t push; let it hang a while.'],['stuck, pointless sacrifice, resistance','Waiting without end, or a sacrifice nobody asked for. Struggling against the rope makes it tighter. Ask what you could let go of.'],{title:'XII \xb7 Le Pendu',note:'In 15th-century Italy, traitors were painted hanging by one foot as a public shame, and the card was sometimes called the Traitor. The reading as willing sacrifice and inner change is much later.'}),'13-death':t('A skeleton with a scythe mows a dark field. Heads, hands and feet lie in the earth, one head still crowned. The card has a number, XIII, and no name.',['ending, clearing, deep change','The card without a name cuts away what is finished so something new can grow. An ending, a transformation, ground cleared. Let it go.'],['resisted change, a slow ending, stagnation','The harvest is left standing, or the ending drags on. Fear of change makes it harder. Put the scythe to what\u2019s already dead.'],{title:'XIII \xb7 the card without a name',note:'Called La Mort in Italy and in some French decks, it is left unnamed in the Marseille pattern; modern readers call it l\u2019Arcane sans nom, the nameless card. It is rarely read as a death.'}),'14-temperance':t('A winged woman pours a stream from one vessel into another. Her feet stand on the ground among small plants.',['moderation, health, the right mixture','Temp\xe9rance blends and balances: water into wine, one feeling into another. Healing, patience, a calm that comes from measure. Take your time and find the mix.'],['excess, imbalance, impatience','Too much of one thing, too little of another. Health or harmony lost a little. Go back to the middle way.'],{title:'XIIII \xb7 Temp\xe9rance'}),'15-the-devil':t('A horned devil with bat\u2019s wings and both breasts and a man\u2019s sex stands on an anvil, a torch or bare blade in his hand. Two small horned imps with tails are tied to it by halters.',['passion, desire, a bond that holds','Le Diable is raw force: desire, ambition, appetite, the pull of what tempts. It binds, but it also gives energy and creative fire. Know what holds you, and use what drives you.'],['release, or a habit worse than it seems','The halter loosens, or you pretend it isn\u2019t there. An obsession, an unhealthy bond, money or lust running the show. Name it, and step back.'],{title:'XV \xb7 Le Diable',note:'French cartomancy reads the Devil as great force, often for ill: violence, passion, illicit gain. Modern Marseille readers such as Ben-Dov also read it as vitality and the energy of desire.'}),'16-the-tower':t('A tower is struck at the top, and its crown-shaped roof is blown off by a plume of fire. Two men fall or tumble to the ground, and coloured balls fill the air.',['upheaval, release, a breaking open','La Maison Dieu is the house that breaks open: sudden news, a shock, the end of something built on pride. What was shut in gets out. Let it fall, and see what\u2019s freed.'],['a shake-up delayed, confinement, a narrow escape','The roof holds, for now, or the walls close in. A crisis avoided, or one waiting to happen. Open a window before it opens for you.'],{title:'XVI \xb7 La Maison Dieu',note:'The name means God\u2019s house, which in old French also meant a hospital. Some read the falling men as leaping free, and the fire as heaven\u2019s touch as much as a punishment. Waite called it the Tower.'}),'17-the-star':t('A naked woman kneels on one knee at the water\u2019s edge and pours out two jugs into the stream. A great star shines among seven smaller ones, and a bird sits on top of a tree.',['hope, generosity, a gift','L\u2019\xc9toile gives freely: water poured out, light to the night. Hope, renewal, a good omen, a gift without strings. Trust that it\u2019s flowing your way.'],['hope delayed, giving too much, doubt','The jugs run dry, or you pour out everything and keep nothing. Disappointment or self-doubt. Refill yourself first.'],{title:'XVII \xb7 L\u2019\xc9toile'}),'18-the-moon':t('A moon with a face in profile draws drops up from the earth. Two dogs howl between two towers, and a crayfish rises from a pool.',['dreams, imagination, the uncertain','La Lune is the night world: dreams, longing, intuition, and the things that look different in the dark. Feelings run deep; nothing is clear yet. Let the imagination speak, but don\u2019t mistake it for fact.'],['confusion lifting, deception, fear','Either the fog begins to clear, or it hides something false. Old fears surface. Wait for daylight before you decide.'],{title:'XVIII \xb7 La Lune',note:'Older French cartomancy reads the Moon as deception, hidden enemies or danger by night; modern Marseille readers make more of dreams, intuition and the mother.'}),'19-the-sun':t('A great sun with a face sheds drops of light over two children, nearly naked, standing before a low wall. One rests a hand on the other.',['joy, friendship, clarity','Le Soleil is warmth shared: friendship, love that is simple, success in the open. Things are clear and good. Enjoy it with someone.'],['a cloudy day, pride, joy postponed','Still a good card, but the warmth is held back, or the wall is built too high. Let people in.'],{title:'XVIIII \xb7 Le Soleil',note:'Not Waite\u2019s child on a horse: the Marseille shows two children or companions under the sun, read as friendship, partnership and brotherhood.'}),'20-judgement':t('An angel in the clouds blows a trumpet hung with a cross banner. Below, a figure rises from a tomb with its back to us, between a man and a woman with hands joined.',['awakening, a call, news','Le Jugement is the call that wakes you: news, a vocation, a fresh start after a long sleep. Something rises again. Answer.'],['a call ignored, doubt, old regret','The trumpet sounds and you turn over. Or old judgements, your own, keep you in the tomb. Get up.'],{title:'XX \xb7 Le Jugement'}),'21-the-world':t('A dancing figure, bare but for a scarf, stands in an oval wreath with a little flask in one hand and a wand in the other. An angel, an eagle, a lion and an ox fill the four corners.',['fulfilment, completion, the whole','Le Monde is the work done and the dance in the middle of it: success, wholeness, the world opening. Everything is in its place. Enjoy it, and know it opens on to the next.'],['incompletion, a closed circle, delay','Almost there, or the wreath has become a wall. Something is left unfinished. Tie the last thread.'],{title:'XXI \xb7 Le Monde',note:'The four corners are the old signs of the four evangelists. Older French cartomancy reads the card as travel, success and the world at large.'})},n={'wands-01':t('A hand comes out of a cloud and grips a great rough club, its branches lopped short, with little flames or leaves falling round it.',['raw energy, a start, desire','The As de B\xe2ton is the whole force of the suit in one hand: drive, desire, the will to make and do. A venture, a new work, a strong urge. Grasp it.'],['energy blocked, a false start, force misused','The club is heavy and won\u2019t swing, or swings too hard. Frustration or wasted strength. Find the right thing to put it to.'],{note:'An ace is read as the root of its suit. B\xe2tons, the clubs of the old card games, stand for work, enterprise and the energy of the body in French cartomancy.'}),'wands-02':t('Two long staves cross in a tall X, with leafy flowers in the gaps between them.',['plans, gathering strength, a partnership','Two is the number of gathering and waiting: energy held before it is spent. Plans being made, a working partnership, desire not yet acted on. Prepare.'],['hesitation, a stand-off, impatience','The two staves block each other. Plans stall, or a partnership pulls two ways. Decide who leads.']),'wands-03':t('Two staves cross like those of the two, with a third standing straight up through the middle.',['enthusiasm, first growth, initiative','Three is the first burst outward: energy that jumps into action before it knows quite how. Enterprise, eagerness, a project leaping forward. Go, and learn as you go.'],['rashness, scattered starts','Too much begun, not enough finished. Haste over craft. Slow the rush a little.']),'wands-04':t('Four staves cross two and two in a broad X, with flowers in the gaps above, below and at the sides.',['stability, a firm base, steady work','Four is the number of the foundation. Work that is secure, a settled place, energy put into building. Rest on it.'],['routine, rigidity, a shaky base','Security turns into a rut, or the ground isn\u2019t as firm as it looks. Check the foundations, and make room to move.']),'wands-05':t('The X of four staves, with a fifth standing upright through the middle.',['challenge, a new ambition, competition','Five breaks the square: a new desire, a test, a contest. Rivalry and struggle, or a step beyond the safe. The effort is worth it.'],['quarrels, pointless rivalry','The struggle has become its own reason. Disputes over small things. Pick your contests.']),'wands-06':t('Six staves cross three and three in a broad X, with flowers set in its gaps.',['pleasure in work, harmony, success','Six is the number of pleasure and of things fitting together. Work you enjoy, energy that flows, recognition for it. Do what you love doing.'],['vanity, pleasure without effort','Enjoyment slides into self-satisfaction, or the pleasure fades. Remember why you began.']),'wands-07':t('The X of six staves, with a seventh upright through the middle.',['action, courage, holding your ground','Seven is action in the world: energy aimed and used. Effort, initiative, standing up for what you do. Keep at it.'],['overwhelm, giving way, strain','Too much to hold at once, and effort turned to strain. Choose what to defend.']),'wands-08':t('Eight staves cross four and four in a great X that fills the card, a flower above and below.',['full power, order, momentum','Eight is fullness and order: energy at its height and well directed. Things move fast and well. Use the momentum.'],['haste, overload, a tangle','Too much at once, or things run ahead of you. Sort out what matters first.']),'wands-09':t('The X of eight staves, with a ninth upright through the middle.',['a turning point, endurance, change ahead','Nine is a crisis of passage: an effort near its end, energy changing form. Hold on; something is closing so another can start.'],['exhaustion, stubbornness, letting go too late','Pushing on after the work is done, weary and on guard. Rest, and let it change.']),'wands-10':t('Ten staves crowd the card: eight crossing in a great X, with two standing straight through the middle.',['the end of a cycle, a load laid down, a new start','Ten closes the suit: the work is done, and its energy is about to begin again in a new shape. A finished task, or a burden nearly put down. Begin to think of what is next.'],['overburdened, unable to finish','Still carrying it all, long after it should be set down. Share the load, or let some of it go.']),'wands-11':t('A young man in a small cap stands in profile with a great green club planted on the ground beside him, both hands on it.',['eagerness, a beginner, a first venture','The Valet de B\xe2ton has more energy than skill: a young worker, an apprentice, news of a venture. Enthusiasm and a strong pair of hands. Learn the craft.'],['clumsiness, impatience, bluster','More force than sense, or the club too heavy for the hands. Be patient with the learning.']),'wands-12':t('A rider on a stepping horse carries a great club upright before him.',['an undertaking, travel, going after it','The Cavalier de B\xe2ton carries the suit\u2019s energy out into the world: a mission, a move, a departure for work. Passion in motion. Ride out.'],['impulsiveness, a broken journey','Galloping without knowing where, or turning back too soon. Steer.']),'wands-13':t('A crowned queen sits holding a great baton against her, her other hand at her waist.',['self-possession, creative strength, warmth','The Reine de B\xe2ton holds her energy well: a capable woman, creative and sure of herself, at home in her house and her work. Be at ease in your own strength.'],['domineering, jealous, held-in energy','Strength turns to control, or desire is kept back until it sours. Let it out kindly.']),'wands-14':t('A king in armour, his crown set on a wide hat, sits holding a long baton slanted down to the floor before him, his other hand on his hip.',['leadership, mastery, an honest man','The Roi de B\xe2ton knows his work and leads by doing it: an employer, a skilled hand, a decisive and fair man. Take charge.'],['harshness, impatience, a tyrant','Leadership turns hard or hasty, and pride in the work becomes pride in himself. Lead, don\u2019t drive.'])},s={'cups-01':t('A single great cup fills the card, its tall lid and stem built up like a little church or castle.',['love, joy, the heart full','The As de Coupe is the heart opening: a new love, a joy, a feeling that fills you. A house well loved, a feast, a gift. Let it brim over.'],['a heart closed, emptiness, a feeling unmet','The cup is shut or poured away. Love held back, or a sadness. Open it a little.'],{note:'An ace is read as the root of its suit. Coupes, the hearts of the old card games, stand for love, feeling and the home in French cartomancy.'}),'cups-02':t('Two cups stand side by side beneath a flowering ornament, with two fish-like heads leaning out over them.',['a meeting, affection, a pair','Two cups: a bond, an attraction, two hearts learning each other. Love beginning, a friendship, a reconciliation. Meet halfway.'],['misunderstanding, a quarrel between friends','The cups face each other but don\u2019t touch. A feeling not returned, or not spoken. Say what you feel.']),'cups-03':t('Three cups, one above and two below, with stems of flowers curling between them.',['joy, celebration, a feeling blossoming','Three is the first burst: happiness that wants to be shared, a celebration, a love that shows itself. Be glad together.'],['excess, a feeling too quick','Too much, too soon: a crush, or an evening that goes on too long. Enjoy it, but steady.']),'cups-04':t('Four cups set two and two in a square, with flowers between them.',['contentment, security in love, a steady home','Four is stability: a settled heart, a home that holds, feelings you can rely on. Enjoy what is safe and good.'],['boredom, a heart gone flat','Security becomes sameness, or you can\u2019t feel the good you have. Look again at what\u2019s in front of you.']),'cups-05':t('Five cups, two above and two below and one in the middle, among flowering stems.',['a new feeling, a stirring, unrest','Five is a new interest that unsettles the old: a feeling that calls you elsewhere, a spiritual opening, or a small grief. Follow what moves you, gently.'],['regret, disappointment, a feeling lost','Something was spilt, and there is sorrow over what\u2019s gone. Look at what remains.']),'cups-06':t('Six cups in two columns of three, with branches and flowers between them.',['pleasure, affection, harmony','Six is delight: love that pleases, kindness, memories and friendships that warm. Enjoy the company.'],['nostalgia, sentimentality','Living in yesterday\u2019s feeling, a love that is more memory than present. Come back to now.']),'cups-07':t('Three cups above, three below and a seventh in the middle, with vines curling round them.',['feeling put to work, generosity, a dream acted on','Seven is action: love that goes out and does something, help given, a dream made real. Let your feelings reach the world.'],['illusion, wishful thinking','Many cups, few full. Daydreams, or choices made on a fancy. Find the one that\u2019s real.']),'cups-08':t('Eight cups, three above, two in the middle and three below, with flowering stems between them.',['fullness of heart, peace, love fulfilled','Eight is fullness: a heart complete and at peace, love given and returned. Rest in it.'],['a heart overfull, or emptied','Overflowing feeling, or giving until there is nothing left. Find the measure.']),'cups-09':t('Nine cups in three rows of three, with flowers between them.',['a wish granted, satisfaction, a turning','Nine is the wish card of old fortune-telling, and feeling ripened and ready to change. Contentment; then let it move on.'],['smugness, a satisfaction that won\u2019t last','Pleased with yourself, or pleased with too little. Look past the moment.'],{note:'The nine of hearts was the wish card of French and English fortune-telling with playing cards, and it carried the meaning over to the nine of cups. The rest is read by number and suit.'}),'cups-10':t('Nine cups in three rows of three, and a tenth, larger, lying on its side across the top over them all.',['the heart\u2019s home, completion, family','Ten closes the suit: love fulfilled, a family or a place where you belong, and feeling ready for a new cycle. Be at home.'],['a home disturbed, an ending in love','A family quarrel, or the close of a chapter of feeling. What ends makes room.']),'cups-11':t('A bareheaded young man with flowers in his hair walks along holding up an open cup in one hand and its lid low in the other, as if about to show what is inside.',['a tender start, an offer, a sensitive young person','The Valet de Coupe offers his heart, still shy: a message of love, a gentle beginner, a feeling just opening. Lift the lid.'],['shyness, emotional immaturity','Feelings kept covered, or too easily hurt. Be brave with your heart.']),'cups-12':t('A rider on a walking horse carries a cup carefully out in front of him.',['a proposal, an offer of love, a gentle approach','The Cavalier de Coupe brings the heart out to someone: an invitation, a proposal, a peacemaker. Accept what is carried to you, or carry it yourself.'],['seduction, false promises','The cup is offered to charm, not to give. Look at the hands as well as the cup.']),'cups-13':t('A crowned queen sits holding a great covered cup in one hand and a sword in the other, its blade resting against her shoulder.',['deep feeling, care, a loving woman','The Reine de Coupe keeps her feelings close and deep, and has a blade by her to guard them: devotion, intuition, a love that holds. Tend your inner life.'],['moodiness, a closed heart','The lid stays shut. Feelings brooded on, or kept from those who need them. Open the cup.'],{note:'Hers is the one closed cup among the courts, and Marseille readers make much of it as feeling held within; the sword in her other hand is read as the clear mind that keeps it. The courts are read by rank: the Reine is mastery turned inward.'}),'cups-14':t('A crowned king in a wide hat sits on a bench holding a great open cup in one hand, his other hand on his hip.',['emotional maturity, kindness, a generous man','The Roi de Coupe is feeling mastered and shared: a kind man, a protector, generosity without show. Be steady and warm.'],['manipulation, indulgence','Feeling used to sway others, or comfort taken too far. Keep your heart honest.'])},h={'swords-01':t('A hand comes out of a cloud holding a sword upright. Its point passes through a crown hung with a palm and a laurel branch.',['clarity, a decision, victory won by thought','The As d\u2019\xc9p\xe9e is the cutting edge of the mind: a clear idea, a firm decision, a battle won. It cuts through doubt. Say it plainly and act on it.'],['confusion, a harsh word, force turned back','The blade cuts the wrong way: a quarrel, a cruel remark, a decision made in anger. Sheathe it until you see clearly.'],{note:'An ace is read as the root of its suit. \xc9p\xe9es, the spades of the old card games, stood for trouble, quarrels and grief in French cartomancy; modern Marseille readers add the mind, decision and the courage to cut.'}),'swords-02':t('Two curved blades cross above and below to make an oval, with a flower blooming in the middle.',['a truce, thought held in balance, waiting','Two is gathering: two ideas weighed side by side, a peace kept, a decision not yet made. Let it ripen before you choose.'],['a stalemate, tension, a choice avoided','The blades lock. Two minds, or two sides, that won\u2019t give. Someone has to speak first.']),'swords-03':t('The oval of curved blades with a straight sword standing through the middle.',['a sharp idea, a first stand, a break','Three is the first burst: a thought that wants out, a line drawn, a break made. It can sting, and it clears the air. Say it kindly.'],['a hurt, quarrels, words too quick','The blade goes in deeper than meant. A grief or a falling-out. Let it heal before you strike again.']),'swords-04':t('Four curved blades make a double oval around a flower, closed and still.',['rest, a settled mind, a truce','Four is stability: a mind at peace, a decision that stands, a pause in the fighting. Rest and gather your thoughts.'],['fixed opinions, enforced rest','Stillness becomes stuck thinking, or rest you didn\u2019t choose. Let a new idea in.']),'swords-05':t('The double oval of curved blades with a straight sword through the middle.',['a new conviction, a test, a dispute','Five breaks the settled square: a new idea that challenges the old, a dispute, a stand taken. Fight fair.'],['a hollow win, spite, loss','Winning the argument and losing the friend. Let it go.']),'swords-06':t('Six curved blades interlace in a tall oval, with flowers at its heart.',['ease of mind, understanding, a way through','Six is pleasure: thinking that flows, understanding reached, a calmer passage after strain. Enjoy the clarity.'],['self-deception, a problem carried along','The calm is only on the surface, or the trouble comes with you. Look honestly at what you\u2019re carrying.']),'swords-07':t('The six curved blades, with a straight sword through the middle.',['action, strategy, a plan carried out','Seven is action: the mind put to work, a plan made and done, cleverness in the world. Think it through and go.'],['scheming, half-truths, a plan undone','Cunning without honesty, yours or another\u2019s. A plan that leaves something out. Be straight.']),'swords-08':t('Eight curved blades make a dense oval lattice around a flower.',['order, discipline, a mind at full stretch','Eight is fullness: thought ordered and thorough, rigour, a mind at its sharpest. Do the careful work.'],['overthinking, a mind boxed in','Too many thoughts, crossed like blades, until you feel trapped. Step outside the oval.']),'swords-09':t('The eight curved blades, with a straight sword through the middle.',['a crisis of mind, a cutting away, endurance','Nine is a turning: an old way of thinking ends, sometimes painfully. Worry, a sleepless night, and then a clearer mind. It will pass.'],['anxiety eased, or anguish held too long','Either the worst of the worry lifts, or it turns over and over. Talk to someone.']),'swords-10':t('Eight curved blades make the oval, and two straight swords cross through the middle of it, ten in all.',['the end of a struggle, a mind made new','Ten closes the suit: a conflict over, an idea fully spent, the ground cleared for new thinking. Let the fight end.'],['an ending that drags, defeat','The struggle won\u2019t quite finish, or you hold on to the loss. Lay down the swords.']),'swords-11':t('A young man in a wide hat stands holding a drawn sword upright in one hand and its scabbard in the other, glancing down and aside.',['vigilance, a quick mind, a beginner\u2019s boldness','The Valet d\u2019\xc9p\xe9e has drawn his blade but hasn\u2019t used it: alert, curious, ready to defend an idea. A young person of wit, or news that needs care. Watch and learn.'],['suspicion, gossip, rashness','The sword waved about, or watched for in everyone. Sharp words, idle talk. Put the blade back until it\u2019s needed.']),'swords-12':t('An armoured rider on a stepping horse holds up his sword as he goes.',['decision in motion, a mission, defence','The Cavalier d\u2019\xc9p\xe9e rides out to settle something: a cause, a decision carried through, help that arrives quickly. Go and do what you\u2019ve decided.'],['aggression, a hasty departure','Charging in before thinking, or a sudden going. Pull the horse up.']),'swords-13':t('A crowned queen sits holding a sword upright, her other hand at her waist.',['clear judgement, independence, honesty','The Reine d\u2019\xc9p\xe9e sees clearly and says what she sees: a woman of sense and independence, perhaps one who has known grief. Be honest, with yourself first.'],['coldness, a sharp tongue, loneliness','Clarity turns cold, or words cut more than they need to. Let warmth back in.']),'swords-14':t('A king in armour, his crown set on a wide hat, sits on a stone block holding an upright sword in one hand and a short sceptre in the other.',['authority, judgement, a man of law','The Roi d\u2019\xc9p\xe9e decides and stands by it: a judge, an adviser, a mind used with authority. Make the decision and own it.'],['severity, cruelty, injustice','Judgement without mercy, or power used to cut people down. Temper the law with kindness.'])},l={'pentacles-01':t('A single great coin fills the card, patterned like a flower, with leafy stems growing round it.',['prosperity, a solid start, the body','The As de Deniers is material life in one coin: money, health, a home, a sound beginning. It is real and it can grow. Plant it well.'],['greed, a loss, a chance not taken','Money held too tightly, or slipping away. Look after the ground you stand on.'],{note:'An ace is read as the root of its suit. Deniers, the old coins, stand for money, work that pays, the body and the home in French cartomancy; the diamonds of the playing cards.'}),'pentacles-02':t('Two coins, one above the other, are joined by a long ribbon that winds round them like an S. Printers put their name or the date on it; this one reads N. Conver and 1760.',['exchange, movement, juggling resources','Two is gathering: money coming in and going out, an exchange, a partnership in business. Keep it moving.'],['money worries, a balance lost','Too much to juggle, and the ribbon tangles. Simplify your accounts.']),'pentacles-03':t('Three coins, one above and two below, with flowering stems between them.',['first growth, skill, a project taking shape','Three is the first burst: work beginning to show, a skill being learned, the first profit. Keep building.'],['amateurism, a slow start','Growth stalls, or the work isn\u2019t done with care. Learn the trade properly.']),'pentacles-04':t('Four coins at the corners of a square, with a shield of three fleurs-de-lis at the centre.',['security, savings, a firm footing','Four is stability: money kept, a house secure, a body well rooted. Enjoy the ground under you.'],['miserliness, holding on too hard','Security becomes clutching, or fear of losing. Loosen your hand a little.']),'pentacles-05':t('Four coins at the corners with a fifth in the middle, among flowering stems.',['a new venture, a change of values, a test','Five is a new step: an opportunity beyond the safe, a change in what you value, a lesson in money or health. Take it, with care.'],['hardship, a loss, feeling left out','Money or health under strain for a while. Ask for help; it is there.']),'pentacles-06':t('Six coins, one at the top, four in a square around a flower and one at the bottom, with leafy stems winding round them.',['generosity, comfort, a fair exchange','Six is pleasure: enough to share, give and take in balance, comfort in the body. Be generous.'],['debts, unequal giving','Gifts with strings, or giving more than you can. Even the scales.']),'pentacles-07':t('Six coins in two columns of three, with a seventh between the top two pairs.',['effort, work that pays, patience','Seven is action in the world: steady work, money earned, things built with your hands. Keep tending it.'],['impatience, effort without return','Waiting for a harvest that hasn\u2019t ripened, or working without reward. Look at where effort is going.']),'pentacles-08':t('Eight coins in two columns of four, with stems of flowers between them.',['prosperity, order, mastery','Eight is fullness: material life in good order, well earned, a skill perfected. Enjoy what is secure.'],['perfectionism, too much detail','Polishing what is already done, or order turned fussy. Good enough is good.']),'pentacles-09':t('Eight coins in two columns of four with a ninth set in the middle.',['ripeness, independence, a change in fortune','Nine is a turning: comfort earned and a readiness to move on, perhaps a change of work or home. Enjoy it, and see what comes next.'],['insecurity, a loss of footing','The ground shifts under what you\u2019ve built. Hold to what matters most.']),'pentacles-10':t('Ten coins fill the card, two columns of four with two more down the middle, and flowering stems between them.',['wealth, inheritance, the end of a cycle','Ten closes the suit: material life complete, a family\u2019s wealth, a cycle ended and a new one ready to start. Share it and pass it on.'],['family quarrels over money, a fortune at risk','Disputes about what is inherited or owed. Keep it fair, and keep perspective.']),'pentacles-11':t('A young man in a wide hat stands holding up a coin, while another lies on the ground at his feet.',['study, a practical beginning, a careful student','The Valet de Deniers looks at his coin and wonders what it is worth: an apprentice, a student, a small sum and its promise. Learn the value of what you have.'],['wasted chances, lack of focus','The coin on the ground goes unnoticed. Distraction, or a lesson skipped. Pick it up.']),'pentacles-12':t('A rider holds a stick or wand, and a coin floats in the air before him.',['steady progress, a sound proposal, travel for work','The Cavalier de Deniers moves slowly but surely after something real: a business trip, a careful offer, money coming. Keep on.'],['stagnation, a deal delayed','Too slow, or riding after something just out of reach. Check that the goal is solid.']),'pentacles-13':t('A crowned queen, seen in profile, holds a coin up before her face, a sceptre resting against her shoulder and her other hand at her waist.',['practical care, abundance, good sense','The Reine de Deniers looks after what she has: a capable, generous woman, a home that runs well, health well kept. Tend your resources.'],['worry over money, neglect','Too much fret about having, or too little care for what you have. Find the balance.']),'pentacles-14':t('A king in a wide hat sits outdoors on the grass with his legs crossed, holding his coin low in his lap.',['prosperity, reliability, a man of means','The Roi de Deniers has built something that lasts: a businessman, a provider, a steady hand with money. Be generous with what you\u2019ve made.'],['greed, materialism, stubbornness','Wealth valued for itself, or a hand that won\u2019t open. Remember what it\u2019s for.'])},u=t=>/-(0[2-9]|1[0-4])$/.test(t),c=Object.fromEntries(Object.entries({...o,...n,...s,...h,...l}).map(([t,o])=>o.note||!u(t)?[t,o]:[t,{...o,note:Number(t.slice(-2))>10?"The Marseille courts are read by rank: the Valet a beginner or a messenger, the Cavalier someone or something on the move, the Reine mastery turned inward, the Roi mastery turned outward. In older cartomancy they also stand for people in your life.":"The numbered cards of the Marseille show only their suit signs, so they are read by number and suit, as in French cartomancy and among modern Marseille readers, not by a scene: the scenes on these cards in the Rider\u2013Waite\u2013Smith are Pamela Colman Smith\u2019s own, of 1909."}]))},2463,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,_m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SOLA_BUSCA_NOTE",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SOLA_BUSCA_MEANINGS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return o}});const t='The Sola Busca has no centuries-old tradition of reading. Nobody knows quite what its makers meant by it, and scholars still argue over who many of its figures are. These meanings are modern ones, built from what each engraving shows and from the stories of the people it names.',n=(t,n)=>({keywords:t,text:n}),o={'00-the-fool':{title:'Mato',picture:'A ragged man in a red shirt and a feathered cap plays a bagpipe as he walks, a crow perched on his bare shoulder and a twisted tree just coming into leaf behind him.',upright:n('freedom, the outsider, the first note','The fool walks to his own tune with a crow for company, outside the order of named heroes who follow him. He has nothing to guard, so he can go anywhere. Begin without waiting to be ready.'),reversed:n('noise, drifting, a warning ignored','The piping drowns out everything, and the crow on his shoulder goes unnoticed. Freedom has slid into drift. Stop playing long enough to hear where you are.'),note:'The only trump without a Roman or biblical name. The crow and the barely leafing tree have been read as the blackening at the start of the alchemical work (the Brera catalogue, via Howard); that is a modern reading.'},'01-the-magician':{title:'Panfilio',picture:'A young soldier seen from behind strides away in a red turban and green tunic, a great oval shield on his arm, a sword at his side and his free hand raised as if calling to someone ahead.',upright:n('setting out, initiative, a call answered','The first of the deck\u2019s soldiers is already on the move, shield up, hailing whoever is ahead. This is the card of starting: committing yourself and going. Take the first step and speak up as you take it.'),reversed:n('haste, turning away, all show','He walks off so fast we never see his face. Beware of starting things only to be seen leaving, or hurrying past the detail that matters. Turn round and look at what you\u2019re doing.'),note:'Number I, the Magician\u2019s place in other decks, but there is no juggler or table here, and the card is titled Panfilio, not Il Bagatto. The name is unidentified; a Venetian game and Boccaccio\u2019s Panfilo have both been suggested. Modern reading.'},'02-the-high-priestess':{title:'Postumio',picture:'A soldier seen from behind, a club and a sword in his hands, stands before a brick pillar with a skull on top; his shield carries his name and a motto.',upright:n('mortality, endurance, facing fate','Postumio stands armed and steady before a skull. The card asks you to look plainly at endings and limits, and carry on regardless. What you can\u2019t change, you can still meet well.'),reversed:n('fatalism, dread, giving up','Looking at the skull too long turns endurance into resignation. Not everything is fated. Find the one thing still in your hands.'),note:'The name points to the Roman Postumii, but which one is unknown. Peter Mark Adams reads Postumio and Tulio (XI) as the paired torchbearers of the Mithraic mysteries; that is his argument, not settled scholarship. Modern reading.'},'03-the-empress':{title:'Lenpio',picture:'A soldier in a light helmet clutches his brow, a tuft of roots or flame in his other hand, his red banner furled on its lance beside him and a basin of blood at his feet.',upright:n('reckoning, remorse, facing a mistake','Lenpio holds his head over a basin of blood, his banner furled. Something has gone wrong and he knows it. Honest regret is where repair begins; let it teach you rather than sink you.'),reversed:n('guilt that stalls, denial, brooding','Remorse has become a place to live, or the mistake is being looked away from. Name it, make what amends you can, and walk on.'),note:'Not identified; Lepidus has been suggested. The Brera catalogue sees an alchemical fire rite here. This reading follows the gesture of distress, and is modern.'},'04-the-emperor':{title:'Mario',picture:'A soldier in a winged helmet and red cloak, seen from behind, strides past a broken tree with his shield and banner, looking up at the sky.',upright:n('drive, ambition, self-made strength','Gaius Marius came from no great family and was consul seven times, by will and soldiering. This is the card of building your own authority. Use your strength and your plan; don\u2019t wait to be given a place.'),reversed:n('ruthlessness, overreaching, old grudges','Marius ended his life in bloody feuding, holding on to power too long. Ambition can curdle into a need to win at any cost. Know when you have enough.'),note:'Gaius Marius, the Roman general. Number IIII, the Emperor\u2019s place elsewhere, and the theme of authority carries over. The red and the winged helmet have been read as signs of Mars. Modern reading.'},'05-the-hierophant':{title:'Catulo',picture:'A laurel-crowned young man with a bandaged leg holds a measuring staff hung with plumb-weights, a surveyor\u2019s groma, beside a tall red standard and a shield.',upright:n('measure, sound judgement, earned honour','Catulo wears the victor\u2019s laurel but holds a measuring tool, not a sword. Wins that last are measured and planned. Take the level of things before you build.'),reversed:n('rules for their own sake, fussiness, a wound ignored','Measuring can become a way of never acting, and the bandaged leg says something still needs tending. Let the rule serve the work, and see to the hurt.'),note:'A Lutatius Catulus: either Gaius, whose sea victory ended the First Punic War, or Quintus, Marius\u2019s colleague against the Cimbri. Number V, the Hierophant\u2019s place elsewhere; Howard relates the groma to Temperance. Modern reading.'},'06-the-lovers':{title:'Sesto',picture:'A young man in pale armour stands facing us with a lit torch in one hand, a sword at his hip and his shield set down beside him.',upright:n('desire, a torch lit, choosing','A young man holds a burning torch: passion, warmth, the will that lights a way. In the place of the Lovers, it asks what you are burning for. Carry the flame where you mean it to go.'),reversed:n('recklessness, desire that harms, burning out','A torch can light a room or set it on fire. If Sesto is Sextus Tarquinius, this is the card of a desire that brought down a kingdom. Hold your fire until you know what it will catch.'),note:'Uncertain: Sextus Pompey, Pompey\u2019s son, and Sextus Tarquinius, whose crime against Lucretia ended Rome\u2019s kings, have both been proposed; the Brera catalogue reads the figure as Mercury. Modern reading.'},'07-the-chariot':{title:'Deo Tauro',picture:'A laurelled man sits in a fantastic chariot whose back curls up like a scaled fish-tail, a halberd in his hand and a shield marked SC on its wheel.',upright:n('triumph, momentum, a steady seat','Deo Tauro rides in state, calm in a strange and splendid car. This is success carried with composure. Keep your seat and your aim, and let the momentum do its work.'),reversed:n('stalled progress, pomp, a shaky alliance','Deiotarus kept his throne by backing whoever was winning in Rome, and nearly lost it for that. Watch for a triumph that depends on someone else\u2019s favour. Steer by your own course.'),note:'Plainly the deck\u2019s Chariot. The name is Deiotarus, king of Galatia, whom Cicero defended; Peter Mark Adams argues the spelling, \u201cgod bull\u201d, deliberately points to Taurus and the Mithraic bull. Modern reading.'},'08-strength':{title:'Nerone',picture:'A wild-haired man in armour holds a baby upside down by the leg over a fire, a staff hung with metal balls over his shoulder and a shield at his side.',upright:n('power and its test, restraint, the fire','Nero stands for strength without conscience, and here he holds a child over the flames. As a reading, the card asks what you will do with power over something small and helpless. Real strength is the hand that holds back.'),reversed:n('cruelty, harm done, power abused','The fire is lit and someone small is in danger. Watch for harshness, yours or another\u2019s, toward the vulnerable. Step in, or step back; don\u2019t look away.'),note:'The emperor Nero. Number VIII, where the Rider\u2013Waite\u2013Smith puts Strength, but it shares nothing with that gentle card. Alchemical readings see the child in the fire as matter put to the flame. Modern reading.'},'09-the-hermit':{title:'Falco',picture:'An old bearded king, crowned, kneels with sceptre and shield and looks up at the sky, his helmet cast down on the ground beside him.',upright:n('humility, looking up, wisdom in age','A crowned king kneels and looks up, his helmet set aside. Even the mighty have something above them. Quiet your own voice and seek counsel beyond yourself.'),reversed:n('pride, refusing help, self-absorption','The king won\u2019t kneel, or kneels only for show. Asking for guidance can feel like losing face. It isn\u2019t.'),note:'Falco is unidentified; a Roman consul, Quintus Sosius Falco, has been suggested. Number VIIII, the Hermit\u2019s place elsewhere; the old man looking skyward fits it loosely. Modern reading.'},'10-wheel-of-fortune':{title:'Venturio',picture:'A soldier in a broad red hat walks with a staff, raising his other hand, a small winged shape at his feet.',upright:n('fortune, a turn, seizing the moment','Venturio\u2019s name holds ventura, fortune, and he lifts a hand as if to catch it. If he is Ventidius Bassus, he was led as a child captive in a Roman triumph and later rode in a triumph of his own. Luck is turning; be ready.'),reversed:n('impatience, bad timing, waiting on luck','Grabbing at fortune too early, or waiting for it to do all the work. The wheel turns at its own pace. Prepare, then watch.'),note:'No wheel is shown, though the card stands where Fortune stands. The link with Publius Ventidius Bassus is a suggestion, not settled. Modern reading.'},'11-justice':{title:'Tulio',picture:'A bearded man in a horned cap, seen from behind with his cloak swirling, holds up a tall flaming torch and looks into the sky.',upright:n('clarity, reason, a light held up','Tulio carries a torch high and looks beyond it. If he is Cicero, he is the voice of argument and law. Bring the matter into the light and judge it fairly.'),reversed:n('fine words, hiding, avoiding judgement','A torch can be held for show, and eloquence can cover an unfair case. Or a question that needs answering is being turned away from. Face it.'),note:'A Tullius: Cicero, or Rome\u2019s kings Tullus Hostilius and Servius Tullius. Number XI, where the Rider\u2013Waite\u2013Smith puts Justice, though no scales are shown. Peter Mark Adams pairs Tulio with Postumio as Mithraic torchbearers. Modern reading.'},'12-the-hanged-man':{title:'Carbone',picture:'An old bearded man in a winged cap walks with a staff under a crescent moon, carrying an oil lamp, a quiver marked S.P.Q.R. at his side.',upright:n('searching, patience, a light in the dark','An old man walks by moonlight with a small lamp. This is patient seeking through uncertain times. You don\u2019t need to see the whole road, only the next stretch.'),reversed:n('confusion, suspicion, getting lost','Moonlight plays tricks and the lamp is small. Fears may be filling the gaps in what you know. Check before you believe.'),note:'The Papirii Carbones were Roman consuls; which one is meant is unknown. Number XII, the Hanged Man\u2019s place elsewhere, but the crescent links it to the Moon. Modern reading.'},'13-death':{title:'Catone',picture:'A young soldier studies an unrolled scroll while his spear pins the head of a fallen man to the ground; a star shines above him.',upright:n('endings, resolve, what must be cut','The only trump with a death on it. Catone stands over the fallen as if checking what fate has written. Something is over; let it be over, and act with integrity in what follows.'),reversed:n('harshness, refusing an ending, violence','An ending is being resisted, or met with more force than it needs. Cato\u2019s stern virtue could be merciless. Let go cleanly, without cruelty.'),note:'Cato, the Elder or the Younger. At XIII, as Death is elsewhere. Peter Mark Adams connects the head and the star to Algol, the \u201cdemon star\u201d in the Gorgon\u2019s head; that is his reading. The deck\u2019s motto TRAHOR FATIS, \u201cI am drawn by the fates\u201d, appears here and on the Aces of Cups and Coins.'},'14-temperance':{title:'Bocho',picture:'A wild-haired man kneels behind a great shield, gasping up at the sky, a red banner on a lance in his hands.',upright:n('pressure, a hard choice, waiting it out','Bocchus, king of Mauretania, was caught between Rome and his son-in-law Jugurtha, and wavered for months. The card is that suspended moment under pressure. Wait if you must, but know the choice will come.'),reversed:n('betrayal, frustration, a choice made badly','Bocchus chose in the end by handing Jugurtha over in chains. Watch for an easy way out that costs someone else. Choose what you could live with.'),note:'Bocchus I of Mauretania, from Sallust\u2019s Jugurthine War. Number XIIII, Temperance\u2019s place elsewhere; Howard sees the kneeling traitor as closer to the Hanged Man. Modern reading.'},'15-the-devil':{title:'Metelo',picture:'A bearded warrior in a red cap sits gesturing toward a purple column crowned with flame, a banner marked SC above him and his shield at his knee.',upright:n('self-command, steadiness, a fire kept','Metelo sits calmly before a column of flame, one hand raised as if to steady it. Strong feeling, desire or anger, kept in its place. Tend your fire; don\u2019t feed it blindly.'),reversed:n('temper, compulsion, being held','The flame has the upper hand. In the Devil\u2019s place, this points to habits and appetites that hold you. Notice what you keep returning to, and why.'),note:'One of the Caecilii Metelli. Lucius Caecilius Metellus rescued the sacred objects from the burning temple of Vesta, which the flaming column may recall; that link is only a suggestion. Number XV, the Devil\u2019s place elsewhere. Modern reading.'},'16-the-tower':{title:'Olivo',picture:'A crowned young man, seen from behind, points up at a dark-rayed sun, a basilisk crouching at his feet.',upright:n('radiance, change by fire, breakthrough','A young king points to a blazing sun while a basilisk crouches at his feet. In alchemical writing the basilisk belongs to the making of gold. Something is being burned clear to make room for something finer.'),reversed:n('pride, sudden upheaval, scorching','The sun that ripens can also scorch. Watch for pride before a shake-up, or a change forced too fast. Step back out of the glare.'),note:'Olivo is unidentified. Number XVI, the Tower\u2019s place elsewhere, though the picture shows the sun. The Brera catalogue links the basilisk to gold-making. Modern reading.'},'17-the-star':{title:'Ipeo',picture:'An old bearded man with great bat wings and a pointed cap, in a long robe, lifts his hands toward a small winged head set on a staff.',upright:n('invocation, reverence, help from beyond','Ipeo, bat-winged, prays to a winged head on a pole. This is the card of reaching for help beyond the everyday. Ask, and ask honestly, and be careful what you ask for.'),reversed:n('false idols, superstition, a bad bargain','What is being prayed to may not deserve it. Watch for trust placed in the wrong thing, or a promise that costs too much. Look hard at what you revere.'),note:'Unidentified; Hippias has been suggested. Number XVII, the Star\u2019s place elsewhere, but the wings and the idol are closer to a Devil, and Howard reads it so. Modern reading.'},'18-the-moon':{title:'Lentulo',picture:'A white-bearded old man in a long robe leans on a stone altar before a tall stand with a flame burning on top, his helmet laid on the ground at his feet.',upright:n('devotion, quiet practice, keeping vigil','Lentulo has laid down his helmet to tend a flame at an altar. This is patient, private devotion. Keep your practice quietly and let it work.'),reversed:n('secrecy, mixed motives, plotting','If he is Lentulus Sura, he is a conspirator behind a dignified face. Things done in private may not be what they seem. Ask what the rite is really for.'),note:'One of the Cornelii Lentuli, perhaps Lentulus Sura of Catiline\u2019s conspiracy. Number XVIII, the Moon\u2019s place elsewhere; the Brera catalogue sees a figure of Time, the flame standing in for an hourglass. Modern reading.'},'19-the-sun':{title:'Sabino',picture:'A crowned, bearded king in armour holds a long staff and a great shell-shaped basin, and looks up and away.',upright:n('plenty, generosity, looking ahead','Sabino holds a full basin and looks ahead with calm confidence. This is warmth and plenty held openly. Share what you have; there is enough.'),reversed:n('hesitation, hoarding, uncertainty','The basin is held tight, or the gaze wavers. Plenty kept back loses its warmth. Decide what to give.'),note:'Unidentified; the Brera catalogue proposes a contemporary humanist. Number XVIIII, the Sun\u2019s place elsewhere, though nothing sun-like is shown. The reading is a modern one from the picture.'},'20-judgement':{title:'Nenbroto',picture:'A young man reels back under a blast of fire from the sky; the top of a column breaks away beside him and a small crown lies on the ground.',upright:n('a fall from pride, shock, a structure broken','Nimrod, the mighty hunter said to have built Babel, is struck from above as his tower breaks. What was built on pride is coming down. Painful as it is, the ground is being cleared.'),reversed:n('disaster averted, clinging to ruins, defiance','The warning is heard, or it is shouted down. Holding on to what is falling only adds to the fall. Get clear, then build lower and truer.'),note:'Nimrod, from Genesis 10 and the Babel story. Number XX, where the Rider\u2013Waite\u2013Smith puts Judgement, but the picture is the Tower\u2019s, and the reading follows the Rider\u2013Waite\u2013Smith Tower closely.'},'21-the-world':{title:'Nabuchodenasor',picture:'A king sits sunk in a bed of grass or straw, his crown cast down beside him and a staff in his hand, beneath a great starry globe crossed by bands where a winged dragon flies.',upright:n('completion, humility, the world restored','In the Book of Daniel, Nebuchadnezzar is driven out to eat grass like an ox, and is restored when he learns humility. Here he sits under the whole world with his crown off. The cycle is complete; you come back wiser.'),reversed:n('stagnation, pride before the lesson, unfinished','The king is still in the field, not yet ready to look up. A lesson is waiting before things can close. Let go of the crown.'),note:'Nebuchadnezzar of Babylon, as in Daniel 4. The deck\u2019s World, with the globe of the heavens; the story is clear in the picture, and the reading built on it is modern.'},'wands-01':{picture:'Two winged children steady a great knotted club planted in an empty suit of blue armour, a helmet and shield beside it, garlands above and the letters M and S on either side.',upright:n('energy, potential, a new venture','Two children raise a mighty club out of an empty breastplate. Strength is there, waiting for someone to take it up. Begin.'),reversed:n('delay, a false start, force with no one to use it','The armour stands empty. Energy with no body behind it, or a start that never gets going. Step into it.'),note:'The letters M S are also on the Ace of Swords, perhaps a maker\u2019s or patron\u2019s mark; the arms on the Aces of Batons and Swords have been read as those of the Venetian Venier family (Brera catalogue). Modern reading.'},'wands-02':{picture:'A stout naked man in a red cap and red boots walks along with two long staves, looking up.',upright:n('planning, setting out, a choice of path','He carries two staves and walks with his face lifted. Ideas are forming into plans. Choose your way.'),reversed:n('limits, fear, staying put','Two staves and no direction. Know your limits, but don\u2019t use them as an excuse.'),note:'Modern reading.'},'wands-03':{picture:'A winged head is pierced through by three batons, one of them stopping its mouth, with a laurel wreath and a red ribbon below.',upright:n('silence, watching, waiting for the moment','A winged head held still by three staves, its mouth closed. Watch, think and say nothing yet. Your time to move is coming.'),reversed:n('frustration, words held back, impatience','Silence turns to choking. Something needs saying. Find a careful way to say it.'),note:'The Brera catalogue and Howard read this card alchemically, without agreeing. The meaning here is a modern reading of the picture.'},'wands-04':{picture:'A man in profile wears a huge red helmet through which four batons are thrust, a round shield behind him.',upright:n('defence, stability, holding your ground','A man wears his staves like a crown and a guard. What you know and have built is well defended. Stand firm.'),reversed:n('stubbornness, a closed mind, insecurity','Defending too fiercely, as if every question were an attack. Lower the guard a little.'),note:'The Rider\u2013Waite\u2013Smith Four of Wands is a celebration; this card is a guard. Modern reading.'},'wands-05':{picture:'A man in a red cloak hurries along with five batons slung across his back and a gourd swinging from his hand.',upright:n('effort, carrying, energy in motion','Laden and hurrying, gourd in hand. Lots of energy, many things on the go. Keep moving, but check where.'),reversed:n('scattered energy, wasted effort, strife','Running in circles with your arms full. Some of this effort is going nowhere. Put down what isn\u2019t yours.'),note:'The Brera catalogue links the gourd to the alchemist\u2019s cucurbit, a distilling vessel. Modern reading.'},'wands-06':{picture:'A youth in blue carries a bundle of batons tied with a ribbon under one arm and a lantern in his other hand.',upright:n('progress, guidance, light for the way','He carries his bundle and a lantern too. Progress made with care, the way ahead lit. Keep going; you can see enough.'),reversed:n('running low, fading light, delay','The lantern burns low and the bundle slips. Something needs replenishing. Stop and restock.'),note:'The Rider\u2013Waite\u2013Smith Six of Wands is a victory ride; this card is a plainer journey. Modern reading.'},'wands-07':{picture:'A man bends low beneath a bundle of batons tied with a purple ribbon, setting staves into the ground.',upright:n('groundwork, perseverance, digging in','He bends to set the staves in the ground. Hard, unglamorous work that stakes your claim. Keep at it.'),reversed:n('exhaustion, giving ground, a needed pause','Bent over too long. A rest isn\u2019t a defeat. Stand up straight before you go on.'),note:'Modern reading.'},'wands-08':{picture:'Eight batons stand gathered in a tub bound with laurel wreaths, set on a red flower-shaped base.',upright:n('abundance, things in place, satisfaction','All the staves gathered and crowned with laurel. Things are in order and going well. Enjoy it.'),reversed:n('stagnation, stuck in place, delay','Gathered so tight nothing can move. Loosen something to let it flow again.'),note:'The Rider\u2013Waite\u2013Smith Eight of Wands is staves in flight; this card is staves at rest. Modern reading.'},'wands-09':{picture:'A naked youth wades through a stream, carrying nine batons in his arms.',upright:n('crossing over, endurance, nearly there','He wades the stream with his arms full. You are in the middle of the crossing. Keep going; the far bank is close.'),reversed:n('fatigue, fear of deep water, defensiveness','The current is strong and you are tired. Set something down before it pulls you over.'),note:'Howard reads the crossing as the washing stage of the alchemical work. Modern reading.'},'wands-10':{picture:'Ten batons stand in a red case, two of them crossed in front of a laurel-wreathed medallion with a Roman profile.',upright:n('achievement, honour, work completed','The staves stand together over a portrait in a laurel wreath. Work finished and honoured. Be proud of what you carried.'),reversed:n('resting on laurels, too much, a burden','The honour becomes a weight. Don\u2019t let past success turn into one more load.'),note:'Pamela Colman Smith\u2019s Ten of Wands, a man bowed under his staves, follows the Sola Busca Ten of Swords rather than this card. Modern reading.'},'wands-11':{picture:'A young man in a pale tunic walks with a long baton over his shoulder, a sword and a purse at his belt.',upright:n('enthusiasm, news, exploring','A youth sets out with his staff and purse. Curiosity and fresh energy. Go and look.'),reversed:n('restlessness, unprepared, careless','Off without a plan. Check the ground before you stride; a little preparation goes a long way.'),note:'Modern reading.'},'wands-12':{title:'Apolino',picture:'A rider in a red cloak and close helmet leans forward on a grey horse, a baton in his hand, pointing ahead.',upright:n('pursuit, eagerness, a goal in sight','Apolino points ahead and rides for it. The goal is in sight. Go after it with energy.'),reversed:n('haste, scattered aims, overconfidence','Pointing everywhere at once: speed without aim. Pick one direction.'),note:'The name may point to Apollo or to an Apollonius; it is unidentified. Modern reading.'},'wands-13':{title:'Palas',picture:'A crowned woman sits on a throne lettered D P, holding a baton and raising her other hand, a shield behind her.',upright:n('confidence, warmth, wise protection','Pallas Athena, goddess of wisdom and of crafts, sits with her staff and lifts a hand. Lead warmly, and protect what you value.'),reversed:n('control, jealousy, temper','Protection can become control. Watch for warmth turning to temper. Lower the staff.'),note:'Palas is Pallas Athena. Modern reading.'},'wands-14':{title:'Levio Plauto R.',picture:'A crowned king in blue armour sits on a throne between two lions, pointing, a baton in his hand and a helmet at his feet.',upright:n('leadership, vision, direction','A king between lions, pointing the way. Vision, and the authority to see it through. Show people where to go.'),reversed:n('domineering, impulsive, bossy','Pointing without listening. Leadership slides into giving orders. Ask before you direct.'),note:'A Roman name, perhaps a Plautius, with R. for re, king; unidentified. Modern reading.'},'cups-01':{picture:'Three winged children play above a great carved vase lettered TRAHOR FATIS, \u201cI am drawn by the fates\u201d; one pours from a jug, one plays a pipe, and a shield of arms stands at its foot.',upright:n('abundance, grace, a gift of fate','Children pour and pipe over a vessel fit for a feast. The Ace of Cups is the heart\u2019s plenty opening, and the motto calls it a gift of fate. Receive it.'),reversed:n('emptiness, a gift refused, waiting','The cup is there but nothing is poured, or what is offered isn\u2019t taken. Feelings may be blocked or held back. Make room to receive.'),note:'The arms on the Aces of Cups and Coins have been read as those of the Venetian Sanudo family (Brera catalogue). Modern reading.'},'cups-02':{picture:'A small winged child sits in the bowl of a tall double vase, two great vessels stacked one on the other.',upright:n('balance, a pairing, care','Two vessels balanced one on another, with a small winged spirit on top. A partnership held in careful balance. Tend it gently.'),reversed:n('imbalance, a wobble, a mismatch','Stacked vessels topple easily. Something between two people is off-level. Steady it before adding more.'),note:'The Rider\u2013Waite\u2013Smith Two of Cups shows two lovers; here the pairing is of vessels. Modern reading.'},'cups-03':{picture:'Two covered cups hang from curling stems that grow out of a third vessel, purple leaves spreading below them.',upright:n('growth, fruit, things shared','The cups grow on stems like fruit from a shared root. What was planted together is ripening. Celebrate what grew.'),reversed:n('overstretched, a cutting off, hanging by a thread','The fruit hangs on thin stems. Something meant to be shared is being drawn off by one side. Feed the root.'),note:'Modern reading.'},'cups-04':{picture:'A naked man and a winged child bundle a cup into a big sack, while three covered cups stand on a shelf above them.',upright:n('storing, effort, keeping safe','Two figures work hard to bag up a cup and put it away. There is a time to gather and keep what you have. Look after it.'),reversed:n('hoarding, feelings shut away, effort wasted','Stuffing things in a sack isn\u2019t the same as keeping them. What are you putting away so as not to look at it? Take it out again.'),note:'Modern reading.'},'cups-05':{picture:'A man walks with a pole across his shoulder and five covered jars hung about him on cords, looking up at the sky, a small dog at his heels.',upright:n('burden, carrying on, hope','He is laden with jars and still looks up. Some loads must be carried for a while. Keep your eyes on the sky.'),reversed:n('overload, a spill, letting go','Too many jars on too many cords: something is going to break. Set some down. Not every load is yours.'),note:'The Rider\u2013Waite\u2013Smith Five of Cups is a scene of loss; this one is about carrying. Modern reading.'},'cups-06':{picture:'Winged children clamber over a huge carved vase, clutching round covered cups.',upright:n('play, childhood, simple pleasure','Children climb a great vase, playing with the cups. Like the Six of Cups in other decks, it has a child\u2019s gladness in it. Let yourself enjoy something simply.'),reversed:n('clinging to the past, mischief, immaturity','Play turns to scrambling for more, or the past is being climbed back into. Come down and live where you are.'),note:'Close in spirit to the Rider\u2013Waite\u2013Smith Six of Cups and its children. Modern reading.'},'cups-07':{picture:'A naked boy stands on a great crown-shaped dish set on a red chest, covered cups all around him, two of them sprouting.',upright:n('display, imagination, standing out','A boy poses on a pedestal of vessels, the centre of the show. This is imagination and the wish to be seen. Enjoy it, and keep your feet on something solid.'),reversed:n('showing off, illusion, empty display','The show is bigger than what is behind it. Watch for fantasies standing in for plans. Step down and check.'),note:'Modern reading.'},'cups-08':{picture:'Two winged children on a purple chest tie covered cups with red cords and hang them down its sides.',upright:n('arranging, setting down, a pause','Children busy hanging and arranging cups. It looks like play, but things are being put in order. Set your feelings out where you can see them.'),reversed:n('distraction, avoidance, a tangle','Busyness that fills the time so you needn\u2019t feel anything, or things left in a tangle. Leave the game for a moment.'),note:'The Rider\u2013Waite\u2013Smith Eight of Cups is a walking away; there is no such scene here. Modern reading.'},'cups-09':{picture:'A merman with a long serpent tail rises from the sea, carrying covered cups in his arms while more float around him.',upright:n('wishes, plenty from the deep, contentment','Out of the sea comes a merman bearing cups. Good things are surfacing from below. Enjoy what arrives.'),reversed:n('excess, secrets surfacing, smugness','What comes up from the deep may not be what you wished for, or enjoyment slides into indulgence. Take only what you need.'),note:'The Brera catalogue links the triton to a contemporary tale of miraculous water. Modern reading.'},'cups-10':{picture:'A bearded old man\u2019s face looks out from among ten covered cups, one balanced on his head, a string of red beads below.',upright:n('wholeness, wisdom, a full heart','An old, wise face sits among all the cups. Fullness of feeling comes with years and reflection. Take stock of how much you have.'),reversed:n('heaviness, brooding, too much to hold','Surrounded by cups and weighed down by one. Plenty can feel like a load. Put something down and breathe.'),note:'The Brera catalogue reads the face as Hermes Trismegistus. Modern reading.'},'cups-11':{picture:'A young man in red raises his hands toward a flaming vessel set on a purple pillar.',upright:n('wonder, devotion, a new feeling','A youth stops before a burning vessel with his hands lifted. Something has caught your heart and you want to understand it. Stay open.'),reversed:n('naivety, moods, infatuation','Wonder can tip into infatuation. Don\u2019t worship the flame before you know it. Look again.'),note:'Modern reading.'},'cups-12':{title:'Natanabo',picture:'A winged, bearded man in red rides a brown horse, turning to gesture behind him; a covered cup stands on the ground.',upright:n('charm, persuasion, an offer','In the Alexander Romance, Nectanebo the magician-king won Queen Olympias by coming to her in the guise of a god. This is a card of charm, offers and messages of the heart. Enjoy it, and know who is really at the door.'),reversed:n('seduction, deceit, a mask','The visitor is not who he says. Watch for flattery, or a promise dressed up as something grander. Look under the wings.'),note:'Nectanebo, the last native pharaoh, who in medieval legend fathered Alexander the Great: one of the court cards drawn from Alexander\u2019s circle. Modern reading.'},'cups-13':{title:'Polisena',picture:'A crowned woman sits on a wheeled throne with a dragon\u2019s tail curling up behind it, reaching toward a vase on a red pedestal from which snakes or flames rise.',upright:n('deep feeling, loyalty, grace','Polyxena of Troy, loved by Achilles, met her fate with dignity. This queen holds strong feeling with grace. Be faithful to what you love.'),reversed:n('sacrifice, being used, feelings turned bitter','The vase is full of serpents. Watch for love that demands too much of you, or feelings gone sour. You need not lay yourself down on anyone\u2019s tomb.'),note:'Polisena is usually taken for Polyxena, Priam\u2019s daughter, sacrificed on Achilles\u2019 tomb. Modern reading.'},'cups-14':{title:'Lucio Cecilio R.',picture:'A young crowned king in red sits on a carved throne, a shield behind him, a covered cup at his feet and a small tree with a bird beside him.',upright:n('calm authority, generosity, steadiness','A king at ease on his throne, his cup set at his feet. This is the person who can hold feeling without being ruled by it. Be steady, and kind.'),reversed:n('moodiness, manipulation, rising anger','The king starts up from the throne. Feelings used to control others, or kept under a lid until they spill. Settle before you speak.'),note:'A Roman name, perhaps one of the Caecilii Metelli, with R. for re, king; unidentified. Modern reading.'},'swords-01':{picture:'Two men, one in red and one in blue, clasp each other around a single upright sword, the letters M and S on either side and a shield of arms behind.',upright:n('alliance, a clear decision, joined force','Two men grip one sword together. The Ace of Swords is a clear mind and a cutting decision; here it is a decision made with someone. Agree, and act.'),reversed:n('a struggle, a split, force misused','The embrace might be a wrestle. Watch for an alliance turning into a fight over who holds the blade. Clear the air.'),note:'The letters M S appear also on the Ace of Batons. The arms have been read as the Venier family\u2019s (Brera catalogue). Modern reading.'},'swords-02':{picture:'Two naked figures with small horns each hold a sword, pressed close and leaning together.',upright:n('truce, standoff, two minds','Two figures each hold a sword, close enough to embrace or to fight. A balance holds, for now. Talk before anyone moves.'),reversed:n('tension breaking, mistrust, a decision forced','The truce won\u2019t last. Someone will have to choose. Make the choice yourself.'),note:'The Rider\u2013Waite\u2013Smith Two of Swords is a blindfolded woman; this card shares only the uneasy balance. Modern reading.'},'swords-03':{picture:'A red heart is pierced by three swords, with a garland hung beneath it.',upright:n('heartache, grief, a painful truth','A heart run through by three swords: sorrow, plainly shown. Let it hurt; it is real. Grief that is felt can heal.'),reversed:n('healing, releasing pain, old wounds','The swords are coming out. The pain eases, or an old hurt is being let go. Be gentle with the place it was.'),note:'Pamela Colman Smith\u2019s Three of Swords follows this card almost exactly, and the reading here follows hers.'},'swords-04':{picture:'Four swords cross through a laurel wreath tied with ribbons, an animal\u2019s skull hanging below.',upright:n('rest, truce, a pause after struggle','Swords laid through a victor\u2019s wreath, a skull beneath. The fight is over for now. Rest while you can.'),reversed:n('restlessness, burnout, back too soon','Picking up the swords before you have recovered. Rest isn\u2019t laziness. Take the pause.'),note:'Close in sense to the Rider\u2013Waite\u2013Smith Four of Swords, a knight at rest. Modern reading.'},'swords-05':{picture:'Five swords stand bundled in a blue vase set on a stand, ribbons tied around their hilts.',upright:n('ideas gathered, strategy, holding back','Swords stored in a vase rather than wielded. Plans and arguments are gathering, and not every quarrel needs to be had. Choose which is worth it.'),reversed:n('conflict, a hollow win, tension','The swords are coming out of the vase. A fight may cost more than it gains. Walk away if you can.'),note:'The reversed reading borrows from the Rider\u2013Waite\u2013Smith Five of Swords, a hollow victory. Modern reading.'},'swords-06':{picture:'A heavy-bellied man bends under a basket of swords on his back, carrying more in his hands.',upright:n('moving on, a burden carried, transition','He carries all his swords with him, bowed under them. You are moving on, but taking the troubles too. Carry them somewhere you can set them down.'),reversed:n('stuck, too heavy, going nowhere','Heavy work that doesn\u2019t move you forward. Lighten the load before taking another step.'),note:'Modern reading.'},'swords-07':{picture:'A laurel-crowned young man clambers into a boot-shaped quiver full of swords, gripping one, a shield marked S.P.Q.R. on the ground beneath him.',upright:n('strategy, stealth, getting what you need','He climbs into the quiver to take a sword. Cleverness, a plan, perhaps a back way in. Be strategic, and honest with yourself about why.'),reversed:n('deceit, getting caught, a poor plan','The climb is awkward and everyone can see. Sneaking rarely works for long. Ask openly.'),note:'Loosely echoes the Rider\u2013Waite\u2013Smith Seven of Swords, a man making off with swords. Modern reading.'},'swords-08':{picture:'A naked young man gathers eight swords into a garlanded urn.',upright:n('gathering, victory, order','He gathers the swords into a fine urn hung with garlands. Arguments settled, weapons put away with honour. Enjoy the clear space.'),reversed:n('feeling trapped, restriction, crowded thoughts','Too many swords in one vessel. Thoughts crowd in on each other. Take some out and look at them one by one.'),note:'The reversed reading borrows from the Rider\u2013Waite\u2013Smith Eight of Swords, a bound woman. Modern reading.'},'swords-09':{picture:'A man stoops to draw a sword from a purple well-head hung with laurel, a winged child standing on its rim among the other swords.',upright:n('a solution found, release, worry answered','He draws a sword out of the well while a winged child watches. The answer to what has kept you awake is within reach. Reach for it.'),reversed:n('anxiety, sleeplessness, despair','The well seems bottomless and worries multiply at night. Say them aloud to someone in daylight.'),note:'The reversed reading borrows from the Rider\u2013Waite\u2013Smith Nine of Swords, a night of anguish. Modern reading.'},'swords-10':{picture:'A man seen from behind bends double, pushing a bundle of ten swords into a sack.',upright:n('an end, packing up, enough','All the swords go into the sack. A struggle is finished and you are putting it away. It is over; let it be.'),reversed:n('dragging it on, overload, a burden carried too far','Bent double under too much. The struggle is over, but you are still carrying it. Set it down.'),note:'Pamela Colman Smith\u2019s Ten of Wands, a man bowed under his bundle, closely follows this card. The Rider\u2013Waite\u2013Smith Ten of Swords, a man struck down, is a different picture.'},'swords-11':{picture:'A young man with roses in his hair plays a lute as he walks, a sword standing upright beside a tree.',upright:n('curiosity, wit, a light touch','A youth with a lute, his sword set aside. Quick thinking goes best with a light heart. Learn, listen and play.'),reversed:n('idle talk, distraction, gossip','All song and no edge. Watch for chatter where thought is needed. Pick up the sword.'),note:'Modern reading.'},'swords-12':{title:'Amone',picture:'A half-naked bearded rider on a dark horse swings an enormous sword over his head, a bare tree behind him.',upright:n('force, speed, conviction','Amone swings a huge sword at full stretch. Bold, decisive action, the kind that clears the way. Strike when you know you are right.'),reversed:n('aggression, rashness, a blow regretted','All force and no aim. Anger is doing the thinking. Lower the blade.'),note:'Usually read as Zeus Ammon, the god whom Alexander claimed as his father; the British Museum\u2019s catalogue suggests Amon, king of Judah. Peter Mark Adams ties Ammon to the deck\u2019s dark Saturn. Modern reading.'},'swords-13':{title:'Olinpia',picture:'A crowned woman sits holding an upright sword in one hand and a slender rod in the other, a great curling leaf behind her.',upright:n('clear sight, independence, sharp judgement','Olympias, mother of Alexander, was known for her sharp mind and her mysteries. This queen sees clearly and isn\u2019t fooled. Say what you see.'),reversed:n('ruthlessness, scheming, coldness','Olympias was also ruthless in her son\u2019s cause. Watch for cleverness turned to cutting others down. Keep the edge, lose the cruelty.'),note:'Olympias, Alexander\u2019s mother, remembered in legend as a sorceress. Modern reading.'},'swords-14':{title:'Alecxandro M.',picture:'Alexander the Great in red armour stands with sword and orb, griffins at his back and a chariot wheel beside him.',upright:n('mastery, ambition, command','Alexander, with the griffins that legend says carried him up into the sky. This is the commanding mind that sees far. Lead with clarity.'),reversed:n('overreach, arrogance, a fall','In the legend, Alexander\u2019s flight is turned back from heaven. Ambition can climb too high. Know the limit.'),note:'Alexander the Great, at the centre of the deck\u2019s court cards. The griffins recall the medieval tale of his flight. Modern reading.'},'pentacles-01':{picture:'A winged child holds up a great medallion ringed with the words SERVIR CHI PERSEVERA INFIN OTIENE, while two more sit or sleep beside him, under garlands and an ox skull, a scroll reading TRAHOR FATIS at one side.',upright:n('reward, patience, a solid beginning','The ring around the coin reads, roughly, \u201cServe: whoever perseveres wins in the end.\u201d This is the seed of material good, earned by patience. Start, and keep at it.'),reversed:n('impatience, short cuts, a chance slept through','One child holds the coin while another sleeps. Wanting the reward without the long service. Wake up and do the steady part.'),note:'The arms have been read as those of the Venetian Sanudo family (Brera catalogue). Modern reading.'},'pentacles-02':{picture:'Two linked laurel medallions hold profile portraits: above, a laurelled man in the antique style; below, an old man in a blue cap and red robe.',upright:n('partnership, exchange, two sides balanced','Two faces joined link to link. A useful partnership, or two needs being balanced. Keep both in view.'),reversed:n('strain, unequal terms, juggling','The link is pulling. One side may be getting more than the other. Rebalance it.'),note:'The Brera catalogue identifies the portraits as Ercole d\u2019Este, duke of Ferrara, and the physician Michele Savonarola; argued, not certain. Modern reading.'},'pentacles-03':{picture:'A small child staggers under a draped purple vessel crowned with three coins and an ox skull, red ribbons flying.',upright:n('effort, learning the work, first results','A child bears a load bigger than he is, and bears it. Skill grows by doing. Keep working and it will get lighter.'),reversed:n('strain, poor footing, work out of reach','The load is more than one small pair of hands can carry. Ask for help, or take on less for now.'),note:'Modern reading.'},'pentacles-04':{picture:'A stout naked woman with a crescent on her brow carries a huge purple basin of coins on her back, holding another coin at her side.',upright:n('holding, security, earthy strength','She carries the whole store herself, steady and broad. This is security built and kept. Hold what you have with a firm grip.'),reversed:n('greed, a heavy load, fear of loss','Carrying everything yourself because you won\u2019t let it go. Holding on can bend your back. Share the load.'),note:'The Brera catalogue reads her as an earth mother. Close in sense to the Rider\u2013Waite\u2013Smith Four of Pentacles. Modern reading.'},'pentacles-05':{picture:'A figure in a feathered bird costume, with horns and a red shield, bends over a bench; coins hang on cords around him and a small fire burns at his foot.',upright:n('disguise, hard times, getting through','A man dressed as a bird, crouched and hidden. Hard times call for odd shifts. Get through them however you must, and keep your dignity.'),reversed:n('deception, hidden motives, exposure','The costume slips. Watch for people or plans that are not what they seem. Come out from behind it.'),note:'The Brera catalogue reads the costume and fire alchemically. The Rider\u2013Waite\u2013Smith Five of Pentacles shows poverty; this reading is modern.'},'pentacles-06':{picture:'A man in a purple cloak and cap kneels at an anvil, hammering a plate, six coins heaped over a doorway behind him.',upright:n('craft, diligence, fair work','A smith beats a plate on the anvil. Good work done well is its own wealth. Keep hammering.'),reversed:n('drudgery, perfectionism, work unpaid','The hammering never ends, or the work isn\u2019t valued. Ask whether the effort is going where it should.'),note:'Often compared with Pamela Colman Smith\u2019s Eight of Pentacles, the craftsman at his bench; the likeness is close, and the reading follows it.'},'pentacles-07':{picture:'A naked young man sits working a staff in a blue vase heaped with coins, beneath purple wings and a red band, a falcon standing by.',upright:n('tending, patience, work in progress','He keeps working at the vessel while the falcon waits. Growth takes time and attention. Keep tending; don\u2019t pull it up to look at the roots.'),reversed:n('impatience, wasted effort, poor returns','Stirring for the sake of it, or giving up just before it is ready. Check whether the work still makes sense.'),note:'Howard reads the youth as regulating the heat of the alchemical work. Modern reading.'},'pentacles-08':{picture:'A basket of coins hangs from a dead tree above a skull and bones, one coin fallen beside them and a little bird in the branches.',upright:n('savings, reckoning, what lasts','Wealth hangs on a dead tree above a skull: you can\u2019t take it with you. Gather sensibly, and remember what it is for.'),reversed:n('hoarding, hidden riches, skills unused','The basket hangs out of reach and does no one any good. What knowledge or means are you sitting on? Bring it down and use it.'),note:'The Rider\u2013Waite\u2013Smith Eight of Pentacles is a craftsman; this card is closer to a memento mori. Modern reading.'},'pentacles-09':{picture:'A basket full of coins is set over a fire, and a small figure burns among the flames beneath it.',upright:n('refining, trial, gain through heat','Coins over a fire, and someone in the flames: wealth won through hardship. What you are going through is refining you. Hold steady.'),reversed:n('burnout, loss, too high a price','The fire costs more than the gold is worth. Watch for gain that is burning you up. Step out of the flames.'),note:'The Brera catalogue reads the burning child as the nigredo, the blackening of the alchemical work. The Rider\u2013Waite\u2013Smith Nine of Pentacles is ease in a garden; this reading is modern.'},'pentacles-10':{picture:'A winged child drops a coin into an open chest already full of coins, a candlestick behind it and a small white animal, perhaps a dog, beside the chest.',upright:n('wealth secured, legacy, home','The last coin goes into a full chest. Something lasting is complete, a store for the household and those to come. Close the lid and be glad.'),reversed:n('family money troubles, something unguarded, secrets','The chest stands open. Watch for disputes over what is shared or inherited, or for something left unguarded. See to the lock.'),note:'Close in sense to the Rider\u2013Waite\u2013Smith Ten of Pentacles. Modern reading.'},'pentacles-11':{picture:'A young man in red points down at a coin by his feet, a falcon on a perch behind him and another bird in flight.',upright:n('study, a new chance, care','A youth with his hawks points to a coin on the ground. A practical opportunity is at your feet. Pick it up and learn its worth.'),reversed:n('hesitation, holding too tight, distraction','Eyes on the bird, not the coin. Or the chance is held so close it can\u2019t grow. Let it fly a little.'),note:'Modern reading.'},'pentacles-12':{title:'Sarafino',picture:'A young man in red reins in a rearing white horse that twists its head back, his shield on his arm and a great coin on the ground below.',upright:n('control, steadiness, work in hand','Sarafino holds a rearing horse in check. Practical matters need a firm, patient hand. Keep the reins.'),reversed:n('stubbornness, things getting away, a bolt','The horse is winning. Something practical is running out of control, or you are holding so hard it fights you. Loosen, then steer.'),note:'The Brera catalogue identifies Sarafino with a contemporary humanist; unconfirmed. Modern reading.'},'pentacles-13':{title:'Elena',picture:'A crowned woman in red sits by a pillar, gazing into a round coin like a mirror and touching it with a slender rod, a glass flask on the pillar beside her.',upright:n('beauty, resourcefulness, skill with what is at hand','Helen studies a coin as if it were a mirror, a small flask beside her. She knows the worth of things, herself included. Use your gifts with care.'),reversed:n('vanity, trading on looks, magical thinking','Looking in the mirror too long, or hoping a charm will do what work should. Put the mirror down.'),note:'Elena is usually taken for Helen of Troy. Some read her gesture as a rite, some as a craft; it is unclear. Modern reading.'},'pentacles-14':{title:'R. Filipo',picture:'A crowned king in red armour sits holding a banner marked PAX, a great shield and a coin beside him and a bird perched above.',upright:n('prosperity, peace through strength, a provider','Philip of Macedon built a kingdom\u2019s wealth and army, and here he holds a banner of peace. This is the person who secures prosperity and keeps it. Lead with a steady hand.'),reversed:n('greed, power before peace, stubbornness','The banner says peace, but the shield is very large. Watch for wealth used to dominate. Ask what the strength is protecting.'),note:'Philip II of Macedon, Alexander\u2019s father, one of the Alexander court figures. Modern reading.'}}},2464,[]);
