__d(function(e,a,t,n,o,i,s){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(i,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(i,"CALENDAR_EAST_PAGES",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return r}});const r=[{ref:'kemetic-wep-ronpet',title:'Wep Ronpet',aka:['Wepet Renpet','Opening of the Year','Egyptian New Year','Kemetic New Year'],tradition:'Kemetic (ancient Egyptian)',summary:'The \u201copening of the year\u201d, the first day of the Egyptian civil calendar, once hoped to fall with the rising of Sirius and the coming of the Nile flood. A day of cleansing, new beginnings and wishing one another a good year.',honours:'The year itself, renewed. Sopdet (Sirius), whose return to the dawn sky after seventy days of absence heralded the flood; Hapi, the flood\u2019s abundance; Ra, newly born in the year\u2019s first sunrise; and Osiris, whose death and return the rising water was felt to repeat. In temples the gods\u2019 images were brought up to the roof to be touched by the first light of the new year.',history:"The Egyptian civil year had twelve months of thirty days and five extra days \u201cupon the year\u201d, 365 in all, with no leap day. Wep Ronpet was its first day, I Akhet 1. In an ideal year it coincided with the heliacal rising of Sirius, its first appearance before dawn in high summer, which in turn came just as the Nile began to rise. Because the civil calendar had no leap day, the two drifted apart by a day every four years and met again only after some fourteen centuries, so for most of Egyptian history the \u201copening of the year\u201d and the star\u2019s return were separate events, and both were marked.\n\nAt the great temples the New Year was a feast of renewal. At Dendera, in the Ptolemaic period, the image of Hathor was carried up to a roof chapel so that the sun\u2019s first rays of the year could fall on it and recharge it: the \u201cunion with the sun disc\u201d. Households exchanged good wishes, and from the later periods come small faience \u201cNew Year flasks\u201d, inscribed with wishes for a good year and probably filled with Nile water or oil as gifts.\n\nThe days before New Year were dangerous ones, the time when Sekhmet\u2019s messengers were feared to roam, and the year was only safe once it had opened. Today Kemetic groups keep Wep Ronpet by different anchors (the rising of Sirius where they live, the Coptic Nayrouz, or 1 August), and mark it with purification, offerings, a clean house and good wishes. The Coptic Church still keeps Nayrouz, the new year of the old calendar\u2019s direct descendant, as a feast of its martyrs.",knowAndDont:{known:'The calendar, the name wpt rnpt, its place on I Akhet 1, the link with Sirius in Egyptian thought, the Dendera roof rites and the inscribed New Year flasks are all well attested. So is the fear of the last days of the old year.',unknown:'Ordinary people\u2019s New Year customs are poorly recorded, and practice changed over three thousand years. No ancient Egyptian kept a year fixed to Sirius as modern groups do, so any modern date for Wep Ronpet is a chosen anchor, not a recovered one.'},celebrate:['Clean the house properly the day before, and wash yourself in fresh water on the morning itself.','Watch the eastern sky before dawn in the days around the date: Sirius rising low in the twilight is the star the year once waited for.','Greet the first sunrise with a lamp, a cup of fresh water and a little incense.','Send short good wishes to friends: \u201ca good opening of the year\u201d.','Write down what you mean to begin, and what you leave behind in the old year.'],ritual:{title:'Opening the year',outline:['The evening before, sweep the house from back to front and put out what you mean to leave in the old year.','Before dawn, wash your face and hands in fresh water, and dress in clean clothes, white if you have them.','If the eastern horizon is clear, look for Sirius, the brightest star, low in the dawn twilight.','At sunrise, light a lamp or candle (on a safe dish), set out fresh water and bread, and greet the new year aloud in your own words.','Name one thing you will begin this year, and one person you will wish well today.','Share the bread and water afterwards; give the remainder to the earth.']},correspondences:{deities:['Ra','Isis','Osiris','Hathor','Thoth'],herbs:['Frankincense','Myrrh'],crystals:['Lapis Lazuli','Turquoise','Clear Quartz'],colors:['White','Gold','Blue'],foods:['Fresh bread','Figs','Honey','Fresh water'],symbols:['Sirius (Sopdet)','The rising Nile','The sun on the horizon (akhet)']},sources:['R. A. Parker, The Calendars of Ancient Egypt (Chicago, 1950)','S. Cauville, Dendara: Le temple d\u2019Isis and the Dendera New Year roof chapel publications','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Egyptian_calendar','https://www.worldhistory.org/article/1032/festivals-in-ancient-egypt/','https://anefest.spbu.ru/en/articles/ancient-minor-asia/140-epagomenal-days-egypt.html','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coptic_calendar']},{ref:'kemetic-wag',title:'Wag Festival',aka:['Wagy','Wag feast','Festival of the dead'],tradition:'Kemetic (ancient Egyptian)',summary:'One of the oldest Egyptian festivals, a feast of the dead early in the new year, when families brought offerings to the tombs and lit lamps for their own.',honours:'The blessed dead (the akhu), and Osiris, lord of the dead, whose cult the festival came to belong to. It is a family festival above all: your own people, fed and remembered.',history:"The Wag appears in the Pyramid Texts of the Old Kingdom and in the lists of feasts carved in private tombs, where the owner asks for offerings \u201cat the Wag festival\u201d among the others. By the Middle Kingdom it was fixed on I Akhet 18, the eighteenth day of the first month, with an eve on the 17th; in some periods there was also a Wag kept by the moon. In the festival calendar of Ramesses III at Medinet Habu it sits beside the great feast of Thoth on the 19th.\n\nFamilies visited the tombs with food and drink, lit lamps to drive the darkness away from the dead, and joined them in a meal. Tomb scenes and model boats show the dead making a journey by water to Abydos, Osiris\u2019s holy city, to share in his festivals; on the Wag, some sources suggest, model boats were set facing the way to Abydos and turned back again the next day for the return.\n\nModern Kemetic groups keep the Wag as a day for the ancestors, often with offerings at a home shrine. Some make small boats of reed or paper and set them on water or face them west: a modern custom built on those ancient models, not an ancient one in itself.",knowAndDont:{known:'The festival\u2019s great age, its date in the civil calendar (I Akhet 18), its link with the dead and with Osiris, and the offerings and lamps at the tombs.',unknown:'The details of the rites are thinly recorded; how the boat models were used is an interpretation, and the paper boats of modern practice are a modern invention. The relation between the civil and lunar Wag is still argued.'},celebrate:['Set a small table for your own dead: a photograph or a name, a lamp, bread and water.','Speak their names aloud: in Egyptian thought, the name spoken keeps a person alive.','Visit and tidy a grave, or leave flowers at a place they loved.','Make a little boat of folded paper, write a name on it and set it facing west on your shrine.','Eat a meal in their company and tell one story about each.'],ritual:{premade:'hedge-ancestor-candle-evening',title:'An evening with your dead'},correspondences:{deities:['Osiris','Anubis','Isis'],herbs:['Myrrh','Frankincense','Cedar'],crystals:['Lapis Lazuli','Obsidian'],colors:['White','Green','Black'],foods:['Bread','Beer or barley water','Dates','Onions'],symbols:['Lamps','Boats facing west','Offering tables']},sources:['https://anefest.spbu.ru/en/articles/ancient-minor-asia/164-wag-festival-egypt.html','R. Krauss, \u201cWenn und aber: das Wag-Fest und die Chronologie des Alten Reiches\u201d, G\xf6ttinger Miszellen 162 (1998)','S. Schott, Alt\xe4gyptische Festdaten (Mainz, 1950)','https://www.worldhistory.org/article/1032/festivals-in-ancient-egypt/']},{ref:'kemetic-thoth',title:'Feast of Thoth',aka:['Djehutyt','Festival of Thoth','Feast of Hermes (Plutarch)'],tradition:'Kemetic (ancient Egyptian)',summary:'The great festival of Thoth on the nineteenth day of his own month, the first of the year: a feast of the god of writing, reckoning and the moon, which Plutarch says was kept with honey, figs and the words \u201csweet is truth\u201d.',honours:'Thoth (Djehuty), the ibis- and baboon-headed god of writing, knowledge, measure and the moon, scribe of the gods, who records the verdict at the weighing of the heart and who, in the myth Plutarch tells, won the five extra days of the year from the Moon.',history:"The first month of the Egyptian year bore Thoth\u2019s name, and his principal feast fell on its 19th day, right after the Wag. The festival calendar of Ramesses III at Medinet Habu lists them together. At Hermopolis (Khemenu), Thoth\u2019s city, and wherever there were scribes, he was honoured with offerings and processions.\n\nWriting in the early second century CE, Plutarch records that on the nineteenth of the first month, at the feast of Hermes (the Greek name for Thoth), Egyptians ate honey and a fig and said \u201cSweet is truth\u201d (On Isis and Osiris 68). Thoth was the lord of maat in speech, the right word rightly said, and the saying ties the feast to him neatly.\n\nFor modern Kemetics, it is the scribe\u2019s feast: a day for gratitude for learning, for blessing pens, books and work, and for honest speech.",knowAndDont:{known:'The month name, the festival on I Akhet 19 in the New Kingdom calendars, Thoth\u2019s roles, and Plutarch\u2019s note on honey and figs.',unknown:'Plutarch was a Greek writing late in Egyptian history; we cannot say how widely or how early the honey-and-figs custom was kept. The temple liturgy of the day is largely lost.'},celebrate:['Eat honey and a fig, and say \u201csweet is truth\u201d before you do.','Clean your desk, sharpen your pencils, and bless the tools of your work or study.','Write something by hand: a letter, a page of notes, a poem.','Keep the day honest: speak plainly and kindly, and correct one thing you said wrongly.','Learn something about the Egyptian scripts, or copy a hieroglyph or two.'],ritual:{premade:'folk-thoth-offering'},correspondences:{deities:['Thoth','Isis'],herbs:['Frankincense','Myrrh'],crystals:['Lapis Lazuli','Sodalite','Clear Quartz'],colors:['White','Blue','Silver'],foods:['Honey','Figs'],symbols:['The ibis','The baboon','The scribe\u2019s palette','The crescent moon']},sources:['Plutarch, On Isis and Osiris 68 (Loeb, 1936, public domain): https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Moralia/Isis_and_Osiris*/E.html','S. Schott, Alt\xe4gyptische Festdaten (Mainz, 1950); the Medinet Habu festival calendar','P. Boylan, Thoth, the Hermes of Egypt (Oxford, 1922)','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thoth']},{ref:'kemetic-opet',title:'Opet Festival',aka:['Beautiful Feast of Opet','Heb nefer en Ipet'],tradition:'Kemetic (ancient Egyptian)',summary:'Thebes\u2019s great festival of the flood season: Amun, Mut and Khonsu travelled in their sacred barques from Karnak to Luxor temple, and the king\u2019s divine power was renewed.',honours:'Amun-Ra, king of the gods, with his consort Mut and their son Khonsu (the moon); and the royal ka, the divine life-force of kingship, renewed by Amun in the sanctuary at Luxor.',history:"In the second month of Akhet, while the Nile was high, the images of the Theban triad left Karnak in their portable barques, carried on the shoulders of priests, and travelled south to Luxor temple (Ipet-resyt, \u201cthe southern sanctuary\u201d), by land along the avenue of sphinxes or by river on great boats. Crowds lined the way; oracles were given as the barque paused; bread, beer and cakes were handed out in vast quantities.\n\nInside Luxor temple, Amun met his own form there and the king entered the sanctuary to be renewed in his ka, emerging as the god\u2019s chosen. The festival lasted eleven days under Thutmose III and over three weeks by Ramesses III. The reliefs of the colonnade at Luxor, carved under Tutankhamun and Horemheb, show the whole procession, with musicians, acrobats and offerings.\n\nIt was a royal and temple festival first, but also a public holiday: the one time many people came close to the hidden god. Modern Kemetic groups keep Opet with procession and feasting, often as a festival of renewal and community.",knowAndDont:{known:'The route, the barques, the duration in different reigns, and much of the festival\u2019s outward form, from the Luxor colonnade reliefs and temple calendars.',unknown:'What happened inside the sanctuary is not shown; the meaning of the king\u2019s renewal is reconstructed from inscriptions. Ordinary people\u2019s part beyond watching and feasting is little recorded.'},celebrate:['Walk a route with purpose: a short procession around your home or garden, carrying a small image or candle.','Share bread, beer or sweet cakes with friends: the festival was famous for its feasting.','Ask one question you need guidance on, and sit with it (the barques gave oracles on the way).','Look at photographs of the Luxor colonnade reliefs and trace the procession.','Renew a commitment you made last year.'],ritual:{title:'A small procession',outline:['Choose a small image, symbol or candle (an electric one is safest to carry) to stand for the god you honour.','Set an offering table at the \u201csouthern\u201d end of your route: bread, a cup of something to drink and flowers.','Carry the image slowly from one room to the other, singing or playing music.','Pause halfway and ask your question; notice what comes.','At the table, set the image down, speak a renewal of your own commitments, and share the food.']},correspondences:{deities:['Amun','Khonsu','Ra','Hathor'],herbs:['Frankincense','Myrrh'],crystals:['Lapis Lazuli','Carnelian'],colors:['Gold','Blue'],foods:['Bread','Beer','Cakes'],symbols:['The barque','The avenue of sphinxes','The ka']},sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Opet_Festival','L. Bell, \u201cLuxor Temple and the Cult of the Royal Ka\u201d, Journal of Near Eastern Studies 44 (1985)','The Epigraphic Survey, Reliefs and Inscriptions at Luxor Temple I: The Festival Procession of Opet in the Colonnade Hall (Chicago, 1994)']},{ref:'kemetic-khoiak',title:'Mysteries of Osiris (Khoiak)',aka:['Khoiak festival','Osiris Mysteries','Raising of the Djed'],tradition:'Kemetic (ancient Egyptian)',summary:'The great Osiris festival of the fourth month of the flood season: figures of the god were moulded of earth and grain and made to sprout, he was mourned and buried, and on the last day the djed pillar was raised.',honours:'Osiris, murdered by Set, gathered and mourned by Isis and Nephthys, and risen as lord of the dead and of the green life that returns each year. Also Sokar, the falcon god of the necropolis, whose feast fell within it, and the grain itself.',history:"As the floodwaters fell in Khoiak, the land reappeared ready for sowing, and the Egyptians kept the death and renewal of Osiris. The fullest account is a Ptolemaic text carved in the Osiris chapels on the roof of Dendera temple, which sets out the rites day by day: figures of Osiris moulded from earth, barley and water and kept moist until the grain sprouted; a figure of Sokar made of earth, dates and spices; the mourning of Isis and Nephthys; the processions and burials of the previous year\u2019s figures.\n\nGrain-sprouting \u201cOsiris beds\u201d and corn-mummies have been found in tombs, as far back as the New Kingdom, and the god was painted with a green face for the life he held. The festival of Sokar fell on IV Akhet 26, when his barque was drawn around the walls of Memphis, and on the 30th the djed pillar, Osiris\u2019s backbone and the sign of stability, was raised with ropes by the king and priests. At Abydos, the Osiris mysteries were played out in public procession, recorded by the official Ikhernofret under Senusret III.\n\nThe god dies with the old year\u2019s water and rises with the new grain: few festivals hold the agricultural year and the hope of the dead so closely together. Modern Kemetic practice keeps it as a season of mourning and renewal, often sprouting grain.",knowAndDont:{known:'The Dendera texts, the Abydos stela of Ikhernofret, grain beds and corn-mummies from tombs, the Sokar festival and the raising of the djed are well documented.',unknown:'The Dendera programme is late (Ptolemaic) and temple-based; earlier and local practice varied. What the mysteries enacted in secret was never fully written down. The dates here follow the late calendar.'},celebrate:['Sprout barley or wheat on damp soil in a tray, in the shape of a figure if you can.','Read or retell the myth of Osiris, Isis and Nephthys (Plutarch tells it in On Isis and Osiris).','Keep a quiet day of mourning for someone lost this year.','On the last day, stand something upright that has fallen: literally, or in your life.','Plant a seed outdoors as the season allows.'],ritual:{title:'An Osiris bed of sprouting grain',outline:['At the start of the festival, fill a shallow tray with damp soil or compost and shape a simple figure in it.','Press barley, wheat or cress seed into the figure, saying who the god is and what you mourn this year.','Keep it moist and in the light; each day, light a lamp beside it for a few minutes and speak a few words.','When the grain is green, give thanks for life returning.','On the last day, raise a small pillar or stick upright beside it, a djed of your own, and say what stands firm for you.','Afterwards return the sprouted grain to the garden or compost.']},correspondences:{deities:['Osiris','Isis','Anubis'],herbs:['Myrrh','Cedar','Frankincense'],crystals:['Malachite','Green Aventurine','Lapis Lazuli'],colors:['Green','Black','White'],foods:['Barley','Bread','Dates'],symbols:['The djed pillar','Sprouting grain','The green face of Osiris']},sources:['S. Cauville, Le temple de Dendara: Les chapelles osiriennes (Cairo, 1997)','\xc9. Chassinat, Le myst\xe8re d\u2019Osiris au mois de Khoiak (Cairo, 1966\u201368)','Stela of Ikhernofret (Berlin 1204)','Plutarch, On Isis and Osiris (Loeb, 1936, public domain): https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Moralia/Isis_and_Osiris*/home.html','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corn_mummy']},{ref:'kemetic-valley',title:'Beautiful Feast of the Valley',aka:['Heb nefer en inet','Valley Festival'],tradition:'Kemetic (ancient Egyptian)',summary:'At the new moon of the second harvest month, Amun crossed the Nile from Karnak to visit the temples of the west bank at Thebes, and families feasted through the night with their dead at the tombs.',honours:'Amun of Karnak, Hathor of the western mountain, the royal dead in their mortuary temples, and every family\u2019s own ancestors, visited and fed in their tombs.',history:"Each year at the new moon of II Shemu, Amun\u2019s barque crossed the river to the west bank of Thebes, the land of the dead, and visited the mortuary temples of the kings and Hathor\u2019s shrine at Deir el-Bahari. The festival is attested from the Middle Kingdom and flourished in the New Kingdom.\n\nFor the people of Thebes it was the night the living and the dead met. Families went up to the family tombs with food, drink, flowers and music, and spent the night there in a feast that tomb paintings show as joyful and sometimes drunken: drink was part of the communion with the goddess and with the dead. They brought back bouquets that had been offered to the god, carrying his blessing home. Many Theban tomb paintings of banquets are thought to show this feast.\n\nBecause it followed the moon, its date in the civil calendar moved; the date shown here is the new moon in II Shemu by modern reckoning, and so approximate.",knowAndDont:{known:'Its timing by the new moon, Amun\u2019s crossing to the west bank, the family visits to the tombs, the bouquets, from inscriptions and tomb paintings.',unknown:'How much of the banquet imagery shows this festival in particular is debated. The exact lunar day (the Egyptians began a month when the old crescent vanished) differs from a computed new moon.'},celebrate:['Have a meal with your family and set a place for those who have died.','Bring flowers to a grave, or make a bouquet and bring it home blessed from your shrine.','Play the music your grandparents loved.','Look through old photographs together and tell the stories.','Stay up a little later than usual, in good company.'],ritual:{premade:'hedge-ancestor-candle-evening',title:'A night feast with your dead'},correspondences:{deities:['Amun','Hathor','Osiris','Anubis'],herbs:['Rose','Myrrh','Jasmine'],crystals:['Turquoise','Lapis Lazuli'],colors:['Blue','Gold','Green'],foods:['Bread','Beer or wine','Fruit','Honey cakes'],symbols:['Bouquets','The western mountain','The banquet']},sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beautiful_Festival_of_the_Valley','S. Schott, Das sch\xf6ne Fest vom W\xfcstentale (Wiesbaden, 1953)','L. Bell, \u201cThe New Kingdom \u2018Divine\u2019 Temple: The Example of Luxor\u201d, in B. Shafer (ed.), Temples of Ancient Egypt (1997)']},{ref:'kemetic-falcon',title:'Coronation of the Sacred Falcon',aka:['Installation of the Living Falcon'],tradition:'Kemetic (ancient Egyptian)',summary:'On the first day of the season of Peret, the temple of Horus at Edfu chose a living falcon and crowned it as the god\u2019s image for the coming year.',honours:'Horus of Behdet, the falcon god of Edfu, and through him the kingship: the king was Horus on earth, and the crowned falcon stood for both.',history:"The walls of the Ptolemaic temple of Horus at Edfu, built between 237 and 57 BCE, describe a festival at the start of the second season, Peret. A falcon was chosen from the birds raised at the temple, brought before the image of Horus, and crowned. It was then shown to the people from a window above the temple gateway, given offerings and installed as the god\u2019s living image for a year, much as a king was crowned.\n\nThe rite joined three ideas: the falcon as Horus, Horus as king, and the new season as a renewal of order. Like the other animal cults of late Egypt, it had a harder side too: many falcons were mummified and buried as offerings by pilgrims.\n\nThe date shown here is I Peret 1 by the calendar you have chosen, and so a modern alignment.",knowAndDont:{known:'The festival, its date at the start of Peret, the choosing and crowning of the falcon and its appearance at the temple window, from the Edfu inscriptions.',unknown:'How long the festival lasted and how it was staged in detail; whether similar rites were kept at other falcon temples; how far back before the Ptolemaic period it goes.'},celebrate:['Greet Horus at sunrise with fresh water and bread.','Draw a falcon or a wedjat eye and set it on your shrine for the coming year.','Take up one responsibility you have been putting off, as a new season\u2019s pledge.','Watch for birds of prey, or give to a raptor rescue or conservation group.'],ritual:{title:'Crowning the year\u2019s falcon',outline:['Wash your hands and face, and set a shrine with water, bread, incense and an image of a falcon.','Light the incense and greet Horus as the falcon far above.','Lift the falcon image toward the light of a window and say what you will protect this year.','Offer the water and bread, and sit a few minutes with the sky in view.','Close, and eat or share the bread later.']},correspondences:{deities:['Horus','Hathor','Ra'],herbs:['Frankincense','Myrrh','Cedar'],crystals:['Lapis Lazuli','Carnelian','Tiger Eye'],colors:['Gold','White','Blue'],foods:['Bread','Beer','Dates'],symbols:['The falcon','The double crown','The window of appearances']},sources:['Barbara Watterson, The House of Horus at Edfu: Ritual in an Ancient Egyptian Temple (1998)','H. W. Fairman, \u201cWorship and Festivals in an Egyptian Temple\u201d, Bulletin of the John Rylands Library 37 (1954)','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Edfu']},{ref:'kemetic-beautiful-meeting',title:'Beautiful Meeting of Horus and Hathor',aka:['Feast of the Beautiful Meeting','Festival of the Beautiful Reunion'],tradition:'Kemetic (ancient Egyptian)',summary:'Each summer Hathor sailed upriver from Dendera to visit Horus at Edfu, arriving at the new moon of the third harvest month, for a fourteen-day festival of their marriage.',honours:'Hathor of Dendera and Horus of Edfu as wife and husband, their child, and the ancestral gods buried at Edfu\u2019s sacred necropolis.',history:"The festival calendars on the walls of Edfu and Dendera describe the Beautiful Meeting. Hathor\u2019s image left her temple at Dendera in her barque and travelled more than 150 kilometres up the Nile, stopping at towns along the way. Horus came out to meet her near Edfu at the new moon of III Shemu, and the two gods spent about fourteen days together.\n\nThe festival mixed a divine marriage with victory and renewal: processions between the temple and the sacred necropolis, offerings to the ancestral gods buried there, rites against the enemies of Horus, and great public feasting, with food and drink handed out to the people. At its end Hathor sailed home to Dendera.\n\nBecause it followed the moon, its date moved in the civil calendar; the days shown here begin at the computed new moon in III Shemu, and so are approximate.",knowAndDont:{known:'The journey from Dendera to Edfu, the timing by the new moon of III Shemu, the length of the stay, the visits to the necropolis and the public feasting, from the Edfu and Dendera inscriptions.',unknown:'How old the festival is before the Ptolemaic temples that record it; how ordinary people along the river took part; the exact lunar day, which the Egyptians counted from the vanishing of the old crescent.'},celebrate:['Share a festive meal with music, flowers and good company.','Make an offering to Horus and Hathor together, side by side.','Visit someone you love who lives at a distance, or plan to.','Remember the old ones of your family, as the gods at Edfu remembered theirs.'],ritual:{title:'The meeting at the shrine',outline:['Set images of Horus and Hathor apart on your shrine, with water, bread, flowers and incense.','Light the incense and greet Hathor, then bring her image to stand beside Horus.','Offer bread, drink and flowers to both, and play or sing something joyful.','Share food with someone afterwards, as Edfu fed its people.','Close, and keep the two images together for the days of the festival.']},correspondences:{deities:['Horus','Hathor'],herbs:['Rose','Myrrh','Frankincense'],crystals:['Turquoise','Lapis Lazuli','Carnelian'],colors:['Gold','Turquoise','Red'],foods:['Bread','Beer or wine','Fruit','Honey cakes'],symbols:['The sacred barque','The sistrum','The winged disc']},sources:['Barbara Watterson, The House of Horus at Edfu: Ritual in an Ancient Egyptian Temple (1998)','Maurice Alliot, Le culte d\u2019Horus \xe0 Edfou au temps des Ptol\xe9m\xe9es (Cairo, 1949\u201354)','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Edfu']},{ref:'kemetic-birth-osiris',title:'Birthday of Osiris',aka:['First epagomenal day','Heriu renpet 1'],tradition:'Kemetic (ancient Egyptian)',summary:'The first of the five days \u201cupon the year\u201d at the close of the Egyptian calendar, the birthday of Osiris, lord of the dead and of the returning flood.',honours:'Osiris, first-born of Nut and Geb, the good king slain by Set and raised by Isis, ruler of the dead and the power in the flood and the green grain.',history:"The five days after the twelfth month belonged to no month: the Egyptians called them heriu renpet, \u201cthose upon the year\u201d. Plutarch gives the myth: Ra had forbidden Nut, the sky, to give birth on any day of the year, so Thoth won five new days from the Moon at a board game and on them she bore her children, Osiris first. A voice was heard at his birth proclaiming the lord of all coming into the light.\n\nThe epagomenal days were a dangerous threshold between years, the time when Sekhmet\u2019s messengers were feared to roam, and Middle Kingdom and later spells (Papyrus Leiden I 346) protected people through them: protective words were written on linen worn at the throat, and offerings were made to each of the gods born. In Ptolemaic reliefs at the Opet temple at Karnak Osiris\u2019s birth is shown alongside his rising on his bed.\n\nModern Kemetics keep the five days as a series of birthdays and as a time of protection and preparation for the new year.",knowAndDont:{known:'The five days, their names, the births of the five gods (already in the Pyramid Texts), protective spells for these days and Plutarch\u2019s myth.',unknown:'The ancient observance of each birthday is thinly described; Plutarch\u2019s tale is late and Hellenised. Modern dates depend on where you fix the new year.'},celebrate:['Light a lamp and wish Osiris a good birthday with bread, water and incense.','Begin the five days with a clean, quiet house, as the old year draws to a close.','Tie a strip of clean linen or cotton with a protective word written on it around your wrist for the five days.','Tend something green: water the garden, repot a plant.','Remember your dead, whom Osiris rules.'],ritual:{title:'The first birthday upon the year',outline:['Wash your hands and face, and set a small shrine with a lamp, water and bread.','Write a short protective word or the five gods\u2019 names on a strip of cloth and tie it on.','Light the lamp and greet Osiris on his birthday in your own words.','Offer the bread and water; sit for a few minutes with the year that is ending.','Snuff the lamp, and share or return the offerings the next morning.']},correspondences:{deities:['Osiris','Nut','Isis','Thoth'],herbs:['Myrrh','Cedar'],crystals:['Malachite','Lapis Lazuli'],colors:['Green','White'],foods:['Bread','Barley','Water'],symbols:['The crook and flail','The djed pillar','Green grain']},sources:['Plutarch, On Isis and Osiris 12 (Loeb, 1936, public domain): https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Moralia/Isis_and_Osiris*/A.html','https://anefest.spbu.ru/en/articles/ancient-minor-asia/140-epagomenal-days-egypt.html','M. Bommas, Die Mythisierung der Zeit: Die beiden B\xfccher \xfcber die alt\xe4gyptischen Schalttage des magischen pLeiden I 346 (Wiesbaden, 1999)']},{ref:'kemetic-birth-horus',title:'Birthday of Horus',aka:['Second epagomenal day','Birthday of Heru-wer','Birthday of Horus the Elder'],tradition:'Kemetic (ancient Egyptian)',summary:'The second of the days \u201cupon the year\u201d, the birthday of Horus the Elder (Heru-wer, Haroeris), the great falcon of the sky.',honours:'Horus the Elder, the far-seeing falcon whose eyes are the sun and moon, a sky god and protector of kingship. He is distinct in the myth from Horus the Child, son of Isis, though the two were often merged.',history:"Plutarch lists the second day as the birth of Arueris (Haroeris, Horus the Elder), \u201cwhom they call Apollo, and some call him also the elder Horus\u201d. Horus the Elder was worshipped at Kom Ombo, Qus and elsewhere, a great sky falcon older than the family drama of Osiris and Isis.\n\nLike the other epagomenal days, it fell in the dangerous threshold time before the new year, and calendar lists give Horus there as \u201cgreat of strength, lord of terror\u201d. Modern Kemetics honour Horus as protector and as the power of the sky on this day.\n\nSee the Birthday of Osiris page for the five days as a whole.",knowAndDont:{known:'Horus the Elder\u2019s birth on the second epagomenal day is in Plutarch and the Egyptian lists.',unknown:'Little is recorded about how the day itself was kept.'},celebrate:['Go outside and look up: watch birds, or the sky at dawn.','Light a lamp for Horus as protector and ask for clear sight in the year to come.','Draw or carry the Eye of Horus (wedjat) as a sign of protection and wholeness.','Do one thing that needs courage.'],ritual:{title:'Clear sight',outline:['At sunrise or noon, stand outside facing the sun (never look into it directly).','Light a lamp or hold a gold or yellow cloth, and greet Horus on his birthday.','Name what you need to see clearly in the coming year.','Draw the wedjat eye on paper and keep it at your door or in your wallet.']},correspondences:{deities:['Horus','Ra','Isis','Osiris'],herbs:['Frankincense','Cinnamon'],crystals:['Tiger Eye','Carnelian','Lapis Lazuli'],colors:['Gold','Blue','Red'],foods:['Bread','Dates'],symbols:['The falcon','The wedjat eye','The winged sun disc']},sources:['Plutarch, On Isis and Osiris 12 (Loeb, 1936, public domain): https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Moralia/Isis_and_Osiris*/A.html','https://anefest.spbu.ru/en/articles/ancient-minor-asia/140-epagomenal-days-egypt.html','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horus']},{ref:'kemetic-birth-set',title:'Birthday of Set',aka:['Third epagomenal day','Birthday of Seth','Birthday of Sutekh'],tradition:'Kemetic (ancient Egyptian)',summary:'The third of the days \u201cupon the year\u201d, the birthday of Set, god of storms, the desert and foreign lands. Ancient calendars counted it an unlucky day, best spent quietly.',honours:'Set, the red god of storm and desert, Osiris\u2019s murderer in the myth and yet also the strong one who stands at the prow of Ra\u2019s boat and spears the serpent Apep each night. A god of necessary chaos and raw strength.',history:"Plutarch says that on the third day Typhon (Set) was born \u201cnot in due season or manner\u201d, and that the kings \u201cconsidered the third of the intercalated days as inauspicious, and transacted no business on that day\u201d. Egyptian lists of lucky and unlucky days likewise treat the days before the new year as perilous.\n\nSet was not simply a devil in Egyptian religion. For long periods he was honoured as a great god, patron of the Ramesside kings (Seti I bears his name), lord of Upper Egypt, and the defender of Ra against Apep. His reputation darkened in the first millennium BCE, and late rites aimed to destroy him and his followers.\n\nModern Kemetic practice varies: some treat the day as a time to stay in and keep things calm; others honour Set\u2019s strength in a measured way.",knowAndDont:{known:'His birth on the third epagomenal day, its unlucky character in calendars of lucky and unlucky days, and Set\u2019s double reputation over time.',unknown:'How ordinary people kept, or avoided keeping, this day is not well recorded.'},celebrate:['Keep the day low-key: no new ventures, no big decisions, no picking fights.','Tidy up a storm: deal with one chaotic corner of life calmly.','If you honour Set, offer something strong and simple (beer, red-coloured food) with respect, at a distance from offerings to Osiris and Isis.','Watch weather and wind with attention; walk in a wild place if it\u2019s safe.'],ritual:{title:'A quiet day',outline:['In the morning, decide on one thing you will not do today (a big purchase, an argument, a new start).','Light a lamp briefly and acknowledge Set as part of the order of things, with no requests.','Spend the day steadily: chores, rest, a walk.','In the evening, write down what unsettled you and fold the paper away until after the new year.']},correspondences:{deities:['Set','Ra','Osiris'],herbs:['Black Pepper','Juniper'],crystals:['Red Jasper','Hematite','Obsidian'],colors:['Red','Black'],foods:['Beer','Red lentils'],symbols:['The Set animal','Storm and desert wind','The spear against Apep']},sources:['Plutarch, On Isis and Osiris 12 (Loeb, 1936, public domain): https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Moralia/Isis_and_Osiris*/A.html','H. te Velde, Seth, God of Confusion (Leiden, 1967)','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Set_(deity)']},{ref:'kemetic-birth-isis',title:'Birthday of Isis',aka:['Fourth epagomenal day','Birthday of Aset'],tradition:'Kemetic (ancient Egyptian)',summary:'The fourth of the days \u201cupon the year\u201d, the birthday of Isis, great of magic, sister-wife of Osiris and mother of Horus.',honours:'Isis (Aset), who gathered and restored Osiris, hid and raised the child Horus in the marshes, and by her cunning learned Ra\u2019s secret name: the great goddess of magic, motherhood and loyal love, whose worship later spread across the Roman world.',history:"Plutarch says Isis was born \u201cin the regions that are ever moist\u201d on the fourth day. In Egypt the fourth and fifth epagomenal days carried a festival called \u201cthe chick in its nest\u201d, tied to the young Horus and his mother. Isis was mourner, protector and magician, and spells against scorpion stings and illness invoked her care for her child.\n\nHer cult outlived the Egyptian state itself: her temple at Philae was one of the last in Egypt to close, in the sixth century CE, and her mysteries were kept across the Roman empire.\n\nModern Kemetics, and many modern pagans who honour Isis, keep her birthday with devotion and celebration.",knowAndDont:{known:'Her birth on the fourth day in Plutarch and the Egyptian lists, and the wide evidence for her cult.',unknown:'Specific customs for her birthday are not well recorded.'},celebrate:['Set flowers and a lamp before an image of Isis, or a simple throne-shaped sign for her name.','Care for someone who needs looking after, or for yourself.','Read a hymn to Isis (the Philae hymns, or Apuleius\u2019s prayer in The Golden Ass, Book 11).','Make an offering of milk and honey.'],ritual:{title:'A birthday offering for Isis',outline:['Wash your hands and set out a lamp, a cup of milk or water and flowers.','Light the lamp and greet Isis on her birthday by her names: great of magic, lady of the throne.','Offer the milk and flowers, and ask her protection for those you love through the turning of the year.','Sit with the lamp a while, then snuff it and pour the offering out on earth.']},correspondences:{deities:['Isis','Osiris','Hathor'],herbs:['Myrrh','Rose','Jasmine'],crystals:['Lapis Lazuli','Moonstone','Carnelian'],colors:['Blue','White','Red'],foods:['Milk','Honey','Bread'],symbols:['The throne','The tyet knot','Wings']},sources:['Plutarch, On Isis and Osiris 12 (Loeb, 1936, public domain): https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Moralia/Isis_and_Osiris*/A.html','https://anefest.spbu.ru/en/articles/ancient-minor-asia/140-epagomenal-days-egypt.html','Apuleius, The Golden Ass, Book 11 (Adlington\u2019s translation, 1566, public domain)','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isis']},{ref:'kemetic-birth-nephthys',title:'Birthday of Nephthys',aka:['Fifth epagomenal day','Birthday of Nebet-Het'],tradition:'Kemetic (ancient Egyptian)',summary:'The fifth and last of the days \u201cupon the year\u201d, the birthday of Nephthys, lady of the house, mourner and protector of the dead, on the eve of the new year.',honours:'Nephthys (Nebet-Het, \u201clady of the house\u201d), sister of Isis, who mourned and guarded Osiris beside her and protects the dead; often called the companion of the threshold and the edge of things.',history:"Plutarch names the fifth day as the birth of Nephthys, \u201cto whom they give the name of Finality\u201d, and also Aphrodite and Victory. With Isis she is one of the two kites who wail over Osiris\u2019s body; the two sisters stand at the head and foot of coffins, protecting the dead.\n\nAs the last day before Wep Ronpet, it was the very edge of the old year, the end of the dangerous days. Modern Kemetics honour Nephthys and prepare the house for the opening of the year.",knowAndDont:{known:'Her birth on the fifth day (Plutarch and Egyptian lists) and her role as mourner and protector.',unknown:'Nephthys has fewer temples and texts of her own than most great gods; her birthday customs are not recorded.'},celebrate:['Prepare the house for the new year: a final tidy, fresh water, clean cloths.','Honour grief: sit with a loss, or keep company with someone grieving.','Light a lamp at dusk for Nephthys and for all who are at an ending.','Write down what you will close with this year, and burn it safely or tear it up.'],ritual:{title:'At the edge of the year',outline:['At dusk, set a lamp in a safe dish by your door.','Greet Nephthys on her birthday, lady of the house, and ask her to guard your home through the night.','Name what is ending, and thank it.','Leave the lamp to burn while you are present, then snuff it; open the door at dawn to let in the new year.']},correspondences:{deities:['Nephthys','Isis','Osiris','Anubis'],herbs:['Myrrh','Cypress'],crystals:['Obsidian','Jet','Smoky Quartz'],colors:['Black','Silver','Purple'],foods:['Bread','Water'],symbols:['The kite','The house','Twilight']},sources:['Plutarch, On Isis and Osiris 12 (Loeb, 1936, public domain): https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Moralia/Isis_and_Osiris*/A.html','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nephthys']},{ref:'kemetic-epagomenal-6',title:'Sixth Epagomenal Day',aka:['Leap day upon the year'],tradition:'Kemetic (modern practice)',summary:'An extra day before the new year, which the ancient civil calendar never had: it appears when the modern anchor for the new year moves a day later.',honours:'No god was born on this day in the myth. Many modern practitioners use it for the household, the ancestors, or simply rest before the new year opens.',history:"The ancient Egyptian civil year was always exactly 365 days. In 238 BCE the Canopus decree tried to add a sixth epagomenal day every four years, for the festival of the \u201cBenefactor Gods\u201d (Ptolemy III and Berenice), but it was not kept up. Augustus\u2019s reform of the Alexandrian calendar later added a leap day in the same place, and the Coptic calendar still has it: a sixth day of the \u201clittle month\u201d every fourth year.\n\nHere a sixth day appears whenever the date chosen for Wep Ronpet moves a day later (a Coptic or Gregorian leap year, or Sirius rising a day later where you are). It has no ancient festival of its own.",knowAndDont:{known:'The Canopus decree and the Alexandrian (Coptic) leap day both put an extra day here.',unknown:'There is no ancient custom for a day that ordinary years lacked; anything you do with it is your own.'},celebrate:['Rest: take a day without obligations before the new year.','Finish small tasks you want done before the year opens.','Give thanks to all five gods born on the days before.'],ritual:{title:'A between-day',outline:['Leave the day unscheduled as far as you can.','Light a lamp for the household and the ancestors.','Write a short list of what you carry into the new year.','Go to bed early so you can rise for the new year\u2019s dawn.']},correspondences:{deities:['Thoth'],herbs:['Frankincense'],crystals:['Clear Quartz'],colors:['White'],symbols:['The threshold','The game of senet']},sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canopus_Decree','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coptic_calendar','R. A. Parker, The Calendars of Ancient Egypt (Chicago, 1950)']},{ref:'hindu-ekadashi-shukla',title:'Shukla Ekadashi',aka:['Ekadashi','Bright-fortnight Ekadashi','Gyaras','Agyaras'],tradition:'Hindu',summary:'The eleventh lunar day of the waxing fortnight, sacred to Vishnu: kept by many Hindus, especially Vaishnavas, with a fast from grains and beans, prayer, and an effort at a quiet, steady mind.',honours:'Vishnu, the preserver, and in many homes his forms as Krishna and Rama. The Puranas personify Ekadashi herself as a goddess who sprang from Vishnu to defeat the demon Mura, and was given the boon that whoever fasted on her day would be freed from sin.',history:"There are two Ekadashis a month, one in each fortnight, and each in the year has its own name and story: Nirjala Ekadashi in Jyeshtha, kept by some without even water; Devshayani (Ashadhi) Ekadashi, when Vishnu is said to lie down to sleep for the four months of the rains (chaturmas), and the great Pandharpur pilgrimage of the Varkaris in Maharashtra reaches Vithoba; Prabodhini (Devutthana) Ekadashi in Kartika, when he wakes; Vaikuntha Ekadashi in Margashirsha or Pausha, when the \u201cgate of Vaikuntha\u201d is opened in south Indian Vishnu temples such as Srirangam and Tirupati.\n\nThe fast is ancient and much praised in the Puranas, above all the Padma Purana. Practice ranges widely: a full fast, a single meal, or simply giving up grains, beans and certain vegetables while eating fruit, milk and nuts. The fast is broken on the next day (Dwadashi) within a time window the panchang gives, called parana. Vaishnava communities sometimes keep Ekadashi a day later than other calendars, when the tenth tithi touches the hours before dawn.\n\nThe day is also for prayer, the reading of the Bhagavad Gita or the Vishnu Sahasranama, visiting a temple and staying awake in kirtan, and for keeping the mind from quarrels and indulgence.",knowAndDont:{known:'Ekadashi is a living, well-documented observance, with its rules set out in the Puranas and in the panchangs and sect manuals used today.',unknown:'Rules differ by family, region and sampradaya (e.g. which foods, which day when the tithi is split). The dates here are computed; confirm with your temple or a panchang for your place.'},celebrate:['If you are Hindu, follow your family or sampradaya\u2019s rule for the fast, and break it at the parana time on Dwadashi.','If you are learning, a simple respectful step is to eat plainly, skip grains and beans for the day, and read a chapter of the Bhagavad Gita.','Visit a Vishnu or Krishna temple, or attend an evening kirtan; many welcome visitors.','Keep the day gentle: fewer screens, no arguments, kindness in speech.','Fasting is not for everyone: anyone pregnant, diabetic, unwell or with a history of disordered eating should not fast; prayer and gentleness are enough.'],ritual:{title:'A quiet Ekadashi',outline:['In the morning, bathe and decide how you will keep the day: a fast within your health, or simply a plain meal without grains.','Light a small lamp before an image of Vishnu or Krishna if you keep one, or simply at a clean, quiet place.','Read a chapter of the Bhagavad Gita, or listen to a recording of a kirtan or of the Vishnu Sahasranama.','Spend the day steadily, avoiding quarrels and indulgence.','Next morning, break the fast simply, ideally within the parana window in a panchang.']},correspondences:{deities:['Vishnu','Krishna'],herbs:['Tulsi (holy basil)','Sandalwood'],colors:['Yellow','White'],foods:['Fruit','Milk','Nuts','Sabudana (tapioca pearls)'],symbols:['The conch and discus','Tulsi','The waxing moon']},respect:'Ekadashi is a living Hindu observance, kept by hundreds of millions, especially in Vaishnava traditions (including ISKCON, the Swaminarayan sampradaya, the Varkaris and Sri Vaishnavas). It is a vow of devotion to Vishnu, not a spell or a moon-magic day. If you are not Hindu, you are welcome to learn: visit a temple (dress modestly, remove shoes, ask before photographing), read the Gita, or join a kirtan. Don\u2019t borrow its vows or mantras for workings of your own, and don\u2019t present a fast as a diet or a cleanse.',sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ekadashi','https://www.drikpanchang.com/vrats/ekadashidates.html','Padma Purana, Uttara Khanda (the Ekadashi mahatmyas)','G. Flood, An Introduction to Hinduism (Cambridge, 1996), on vrata']},{ref:'hindu-ekadashi-krishna',title:'Krishna Ekadashi',aka:['Ekadashi','Dark-fortnight Ekadashi','Gyaras'],tradition:'Hindu',summary:'The eleventh lunar day of the waning fortnight, sacred to Vishnu like its bright-fortnight twin, and kept the same way: fasting from grains and beans, prayer and a quiet mind.',honours:'Vishnu, the preserver, and his forms as Krishna and Rama. (\u201cKrishna\u201d here names the dark fortnight, krishna paksha, not the god, though he is often worshipped on it.)',history:"Ekadashi falls twice each lunar month, and the dark-fortnight days have their own names and stories just as the bright ones do: Utpanna Ekadashi, which tells the birth of the goddess Ekadashi herself from Vishnu to slay the demon Mura, is on the dark eleventh of Margashirsha (or Kartika, by the full-moon-ending months); Shattila Ekadashi in Magha, with gifts of sesame; Papamochani Ekadashi in Chaitra, \u201creleasing from sin\u201d; Indira Ekadashi in the fortnight of the ancestors, kept for their sake.\n\nThe rules are the same as for the bright fortnight: see the Shukla Ekadashi page for the fast, its breaking (parana) on Dwadashi, and the differences between sampradayas.",knowAndDont:{known:'A living observance, documented in the Puranas and kept today.',unknown:'Family, regional and sect rules differ. Confirm the day with a local panchang or temple.'},celebrate:['Follow your family or sampradaya\u2019s rule for the fast, or simply eat plainly and skip grains and beans.','Read or listen to the story of this month\u2019s Ekadashi (most panchangs give its name).','Give food or a donation to a temple kitchen or food bank.','Anyone pregnant, unwell, diabetic or with a history of disordered eating should not fast.'],ritual:{title:'A quiet Ekadashi',outline:['Bathe in the morning and decide how to keep the day within your health.','Light a small lamp before an image of Vishnu or Krishna, or at a clean, quiet place.','Read or listen to the story of the day\u2019s Ekadashi, or a chapter of the Gita.','Give something to someone in need.','Break the fast simply the next morning.']},correspondences:{deities:['Vishnu','Krishna'],herbs:['Tulsi (holy basil)','Sandalwood'],colors:['Yellow','White'],foods:['Fruit','Milk','Nuts','Sesame (on Shattila Ekadashi)'],symbols:['Tulsi','The waning moon']},respect:'Ekadashi is a living Hindu vow of devotion to Vishnu, not a witchcraft working. Outsiders are welcome to learn, to visit temples respectfully (modest dress, shoes off, ask before photographing) and to share in kirtan and temple meals when invited. Please don\u2019t adapt its vows or mantras into spells, or market the fast as a detox.',sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ekadashi','https://www.drikpanchang.com/vrats/ekadashidates.html','Padma Purana, Uttara Khanda (Utpanna Ekadashi)']},{ref:'hindu-purnima',title:'Purnima',aka:['Poornima','Pournami','Full moon day'],tradition:'Hindu',summary:'The full-moon day of the Hindu month: a day for vows (vrat), worship, the Satyanarayana puja in many homes, holy bathing, and many of the year\u2019s great festivals.',honours:'Different deities on different Purnimas: Vishnu as Satyanarayana in the monthly home puja; Shiva; the Goddess; one\u2019s guru on Guru Purnima; and Chandra, the Moon, himself.',history:"In the p\u016br\u1e47im\u0101nta reckoning of north India, Purnima ends the month; in the am\u0101nta reckoning of the south and west, it is the middle. Either way, a great many festivals fall on it: Holi on Phalguna Purnima; Buddha Purnima (Vesak) in Vaishakha; Guru Purnima in Ashadha, when teachers are honoured; Raksha Bandhan on Shravana Purnima, when sisters tie a protective thread on their brothers\u2019 wrists; Sharad Purnima in Ashvin, when rice pudding (kheer) is left out in the moonlight and eaten in the morning; Kartika Purnima, with lamps floated on rivers and Dev Deepawali at Varanasi.\n\nMany families keep a monthly Purnima vrat with the Satyanarayana puja and katha: an offering to Vishnu as the lord of truth, the reading of its story, and the sharing of prasad (often a sweet semolina dish). Others bathe in a sacred river at dawn, give gifts (dana), or visit temples.",knowAndDont:{known:'A living practice, with festivals documented in the Puranas, panchangs and the life of temples and homes today.',unknown:'Which Purnima customs a family keeps depends on region and tradition. The day a Purnima is observed can differ from the astronomical full moon; confirm with a panchang.'},celebrate:['Find out which festival, if any, this month\u2019s Purnima carries (a panchang will say), and learn its story.','If invited, attend a Satyanarayana puja at a friend\u2019s home or temple; bring fruit or flowers.','Look at the full moon in quiet, and give something to someone in need.','On Sharad Purnima, make kheer and share it.'],ritual:{premade:'folk-diya-gayatri',title:'A lamp at moonrise'},correspondences:{deities:['Vishnu','Shiva','Chandra'],herbs:['Tulsi (holy basil)','Sandalwood','Jasmine'],crystals:['Moonstone','Selenite'],colors:['White','Silver','Yellow'],foods:['Kheer','Sheera (semolina prasad)','Fruit','Milk'],symbols:['The full moon','The lamp (diya)','The rakhi thread']},respect:'Purnima belongs to living Hindu practice, and each full moon may carry a festival with its own deities and family customs. If you\u2019re not Hindu, learn the festival\u2019s story, take part when you are invited, and bring something (fruit, sweets, flowers) to share. Prasad offered to you should be received with the right hand and eaten, not thrown away. Please don\u2019t fold Hindu full-moon rites into Wiccan esbat workings as if they were the same thing.',sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Purnima','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Satyanarayan_Puja','https://www.drikpanchang.com/ (Purnima dates)']},{ref:'hindu-amavasya',title:'Amavasya',aka:['Amavas','Amavasai','New moon day'],tradition:'Hindu',summary:'The new-moon day of the Hindu month, the end of the dark fortnight: a day for offerings to the ancestors (tarpan and shraddha), quiet worship and, for many, care about beginning new undertakings.',honours:'The pitrs, the ancestors, who are offered water, sesame and rice-balls (pinda); and in places Shiva, Kali or other forms of the Goddess.',history:"Amavasya, \u201cdwelling together\u201d, is when the Moon dwells with the Sun and cannot be seen. In the am\u0101nta reckoning of south and west India it ends the month. It is above all the ancestors\u2019 day: water mixed with black sesame is offered for the pitrs (tarpan), often at a river or by a priest\u2019s guidance. Mahalaya Amavasya, at the end of the fortnight of the ancestors (Pitru Paksha) in Bhadrapada/Ashvin, is the greatest of these, and in Bengal it also opens Durga Puja.\n\nOther Amavasyas carry their own customs: Mauni Amavasya in Magha, kept in silence with bathing at the Sangam in Prayagraj; Somvati Amavasya, when the new moon falls on a Monday; and, above all, the Amavasya of Kartika (Ashvin by the am\u0101nta months), which is Diwali, the brightest night of the year. So the new moon is not only an inauspicious day: it depends on the occasion. Many people still avoid starting new ventures or buying major things on an ordinary Amavasya.",knowAndDont:{known:'A living observance with shraddha and tarpan rules set out in the dharmashastra and Puranas and in family practice.',unknown:'Customs vary greatly by community; shraddha rites are performed with a priest or by family members according to their tradition. Confirm the day with a local panchang.'},celebrate:['If you are Hindu, offer tarpan for your ancestors as your family does, or ask a priest.','Anyone can remember their dead: light a lamp, speak their names, give food to someone hungry in their memory.','Feed animals and birds: crows in particular are fed for the ancestors in many places.','Keep the day simple and reflective.'],ritual:{title:'Remembering your ancestors',outline:['At a quiet time, set a lamp and a cup of clean water on a clean surface.','Light the lamp and name your ancestors, as far back as you know them.','Offer the water to the earth (a garden or a plant), saying a word of thanks for each.','Give food or a donation to someone in need in their memory.','If you are not Hindu, keep your own family\u2019s way of remembering, rather than imitating tarpan or pinda rites.']},correspondences:{deities:['Shiva','Kali'],herbs:['Sandalwood'],crystals:['Obsidian','Smoky Quartz'],colors:['Black','White'],foods:['Black sesame','Rice','Kheer'],symbols:['Crows','Kusha grass','Flowing water']},respect:'Amavasya rites for the ancestors are sacred family duties in Hindu life, carried out according to family, caste and regional custom, often with a priest. Outsiders should not perform shraddha or pinda rites as spells or borrow them for necromancy-style workings. Honour your own dead in your own way, and learn about Hindu practice from those who keep it.',sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amavasya','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shraddha','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pitru_Paksha','https://www.drikpanchang.com/ (Amavasya dates)']},{ref:'hindu-vinayaka-chaturthi',title:'Vinayaka Chaturthi',aka:['Vinayaki Chaturthi','Monthly Ganesh Chaturthi','Varad Chaturthi'],tradition:'Hindu',summary:'The fourth lunar day of the waxing fortnight, kept every month for Ganesha with worship at midday; the great Ganesh Chaturthi of Bhadrapada is the year\u2019s grandest of these.',honours:'Ganesha (Vinayaka, Ganapati), the elephant-headed son of Shiva and Parvati, remover of obstacles, lord of beginnings, of learning and of thresholds, honoured first in nearly every Hindu rite.',history:"Each lunar month has two Ganesha days: the bright fourth (Vinayaka Chaturthi) and the dark fourth (Sankashti Chaturthi). On Vinayaka Chaturthi devotees worship Ganesha at midday, when he is said to have been born, with durva grass, red flowers and modak (sweet dumplings), and many keep a fast until the evening.\n\nBecause Ganesha is invoked at the start of anything, his days are favoured for beginnings. The tradition also warns against looking at the moon on the evening of Bhadrapada\u2019s Chaturthi, after the story of the Moon mocking Ganesha and being cursed for it.",knowAndDont:{known:'A living monthly observance described in panchangs and temple calendars.',unknown:'The emphasis given to the monthly day varies by region; confirm the date with a local panchang.'},celebrate:['Visit a Ganesha temple or shrine at midday; offer a flower, a sweet or a few blades of grass.','Begin something new today, quietly, with a thought for the remover of obstacles.','Make or buy modak or laddoo and share them.','Clear an obstacle: one stuck task, one blocked doorway.'],ritual:{title:'A simple offering to Ganesha',outline:['If you keep an image of Ganesha, clean the place and set fresh flowers before it.','Light a lamp (on a safe plate) and a stick of incense at midday.','Offer a sweet and a few blades of fresh grass, with a simple prayer in your own words to remove what blocks you.','Afterwards eat the sweet as prasad and share it.']},correspondences:{deities:['Ganesha','Shiva','Parvati'],herbs:['Durva grass','Sandalwood'],crystals:['Carnelian','Red Jasper'],colors:['Red','Yellow'],foods:['Modak','Laddoo','Coconut','Jaggery'],symbols:['The elephant head','The mouse (mushika)','The broken tusk']},respect:'Ganesha is a living god of Hindu worship, not a good-luck mascot. If you are drawn to him, learn from Hindu sources and temples, treat his images with care (not on the floor, not in bathrooms), and offer vegetarian food. Outsiders are usually welcome at temples: dress modestly, remove shoes, follow the lead of devotees, and ask before taking photographs.',sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ganesha','https://www.drikpanchang.com/vrats/vinayaka-chaturthi-dates.html','P. B. Courtright, Ga\u1e47e\u015ba: Lord of Obstacles, Lord of Beginnings (Oxford, 1985)']},{ref:'hindu-sankashti-chaturthi',title:'Sankashti Chaturthi',aka:['Sankatahara Chaturthi','Sakat Chauth','Angaraki (on a Tuesday)'],tradition:'Hindu',summary:'The fourth lunar day of the waning fortnight: a monthly fast for Ganesha, \u201cremover of obstacles\u201d, broken after sighting the moon as it rises.',honours:'Ganesha in his form as Vighnaharta, the remover of troubles (sankata, \u201cdifficulty\u201d; sankashti, \u201cdeliverance from difficulty\u201d), and the Moon, to whom an offering of water is made before the fast is broken.',history:"Sankashti is a popular vrat especially in Maharashtra and south India. Devotees fast during the day, worship Ganesha in the evening, read the vrat katha for the month (each month has its own form of Ganesha and its own story), and after moonrise offer water (arghya) to the Moon and break the fast.\n\nWhen it falls on a Tuesday it is Angaraki Sankashti, held especially auspicious. In north India the Sankashti of Magha is Sakat Chauth, kept by mothers for their children.",knowAndDont:{known:'A living vrat, with rules and stories in panchangs and vrat collections.',unknown:'Which day is kept depends on the tithi at moonrise where you are; confirm with a local panchang.'},celebrate:['Watch for the moonrise tonight, and offer it a little water poured on the earth.','If you fast, do so within your health, and break it after moonrise.','Visit a Ganesha temple in the evening.','Name a difficulty you face, and one small step to ease it.'],ritual:{title:'Waiting for the moon',outline:['During the day, keep a light fast if your health allows, or simply eat plainly.','At dusk, light a lamp before an image of Ganesha or at a quiet place, and offer a flower or sweet.','Watch for the moon to rise (a moonrise table helps).','Pour a little water to the earth for the moon, then eat.']},correspondences:{deities:['Ganesha','Chandra'],herbs:['Durva grass'],crystals:['Moonstone','Carnelian'],colors:['Red','White'],foods:['Modak','Sesame and jaggery sweets','Fruit'],symbols:['The rising moon','The mouse','Water poured as arghya']},respect:'Sankashti is a Hindu vow of devotion to Ganesha. If you are not Hindu, you are welcome to learn and to attend temple worship respectfully; please don\u2019t repackage the vrat or its mantras as moon magic. Anyone pregnant, unwell or with a history of disordered eating should not fast.',sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sankashti_Chaturthi','https://www.drikpanchang.com/vrats/sankashti-chaturthi-dates.html']},{ref:'hindu-pradosh-shukla',title:'Pradosh (bright fortnight)',aka:['Pradosham','Pradosha Vrat','Shukla Pradosh'],tradition:'Hindu',summary:'The thirteenth lunar day of the waxing fortnight, when Shiva is worshipped at dusk, the hour called pradosh kaal.',honours:'Shiva and Parvati, and Nandi the bull, Shiva\u2019s mount and doorkeeper. The story most often told is of the churning of the ocean: when it threw up the poison halahala, Shiva drank it to save the worlds, and the gods praised him at dusk on the thirteenth day.',history:"Pradosh vrat is kept twice a month, on the waxing and waning thirteenth tithis, at the twilight period after sunset (about an hour and a half). In Shiva temples, especially in Tamil Nadu, abhishekam is performed for Nandi and the linga, and devotees circle the shrine in a special pattern (soma sutra pradakshina).\n\nIts name changes with the weekday: Soma Pradosh on a Monday and Shani Pradosh on a Saturday are counted especially fruitful. The same customs apply to both fortnights; see also the page for the dark-fortnight Pradosh.",knowAndDont:{known:'A living observance, with its stories in the Skanda Purana and other texts and its rites in temple practice.',unknown:'Exact timings differ by panchang; the day is chosen by the tithi at dusk where you are.'},celebrate:['Visit a Shiva temple at dusk, when the Pradosh worship is done.','Light a lamp at sunset and sit in quiet for a few minutes.','Offer water or milk to a Shiva linga if you keep one, as your tradition does.','Let the twilight mark a pause between work and evening.'],ritual:{premade:'folk-diya-gayatri',title:'A lamp at twilight'},correspondences:{deities:['Shiva','Parvati'],herbs:['Bilva (bael) leaves','Sandalwood'],crystals:['Clear Quartz'],colors:['White','Blue'],foods:['Milk','Fruit'],symbols:['Nandi the bull','The linga','Twilight','Rudraksha beads']},respect:'Pradosh is Hindu worship of Shiva. If you are not Hindu, attending temple worship respectfully (modest dress, shoes off, following devotees\u2019 lead) is welcome in most places; please don\u2019t treat Shiva\u2019s rites as a spell timing.',sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pradosha','https://www.drikpanchang.com/ (Pradosh dates)']},{ref:'hindu-pradosh-krishna',title:'Pradosh (dark fortnight)',aka:['Pradosham','Pradosha Vrat','Krishna Pradosh'],tradition:'Hindu',summary:'The thirteenth lunar day of the waning fortnight, when Shiva is worshipped at dusk; the dark Pradosh of Magha is followed the next night by Maha Shivaratri.',honours:'Shiva, Parvati and Nandi, as on the bright-fortnight Pradosh.',history:"The waning Pradosh is kept exactly like the waxing one: worship at the dusk hour, abhishekam for Nandi and the linga, and a fast for those who keep it. It falls on the eve of the monthly Shivaratri (the dark fourteenth), so for many it opens two days of Shiva worship, and once a year, in Magha (Phalguna in the north), that Shivaratri is the great one.\n\nSee the bright-fortnight Pradosh page for the story and customs.",knowAndDont:{known:'A living observance in panchangs and temple life.',unknown:'Timings vary by place and panchang; confirm locally.'},celebrate:['Visit a Shiva temple at dusk.','Light a lamp at sunset and keep a few minutes of quiet.','If it falls on a Monday or Saturday, note it: Soma or Shani Pradosh.'],ritual:{premade:'folk-diya-gayatri',title:'A lamp at twilight'},correspondences:{deities:['Shiva','Parvati'],herbs:['Bilva (bael) leaves','Sandalwood'],colors:['White','Blue'],foods:['Milk','Fruit'],symbols:['Nandi the bull','The linga','Twilight']},respect:'Pradosh is Hindu worship of Shiva. Outsiders are welcome to learn and attend respectfully; please don\u2019t adapt its rites into spellwork.',sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pradosha','https://www.drikpanchang.com/ (Pradosh dates)']},{ref:'hindu-maha-shivaratri',title:'Maha Shivaratri',aka:['Mahashivratri','The Great Night of Shiva','Shivratri'],tradition:'Hindu',summary:'The great night of Shiva, in late winter: a night of fasting, vigil, chanting and offerings of water, milk and bilva leaves to the linga.',honours:'Shiva, the auspicious one, ascetic and householder, destroyer and renewer. Different stories are told of this night: that it is the night of his marriage to Parvati; the night he appeared as an endless pillar of fire (the lingodbhava) before Brahma and Vishnu; the night he danced the cosmic dance; or the night he held the poison of the churned ocean in his throat.',history:"Shivaratri, \u201cShiva\u2019s night\u201d, comes every month on the dark fourteenth, but the Maha (great) Shivaratri of Magha (Phalguna in the north) is one of the most important nights of the Hindu year. The Puranas, notably the Shiva and Skanda Puranas, praise it, and tell the story of a hunter who, stuck in a bilva tree all night, unknowingly dropped leaves and water on a linga below and was blessed.\n\nDevotees fast through the day, and the night is divided into four watches (prahar), with a puja and abhishekam of the linga in each: water, milk, curd, honey and ghee poured over it, and bilva leaves offered. Many stay awake all night chanting \u201cOm Namah Shivaya\u201d. Huge crowds gather at Shiva temples, from Varanasi\u2019s Kashi Vishwanath to Pashupatinath in Nepal, and in recent decades large public all-night events have also grown.",knowAndDont:{known:'A living major festival documented in the Puranas, panchangs and temple life.',unknown:'The date depends on which night the Chaturdashi holds at midnight, so it can differ by a day between panchangs and places.'},celebrate:['Visit a Shiva temple, where many keep the night open; go respectfully and follow the devotees.','Keep a vigil, or at least stay up a little longer, with quiet music or chanting.','Fast within your health, or eat simply.','Offer cool water to a plant or the earth, as water is poured over the linga.'],ritual:{title:'A night of attention',outline:['Eat simply during the day, or keep a fast if your health and tradition allow.','In the evening, light a lamp (never left unattended) at a clean, quiet place.','Divide the night as you can into watches; at each, sit for a while in silence or listen to Shiva bhajans.','If you are Hindu, perform the abhishekam as your family does; if not, simply offer cool water to a plant at each watch.','At dawn, eat a simple meal and rest.']},correspondences:{deities:['Shiva','Parvati','Ganesha'],herbs:['Bilva (bael) leaves','Sandalwood'],crystals:['Clear Quartz'],colors:['White','Blue','Grey'],foods:['Milk','Fruit','Thandai'],symbols:['The linga','The trident (trishula)','The crescent in Shiva\u2019s hair','Ash','Rudraksha beads']},respect:'Maha Shivaratri is a sacred night of Hindu devotion to Shiva. Outsiders are welcome at many temples and public celebrations: dress modestly, remove shoes, follow queues and devotees\u2019 lead, and ask before photographing. Please don\u2019t treat Shiva or the linga as an exotic prop, or bhang culture as the point of the festival.',sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maha_Shivaratri','https://www.drikpanchang.com/festivals/maha-shivaratri/maha-shivaratri-date-time.html','Shiva Purana, Vidyeshvara Samhita; Skanda Purana (the story of the hunter)']},{ref:'hindu-ganesh-chaturthi',title:'Ganesh Chaturthi',aka:['Vinayaka Chavithi','Ganeshotsav','Ganpati festival'],tradition:'Hindu',summary:'The birthday of Ganesha in late summer: his image is welcomed into homes and public pandals for one to ten days of worship, then carried in procession and immersed in water.',honours:'Ganesha, remover of obstacles and lord of beginnings, son of Parvati, who in the best-known story made him from turmeric paste and set him to guard her door; Shiva, not knowing him, cut off his head and gave him an elephant\u2019s in its place.',history:"Ganesh Chaturthi falls on Bhadrapada Shukla Chaturthi. In homes, a clay image of Ganesha is installed with prana pratishtha, worshipped with flowers, durva grass, red sandalwood and modak, and after one and a half, three, five, seven or ten days carried out for immersion (visarjan) in a river, lake or the sea, with the cry \u201cGanpati Bappa Morya, come back soon next year\u201d.\n\nHousehold worship is old; the great public festival is newer. In 1893 the nationalist leader Bal Gangadhar Tilak promoted public Ganeshotsav in Pune as a way of bringing people together under colonial rule, and the vast pandals of Mumbai and Pune grew from that. It is the major festival of Maharashtra, Goa, Karnataka, Telangana and Andhra Pradesh, and is celebrated by diaspora communities worldwide.\n\nIn recent years many have turned to unpainted clay images and artificial immersion tanks, as plaster of Paris and paint harm rivers and the sea.",knowAndDont:{known:'A living festival with well-documented household and public customs, and a well-known modern history.',unknown:'The date and number of days are set by family and community custom; confirm the date with a local panchang.'},celebrate:['Visit a public Ganesh pandal or a temple; many welcome everyone, and the atmosphere is joyful.','If you are invited into a home during the festival, bring sweets or fruit, and accept prasad gratefully.','Make or buy modak and share them.','Choose eco-friendly clay if you keep an image, and immerse it in a bucket at home rather than a river.','Don\u2019t look at the moon tonight, if you want to keep the old custom.'],ritual:{title:'Welcoming the remover of obstacles',outline:['Clean and decorate a small place in your home with flowers.','If you keep an image of Ganesha, set it there; light a lamp beside it.','Offer modak or another sweet, a red flower and a few blades of grass.','Name the obstacles before you and ask for wisdom in meeting them.','Share the sweets with others as prasad.']},correspondences:{deities:['Ganesha','Parvati','Shiva'],herbs:['Durva grass','Sandalwood'],crystals:['Carnelian','Red Jasper'],colors:['Red','Yellow','Orange'],foods:['Modak','Laddoo','Coconut','Jaggery'],symbols:['The elephant head','The mouse','Water for visarjan']},respect:'Ganesh Chaturthi is a Hindu festival and, in many cities, a huge public celebration that welcomes everyone. Join with joy and respect: follow devotees\u2019 lead at pandals, dress modestly, don\u2019t treat images as decor, and ask before photographing people at worship. Please don\u2019t take Ganesha into non-Hindu spellwork; learn about him from Hindu sources and communities.',sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ganesh_Chaturthi','https://www.drikpanchang.com/festivals/ganesh-chaturthi/ganesh-chaturthi-date-time.html','R. L. Cashman, \u201cThe Political Recruitment of God Ganapati\u201d, Indian Economic and Social History Review 7 (1970)','P. B. Courtright, Ga\u1e47e\u015ba: Lord of Obstacles, Lord of Beginnings (Oxford, 1985)']},{ref:'hindu-navaratri',title:'Sharad Navaratri',aka:['Navratri','Durga Puja (Bengal)','Dussehra / Vijayadashami','Golu','Nine Nights of the Goddess'],tradition:'Hindu',summary:'Nine nights of the Goddess in the autumn month of Ashvin, ending in Vijayadashami (Dussehra): fasting, worship of Durga in her nine forms, Garba dancing, Durga Puja and the victory of good over evil.',honours:'Durga and the Goddess (Devi) in many forms, above all her victory over the buffalo-demon Mahishasura, told in the Devi Mahatmya; also, in north India, Rama\u2019s victory over Ravana, celebrated on the tenth day as Dussehra. In south India the three triads of nights honour Durga, Lakshmi and Saraswati.',history:"Navaratri opens with Ghatasthapana: a pot of water is installed and barley sown around it, to sprout through the nine days. Each day honours one of the nine forms of Durga (Navadurga), from Shailaputri to Siddhidatri, and many fast or eat only certain foods.\n\nThe festival looks very different by region. In Gujarat, people dance Garba and Dandiya Raas in circles each night. In Bengal and among Bengalis everywhere, Durga Puja fills the last five days with great pandals of Durga slaying Mahishasura, and ends with the images immersed in the river. In Tamil Nadu and Karnataka families set up golu, stepped displays of dolls, and visit one another; Saraswati Puja honours books and tools on the ninth day. On the eighth or ninth day young girls are worshipped as the Goddess (kanya puja). In the north, the Ramlila plays the story of Rama, and on Dussehra great effigies of Ravana are burned. Mysore\u2019s Dasara procession is famous.\n\nVijayadashami is held a good day to begin learning, and children are introduced to letters (Vidyarambham) in Kerala.",knowAndDont:{known:'A living major festival with its texts (the Devi Mahatmya in the Markandeya Purana) and abundant regional customs.',unknown:'The number of days can shift when a tithi is skipped or doubled, and regional customs vary greatly; confirm with a local panchang or community.'},celebrate:['Go to a public Garba night or a Durga Puja pandal: many are open to all and very welcoming.','Sprout barley in a pot of soil through the nine days, as in Ghatasthapana.','Read the Devi Mahatmya in translation, a little each night.','On Vijayadashami, begin something you want to learn.','If you fast, do so within your health.'],ritual:{title:'Nine evenings',outline:['On the first day, sow a few grains of barley in a pot of soil and set it in a clean place.','Each evening, light a lamp beside it (never unattended) and spend a few minutes in quiet, or read a passage of the Devi Mahatmya.','Water the grain and watch it grow over the nine days.','On the tenth day, give thanks, return the grass to the garden, and begin something new.']},correspondences:{deities:['Durga','Lakshmi','Saraswati','Rama'],herbs:['Jasmine','Marigold'],crystals:['Carnelian','Garnet','Ruby'],colors:['Red','Gold','A different colour each day (as many communities assign)'],foods:['Kuttu (buckwheat)','Sabudana','Fruit','Halwa, puri and chana (kanya puja)'],symbols:['The lion','The trident','The sprouting pot','The dandiya sticks']},respect:'Navaratri, Durga Puja and Dussehra are Hindu festivals and great community celebrations. Many Garba nights and pandals warmly welcome visitors; dress modestly (or in traditional clothes if invited), follow the dance etiquette, and respect worship spaces. Please don\u2019t treat Durga as a generic \u201cwarrior goddess\u201d for spellwork, or the festival as a costume party.',sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Navaratri','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Durga_Puja','https://www.drikpanchang.com/navratri/shardiya-navratri-ghatasthapana.html','T. B. Coburn, Encountering the Goddess: A Translation of the Dev\u012b-M\u0101h\u0101tmya (Albany, 1991)']},{ref:'hindu-diwali',title:'Diwali',aka:['Deepavali','Lakshmi Puja','Festival of Lights','Divali'],tradition:'Hindu',summary:'The festival of lamps, on the new moon of Kartika (Ashvin by the am\u0101nta months): houses are cleaned and lit with rows of lamps, Lakshmi is welcomed, sweets are shared and families gather.',honours:'Lakshmi, goddess of prosperity and good fortune, welcomed into clean and lit homes, with Ganesha honoured first; in north India, Rama\u2019s return to Ayodhya after defeating Ravana; in the south, Krishna\u2019s victory over Narakasura (on the day before); in Bengal, Kali. Jains keep it for Mahavira\u2019s liberation and Sikhs celebrate Bandi Chhor Divas.',history:"Diwali is usually kept as five days: Dhanteras (buying metal or utensils, and honouring Dhanvantari), Naraka Chaturdashi or Choti Diwali (an oil bath before dawn in the south), the main night of Lakshmi Puja on the Amavasya, Govardhan Puja and Annakut (a \u201cmountain of food\u201d for Krishna) the day after, and Bhai Dooj, when sisters honour their brothers. In Gujarat the new year begins after it.\n\nBefore the night, homes are cleaned and repaired, rangoli patterns are drawn at the doorway, and new clothes bought. At dusk, rows of clay lamps (diyas) are lit along windows and doorways, Lakshmi and Ganesha are worshipped, and then come sweets, visits, gifts and fireworks. Businesses open new account books.\n\nDiwali is celebrated across India and by diaspora communities worldwide. Air pollution from fireworks is a real concern in Indian cities, and many now celebrate with lamps alone.",knowAndDont:{known:'A living, major festival kept by Hindus, Jains, Sikhs and some Buddhists, with well-documented customs.',unknown:'The day can differ between India and the Americas, and between regional panchangs in years when the Amavasya only just touches dusk. Confirm with a local temple or panchang.'},celebrate:['Clean your home thoroughly in the days before, and fix one broken thing.','Light lamps or candles at dusk in windows and at the door, safely and never left unattended.','Draw a simple rangoli at your doorway with coloured rice, chalk or flowers.','Share sweets with neighbours and friends, and go to a community Diwali event if there is one near you.','If invited to a Lakshmi Puja, go gladly: bring sweets or fruit.'],ritual:{premade:'folk-lakshmi-diwali'},correspondences:{deities:['Lakshmi','Ganesha','Rama','Kali'],herbs:['Marigold','Sandalwood','Rose'],crystals:['Citrine','Pyrite'],colors:['Gold','Red','Yellow'],foods:['Laddoo','Barfi','Kheer','Puffed rice and sugar sweets (batashe)'],symbols:['The diya','Rangoli','Lakshmi\u2019s footprints','The lotus']},respect:'Diwali is a sacred festival for Hindus, Jains and Sikhs, and also a joyful community celebration that often welcomes friends and neighbours of every background. Take part when invited, learn the stories, and bring something to share. Please don\u2019t reframe it as a money spell, or treat Lakshmi as interchangeable with a Wiccan prosperity goddess; the home devotion offered here is a simple welcome, not a substitute for Hindu puja.',sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diwali','https://www.drikpanchang.com/diwali/diwali-puja-calendar.html','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bandi_Chhor_Divas']},{ref:'hindu-holika-dahan',title:'Holika Dahan',aka:['Choti Holi','Holika bonfire','Kamadahanam (south India)'],tradition:'Hindu',summary:'The bonfire on the evening of Phalguna Purnima, the eve of Holi, burning the demoness Holika and celebrating the devotee Prahlad saved from the fire.',honours:'Vishnu, who as Narasimha protected his boy-devotee Prahlad from his demon father Hiranyakashipu; Prahlad himself, the model of steadfast devotion. In parts of south India, the fire recalls instead Kama, the god of love, burned by Shiva\u2019s third eye.',history:"The story told is that the demon king Hiranyakashipu, angry that his son Prahlad worshipped Vishnu, tried again and again to kill him. His sister Holika, who could not be burned, took the boy into a fire on her lap; but the fire consumed Holika and Prahlad came out unharmed. The bonfire burns her, and with her the year\u2019s evils.\n\nIn the days before, wood and cow-dung cakes are collected and heaped at crossroads and open spaces. On the evening of the full moon, after the inauspicious period of Bhadra has passed, the fire is lit with prayers, and people circle it, offering coconuts, new grain (wheat and chickpea ears roasted in the fire) and other offerings. In many villages embers are taken home to light the hearth.\n\nThe next morning comes Holi, the festival of colours.",knowAndDont:{known:'A living festival with the Prahlad story in the Bhagavata and Vishnu Puranas, and well-known customs today.',unknown:'Panchangs apply the Bhadra rule differently, so the evening can differ by a day; confirm locally.'},celebrate:['Go to a community Holika Dahan; many temples and community groups hold one.','If you light any fire yourself, keep it small and safe: in a fire pit, away from buildings and with water to hand, and only where it is permitted.','Roast new grain or chickpeas and share them.','Write down what you want to let burn away, and give it to the fire, or tear it up if you have no fire.'],ritual:{title:'Letting the fire take it',outline:['If you can join a community bonfire, go, and circle it with the others.','At home, use a single candle in a deep, heatproof dish, never left alone.','Hear or read the story of Prahlad and Holika.','Name, silently, something harmful you want to leave behind.','Share something sweet, and prepare (old clothes, natural colours) for Holi in the morning.']},correspondences:{deities:['Vishnu','Narasimha','Prahlad'],herbs:['Neem','Sandalwood'],crystals:['Carnelian'],colors:['Orange','Red'],foods:['Roasted wheat and chickpeas','Coconut','Gujiya'],symbols:['The bonfire','The full moon','Coconut']},respect:'Holika Dahan is a Hindu sacred fire with a devotional story at its heart. Outsiders are often welcome at community bonfires: follow the lead of those who organise it and treat the fire as a place of prayer, not a party bonfire. Please don\u2019t restyle it as a generic banishing spell.',sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Holika_Dahan','https://www.drikpanchang.com/festivals/holi/festivals-holika-dahan-timings.html','Bhagavata Purana, Book 7 (Prahlad and Narasimha)']},{ref:'hindu-holi',title:'Holi',aka:['Rangwali Holi','Dhulandi','Festival of Colours','Dol Jatra','Phagwah'],tradition:'Hindu',summary:'The festival of colours on the morning after Holika Dahan: people throw coloured powder and water, share sweets, visit friends and make up quarrels, celebrating spring and the love of Radha and Krishna.',honours:'Krishna and Radha above all: the story goes that dark-skinned Krishna, worried that fair Radha would not love him, was told by his mother to colour her face as he liked. Also the spring, and the triumph of devotion (Prahlad) over the night before.',history:"Holi is among the oldest Hindu festivals, mentioned in early medieval literature and celebrated in the Braj country around Mathura and Vrindavan with special joy: Lathmar Holi in Barsana, where women playfully beat the men of Nandgaon with sticks, and a week or more of festivities in the temples. In Bengal and Odisha the same full moon is Dol Purnima, with Krishna and Radha swung on decorated swings; in Maharashtra colour is played on Rang Panchami, five days later. The Caribbean and Fiji Indian diaspora call it Phagwah.\n\nThe rules of ordinary life loosen for a morning: everyone is fair game, rank and caste are blurred in the colour, and \u201cbura na mano, Holi hai\u201d (\u201cdon\u2019t be offended, it\u2019s Holi\u201d) is the cry. In the afternoon people wash, dress in clean clothes, and visit friends and family with sweets such as gujiya, and thandai to drink.\n\nTwo cautions: synthetic colours can harm skin and eyes, so natural colours (gulal from flowers and herbs) are better; and the festival\u2019s licence must never become an excuse for harassment. Colour is for those who are willing to play.",knowAndDont:{known:'A living, major festival with old roots and well-documented regional customs.',unknown:'The day can differ by region and panchang; confirm locally.'},celebrate:['Join a community or temple Holi celebration; many are open to everyone.','Use natural, skin-safe colours, protect your eyes, and play only with people who want to.','Make or buy gujiya and share them with neighbours.','Mend a quarrel: Holi is a day for forgiving.','Wear old white clothes you don\u2019t mind losing to the colour.'],ritual:{title:'Colour and reconciliation',outline:['In the morning, put a pinch of colour on your own forehead or cheek, or on a picture of Krishna and Radha if you keep one.','Greet those you live with by putting a little colour on their cheek, with their consent.','Contact one person you have quarrelled with, and offer peace.','In the afternoon, wash, change into clean clothes, and share sweets.']},correspondences:{deities:['Krishna','Radha'],herbs:['Marigold','Rose','Saffron'],crystals:['Rose Quartz','Carnelian'],colors:['Pink','Green','Yellow','Red','Blue'],foods:['Gujiya','Thandai','Malpua','Puran poli'],symbols:['Coloured powder (gulal)','The flute','The water-squirter (pichkari)']},respect:'Holi is a Hindu festival, now joined in by people of many backgrounds. Commercial \u201ccolour runs\u201d and Holi-themed parties often strip out its meaning; if you take part, learn the stories, go to a community or temple celebration if you can, and support the people whose festival it is. Never throw colour at anyone who doesn\u2019t want it.',sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Holi','https://www.drikpanchang.com/ (Holi dates)','M. Marriott, \u201cThe Feast of Love\u201d, in M. Singer (ed.), Krishna: Myths, Rites, and Attitudes (Honolulu, 1966)']},{ref:'slavic-sviatvechir',title:'Sviatvechir (Holy Evening)',aka:['Svyat Vechir','Orthodox Christmas Eve','Sochelnik','Kuccia (Belarus)','Wigilia (Polish, New Style)'],tradition:'Slavic (Orthodox and Greek Catholic folk year)',summary:'Christmas Eve by the Old Style (6 January in the ordinary calendar): the family gathers at the first star for a meatless supper of twelve dishes, beginning with kutia, with a sheaf for the ancestors in the corner.',honours:'The coming birth of Christ, above all; and around it, the household, its living and its dead. The didukh (\u201cgrandfather\u201d), a sheaf of the last-harvested grain, stands in the corner of the main room as the spirit of the ancestors and of the harvest, and some families leave a place, or a spoonful of kutia, for those who have died.',history:"Sviatvechir closes the Nativity fast, so the supper is meatless (and in many homes dairy-free), with twelve dishes, often said to be for the twelve apostles: kutia (boiled wheat with poppy seed, honey and nuts) first, then borshch with little mushroom dumplings (vushka), varenyky, fish, cabbage rolls, beans, mushrooms and uzvar, a drink of dried fruit. Hay is spread under the tablecloth for the manger, and the meal begins when the children see the first star in the sky, for the star of Bethlehem.\n\nThe head of the house carries in the didukh; spoons of kutia were once thrown to the ceiling (the more that stuck, the better the year, or the swarms of bees), and kutia was offered to the frost so it would spare the crops. After supper the carollers come out, and many go to the night service. Similar suppers are kept in Belarus (Kuccia), Russia (Sochelnik), and among Catholic Poles and Slovaks as Wigilia on 24 December New Style.\n\nHow much of the didukh and kutia is older than Christianity is a fair question: ethnographers have long seen in them a winter feast for the dead and the harvest, folded into the Christian Christmas. That is a likely reading, but no pre-Christian source describes such a feast, so it remains an inference.",knowAndDont:{known:'The supper, its dishes, the didukh, the hay and the first star are richly recorded by 19th- and 20th-century ethnographers and kept today across Ukraine, Belarus and their diasporas.',unknown:'How old the ancestral elements are. Customs vary by region and family; since 2023 many Ukrainians keep the feast on 24 December New Style instead.'},celebrate:['Cook kutia, or a few meatless dishes, and wait for the first star before eating.','Put a little hay under the tablecloth, or a sheaf of grain or dried grasses in the corner.','Leave a candle and a spoonful of food for those of your family who have died.','Learn and sing a koliadka, or listen to recordings of Ukrainian carols.','If you are near an Orthodox or Greek Catholic parish, go to the evening service: visitors are welcome.'],ritual:{title:'Supper at the first star',outline:['Cook kutia (wheat berries, poppy seed, honey, nuts) and a few other meatless dishes.','Lay hay or straw under a white tablecloth, and stand a sheaf or bundle of grasses in the corner.','Light a candle on the table, safely, and set a place or a spoonful for your dead.','Watch for the first star; when it appears, greet the evening and begin with the kutia.','After supper, sing or listen to carols, and remember those absent.']},correspondences:{herbs:['Birch','Oak'],colors:['White','Red','Gold'],foods:['Kutia (wheat, poppy seed, honey)','Varenyky','Borshch with vushka','Uzvar','Pampushky'],symbols:['The first star','The didukh sheaf','Hay under the tablecloth','An empty place for the dead']},respect:'Sviatvechir is the Christmas Eve of Orthodox and Greek Catholic Christians, above all Ukrainians, Belarusians and Rusyns, and it is a deeply family occasion, kept with special feeling now in Ukraine and its diaspora. If you are not part of these communities, learn the customs, cook the dishes, go to a parish service or a community supper if invited, and support Ukrainian artists and charities. Please don\u2019t present the didukh or kutia as a \u201cpagan Slavic\u201d spell, or reinvent the feast as a solstice rite: the people who keep it keep it for Christ\u2019s birth.',sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christmas_in_Ukraine','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Didukh','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kutia','O. Voropai, Zvychai nashoho narodu (Munich, 1958)']},{ref:'slavic-rizdvo',title:'Rizdvo / Koliada (Old Style Christmas)',aka:['Rizdvo Khrystove','Orthodox Christmas','Koliada','Rozhdestvo'],tradition:'Slavic (Orthodox and Greek Catholic folk year)',summary:'The Nativity of Christ by the Old Style (7 January): church services, family feasting after the fast, and carollers going from house to house with the star, singing koliadky.',honours:'The birth of Christ; and, in the carols, the household, its master and mistress, its children, its fields and beasts, all wished a good year.',history:"On Christmas morning the greeting is \u201cKhrystos rodyvsia!\u201d (Christ is born!), answered \u201cSlavimo Yoho!\u201d (Let us glorify him!). The fast is over, and the table fills with meat and rich dishes.\n\nFrom Christmas to Theophany, groups of carollers (koliadnyky) go from house to house carrying a many-pointed star on a pole, singing koliadky and performing the vertep, a Nativity play once given with puppets in a two-storey box and later with live actors, with Herod, angels, shepherds, a Jew, a Cossack, a devil and Death. Householders reward them with food or money, which today usually goes to a church or a charity.\n\nMany koliadky are clearly Christian; others sing of a golden-horned stag, of the world created from the sea, or of the householder\u2019s plentiful barns, and scholars have long seen in them older songs of the winter season. The name koliada itself comes from the Latin calendae, the Roman first of the month, by way of the New Year Kalends; there is no evidence for a god called Koliada.",knowAndDont:{known:'The feast, the koliadky, the star and the vertep are well recorded and still kept.',unknown:'How old individual carols are, and what the non-Christian motifs once meant. Modern Rodnover festivals of \u201cKoliada\u201d at the solstice are reconstructions.'},celebrate:['Greet friends with \u201cKhrystos rodyvsia!\u201d and learn the answer.','Learn a koliadka, or listen to recordings of Ukrainian or Belarusian carols.','Make a simple carolling star from card and a stick, with a candle-shaped light in the middle.','Give to a charity, as carollers\u2019 gifts now do.','If you are near a parish, go to the Christmas Liturgy.'],ritual:{title:'Carolling the house',outline:['Make or find a star and a carol or two you like.','Go through your home, or visit a friend, and sing at the door.','Wish the household health, plenty and peace for the year in your own words.','Share food afterwards, and give a gift to a charity in thanks.']},correspondences:{herbs:['Birch','Holly'],colors:['Gold','Red','White'],foods:['Kutia','Holubtsi','Kovbasa','Pampushky'],symbols:['The carolling star','The vertep','The didukh']},respect:'This is the Christmas of Orthodox and Greek Catholic Christians. Visitors are welcome at most parishes for the Divine Liturgy: stand with the congregation, follow their lead, dress modestly (some parishes expect women to cover their heads) and don\u2019t receive communion unless you are Orthodox or have asked the priest. Learn the carols from the communities that sing them, and don\u2019t recast the feast as a sun-god festival.',sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Koliada','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vertep','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christmas_in_Ukraine','V. Hnatiuk, Koliadky i shchedrivky (Lviv, 1914)']},{ref:'slavic-shchedryi-vechir',title:'Shchedryi Vechir / Malanka',aka:['Generous Evening','Malanka','Vasyl\u2019s Eve','Orthodox New Year\u2019s Eve'],tradition:'Slavic (Orthodox and Greek Catholic folk year)',summary:'New Year\u2019s Eve by the Old Style (13 January), the feast of St Melania (Malanka): a second rich supper, well-wishing songs (shchedrivky), and masked mummers with Malanka, Vasyl and the goat.',honours:'St Melania the Younger, whose day it is, and St Basil, whose day follows; but above all the coming year and the household\u2019s luck, wished upon it in song.',history:"This is the second of the three holy suppers of the winter feasts, called shchedra (generous) kutia because, unlike Christmas Eve, meat and rich food may be served. After dark the young people go out with shchedrivky, songs of well-wishing for the new year. The most famous is \u201cShchedryk\u201d, in which a swallow flies in to tell the householder of the plenty the year will bring; Mykola Leontovych\u2019s choral arrangement (1916) became, with new English words, \u201cCarol of the Bells\u201d.\n\nThe Malanka troupe is a masked folk play: a young man dressed as the bride Malanka, with Vasyl, the goat (koza) who \u201cdies\u201d and is revived, a bear, a Gypsy, a Jew, a soldier and others, who play pranks and are given treats. In Bukovina, Malanka is still a huge carnival. Diaspora communities hold \u201cMalanka\u201d dances as their New Year celebration.",knowAndDont:{known:'The supper, shchedrivky, Malanka plays and their characters are well recorded and still kept, especially in western Ukraine and the diaspora.',unknown:'The goat\u2019s death and revival and the cross-dressing are often called pre-Christian fertility rites; that is plausible but unproven.'},celebrate:['Listen to \u201cShchedryk\u201d sung in Ukrainian, and learn its story.','Cook a generous supper and invite friends.','Go to a Malanka dance if your town has a Ukrainian community.','Wish each person you meet a good year in plain words.'],ritual:{title:'A generous evening',outline:['Cook a rich supper and set a candle on the table.','Before eating, wish each person at the table something specific for the new year.','Sing or play \u201cShchedryk\u201d and tell the story of the swallow.','Put aside some food or money for someone who has less, and give it the next day.']},correspondences:{herbs:['Birch'],colors:['Red','Gold'],foods:['Shchedra kutia','Varenyky','Pyrohy','Kovbasa'],symbols:['The swallow','The goat mask','Masks and costumes']},respect:'Shchedryi Vechir and Malanka belong to Ukrainian and Rusyn Christian folk culture, kept with special feeling in wartime and in the diaspora. Join community celebrations when they\u2019re open to the public, and give credit to the culture behind \u201cCarol of the Bells\u201d. Please don\u2019t present Malanka as a pagan rite to be reconstructed for spellwork.',sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malanka','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shchedryk_(song)']},{ref:'slavic-old-new-year',title:'Old New Year (St Basil\u2019s Day)',aka:['Staryi Novyi Rik','Vasyl','Feast of St Basil','Staryi Novy God'],tradition:'Slavic (Orthodox and Greek Catholic folk year)',summary:'New Year by the Old Style (14 January), the feast of St Basil the Great: boys go from house to house \u201csowing\u201d grain for a fruitful year.',honours:'St Basil the Great (Vasyl), bishop of Caesarea in the 4th century, whose feast is 1 January in the church calendar; and the circumcision of Christ, kept the same day. In folk custom, the year\u2019s first day and its luck.',history:"Early on New Year\u2019s morning, boys go round houses with pockets full of wheat, rye or oats, throwing a handful into each home with a sowing song, such as \u201cSiiu, viiu, posivaiu, z Novym rokom vitaiu\u201d (\u201cI sow, I scatter, I seed, I greet you with the New Year\u201d). The first visitor to the house should be a boy or man, for luck, and the grain is swept up and kept for the spring sowing.\n\n\u201cOld New Year\u201d is also widely kept, more lightly, across the former Soviet world, Serbia and North Macedonia as a second New Year celebration, a relic of the Julian calendar in daily life.",knowAndDont:{known:'The custom of sowing and the songs are well recorded and still practised.',unknown:'Whether the sowing goes back before Christianity is not known.'},celebrate:['Throw a few grains of wheat or oats across your threshold for a fruitful year, and keep them for the garden.','Make your first visit of the day a cheerful one.','Read about St Basil, who founded hospitals and fed the poor, and give to a food bank in his name.','Make vasylopita or a simple bread to share.'],ritual:{title:'Sowing the year',outline:['In the morning, take a handful of grain.','Walk through the house, scattering a little in each room with a wish for the year.','Sweep it up and keep it in a jar until spring, then sow it or give it to the birds.','Give something to someone in need, as St Basil did.']},correspondences:{herbs:['Oak'],colors:['Gold','Green'],foods:['Wheat','Bread','Vasylopita'],symbols:['Grain scattered on the floor','The first visitor']},respect:'St Basil\u2019s Day is a Christian feast and the posivannia custom belongs to Ukrainian, Belarusian and Rusyn families. Learn it, enjoy it, and keep it with its meaning, rather than turning it into a prosperity spell divorced from the people who keep it.',sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_New_Year','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basil_of_Caesarea','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christmas_in_Ukraine']},{ref:'slavic-vodokhreshche',title:'Vodokhreshche / Jordan (Theophany)',aka:['Yordan','Theophany','Epiphany (Old Style)','Kreshchenie','Blessing of the Waters'],tradition:'Slavic (Orthodox and Greek Catholic folk year)',summary:'The Baptism of Christ by the Old Style (19 January): the Great Blessing of Waters at rivers and springs, crosses cut in the ice, and blessed water carried home for the whole year. The end of the winter feasts.',honours:'The Baptism of Christ in the Jordan and the revelation of the Trinity (theophany, \u201cGod shown\u201d), and through it the blessing of all water.',history:"On the eve, the third holy supper is kept: \u201chungry kutia\u201d, meatless again, and the house is marked with chalk crosses or sprinkled. On the day, the clergy and people process to a river, lake or spring, where a cross (often carved from ice, called the Yordan) has been set, and the priest plunges a cross into the water three times. Doves are released in some places; the brave plunge into the icy water.\n\nPeople carry the blessed water home and keep it all year: for sprinkling the house and stable, for the sick, for blessing seed. In some villages young men fired guns at the moment of blessing, and girls watched the weather and the water for omens.",knowAndDont:{known:'The service of the Great Blessing of Waters is ancient in the Eastern churches; the folk customs are well recorded.',unknown:'Older water customs may lie beneath some of the folk practice, but that is speculation.'},celebrate:['Go to a parish water blessing if there is one near you; you may be welcome to take some water home.','Bless the water of your home simply: clean the taps, fill a jug and give thanks for clean water.','Give to a charity that brings clean water to people.','Do not plunge into icy water without experience and someone with you: cold shock can kill.'],ritual:{title:'Thanks for water',outline:['Fill a clean bowl or jug with fresh water.','Hold it and give thanks in your own words for the water that keeps your household alive.','Use it to water your plants or wipe your door and windows.','Make a donation to a clean-water charity.']},correspondences:{herbs:['Hyssop','Willow'],crystals:['Aquamarine','Clear Quartz'],colors:['White','Blue','Silver'],foods:['Hungry kutia (on the eve)','Uzvar'],symbols:['The ice cross','The dove','Flowing water']},respect:'The Great Blessing of Waters is a sacred rite of the Orthodox and Greek Catholic churches. Visitors are often welcome to watch and even to take water, but ask first, follow the clergy and community, and treat blessed water with reverence. Please don\u2019t take it for spells.',sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Blessing_of_Waters','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Epiphany_(holiday)','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christmas_in_Ukraine']},{ref:'slavic-stritennia',title:'Stritennia (the Meeting)',aka:['Sretenie','Candlemas (Old Style)','The Presentation of the Lord','Hromnychna'],tradition:'Slavic (Orthodox and Greek Catholic folk year)',summary:'The Meeting of the Lord by the Old Style (15 February): candles are blessed in church and kept against storms and illness, and folk say winter meets summer today.',honours:'The meeting of the infant Christ with the elder Simeon and the prophetess Anna in the Temple, forty days after his birth; Simeon\u2019s hymn hails him as \u201ca light to lighten the Gentiles\u201d.',history:"Stritennia means \u201cmeeting\u201d, and in folk speech it is also the meeting of winter and summer: \u201cNa Stritennia zyma z litom zustrilasia\u201d. If it was cold, winter would linger; if there was a thaw, spring would come early. The weather was watched as in the West on Candlemas.\n\nCandles blessed in church today, the hromnychni svichky (\u201cthunder candles\u201d), were taken home and lit when storms came, when someone was ill, and placed in the hand of the dying. In Poland the Catholic feast of the Mother of God of the Thunder Candle (Matka Boska Gromniczna) on 2 February has the same custom.",knowAndDont:{known:'The feast, the candle blessing and the weather lore are well recorded.',unknown:'The folk saying about winter and summer is a seasonal custom; any older pre-Christian layer is speculation.'},celebrate:['Bless (or simply set aside) a candle for the year, to light in storms or sorrow.','Watch the weather and see what it says about spring.','Visit someone elderly, as the infant Christ was met by Simeon and Anna.','Go to the service of the feast if a parish is near.'],ritual:{premade:'folk-candlemas-candles',title:'Blessing the year\u2019s candles'},correspondences:{herbs:['Frankincense'],crystals:['Clear Quartz'],colors:['White','Gold'],foods:['Pancakes','Bread'],symbols:['The blessed candle','Two doves','The meeting of winter and spring']},respect:'This is a Christian feast. If you are not Orthodox or Greek Catholic, learn its meaning and be respectful in church; a parish may let you bring a candle to be blessed if you ask. The candle-blessing working offered here is the Western Candlemas one, kept by Christian witches and others; it isn\u2019t the Orthodox rite itself.',sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Presentation_of_Jesus','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Candlemas','O. Voropai, Zvychai nashoho narodu (Munich, 1958)']},{ref:'slavic-yuri',title:'Yuriy\u2019s Day (St George, spring)',aka:['Yuriia','Yurii Veshnii','\u0110ur\u0111evdan','Gergyovden','St George\u2019s Day'],tradition:'Slavic (Orthodox and Greek Catholic folk year)',summary:'St George\u2019s Day by the Old Style (6 May): cattle were driven to pasture for the first time through the dew, the fields were walked and blessed, and people rolled in the dew for health.',honours:'St George the Great Martyr, the soldier saint who slays the dragon, protector of herds and herdsmen. In folk sayings \u201cYuriy unlocks the earth\u201d and sends the grass up.',history:"On Yuriy\u2019s Day herders drove the cattle out to the spring pasture for the first time, often touching them with a willow branch blessed on Palm Sunday, and laying an egg or an axe at the gate for them to step over. Young people rolled in the morning dew, \u201cYuriy\u2019s dew\u201d, for health and beauty, and the fields of new grain were walked in procession with the priest and blessed. In Serbia (\u0110ur\u0111evdan) and Bulgaria (Gergyovden) the same day is one of the great feasts of the year, with lamb, green branches and flowers.\n\nSome scholars have connected these customs with Jarilo or other spring powers, but those links are reconstruction; the feast as recorded is George\u2019s.",knowAndDont:{known:'The herding and dew customs are richly recorded by ethnographers across the Slavic and Balkan world.',unknown:'Any pre-Christian deity behind the customs is guesswork.'},celebrate:['Get up early and walk through dewy grass, or wash your face in the dew.','Walk the edges of your garden or a field, and bless what grows.','Pick fresh green branches for the house.','If you keep animals, give them a special treat or a walk somewhere new.'],ritual:{premade:'folk-bealtaine-dew',title:'Washing in the morning dew'},correspondences:{herbs:['Willow','Birch','Nettle'],crystals:['Green Aventurine','Bloodstone'],colors:['Green','Red'],foods:['Lamb','Eggs','Fresh greens'],symbols:['The dragon-slayer','Morning dew','Green branches','Cattle at pasture']},respect:'This is a Christian saint\u2019s day, kept by Orthodox, Greek Catholic and other Christian communities, with herding customs that belong to village life. The dew-washing working linked here is the Irish May Day custom, a near cousin; it isn\u2019t the Slavic rite.',sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_George%27s_Day','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C4%90ur%C4%91evdan','S. A. Tokarev (ed.), Kalendarnye obychai i obriady v stranakh zarubezhnoi Evropy (Moscow, 1977\u201383)']},{ref:'slavic-kupala-old-style',title:'Ivan Kupala (Old Style)',aka:['Kupala Night','Ivana Kupala','Kupalle','Noc Kupa\u0142y','St John\u2019s Eve (Old Style)'],tradition:'Slavic (Orthodox and Greek Catholic folk year)',summary:'Midsummer night by the Old Style (the night of 6\u20137 July), the eve and feast of the birth of John the Baptist: bonfires, wreaths floated on the water, herbs gathered, and the search for the fern flower.',honours:'John the Baptist, whose birth the church keeps on this day; and, in folk custom, fire, water, herbs and young love at the height of summer.',history:"On Kupala night young people gathered by the river. Girls wove wreaths of wildflowers and herbs and set them floating on the water with candles: where the wreath went, or whether it sank, foretold love and marriage. Couples jumped over bonfires holding hands. Herbs gathered that night or at dawn were held especially powerful, and the dew that morning especially healing. Daring ones went into the forest to look for the fern flower, which was said to bloom for a single moment at midnight and grant luck and the finding of treasure.\n\nThe church condemned the bonfires and games for centuries, which is partly how we know of them: a 1505 letter of the Pskov Eleazar Monastery describes them. Chroniclers sometimes wrote of \u201cKupala\u201d as a pagan god, but it is more likely the name of the feast itself, from the word for bathing (kupaty), as John baptised in water. Kupala is the best-loved of the Slavic midsummer customs, kept in Ukraine, Belarus, Poland, Russia and beyond. The app also shows Kupala Night on the New Style date (23\u201324 June); both are kept, by different communities.",knowAndDont:{known:'The customs are recorded from the 16th century onward and richly by 19th-century ethnographers; they are still kept.',unknown:'Whether Kupala was ever a deity (probably not), and how much of the festival is older than Christianity. Rodnover festivals of Kupala are modern reconstructions.'},celebrate:['Weave a wreath of wildflowers and herbs, and float it on a river or pond (with no candle or wire, so it harms nothing).','Light a fire only where it is safe and permitted, and keep water to hand; jumping over flames is not for small children or long skirts.','Gather herbs at dawn with care and thanks, and dry them for the year.','Stay up for the short night with friends.'],ritual:{premade:'folk-kupala-wreath'},correspondences:{herbs:['St John\u2019s Wort','Mugwort','Yarrow','Wormwood','Birch'],crystals:['Sunstone','Carnelian'],colors:['Red','Gold','Green'],foods:['Varenyky','Bread','Kvass','Early berries'],symbols:['The fern flower','Floating wreaths','The bonfire','The river']},respect:'Kupala is a folk festival of Slavic villages, kept within the Christian calendar on the feast of St John and now also by Rodnovers and many others. Take part with an eye to the people whose custom it is: learn its songs and meaning, and don\u2019t claim your version is the \u201coriginal pagan\u201d one.',sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kupala_Night','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nativity_of_John_the_Baptist','W. F. Ryan, The Bathhouse at Midnight: Magic in Russia (Stroud, 1999)']},{ref:'slavic-illia',title:'Illia\u2019s Day (Elijah the Thunderer)',aka:['Illin Den','Ilyin Den','Ilinden','St Elijah\u2019s Day'],tradition:'Slavic (Orthodox and Greek Catholic folk year)',summary:'The feast of the prophet Elijah by the Old Style (2 August), who rides the thunder in his fiery chariot: the end of high summer, the harvest under way, and no more swimming after today.',honours:'The prophet Elijah (Illia), who called down fire from heaven and was taken up in a chariot of fire; in folk belief, the master of thunder, rain and lightning, who strikes at devils with his bolts.',history:"Elijah was the saint of storms all over the Orthodox Slavic world. On his day people were careful not to work in the fields lest he strike them; they blessed the first loaf of new grain, and they said the water grew cold after Illia, because the saint, or a stag, had dipped into it, so no more swimming. In Macedonia, Ilinden is also remembered for the uprising of 1903.\n\nMany scholars, from 19th-century folklorists to Ivanov and Toporov in the 1970s, have argued that the thunder-riding Illia inherited the role of the old thunder god Perun. That is a reasonable idea, but it is argued over: Elijah\u2019s fiery chariot and his power over rain come straight from the Bible, and the evidence for Perun\u2019s own cult is thin. The feast itself is Christian.",knowAndDont:{known:'The feast and its storm and harvest customs are well recorded.',unknown:'The link with Perun is a scholarly reconstruction, debated, and cannot be proven.'},celebrate:['Watch a summer storm from somewhere safe, and listen.','Bake or buy a loaf of new-season bread and give thanks for the harvest.','Take your last swim of the summer, in safe water.','Read the story of Elijah on Mount Carmel (1 Kings 18).'],ritual:{title:'Thanks for the first bread',outline:['Buy or bake a loaf from the new harvest.','Before you cut it, give thanks for the rain and sun that grew it.','Share the first slices with others, and leave a little crumb for the birds.','If a storm comes, sit and watch it safely indoors.']},correspondences:{deities:['Perun'],herbs:['Oak','Nettle'],crystals:['Hematite','Garnet'],colors:['Red','Gold'],foods:['New bread','Honey'],symbols:['The fiery chariot','Thunder and lightning','The first sheaf']},respect:'Illia\u2019s Day is a Christian feast. Rodnovers and others honour Perun at this season in their own reconstructed ways, which is their right; but please don\u2019t present the saint\u2019s feast as a disguised Perun festival, as if the people who keep it didn\u2019t know their own faith.',sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elijah','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Perun','V. V. Ivanov and V. N. Toporov, Issledovaniia v oblasti slavianskikh drevnostei (Moscow, 1974)']},{ref:'slavic-makovei',title:'Makovei (Honey Saviour)',aka:['Medovyi Spas','Makoviia','Feast of the Maccabees','First Saviour'],tradition:'Slavic (Orthodox and Greek Catholic folk year)',summary:'The first of the three Saviours\u2019 feasts by the Old Style (14 August): poppy heads, honey and bouquets of herbs and flowers are blessed in church, and wells and water are blessed.',honours:'The seven Maccabee martyrs and their mother (2 Maccabees 7), and the procession of the wood of the Holy Cross; and with them Christ the Saviour, whose feasts the three Spasy are.',history:"In August come three \u201cSaviour\u201d feasts: Makovei or Honey Saviour on the 1st, Apple Saviour (the Transfiguration) on the 6th and Nut or Bread Saviour on the 16th, all Old Style. Makovei also opens the two-week Dormition fast.\n\nThe name comes from the Maccabees (Makave\xef), and folk speech heard mak, \u201cpoppy\u201d, in it: the feast became the day for blessing poppies. Women make bouquets (makoviiky) of poppy heads, mint, marigolds, wormwood, cornflowers and other herbs, have them blessed in church, and keep them behind the icons through the year. Honey from the new harvest is blessed and tasted for the first time; poppy-seed cakes (makivnyky) and pancakes with honey are made; and wells are cleaned and blessed, the water carried in procession.",knowAndDont:{known:'The church feasts and the poppy, honey and water customs are well recorded.',unknown:'The poppy custom is a folk etymology made real; any older layer behind it is not known.'},celebrate:['Make a little bouquet of poppy heads, mint and garden flowers, and hang it to dry in your home.','Taste new-season honey and give thanks for the bees.','Bake something with poppy seed.','Clean a water source: a bird bath, a water butt, the kettle.'],ritual:{title:'A Makovei bouquet',outline:['Gather poppy heads (dried, from a florist or garden), mint, marigold and other herbs.','Tie them into a bouquet with a ribbon.','Give thanks over them for the summer\u2019s plenty, and for honey and water.','Hang the bouquet in your home until next year, then return it to the earth.']},correspondences:{herbs:['Mint','Calendula','Wormwood','Yarrow'],crystals:['Citrine','Amber'],colors:['Red','Gold','Blue'],foods:['Honey','Poppy-seed cakes (makivnyky)','Pancakes with honey'],symbols:['Poppy heads','The honeycomb','The well']},respect:'Makovei is a Christian feast with village customs that belong to Ukrainian, Belarusian and Rusyn families. You\u2019re welcome to make a bouquet and enjoy the honey, but please don\u2019t present the blessed makoviiky as an ingredient in spells.',sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honey_Feast_of_the_Saviour','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Holy_Maccabees','O. Voropai, Zvychai nashoho narodu (Munich, 1958)']},{ref:'slavic-apple-spas',title:'Yablichnyi Spas (Apple Saviour)',aka:['Apple Spas','Yablochny Spas','Transfiguration (Old Style)','Second Saviour'],tradition:'Slavic (Orthodox and Greek Catholic folk year)',summary:'The Transfiguration by the Old Style (19 August): the first apples, pears, plums and grapes are brought to church to be blessed before they are eaten.',honours:'The Transfiguration of Christ on Mount Tabor, when he shone with light before Peter, James and John; and the fruit of the year, offered in thanks.',history:"The Byzantine church has long blessed the first fruits on the Transfiguration, originally grapes; in the Slavic north, where grapes were few, apples took their place. Baskets of fruit are carried to church, blessed, and then shared, with the poor and with neighbours. Many people wait until this day to eat the new season\u2019s apples.\n\nThere is a tender folk belief behind this, especially among mothers who have lost a child: that in heaven, children are given apples only if their mothers on earth have waited until the Saviour. Weather was watched too: as the Apple Saviour goes, so goes January.",knowAndDont:{known:'The feast and the fruit blessing are long established and still kept.',unknown:'Nothing particular: it is a Christian first-fruits feast, with local variation.'},celebrate:['Eat your first apple of the season today, and give thanks.','Take a basket of fruit to a neighbour, a shelter or a food bank.','Bake an apple cake (sharlotka) and share it.','Climb a hill or watch the sunrise: the feast is about light on a mountain.'],ritual:{title:'First fruits',outline:['Gather a small basket of new apples or other fruit.','Set it on the table with a candle, and give thanks for the harvest.','Share the first fruit with the people around you.','Give some away to someone who needs it.']},correspondences:{herbs:['Mint','Rose'],crystals:['Clear Quartz','Peridot'],colors:['White','Gold','Green'],foods:['Apples','Pears','Plums','Grapes','Apple cake'],symbols:['Mount Tabor','Light','The basket of fruit']},respect:'The Transfiguration is a major Christian feast. Visitors who bring fruit to be blessed are often welcomed, but ask first and follow the parish\u2019s custom; the blessed fruit is for eating and sharing, not for spellwork.',sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Feast_of_the_Transfiguration','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_Feast_of_the_Saviour']},{ref:'slavic-pokrova',title:'Pokrova (the Protecting Veil)',aka:['Pokrov','Feast of the Intercession','Protection of the Theotokos'],tradition:'Slavic (Orthodox and Greek Catholic folk year)',summary:'The feast of the Protecting Veil of the Mother of God by the Old Style (14 October), at the turn to winter: the start of the wedding season, when girls asked Pokrova for a husband.',honours:'The Mother of God (Theotokos), who in a vision seen by St Andrew the Fool-for-Christ in the Blachernae church at Constantinople in the 10th century spread her veil over the praying people to protect them.',history:"The feast hardly mattered in Greek lands, but became one of the great feasts of the East Slavs from the 12th century. It falls as the harvest is in and the first frosts come, so it marked the end of field work and the beginning of the season for weddings. Girls prayed: \u201cHoly Pokrova, cover the earth with snow, and me, young one, with a kerchief\u201d, the kerchief being the married woman\u2019s headscarf.\n\nThe Zaporozhian Cossacks took Pokrova as their patron feast, and in Ukraine it became a day of the defenders of the homeland; since 2023, with the church\u2019s change of calendar, both are kept on 1 October.",knowAndDont:{known:'The feast, its Byzantine origin and its Slavic folk customs are well recorded.',unknown:'Nothing especially uncertain; the folk sayings vary by region.'},celebrate:['Cover something you love: wrap a shawl around your shoulders, or put a warm cover on a bed for winter.','Pray, or simply ask, for protection over those you love.','Finish putting the garden to bed.','Remember those who defend others, and give to a charity that supports them.'],ritual:{title:'Under a protecting cover',outline:['Take a shawl, scarf or blanket.','Light a candle and say who you ask protection for this winter.','Wrap the cloth around yourself or lay it over a loved one\u2019s bed.','Keep it as a winter blanket, a reminder of shelter.']},correspondences:{herbs:['Rowan','Oak'],crystals:['Blue Lace Agate','Moonstone'],colors:['Blue','White'],foods:['Pancakes','Apples','Pumpkin'],symbols:['The veil','The first snow','The wedding kerchief']},respect:'Pokrova is a feast of the Mother of God, dear to Orthodox and Greek Catholic Ukrainians, Belarusians and Russians. Please honour it as a Christian feast, and don\u2019t recast the Theotokos as a generic \u201cmother goddess\u201d in workings.',sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intercession_of_the_Theotokos','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andrew_the_Fool','O. Voropai, Zvychai nashoho narodu (Munich, 1958)']},{ref:'slavic-andrii',title:'Andrii\u2019s Eve (St Andrew\u2019s Night)',aka:['Andriivski vechornytsi','Andrzejki','Kalyta','St Andrew\u2019s Eve'],tradition:'Slavic (Orthodox and Greek Catholic folk year)',summary:'The eve of St Andrew by the Old Style (the night of 12\u201313 December), the great night of girls\u2019 fortune-telling about love and marriage, and the game of biting the kalyta loaf.',honours:'St Andrew the First-Called, the apostle who, by legend, preached on the hills of Kyiv; and, in folk custom, the hope of love and a good match.',history:"On Andrii\u2019s Eve girls gathered for evening parties (vechornytsi) to tell their fortunes: pouring melted wax into water and reading the shapes; counting fence posts in the dark; listening at windows for a name; putting out dumplings for a dog to see whose it ate first, to tell who would marry first; asking the name of the first man they met in the street.\n\nThe boys had the kalyta: a large round honey cake hung from the ceiling, guarded by a comic \u201cPan Kalytynskyi\u201d with a brush dipped in soot. Each boy rode up on a poker as a horse and tried to bite the kalyta without laughing; if he laughed, his face was blackened. In Poland, Andrzejki on 29\u201330 November New Style is now a merry evening of wax-pouring and party games.",knowAndDont:{known:'The divination customs and the kalyta are richly recorded and still played.',unknown:'Why St Andrew became the patron of love divination is not clear; the customs are folk practice beside the church feast, not part of it.'},celebrate:['Hold a party with friends and play the old games, for fun.','Pour melted wax into cold water and read the shapes together.','Bake a round honey cake and hang it for a game of kalyta.','Keep it light: these were games of hope, played in company.'],ritual:{title:'Wax pouring',outline:['Melt a few candle stubs in an old metal ladle or a tin set in a pan of hot water (never over an open flame unattended).','Fill a bowl with cold water.','Each person asks a question in their mind, then pours a little wax into the water.','Lift out the shape, hold it to the candlelight, and read its shadow on the wall together.','Laugh, eat honey cake, and keep the shapes you like.']},correspondences:{herbs:['Rose','Mint'],crystals:['Rose Quartz','Moonstone'],colors:['Red','White'],foods:['Kalyta (round honey cake)','Varenyky','Pampushky'],symbols:['Poured wax','The kalyta','The X-shaped cross of St Andrew']},respect:'Andrii\u2019s Eve games are folk custom kept by Ukrainian, Polish and other Slavic communities, Christian in setting and playful in spirit. Enjoy them as such, rather than as serious love spells.',sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St._Andrew%27s_Day','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andrzejki','O. Voropai, Zvychai nashoho narodu (Munich, 1958)']},{ref:'slavic-mykolai',title:'St Mykolai\u2019s Day',aka:['Sviatyi Mykolai','St Nicholas Day (Old Style)','Nikola Zimnii','Miko\u0142ajki'],tradition:'Slavic (Orthodox and Greek Catholic folk year)',summary:'St Nicholas by the Old Style (19 December), the protector of children, travellers and the poor: children find gifts under their pillows in the morning.',honours:'St Nicholas of Myra, the 4th-century bishop who by legend secretly gave dowries to three poor girls; patron of children, sailors, travellers and the poor, the most loved saint of the Eastern Slavs.',history:"On the night before, children put their letters to Mykolai on the windowsill and find small gifts under their pillows in the morning, or a twig (rizka) if they have been naughty. In Ukraine, Mykolai rather than Father Frost was the gift-giver of tradition, and his day opened the winter festival season. Across the Slavic world many churches are dedicated to him, and he is called on by travellers and anyone in trouble.\n\nCatholics keep his day on 6 December New Style, as do Ukrainians since 2023.",knowAndDont:{known:'The feast and the gift custom are well established.',unknown:'Most St Nicholas stories are legends written down centuries after his life.'},celebrate:['Leave a small gift for a child, or secretly for anyone, as Nicholas did.','Give to a children\u2019s charity or a shelter.','Bake mykolaichyky, spiced honey biscuits.','Pray, or wish, safe travel for someone on the road.'],ritual:{title:'A secret gift',outline:['Choose someone who could use a kindness.','Prepare a small gift or note, and leave it where they\u2019ll find it, without your name.','Light a candle for children and travellers.','Give to a charity that helps children.']},correspondences:{herbs:['Cinnamon','Clove','Star Anise'],crystals:['Aquamarine'],colors:['Red','White','Gold'],foods:['Mykolaichyky (spiced honey biscuits)','Oranges','Gingerbread'],symbols:['Three gold coins','The ship','The bishop\u2019s staff']},respect:'St Nicholas\u2019s Day is a Christian feast, much loved by Orthodox, Greek Catholic and Catholic families. Join in the giving: that is its whole spirit.',sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Nicholas_Day','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Nicholas']},{ref:'slavic-pascha',title:'Velykden (Orthodox Easter)',aka:['Pascha','Velykden','Paskha','Vialikdzie\u0144','Orthodox Easter'],tradition:'Slavic (Orthodox and Greek Catholic folk year)',summary:'Pascha, the Resurrection, the \u201cGreat Day\u201d: baskets of paska bread, pysanky and krashanky eggs, butter, salt and meats are blessed at dawn, and the greeting \u201cKhrystos voskres!\u201d rings out.',honours:'Christ risen from the dead, the heart of the Christian faith; and with him the dead, remembered in the days after.',history:"After the seven weeks of Great Lent and Holy Week, the Paschal service begins at midnight with a procession around the church by candlelight and the proclamation \u201cChrist is risen!\u201d \u2014 \u201cIndeed he is risen!\u201d (Khrystos voskres! \u2013 Voistynu voskres!). At dawn the faithful stand outside with their baskets (koshyky), covered with embroidered cloths, holding paska (tall sweet bread, decorated with dough crosses and braids), eggs, butter, cheese, sausage, horseradish and salt, and the priest sprinkles them with holy water. The family\u2019s Easter breakfast begins with a shared blessed egg.\n\nPysanky, eggs decorated in wax-resist with ancient symbols (suns, stars, wheat, the endless line), are among Ukraine\u2019s best-known folk arts; krashanky are eggs dyed a single colour. In western Ukraine young people dance the spring circle dances (hahilky) around the church. On Pascha and the days after, people greet one another with Christ is risen, and Easter eggs are rolled and exchanged.\n\nPascha is reckoned by the Julian computus, so it usually falls later than Western Easter, and sometimes on the same day.",knowAndDont:{known:'The service, the basket blessing and the egg arts are richly recorded and very much alive.',unknown:'Pysanka symbols are often said to be pre-Christian; some motifs may be old, but their meanings are mostly modern interpretations.'},celebrate:['Learn to write a pysanka (wax-resist egg); many Ukrainian centres hold workshops.','Bake paska, or buy one from a Ukrainian bakery or church sale.','Greet friends who keep Pascha with \u201cKhrystos voskres!\u201d \u2014 they will answer \u201cVoistynu voskres!\u201d','If you\u2019re invited, go to the midnight service: it is one of the most beautiful in the world.','Give generously: the feast is meant to be shared.'],ritual:{title:'An Easter basket',outline:['Dye or decorate eggs (krashanky are simple: boiled eggs with onion skins give a deep red).','Bake or buy a sweet bread.','Arrange bread, eggs, butter and salt in a basket under an embroidered or white cloth.','If you are Orthodox or a parish welcomes you, take it to be blessed; otherwise, share it at breakfast with thanks.']},correspondences:{herbs:['Willow','Birch'],colors:['Red','White','Gold','Black'],foods:['Paska','Pysanky and krashanky','Butter','Salt','Horseradish','Kovbasa'],symbols:['The pysanka','The paschal candle','The basket under an embroidered cloth']},respect:'Pascha is the holiest day of the Orthodox and Greek Catholic year. Pysanky are a living Ukrainian art with deep meaning to the people who make them: buy from Ukrainian makers, learn from Ukrainian teachers, and don\u2019t present them as \u201cpagan charms\u201d stripped of their Easter context. Visitors are welcome at most Paschal services; stand respectfully, follow the congregation, and don\u2019t take communion unless you are Orthodox or have asked.',sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pysanka','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paskha','J. Meeus, Astronomical Algorithms, ch. 8 (the Julian computus)']},{ref:'slavic-radunytsia',title:'Radunytsia (Remembrance of the Dead)',aka:['Radonitsa','Radaunica','Provody','Hrobki','Day of Rejoicing'],tradition:'Slavic (Orthodox and Greek Catholic folk year)',summary:'The Tuesday after Thomas Sunday, nine days after Pascha, when families go to the graves of their dead with eggs and bread, eat with them, and share the Easter joy.',honours:'The family dead, greeted with \u201cChrist is risen!\u201d so they share in the Resurrection; the church calls it a day of rejoicing.',history:"On Radunytsia families tidy the graves, spread a cloth over them or beside them, and bring Easter food: red eggs, paska, sausage, sometimes a drink. A priest serves a memorial (panakhyda) at the cemetery, and an egg or a piece of bread is left on the grave, or crumbled for the birds. In Belarus and Russia it is a public holiday; in Ukraine the same visits (Provody, \u201cseeing off\u201d) are often made on Thomas Sunday or Monday.\n\nThe name is usually explained from radovatysia, \u201cto rejoice\u201d, because it is a day of Easter joy with the dead; some scholars connect it instead with older words for the dead. Ethnographers have long seen in it one of the oldest layers of Slavic custom, feeding and visiting the dead in spring, gathered into the Christian Easter season. That is likely, though not provable.",knowAndDont:{known:'The day, its customs and its place in the Easter season are well recorded and kept.',unknown:'The origin of the name and how old the grave meal is are uncertain.'},celebrate:['Visit and tidy a family grave; bring flowers and a red egg.','If your dead are far away, set their photographs out with a candle and an egg.','Tell their stories over a meal.','Feed the birds with crumbs in their memory.'],ritual:{premade:'hedge-ancestor-candle-evening',title:'Remembering your own dead'},correspondences:{herbs:['Birch','Willow','Rosemary'],colors:['Red','White'],foods:['Red eggs','Paska','Kutia','Bread'],symbols:['The red egg on the grave','The embroidered towel (rushnyk)','Birds']},respect:'Radunytsia is an Orthodox and Greek Catholic remembrance, kept by families at the graves of their own dead. If you are not part of this tradition, honour your own ancestors in your own way; the ancestor evening linked here is a general remembrance, not the Orthodox rite. Don\u2019t use cemeteries for workings.',sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Radonitsa','S. A. Tokarev (ed.), Kalendarnye obychai i obriady v stranakh zarubezhnoi Evropy (Moscow, 1977\u201383)']},{ref:'slavic-zeleni-sviata',title:'Zeleni Sviata (Green Holidays, Trinity)',aka:['Triitsia','Troitsa','Klechalnaia Nedelia','Rusalii','Green Week','Orthodox Pentecost'],tradition:'Slavic (Orthodox and Greek Catholic folk year)',summary:'Pentecost by the Orthodox reckoning, called Trinity: houses and churches are decked with green branches, herbs and sweet flag. The week around it is Rusalii, when the rusalky were said to walk.',honours:'The Holy Spirit and the Holy Trinity; and in folk custom the green life of early summer, and the dead, remembered on the Saturday before.',history:"For Trinity, homes and churches are filled with green: branches of linden, maple, birch and oak stuck in the gates and walls, and herbs and sweet flag (calamus) strewn on the floor, with lovage and wormwood among them. People come to church holding bunches of herbs and flowers, which are then dried and kept for healing. The Saturday before is a day for the dead (Batkivska subota), and graves are tidied and decorated with green.\n\nThe week around Pentecost is Rusalii or Rusalnyi tyzhden. In folk belief the rusalky, spirits of young women who had died unmarried or by drowning, came out of the water into the fields and forests, swinging in birch branches and luring young men; people carried wormwood for protection and avoided swimming. In Russia the Thursday before (Semik) had girls curling birch branches and floating wreaths. The name Rusalii comes from the Latin Rosalia, a Roman spring feast of roses for the dead, which reached the Slavs through Byzantium.",knowAndDont:{known:'The green decoration, the herbs, the day of the dead and the rusalka beliefs are well recorded by ethnographers.',unknown:'How the rusalka beliefs grew, and whether they reflect pre-Christian spirits of water and field, is debated; the word itself is borrowed.'},celebrate:['Bring green branches and herbs into your home, and strew a few fragrant herbs on the floor.','Gather a bunch of herbs and flowers and dry them for the year.','Visit and green a family grave on the Saturday before.','Learn the rusalka stories (and swim only in safe water).'],ritual:{title:'Greening the house',outline:['Gather a few green branches (fallen or pruned, not torn from trees) and fragrant herbs.','Put branches by the door and in a vase on the table.','Strew some fresh herbs on the floor or a windowsill.','Give thanks for the green season, and hang a bundle to dry for the year.']},correspondences:{herbs:['Birch','Oak','Wormwood','Mint','Lemon Balm'],crystals:['Moss Agate','Green Aventurine','Jade'],colors:['Green','White'],foods:['Eggs','Green borshch (with sorrel)','Bread'],symbols:['Green branches','Sweet flag','The birch','Rusalky']},respect:'Trinity is a Christian feast, and the greenery and the day of the dead belong to its parish and family customs. Rusalka lore is beloved folklore; enjoy it, but please don\u2019t present the Christian feast as a disguised pagan festival.',sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rusalii','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rusalka','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pentecost','W. F. Ryan, The Bathhouse at Midnight: Magic in Russia (Stroud, 1999)']},{ref:'coligny-samonios',title:'Samonios begins',aka:['SAMON','Samonios','First month of the Gaulish year'],tradition:'Gaulish (Coligny calendar, reconstructed)',summary:'The first month of the Gaulish year on the Coligny bronze, a \u201cgood\u201d (MAT) month of 30 days. Its name means something to do with summer; whether it opened the summer or closed it is the great question of the calendar.',honours:'The bronze names no gods for its months. The month name comes from samo-, \u201csummer\u201d, the same root as Irish Samain, Welsh Haf. Anything more is our own choice.',history:"The Coligny calendar is a bronze tablet of the late 2nd century CE, found in 1897 in some 150 pieces in a field at Coligny in eastern France, along with fragments of a bronze statue of a god. It is written in Latin letters but in the Gaulish language, and sets out five years of lunar months as a grid of days, with a hole beside each day for a peg to mark the date. It is now in the Lugdunum museum in Lyon.\n\nSamonios heads every year. On its 17th day the bronze writes TRINVX[...] SAMONI SINDIV, usually read as \u201cthree nights of Samonios today\u201d, which has led many people to link Samonios with the Irish Samain at the start of November. But the name means \u201csummer\u201d, and several scholars (McKay, Bonsing, Le Contel and Verdier) argue the month began near the start of summer, in May or June, and the winter month Giamonios six months later. The app follows the late-spring reading by default and lets you choose.\n\nSo: a new year, its date uncertain. Modern Gaulish polytheists (Senobessus Gallicos and others) keep it according to their own readings.",knowAndDont:{known:'The month\u2019s name, its place at the head of the year, its 30 days and MAT marking, and the TRINVX inscription on day 17, all on the bronze.',unknown:'When in the solar year it fell, when in the moon\u2019s cycle the month began, and what, if anything, was done in it. No text describes Gaulish new-year customs.'},celebrate:['Look at the moon tonight and note its phase: Gaulish months were lunar.','Read about the Coligny bronze, and look at photographs of it: a beautiful, puzzling object.','Begin a calendar journal for the Gaulish year, noting the weather and what grows.','If you keep a Gaulish practice, honour the gods of your hearth in your own way.'],ritual:{title:'Opening a Gaulish year',outline:['At dusk, step outside and find the moon if it is up.','Light a candle or lamp safely on a windowsill.','Speak the twelve month names aloud, beginning with Samonios.','Write in a notebook what you hope the year will hold, and what you notice in the season now.','Keep the notebook through the year, adding a line at each month\u2019s start.']},correspondences:{colors:['Gold','Green'],symbols:['The bronze grid','The peg hole','Summer']},sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coligny_calendar','G. S. Olmsted, The Gaulish Calendar (Bonn: Habelt, 1992)','P.-M. Duval and G. Pinault, Recueil des inscriptions gauloises III: Les calendriers (Paris: CNRS, 1986)','H. McKay, \u201cThe Coligny calendar as a Metonic lunar calendar\u201d, \xc9tudes Celtiques 42 (2016): https://codecs.vanhamel.nl/McKay_(Helen)_2016_42awe','https://nouiogalatis.org/2025/10/30/when-the-year-was-born/']},{ref:'coligny-trinvx-samoni',title:'TRINVX SAMONI (Three Nights of Samonios)',aka:['Trinoxtion Samoni','Three Nights of Samonios','Trinouxtion'],tradition:'Gaulish (Coligny calendar, reconstructed)',summary:'Three nights around the 17th day of Samonios, where the bronze writes TRINVX[...] SAMONI SINDIV, usually read as \u201cthree nights of Samonios today\u201d: the only named festival on the whole calendar.',honours:'We do not know. The words name a time, not a god. Many modern people connect it with the Irish Samain and honour the dead; others, reading Samonios as early summer, keep it as a summer feast.',history:"On the bronze, against day 17 of Samonios (the second day after the month\u2019s midpoint, ATENOVX), stands a note usually read TRINVX SAMONI SINDIV, \u201cthree nights of Samonios today\u201d. It is the clearest sign anywhere on the calendar of a festival. Other marks on the bronze (such as PRINNI LAGET, PRINNI LOVDIN and IVOS) may also note observances, but their meanings are argued.\n\nBecause Samonios means \u201csummer\u201d, and because Irish Samain (1 November) was the great feast opening the dark half of the year, many have identified the three nights with Samain. That fits if Samonios fell in autumn; if, as several recent scholars argue, Samonios began near the start of summer, the three nights were a summer feast and the link with Samain is a matter of the shared name only. In this app the three nights are shown as the 16th to the 18th, around the inscription\u2019s own day.",knowAndDont:{known:'The inscription exists, on day 17 of Samonios, and probably refers to three nights.',unknown:'Everything else: what was done, whom it honoured, and when in the year it fell. Every modern observance is a reconstruction.'},celebrate:['Keep three evenings: a lamp lit each night, and a little time spent outside.','If you follow the Samain reading, honour your dead; if the summer reading, celebrate the light and the season.','Share a meal with friends on one of the nights.','Read the inscription for yourself (photographs and transcriptions are online), and think about what you don\u2019t know.'],ritual:{title:'Three nights',outline:['On each of the three evenings, light a lamp or candle in a safe place.','First night: give thanks for what the past year brought.','Second night (the bronze\u2019s own day): share food and drink with friends or family, and leave a portion outside.','Third night: name what you hope for in the year ahead.','On each night, before snuffing the light, spend a few minutes looking at the sky.']},correspondences:{herbs:['Oak','Mistletoe'],colors:['Gold','Black','White'],symbols:['Three lights','The peg in the bronze','The moon past full']},sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coligny_calendar','G. S. Olmsted, The Gaulish Calendar (Bonn: Habelt, 1992)','P.-M. Duval and G. Pinault, Recueil des inscriptions gauloises III: Les calendriers (Paris: CNRS, 1986)','H. McKay, \u201cThe Coligny calendar as a Metonic lunar calendar\u201d, \xc9tudes Celtiques 42 (2016)']},{ref:'coligny-dumannios',title:'Dumannios begins',aka:['DVMAN','Dumannios'],tradition:'Gaulish (Coligny calendar, reconstructed)',summary:'The second month of the Gaulish year on the Coligny bronze, marked ANM, \u201cnot good\u201d, with 29 days. Its name\u2019s meaning is uncertain: perhaps \u201cdark\u201d or \u201csmoky\u201d.',honours:'The bronze names no gods for its months. Dumannios has been connected with words for smoke or darkness, but the meaning is a guess.',history:"Dumannios follows Samonios. If the year began in late spring (the app\u2019s default), Dumannios falls around midsummer; if it began at Samhain, it falls in the dark of late autumn, which would fit a meaning of \u201cdark\u201d or \u201csmoky\u201d. Either way, we only know the name, its 29-day ANM marking, and the days and marks of its grid.\n\nWhat ANM (\u201cnot good\u201d) meant is itself debated: it may simply mark a 29-day month rather than an unlucky one.",knowAndDont:{known:'The name, its place, its ANM status and 29 days on the bronze.',unknown:'The meaning of the name, its season, and any customs.'},celebrate:['Note the moon\u2019s phase and the season in your Gaulish-year journal.','Notice what is dark or hidden in your life this month, and what is in full light.','Tend a hearth or a candle quietly one evening.'],ritual:{title:'Marking the month',outline:['At the month\u2019s start, look for the moon.','Light a candle and say the month\u2019s name aloud.','Write a line in your journal: the weather, what grows, what you\u2019re working on.','Snuff the candle and carry on with the ordinary day.']},correspondences:{colors:['Grey','Black'],symbols:['Smoke','The hearth']},sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coligny_calendar','G. S. Olmsted, The Gaulish Calendar (Bonn: Habelt, 1992)']},{ref:'coligny-riuros',title:'Riuros begins',aka:['RIVROS','Riuros'],tradition:'Gaulish (Coligny calendar, reconstructed)',summary:'The third month of the Gaulish year on the Coligny bronze, a \u201cgood\u201d MAT month of 30 days. The name\u2019s meaning is uncertain: perhaps \u201cfrost\u201d, or \u201cfat\u201d, as in the fatness of the year.',honours:'The bronze names no gods for its months.',history:"Riuros is the third month. The two proposed meanings pull in opposite directions, which shows how much depends on where the year began: \u201cfrost\u201d would suit a winter month (if Samonios is November), \u201cfatness\u201d a high-summer one (if Samonios is May). Neither is certain.\n\nWhat we have is the name, the MAT (\u201cgood\u201d) marking and the grid of its 30 days, with the bronze\u2019s small notes on particular days.",knowAndDont:{known:'The name, its place and its MAT status with 30 days.',unknown:'Its meaning and season, and anything that was done in it.'},celebrate:['Notice the season: is it a time of frost, or of plenty, where you are?','Record the moon and weather in your journal.','Share a good meal with someone, for the \u201cfat\u201d of the year.'],ritual:{title:'Marking the month',outline:['Look for the moon at the month\u2019s start.','Light a candle and say the month\u2019s name.','Write what the season is doing where you live.','Snuff the candle.']},correspondences:{colors:['White','Gold'],symbols:['Frost','Plenty']},sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coligny_calendar','G. S. Olmsted, The Gaulish Calendar (Bonn: Habelt, 1992)']},{ref:'coligny-anagantios',title:'Anagantios begins',aka:['ANAGAN','Anagantios'],tradition:'Gaulish (Coligny calendar, reconstructed)',summary:'The fourth month of the Gaulish year on the Coligny bronze, marked ANM with 29 days. The name may mean \u201cnot travelling\u201d: a time of staying at home.',honours:'The bronze names no gods for its months.',history:"Anagantios is often analysed as an-, \u201cnot\u201d, with a root for going or travelling: the stay-at-home month. If the Gaulish year began at Samhain, it would fall in midwinter, when travel stopped; if the year began in May, it would fall in late summer or early autumn. Both readings have their supporters.\n\nThe name, the ANM marking and the 29 days are on the bronze; the rest is interpretation.",knowAndDont:{known:'The name, its place and its ANM status.',unknown:'The meaning is a proposal; the season and customs are unknown.'},celebrate:['Stay home one evening on purpose: cook, mend, read.','Tidy and bless your house.','Record the moon and season in your journal.'],ritual:{title:'A home-staying evening',outline:['Choose an evening at the month\u2019s start to stay in.','Light a candle at your hearth or table.','Do one task that cares for the house.','Say the month\u2019s name and write a line in your journal.']},correspondences:{colors:['Brown','Red'],symbols:['The hearth','The closed door']},sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coligny_calendar','G. S. Olmsted, The Gaulish Calendar (Bonn: Habelt, 1992)']},{ref:'coligny-ogronnios',title:'Ogronnios begins',aka:['OGRON','Ogronnios'],tradition:'Gaulish (Coligny calendar, reconstructed)',summary:'The fifth month of the Gaulish year on the Coligny bronze, a MAT month of 30 days. The name may mean \u201ccold\u201d (compare Welsh oer).',honours:'The bronze names no gods for its months.',history:"Ogronnios has been compared with Welsh oer and Irish uar, \u201ccold\u201d. In the default reading it falls in early autumn, and in the Samhain reading in early spring; neither is a perfect fit for \u201ccold\u201d, and the comparison itself is not secure.\n\nAs with all the months, we have its name, its MAT marking and its grid of days.",knowAndDont:{known:'The name, its place and its MAT status.',unknown:'Its meaning, season and customs.'},celebrate:['Notice the cold, or its absence, and record it.','Warm someone: share soup, lend a blanket.','Note the moon\u2019s phase in your journal.'],ritual:{title:'Marking the month',outline:['Look for the moon at the month\u2019s start.','Light a candle and say the month\u2019s name.','Write a line about the season.','Do one warming kindness for someone.']},correspondences:{colors:['White','Blue'],symbols:['Cold','The hearth fire']},sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coligny_calendar','G. S. Olmsted, The Gaulish Calendar (Bonn: Habelt, 1992)']},{ref:'coligny-cutios',title:'Cutios begins',aka:['CVTIOS','Cutios'],tradition:'Gaulish (Coligny calendar, reconstructed)',summary:'The sixth month of the Gaulish year on the Coligny bronze, a MAT month of 30 days, the last before the year\u2019s second half. Its meaning is uncertain: perhaps \u201cwinds\u201d or \u201cinvocations\u201d.',honours:'The bronze names no gods for its months.',history:"Cutios closes the first half of the year. In the third year of the bronze\u2019s five-year cycle, an extra month (Rantaranos) is inserted after it, before Giamonios, to keep the moons in step with the sun.\n\nProposed meanings include \u201cwinds\u201d and \u201cinvocations\u201d (\u201cthe month of calling\u201d), neither secure.",knowAndDont:{known:'The name, its place at the end of the first half-year, its MAT status, and the intercalary month after it in the third year.',unknown:'Its meaning, season and customs.'},celebrate:['Take stock of the half-year that is ending.','Notice the wind, and fly a kite or hang a wind chime.','Record the moon in your journal.'],ritual:{title:'Halfway through the year',outline:['At the month\u2019s start, light a candle.','Read back over your journal for the half-year.','Write down what you want to carry into the second half.','Open a window for a minute and let the air move through the house.']},correspondences:{colors:['Grey','White'],symbols:['Wind','A calling voice']},sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coligny_calendar','G. S. Olmsted, The Gaulish Calendar (Bonn: Habelt, 1992)']},{ref:'coligny-giamonios',title:'Giamonios begins',aka:['GIAMON','Giamonios'],tradition:'Gaulish (Coligny calendar, reconstructed)',summary:'The seventh month on the Coligny bronze, opening the second half of the Gaulish year; marked ANM with 29 days. Its name comes from giamo-, \u201cwinter\u201d.',honours:'The bronze names no gods for its months. The name shares its root with Irish gem-, Welsh gaeaf, \u201cwinter\u201d.',history:"Samonios (\u201csummer\u201d) and Giamonios (\u201cwinter\u201d) divide the Gaulish year in two, six months apart. That much is clear from the names. Which way round they sat against the seasons is not: if Samonios opened the summer half in May, Giamonios opened the winter half around November, and the two names describe what was beginning; if Samonios was November, Giamonios fell in May, and the names must describe what was ending.\n\nThe app\u2019s default places Giamonios around Samhain, at the start of winter. The other readings are available in Settings.",knowAndDont:{known:'The name, its root, its place opening the second half of the year, and its ANM status.',unknown:'Its season (it depends on your reading), and any customs.'},celebrate:['Mark the turn of the year\u2019s halves: put away one season\u2019s things and bring out the next.','Light the first fire or the first candle of the winter evenings.','Record the moon in your journal.'],ritual:{title:'The turn of the half-year',outline:['At the month\u2019s start, tidy away one thing that belongs to the past season.','Light a candle and say the month\u2019s name.','Name what this half of the year is for.','Write it in your journal.']},correspondences:{colors:['Black','White'],symbols:['Winter','The year\u2019s second half']},sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coligny_calendar','H. McKay, \u201cThe Coligny calendar as a Metonic lunar calendar\u201d, \xc9tudes Celtiques 42 (2016)']},{ref:'coligny-simivisonnios',title:'Simivisonnios begins',aka:['SIMIVIS','Simivisonnios'],tradition:'Gaulish (Coligny calendar, reconstructed)',summary:'The eighth month of the Gaulish year on the Coligny bronze, a MAT month of 30 days. The name may mean \u201chalf-spring\u201d.',honours:'The bronze names no gods for its months.',history:"Simivisonnios has been analysed as simi-, \u201chalf\u201d, with a word for spring (compare Latin ver). If so, it marks a point on the way toward spring. In the default reading it falls around midwinter, halfway from autumn to spring; in the Samhain reading it falls in early summer.\n\nThe name, the MAT marking and the grid are on the bronze; the meaning is a proposal.",knowAndDont:{known:'The name, its place and its MAT status.',unknown:'The meaning, its season and any customs.'},celebrate:['Look for the first signs of spring, however small.','Sow seeds indoors.','Record the moon in your journal.'],ritual:{premade:'green-seed-blessing-before-sowing',title:'Halfway to spring'},correspondences:{colors:['Green','White'],symbols:['Buds','Lengthening days']},sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coligny_calendar','G. S. Olmsted, The Gaulish Calendar (Bonn: Habelt, 1992)']},{ref:'coligny-equos',title:'Equos begins',aka:['EQVOS','Equos'],tradition:'Gaulish (Coligny calendar, reconstructed)',summary:'The ninth month of the Gaulish year on the Coligny bronze, marked ANM, yet 30 days long in some years and fewer in others, as if it were the month that kept pace with the moon. The name may mean \u201chorse\u201d.',honours:'The bronze names no gods. The Gauls did honour a horse goddess, Epona, whose cult spread across the Roman army, but there is no evidence linking her to this month.',history:"Equos is the odd month out: although marked ANM (\u201cnot good\u201d, normally 29 days), it has 30 days in some years of the bronze. Scholars have seen in it the adjustable month that kept the calendar\u2019s count of days in line with the real moon.\n\nThe name looks like the Gaulish word for horse (compare Latin equus, Old Irish ech), though some read it otherwise. If the year began in late spring, Equos falls around January or February.",knowAndDont:{known:'The name, its place, its ANM marking and its varying length on the bronze.',unknown:'Whether the name really means \u201chorse\u201d, its season, and any customs.'},celebrate:['If you love horses, visit some or support a horse sanctuary.','Notice how the real moon and the calendar drift: compare the month\u2019s start with the moon tonight.','Record the season in your journal.'],ritual:{title:'Marking the month',outline:['Look for the moon at the month\u2019s start.','Light a candle and say the month\u2019s name.','If you honour Epona, offer her a little grain or an apple, knowing the link is your own.','Write a line in your journal.']},correspondences:{deities:['Epona'],colors:['White','Brown'],foods:['Apples','Oats'],symbols:['The horse']},sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coligny_calendar','G. S. Olmsted, The Gaulish Calendar (Bonn: Habelt, 1992)','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Epona']},{ref:'coligny-elembivios',title:'Elembivios begins',aka:['ELEMBIV','Elembivios'],tradition:'Gaulish (Coligny calendar, reconstructed)',summary:'The tenth month of the Gaulish year on the Coligny bronze, marked ANM with 29 days. The name may refer to deer, like the Greek month Elaphebolion, \u201cdeer-shooting\u201d.',honours:'The bronze names no gods. Cernunnos, the antlered Gaulish god of the Pillar of the Boatmen, is a tempting association, but there is no evidence for it.',history:"Elembivios has been compared with the Greek Elaphebolion (the Attic month of Artemis the deer-shooter) and with Celtic words for deer. That is an appealing reading but not a proven one.\n\nIf the year began in late spring, Elembivios falls in late winter; if at Samhain, around late summer.",knowAndDont:{known:'The name, its place and its ANM status.',unknown:'The meaning, season and any customs.'},celebrate:['Walk somewhere you might see deer, quietly, at dawn or dusk.','Record the moon and the season in your journal.','Learn about the antlered god on the Pillar of the Boatmen in Paris (and the limits of what we know).'],ritual:{title:'A quiet walk',outline:['At the month\u2019s start, take a walk at dusk somewhere wild or green.','Move slowly and watch for animals.','At home, light a candle and say the month\u2019s name.','Write what you saw.']},correspondences:{deities:['Cernunnos'],herbs:['Oak'],colors:['Brown','Green'],symbols:['The deer','Antlers']},sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coligny_calendar','G. S. Olmsted, The Gaulish Calendar (Bonn: Habelt, 1992)','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pillar_of_the_Boatmen']},{ref:'coligny-aedrinios',title:'Aedrinios begins',aka:['AEDRIN','Edrinios','Aedrinios'],tradition:'Gaulish (Coligny calendar, reconstructed)',summary:'The eleventh month of the Gaulish year on the Coligny bronze, a MAT month of 30 days. The name may mean \u201cfiery\u201d or \u201chot\u201d, from aidu-, fire.',honours:'The bronze names no gods for its months.',history:"Aedrinios is usually connected with aidu-, \u201cfire\u201d, \u201cheat\u201d (compare Old Irish \xe1ed, fire). In the default reading Aedrinios falls in early spring, an odd time for heat (unless it refers to fires lit then); in the Samhain reading it falls in late summer, which suits it better.\n\nAs always, the name, the marking and the grid are certain; the rest is interpretation.",knowAndDont:{known:'The name, its place and its MAT status.',unknown:'The meaning, its season, and any customs.'},celebrate:['Light a fire or candle safely and sit with it a while.','Spend time in the sun.','Record the moon and season in your journal.'],ritual:{premade:'green-fire-candle-scrying',title:'Sitting with the flame'},correspondences:{colors:['Red','Orange','Gold'],crystals:['Carnelian','Sunstone'],symbols:['Fire','Heat']},sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coligny_calendar','G. S. Olmsted, The Gaulish Calendar (Bonn: Habelt, 1992)']},{ref:'coligny-cantlos',title:'Cantlos begins',aka:['CANTLOS','Cantlos'],tradition:'Gaulish (Coligny calendar, reconstructed)',summary:'The twelfth and last month of the Gaulish year on the Coligny bronze, marked ANM with 29 days. The name may mean \u201csong\u201d, or the closing of the circle.',honours:'The bronze names no gods for its months.',history:"Cantlos closes the year. It has been compared with Welsh cathl and Old Irish c\xe9tal, \u201csong\u201d, and alternatively with cant-, \u201ccircle\u201d or \u201crim\u201d: the month that rounds the year off. In the first year of the bronze\u2019s cycle, an extra month, Quimonios, follows it before the next Samonios.\n\nThe name, its marking and its place are on the bronze; its meaning is a proposal.",knowAndDont:{known:'The name, its place at the end of the year and its ANM status.',unknown:'The meaning, season and any customs.'},celebrate:['Sing, or listen to music you love, to close the year.','Read back over your Gaulish-year journal.','Mend or finish something before the new year.'],ritual:{title:'Closing the circle',outline:['At the month\u2019s start, light a candle.','Read through your journal for the year.','Sing or hum a song you love.','Write a short note of thanks to the year, and close the notebook.']},correspondences:{colors:['Gold','Purple'],symbols:['Song','The circle']},sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coligny_calendar','G. S. Olmsted, The Gaulish Calendar (Bonn: Habelt, 1992)']},{ref:'coligny-quimonios',title:'Quimonios begins',aka:['QVIMON','Quimonios','Intercalary month before Samonios'],tradition:'Gaulish (Coligny calendar, reconstructed)',summary:'An extra (intercalary) month set before Samonios at the start of the bronze\u2019s five-year cycle, to keep the moon months in step with the sun. Its name is read from the damaged QVIMON\u2026, and its meaning is unknown.',honours:'Nothing is known. The bronze fills the intercalary months with notes that seem to name the other months, as if summing up the year.',history:"A year of twelve lunar months is about eleven days short of the solar year, so lunisolar calendars add an extra month every two or three years. The Coligny bronze puts two in each five-year cycle: one before Samonios in the first year, and another before Giamonios in the third.\n\nThe first one\u2019s heading is broken; it is read as QVIMON[IOS] or similar. Its days carry curious notes that seem to list the twelve ordinary months, which has led to suggestions that the intercalary month was a kind of summary of the year. In this reconstruction it appears when the moons and the season call for an extra month before Samonios.",knowAndDont:{known:'An intercalary month stands here on the bronze, with a damaged name.',unknown:'Its full name, its meaning, and any customs.'},celebrate:['Treat it as a between-month: a time to rest, review and prepare for the new year.','Read back over the year that has ended.','Do nothing new; tidy, mend, wait.'],ritual:{title:'A between-month',outline:['Light a candle at the month\u2019s start.','Name each of the twelve months aloud and say one thing about each from your journal.','Leave the next month\u2019s page blank until Samonios.']},correspondences:{colors:['Grey','Silver'],symbols:['The threshold','The extra moon']},sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coligny_calendar','G. S. Olmsted, The Gaulish Calendar (Bonn: Habelt, 1992)','P.-M. Duval and G. Pinault, Recueil des inscriptions gauloises III: Les calendriers (Paris: CNRS, 1986)']},{ref:'coligny-rantaranos',title:'Rantaranos begins',aka:['\u2026ANTARAN\u2026','Rantaranos','Bantaranos','Ciallos','Intercalary month before Giamonios'],tradition:'Gaulish (Coligny calendar, reconstructed)',summary:'The extra (intercalary) month set between Cutios and Giamonios in the third year of the bronze\u2019s cycle. Only \u2026ANTARAN\u2026 survives of its name; its meaning is unknown.',honours:'Nothing is known.',history:"The second intercalary month stands in the middle of the third year, between Cutios and Giamonios. Only part of its name survives, \u2026ANTARAN\u2026, with the first letter read as R, B or S; some modern calendars call it Ciallos, from a word in its heading whose meaning is also unclear.\n\nIts position is one of the few things that tells us how the calendar worked: the extra month could go mid-year, so the calendar adjusted twice per cycle. In this reconstruction it appears when Giamonios would otherwise start a month too early for its season.",knowAndDont:{known:'An intercalary month stands here on the bronze; part of its name survives.',unknown:'Its full name, meaning, and any customs.'},celebrate:['Treat it as a pause at the middle of the year.','Review the half-year and rest before the second half begins.','Note the moon and season in your journal.'],ritual:{title:'A mid-year pause',outline:['Light a candle at the month\u2019s start.','Read back over the half-year in your journal.','Choose one thing to let go of before Giamonios.','Leave a blank page for the extra month, and write in it only what you notice.']},correspondences:{colors:['Grey','Silver'],symbols:['The extra moon','The middle of the year']},sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coligny_calendar','G. S. Olmsted, The Gaulish Calendar (Bonn: Habelt, 1992)','P.-M. Duval and G. Pinault, Recueil des inscriptions gauloises III: Les calendriers (Paris: CNRS, 1986)']},{ref:'moon-new',title:'New Moon',aka:['Conjunction','Noumenia (Greek)','Amavasya (Hindu, the day before)','Rosh Chodesh (Hebrew)'],tradition:'The moon',summary:'The moment the Moon passes between the Earth and the Sun, its lit side turned away from us: the start of a new lunar month. It cannot be seen; within a day or two a thin crescent appears at dusk.',honours:'The Moon at the start of her road. Many cultures have counted their months from here, or from the first crescent just after: Greek households honoured Hekate and the household gods at the turn of the month, the Hebrew Bible keeps the new moon as a holy day, and the Chinese and Hindu (am\u0101nta) months begin with it.',history:"Astronomically, new moon is the instant of conjunction, when the Moon\u2019s longitude equals the Sun\u2019s. The Moon is then too close to the Sun\u2019s glare to be seen (except in a solar eclipse, which can only happen at new moon). In the app, the moon is called \u201cnew\u201d within about 18 hours of that instant; the days of invisibility just before are the dark moon.\n\nAncient calendars marked the month\u2019s start either at this invisible moment or at the first sighting of the crescent. Athens kept the Noumenia (\u201cnew moon\u201d) as the first day of the month, with offerings to the household gods; the Roman Kalends were originally announced by a priest when the new crescent was seen; Israel kept the new moon (Rosh Chodesh) with offerings and trumpets, still a minor holiday in Judaism. The Chinese calendar begins each month on the day of conjunction, so the Lunar New Year is a new moon.\n\nThe modern witch\u2019s new moon, for setting intentions and beginning things, is a 20th-century practice, from Wicca and the wider New Age; it rests on an old and widespread instinct that beginnings belong with the moon\u2019s return, but there is no ancient rite of \u201cnew moon intentions\u201d behind it. Folk custom, for its part, often held the moon\u2019s first days lucky for starting things and for showing the new moon a silver coin.",knowAndDont:{known:'The astronomy is exact. The Greek Noumenia, the Roman Kalends, Rosh Chodesh and the lunar months of China and India are well documented.',unknown:'There is no good scientific evidence that the new moon itself affects people\u2019s moods or luck. \u201cIntention setting\u201d at the new moon is a modern practice, not an ancient one, though no worse for that.'},celebrate:['Write down what you want to begin this month, and keep the page somewhere you\u2019ll see it.','Clean one place: a shelf, an altar, your desk.','Watch the western sky at dusk over the next two evenings for the first thin crescent.','Make a household offering, as the Greeks did on the Noumenia: a little incense, a candle, some bread.','Plan meals, work or study for the month ahead.'],ritual:{premade:'cosmic-new-moon-intentions'},correspondences:{deities:['Hekate','Selene','Artemis','Thoth'],herbs:['Mugwort','Jasmine','Lemon Balm'],crystals:['Moonstone','Labradorite','Clear Quartz'],colors:['White','Silver','Black'],foods:['Bread','Seeds','Water'],symbols:['The empty circle','Seeds','A blank page']},sources:['J. Meeus, Astronomical Algorithms (2nd ed., 1998), ch. 49','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_moon','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Noumenia','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rosh_Chodesh','Varro, On the Latin Language 6.27, and Macrobius, Saturnalia 1.15 (the Kalends announced at the new moon)']},{ref:'moon-waxing-crescent',title:'Waxing Crescent',aka:['Young moon','New crescent','Hilal'],tradition:'The moon',summary:'The thin, growing crescent seen in the western sky after sunset in the days after new moon: the first light of the lunar month, by which many calendars still begin their months.',honours:'The young Moon. Islam begins each month with the sighting of the new crescent (hilal), and so did the old Hebrew, Babylonian and many other calendars. Its slim bow became the emblem of Artemis and Diana, and later of Mary as the woman \u201cwith the moon under her feet\u201d.',history:"Astronomically the waxing crescent lasts from just after new moon to first quarter, about a week, with the lit part growing from a sliver to half the disc. It is seen only in the evening, setting a little later each night. Often the rest of the disc glows faintly too: earthshine, sunlight reflected from the Earth, which Leonardo da Vinci first explained.\n\nThe first sighting of the crescent is one of the oldest calendar events in the world. Babylonian astronomers kept careful records of it; Islamic months still begin when it is seen (or, in many countries, when it is calculated to be visible), so the start and end of Ramadan depend on it. Jewish tradition says a blessing over the waxing moon (Kiddush Levana) between about the third and fifteenth days of the month.\n\nFolk custom across Europe greeted the new crescent: bowing to it, turning silver in the pocket, making a wish, and not seeing it first through glass, which was unlucky. Almanac gardeners sow leafy crops in this first week. The modern witch\u2019s use of the waxing moon for growth and attraction builds on this old sense that what grows with the moon will thrive.",knowAndDont:{known:'The astronomy, the crescent-based calendars and the folk customs of greeting the new moon are well documented.',unknown:'Folk and modern ideas that things begun now will grow are customs and beliefs, not demonstrated effects.'},celebrate:['Look for the crescent low in the west after sunset, and greet it: bow, wave, or make a wish, as people have for centuries.','Turn a silver coin in your pocket when you first see it.','Start a small habit you want to grow with the month.','Sow seeds of leafy greens, if the season allows.'],ritual:{premade:'cosmic-waxing-moon-growth-jar'},correspondences:{deities:['Artemis','Diana','Selene'],herbs:['Basil','Mint','Jasmine'],crystals:['Moonstone','Green Aventurine','Citrine'],colors:['White','Silver','Green'],foods:['Sprouts','Fresh greens','Milk'],symbols:['The crescent','Silver coins','A seedling']},sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crescent','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hilal_(crescent_moon)','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kiddush_levana','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earthshine','I. Opie and M. Tatem, A Dictionary of Superstitions (Oxford, 1989), \u201cmoon\u201d']},{ref:'moon-first-quarter',title:'First Quarter',aka:['Half moon (waxing)','Shukla Ashtami (near it)'],tradition:'The moon',summary:'The Moon a quarter of the way through its month, its right half lit (in the northern hemisphere): high in the evening sky and setting around midnight. A turning point between beginning and fullness.',honours:'The Moon at her half. Many calendars marked the quarter days: the Babylonians, the Buddhist uposatha days, and the Hindu eighth lunar day (Ashtami), on which the bright-fortnight Durga Ashtami falls. Pliny says the Gauls began their months and years on the sixth day of the moon, near this point.',history:"The first quarter is the instant when the Moon is 90\xb0 east of the Sun, about seven and a half days after new moon. Despite the name, half the disc is lit: \u201cquarter\u201d is the quarter of the cycle. It rises around noon and sets around midnight, so it is the best phase for looking at the Moon with binoculars: along the line between light and dark (the terminator), the craters stand out in long shadows.\n\nSeveral calendars treat the quarter days as special. Babylonian hemerologies marked the 7th, 14th, 21st and 28th days of some months as days of caution for the king; the Theravada Buddhist uposatha days fall on the new, full and quarter moons, and lay people keep extra precepts and visit monasteries on them. The Coligny calendar of the Gauls may have begun its months here, if Pliny\u2019s \u201csixth day of the moon\u201d is read that way.\n\nModern astrology (after Dane Rudhyar\u2019s The Lunation Cycle, 1967) calls the first quarter a \u201ccrisis of action\u201d, a time to push through obstacles. That is a modern interpretation, but a useful prompt.",knowAndDont:{known:'The astronomy, the Buddhist quarter-day observances and the Babylonian quarter days are documented.',unknown:'The meaning of the phase as a \u201ccrisis of action\u201d is modern astrology; there is no ancient witch\u2019s rule for the first quarter.'},celebrate:['Look at the Moon through binoculars this evening, along the terminator.','Check how your new-moon intentions are going, and do the hard next step.','Clear an obstacle: make the call, send the email, fix the thing.','Bind a wish with knots, as below.'],ritual:{premade:'folk-nine-knots'},correspondences:{deities:['Artemis','Diana'],herbs:['Bay','Rosemary','Ginger'],crystals:['Carnelian','Tiger Eye','Moonstone'],colors:['White','Red','Silver'],foods:['Bread','Eggs'],symbols:['The half disc','A knot','A bridge']},sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lunar_phase','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uposatha','Pliny the Elder, Natural History 16.95 (the Gauls\u2019 sixth day of the moon)','D. Rudhyar, The Lunation Cycle (1967)','F. Rochberg, The Heavenly Writing (Cambridge, 2004), on Babylonian hemerologies']},{ref:'moon-waxing-gibbous',title:'Waxing Gibbous',aka:['Swelling moon','Growing moon'],tradition:'The moon',summary:'The Moon more than half lit and growing toward full, in the week between first quarter and full moon: bright in the evening sky and up for most of the night.',honours:'The Moon swelling to fullness. The almanac gardener\u2019s moon of fruiting crops, and in many traditions a time of building toward a festival: the Hindu bright-fortnight Ekadashi falls in it, and the Jewish blessing of the moon is said until mid-month.',history:"\u201cGibbous\u201d comes from the Latin gibbus, \u201chump\u201d. In this phase the Moon is more than half lit and still growing; it rises in the afternoon and is high in the sky by evening.\n\nThere are few ancient rites for this phase in particular; it is the run-up to the full moon, when many festivals were held. Folk and almanac gardening, however, gives it a clear job: sow and plant crops that bear their fruit above ground (beans, tomatoes, squash, grains) as the light swells. Roman writers already advised doing some farm tasks while the moon waxes and others while it wanes. Varro has a character say he keeps the rule his father taught him \u201cnot only in shearing my sheep, but in cutting my hair, for fear that if I have it done when the moon is waxing I may become bald.\u201d\n\nModern witches use the waxing gibbous for building, refining and adding strength to work already begun. Modern astrology calls it a time of analysis and adjustment. Both are modern readings.",knowAndDont:{known:'The astronomy, the Roman agricultural advice (Varro, Pliny) and the long life of almanac moon-gardening are well documented.',unknown:'Controlled trials have not shown that planting by the moon\u2019s phase makes crops grow better. It remains a cherished folk practice and a good way to organise a garden.'},celebrate:['Tend what you started at the new moon: water it, feed it, refine it.','Sow or plant fruiting crops, if the season allows.','Take an evening walk by moonlight.','Cook something that rises: bread or a cake.'],ritual:{premade:'green-planting-by-the-moon'},correspondences:{deities:['Selene','Artemis'],herbs:['Basil','Cinnamon','Calendula'],crystals:['Citrine','Green Aventurine','Pyrite'],colors:['Green','Gold','Silver'],foods:['Bread','Honey','Fruit'],symbols:['The swelling disc','Rising dough','Fruit on the vine']},sources:['Varro, On Agriculture 1.37 (Loeb, 1934, public domain): https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Varro/de_Re_Rustica/1*.html','Pliny the Elder, Natural History, Book 18 (on the moon and farming)','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lunar_phase','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kiddush_levana']},{ref:'moon-full',title:'Full Moon',aka:['Purnima (Hindu)','Esbat (Wiccan)','Uposatha (Buddhist)'],tradition:'The moon',summary:'The Moon opposite the Sun, its whole face lit: it rises at sunset and shines all night. The most celebrated phase in the world\u2019s calendars, and the night of the modern witch\u2019s esbat.',honours:'The Moon in her fullness. Across cultures the full moon carries great festivals: Passover and Sukkot begin at it, Easter is set by it, the Buddha\u2019s birth, awakening and passing are kept at the Vesak full moon, the Chinese Mid-Autumn and Lantern festivals fall on it, and Hindu Purnima carries Holi, Guru Purnima and Raksha Bandhan. Selene, Artemis, Diana and later Isis and Hekate were all hailed as the shining Moon.',history:"Astronomically, full moon is the instant when the Moon is 180\xb0 from the Sun. It rises around sunset and sets around sunrise. A lunar eclipse can only happen at full moon, when the Moon passes through the Earth\u2019s shadow. Near the autumn equinox, full moons rise at nearly the same time on several evenings, giving the \u201cHarvest Moon\u201d its long, useful light.\n\nFull-moon festivals are ancient and worldwide; see above. The names given to each month\u2019s full moon in the Old Farmer\u2019s Almanac (Wolf, Snow, Worm, Pink\u2026) are a modern compilation mixing Algonquian, colonial and European names, popularised in the 20th century.\n\nThe full moon as the witches\u2019 meeting comes into English print with Charles Leland\u2019s Aradia (1899), where Aradia tells her followers: \u201cWhenever ye have need of anything, / Once in the month, and when the moon is full, / Ye shall assemble in some desert place\u201d. Margaret Murray called the witches\u2019 regular meetings \u201cesbats\u201d (1921), and Gerald Gardner\u2019s Wicca made the full-moon esbat central from the 1950s. So the esbat is modern, built from a 19th-century folklorist\u2019s source and 20th-century practice. Charging crystals and making moon water are also modern.\n\nThe belief that the full moon brings madness (lunacy) is old. Studies of hospital admissions, crime and births mostly find no reliable effect, though a few find people sleep a little less around the full moon.",knowAndDont:{known:'The astronomy, and the many full-moon festivals of living religions, are well documented; so are the modern origins of the esbat.',unknown:'Popular claims about the full moon\u2019s effects on mood, behaviour and births are not supported by most studies.'},celebrate:['Go outside and look: watch it rise at sunset, huge and coloured near the horizon.','Leave a jar of water in the moonlight if you like making moon water (for washing or plants, not drinking unless it\u2019s clean tap water in a clean jar).','Give thanks for what has come to fullness this month.','Gather with friends, or keep an esbat alone.','If a festival falls on this full moon in a living tradition, learn about it, or join if you\u2019re invited.'],ritual:{premade:'wicca-full-moon-esbat'},correspondences:{deities:['Selene','Artemis','Diana','Isis','Hekate'],herbs:['Jasmine','Mugwort','Rose','Willow'],crystals:['Moonstone','Selenite','Clear Quartz','Opal'],colors:['White','Silver'],foods:['Round cakes','Milk','White wine or juice','Moon cakes'],symbols:['The full disc','Water in a bowl','The mirror']},sources:['C. G. Leland, Aradia, or the Gospel of the Witches (1899), ch. 1 (public domain): https://archive.org/details/aradiaorgospelw00lelagoog','M. Murray, The Witch-Cult in Western Europe (1921)','R. Hutton, The Triumph of the Moon (Oxford, 1999)','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Full_moon','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lunar_effect']},{ref:'moon-waning-gibbous',title:'Waning Gibbous',aka:['Disseminating moon (modern astrology)'],tradition:'The moon',summary:'The Moon past full, still more than half lit but shrinking, in the week before last quarter: rising later each evening and still bright in the morning sky.',honours:'The Moon beginning to give back her light. The Hindu dark fortnight (Krishna paksha) begins now; its first half carries the fortnight of the ancestors (Pitru Paksha) once a year.',history:"After full moon, the lit part shrinks from the right (in the northern hemisphere). The waning gibbous rises an hour or so later each night, so it is often seen in the morning sky after sunrise.\n\nFew traditions single this phase out. Almanac gardeners use the waning moon for planting roots and bulbs, for harvesting crops meant to keep, for pruning and for drying herbs, the idea being that the sap and moisture are drawn down. Varro and other Roman writers already advised harvesting grain and cutting wood in the waning moon.\n\nModern astrology calls it the \u201cdisseminating\u201d phase, a time to share what has been learned. Modern witches often use it for gratitude, sharing, cleansing and the beginning of letting go. These are modern frameworks, but they fit the shape of the month well.",knowAndDont:{known:'The astronomy, the Hindu fortnights, and the long almanac tradition of waning-moon gardening are documented.',unknown:'There is no evidence that crops harvested or herbs dried in the waning moon keep better; it is a folk rule.'},celebrate:['Share something you have: knowledge, food, time.','Cleanse and recharge your tools or stones.','Harvest and dry herbs, or plant bulbs and roots.','Write down what you learned this month.'],ritual:{premade:'crystal-cleanse-and-charge'},correspondences:{deities:['Selene','Hekate'],herbs:['Sage','Rosemary','Lavender'],crystals:['Amethyst','Smoky Quartz','Selenite'],colors:['Silver','Purple','Blue'],foods:['Root vegetables','Soup','Herbal tea'],symbols:['Drying herbs','An open hand']},sources:['Varro, On Agriculture 1.37 (Loeb, 1934, public domain): https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Varro/de_Re_Rustica/1*.html','D. Rudhyar, The Lunation Cycle (1967)','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lunar_phase']},{ref:'moon-last-quarter',title:'Last Quarter',aka:['Third quarter','Half moon (waning)','Krishna Ashtami (near it)'],tradition:'The moon',summary:'The Moon three-quarters of the way through its month, its left half lit (in the northern hemisphere): rising around midnight and high in the morning sky. A turning point toward release and rest.',honours:'The Moon at her waning half. In the Hindu calendar the dark eighth day (Krishna Ashtami) falls near it: Krishna was born at midnight on the dark eighth of Bhadrapada, as the half moon rose, and Janmashtami keeps his birth then. Buddhist uposatha days and the Babylonian quarter days fall here too.',history:"The last (or third) quarter is the instant when the Moon is 90\xb0 west of the Sun, about 22 days into the month. Half the disc is lit; it rises around midnight and sets around noon, so it is a morning moon, often seen in daylight.\n\nAs with the first quarter, several calendars marked it: the Theravada uposatha, Babylonian days of caution, and the Hindu Ashtami. Each month\u2019s dark Ashtami is Kalashtami, for Bhairava, a fierce form of Shiva.\n\nModern witchcraft uses the last quarter for releasing, breaking habits, banishing and clearing. Folk gardening uses it for weeding and cutting back. Modern astrology calls it a \u201ccrisis of consciousness\u201d, a time to reassess.",knowAndDont:{known:'The astronomy, the Hindu Ashtami festivals and the Buddhist quarter days are documented.',unknown:'The idea that release-work is stronger now is modern practice, not ancient rule.'},celebrate:['Let go of one thing: a habit, a grudge, a cluttered drawer.','Weed the garden.','Look for the half moon in the morning sky.','Review the month and decide what not to carry forward.'],ritual:{premade:'cosmic-waning-moon-release'},correspondences:{deities:['Hekate','Persephone'],herbs:['Rosemary','Sage','Garlic'],crystals:['Smoky Quartz','Obsidian','Black Tourmaline'],colors:['Grey','Black','Silver'],foods:['Soup','Bitter greens'],symbols:['Untied knots','The broom','The half disc']},sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lunar_phase','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Krishna_Janmashtami','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uposatha','D. Rudhyar, The Lunation Cycle (1967)']},{ref:'moon-waning-crescent',title:'Waning Crescent',aka:['Old moon','Balsamic moon (modern astrology)'],tradition:'The moon',summary:'The last thin crescent, seen in the east before dawn in the days before new moon: the old moon fading, a time for clearing, finishing and rest.',honours:'The old Moon. In ancient Egypt the lunar month began on the morning the old crescent could no longer be seen; in Athens the month\u2019s last day belonged to Hekate and the restless dead.',history:"The waning crescent shrinks from half to nothing over the last week of the month. It rises before the Sun and is seen only in the dawn sky, a little closer to the sunrise each morning, until it disappears.\n\nThe Egyptian lunar calendar watched for this last crescent: the first day of the lunar month (pesdjentyu) was the morning it could no longer be seen. The Athenians counted the last ten days of the month backwards, and the last day belonged to Hekate. In folk practice, the waning moon was the time for cutting, cleaning, weeding and ending things.\n\nModern astrology (Rudhyar) calls this the \u201cbalsamic\u201d phase, of healing and surrender; modern witches use it for banishing, cleansing, rest and preparation for the new cycle. These frameworks are modern; the instinct to finish and clear at the month\u2019s end is very old.",knowAndDont:{known:'The astronomy, the Egyptian lunar month rule (Parker) and the Greek month\u2019s end are documented.',unknown:'No evidence shows that cleansing or banishing works better now; it is a fitting custom.'},celebrate:['Get up early once and look for the old crescent in the east before sunrise.','Sweep and clean the house thoroughly.','Finish small tasks before the new month.','Rest more; go to bed early.'],ritual:{premade:'kitchen-sweeping-out-the-old'},correspondences:{deities:['Hekate','Nyx'],herbs:['Rosemary','Lavender','Chamomile'],crystals:['Amethyst','Smoky Quartz','Lepidolite'],colors:['Grey','Black','Indigo'],foods:['Herbal tea','Simple broth'],symbols:['The broom','The old crescent','Dawn']},sources:['R. A. Parker, The Calendars of Ancient Egypt (Chicago, 1950)','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Attic_calendar','D. Rudhyar, The Lunation Cycle (1967)','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lunar_phase']},{ref:'moon-dark',title:'Dark Moon',aka:['The dark of the moon','Interlunium','Hekate\u2019s Deipnon (Greek)','Amavasya (Hindu)'],tradition:'The moon',summary:'The night or two before new moon when the Moon cannot be seen at all, lost in the Sun\u2019s glare: the quiet, hidden end of the lunar month.',honours:'The Moon hidden. In Athens, the last day of the month was Hekate\u2019s: a supper (deipnon) was left for her at the crossroads, and the house was cleansed. Hindu Amavasya, the ancestors\u2019 day, falls here. Many modern witches honour Hekate, the crone and the dead at the dark moon.',history:"Astronomers don\u2019t name a \u201cdark moon\u201d phase; the term is popular and pagan usage for the days around conjunction when the Moon is too close to the Sun to see, usually one to three nights. In the app it is the last nights of the month before new moon. In some modern pagan usage \u201cnew moon\u201d means the first crescent and \u201cdark moon\u201d the conjunction itself, which can confuse: here, the dark moon comes first and the new moon is the conjunction.\n\nOld practices for this time are among the best attested of any moon customs. Athenians kept Hekate\u2019s Deipnon on the month\u2019s last day, leaving food at the crossroads, and carried out the leavings of the household\u2019s purifications with it. Pliny advises that timber be felled at the interlunium, when the Moon is with the Sun, to keep it free of rot. Hindu tradition makes Amavasya a day for the ancestors, and many avoid beginning new ventures on it.\n\nModern witches often use the dark moon for rest, divination, shadow work, banishing and honouring the dead, or keep it as a night without spellwork at all.",knowAndDont:{known:'The Greek Deipnon, the Hindu Amavasya and Roman advice on timber are documented.',unknown:'\u201cDark moon\u201d as a phase is popular usage, not astronomy, and how many nights it lasts depends on the sky and the observer. Its modern uses for shadow work are modern.'},celebrate:['Rest: a night with no tasks and no spellwork, if you like.','Clean the house, take out the rubbish and empty the bins.','Leave a small supper for Hekate at your door or a crossroads, and don\u2019t take it back.','Journal honestly about the month.','Look at the stars: the dark moon is the best time for stargazing.'],ritual:{premade:'cosmic-dark-moon-rest-shadow'},correspondences:{deities:['Hekate','Nyx','Persephone','Hel'],herbs:['Mugwort','Wormwood','Myrrh','Cypress'],crystals:['Obsidian','Black Tourmaline','Jet','Smoky Quartz'],colors:['Black','Indigo','Silver'],foods:['Eggs','Bread','Garlic (left for Hekate, not where animals can eat it)'],symbols:['The crossroads','The key','The torch','Stars']},sources:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deipnon','Pliny the Elder, Natural History 16.74 (felling timber at the interlunium)','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amavasya','R. Hutton, The Triumph of the Moon (Oxford, 1999)']}]},2502,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"CALENDAR_WEST_PAGES",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return l}});const t='Roman',o='H. H. Scullard, Festivals and Ceremonies of the Roman Republic (Thames & Hudson, 1981)',n=t=>`Ovid, Fasti ${t} (Latin text): https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/ovid/ovid.fasti${t}.shtml`,s='W. Smith, Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities (1875), \u201cCalendarium\u201d, at LacusCurtius: https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Calendarium.html',h='M. Beard, J. North & S. Price, Religions of Rome (Cambridge, 1998), vols. 1\u20132',l=[{ref:'roman-compitalia',title:'Compitalia',aka:['Feast of the Crossroads','Lares Compitales','Ludi Compitalicii'],tradition:t,summary:'The feast of the Lares of the crossroads, kept by neighbourhoods and farms at the little shrines where paths and boundaries met, in the first days of the new year.',honours:'The Lares Compitales, the guardian spirits of the crossroads and of everyone who lived around them: free, freed and enslaved alike. The Lares were the closest gods a Roman had, the ones who watched over a house, a field, a street. At the Compitalia the whole neighbourhood honoured them together, and the household\u2019s own Lares were not forgotten.',history:"Where the paths of several farms met, or where streets crossed in the city, stood a small shrine, the compitum, open on each side so that each household faced its own Lares. The Compitalia was a movable feast: each year the praetor announced it for a day soon after the Saturnalia. Under the Empire it settled on 3\u20135 January, the dates kept here.\n\nEach household hung a woollen doll at the shrine for every free person in it, and a woollen ball for every enslaved person, as Festus and Macrobius report; ancient writers already argued about what this meant, some calling the dolls substitutes for the people themselves. On farms the enslaved workers had a share in the feast and, says Cato, an extra ration of wine. In the city the feast was run by neighbourhood associations, many of them led by freedmen, and the games that went with it could become rowdy enough that the Senate banned them for a time in the late Republic.\n\nAugustus reorganised the street shrines around 7 BCE, adding his own Genius beside the Lares, now called the Lares Augusti, so that the neighbourhood cult also became a cult of the emperor. Small altars and paintings from Pompeii show crossroad shrines with their two dancing Lares and a snake below.",knowAndDont:{known:'That it was a feast of the Lares at the crossroads, set yearly by the praetor in early January; that households hung woollen figures and balls at the shrine; that the enslaved took part and had extra rations; that Augustus reshaped the neighbourhood shrines. Many shrines survive at Pompeii.',unknown:'Exactly what the woollen dolls and balls meant, and whether they once stood in for human offerings, as some ancient writers guessed (there is no evidence that they did). The precise words and order of the rite. How much of the rural and the city feast were the same.'},celebrate:['Visit a crossroads, a corner or a path near your home, and leave a small offering for the spirits of the neighbourhood: a little grain, a flower, a drop of wine poured on soil.','Honour your own household Lares at home with incense, salted flour and a few words for everyone who lives with you.','Hang a small woollen tassel or pompom by your door for each person in the home, as a reminder of who the Lares keep.','Do something kind for the people you live among: shovel a neighbour\u2019s path, share food, learn a name you didn\u2019t know.'],ritual:{premade:'folk-lararium-offering',title:'Offering at the Lararium, for the Lares of the house and street'},correspondences:{deities:['Lares Compitales','Lares','Genius of the household'],herbs:['Frankincense','Bay','Rosemary'],colors:['White Candle','Red Candle'],foods:['Honey cakes','Salted flour (mola salsa)','Wine'],symbols:['Crossroads','Woollen dolls and balls','Two dancing Lares']},sources:[o,"Macrobius, Saturnalia 1.7\u20131.16 (Saturnalia 1.7.34\u201335 on the dolls)",'Cato, De Agri Cultura 57','H. Flower, The Dancing Lares and the Serpent in the Garden (Princeton, 2017)','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Compitalia',s]},{ref:'roman-agonalia',title:'Agonalia of Janus',aka:['Agonium','Dies Agonalis'],tradition:t,summary:'The first great sacrifice of the Roman year, a ram offered to Janus, god of doors and beginnings, by the king of sacred things.',honours:'Janus, the two-faced god of doorways, gates, passages and beginnings, who was named first in Roman prayers because every act begins by passing through him. A Roman praying to any god began with Janus and ended with Vesta.',history:"On 9 January the rex sacrorum, the \u201cking of sacred things\u201d who kept the old priestly duties of the kings, sacrificed a ram in the Regia, the old king\u2019s house in the Forum. Varro explains the name from the sacrificer\u2019s question before he struck, \u201cagone?\u201d, \u201cshall I do it?\u201d Ovid, who opens his Fasti with Janus, offers several more guesses and admits none is certain.\n\nThere were four Agonia in the year: 9 January, 17 March, 21 May and 11 December, honouring different gods. The January one belonged to Janus, fitting the month that bore his name. Janus had no myth to speak of in early Rome; he was the god of the threshold itself, his double-faced image looking out and in, back to the old year and on to the new. The gates of his shrine in the Forum stood open in time of war and were closed only in rare years of peace.",knowAndDont:{known:'The date, the priest (the rex sacrorum), the place (the Regia) and the victim (a ram), from the calendars, Varro and Ovid.',unknown:'What \u201cAgonalia\u201d really means: the ancients themselves offered rival etymologies. The prayers spoken. Why the Agonia fell on the four dates they did.'},celebrate:['Make the first act of a project or of the day a deliberate one: pause at the door, name what you are starting, and step through.','Clean or bless your front door and threshold, Janus\u2019s place.','Offer Janus a small cake or a little spelt or flour with salt, and a drop of wine or juice, first before any other god.','Look back over the past year and forward to the next, writing one line for each face of Janus.'],ritual:{title:'A blessing at the door for Janus',outline:['Stand inside your front door with a small dish of flour and salt, a candle and a cup of wine or juice.','Light the candle (keep it away from curtains and coats) and wash your hands.','Open the door. Greet Janus first, as Romans did, and ask him to open well what you begin this year.','Sprinkle a pinch of the salted flour on the threshold and pour a few drops from the cup outside.','Name one thing you are leaving behind as you look out, and one thing you are beginning as you turn to look in.','Close the door, snuff the candle, and greet Vesta last, at the hearth or the stove.']},correspondences:{deities:['Janus','Vesta'],herbs:['Bay','Frankincense'],colors:['White Candle','Gold Candle'],foods:['Spelt or flour with salt','Honey cakes','Wine'],symbols:['Two faces','Doorway','Key','Ram']},sources:["Varro, De Lingua Latina 6.12\u201326 (Latin text): https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/varro.ll6.html (6.12 on the name)",n(1)+' (1.317\u2013336)',o,s]},{ref:'roman-carmentalia',title:'Carmentalia',aka:['Feast of Carmenta'],tradition:t,summary:'The first day of the feast of Carmenta, the prophetic goddess who sings the fate of every child born, kept by Roman women; no leather was allowed into her shrine.',honours:'Carmenta, or Carmentis, a goddess of prophecy and childbirth whose name was linked with carmen, a sung charm or prophecy. In legend she was the mother of Evander, the Arcadian who settled on the Palatine before Rome was founded, and she foretold Rome\u2019s greatness. She had her own priest, the flamen Carmentalis, and a shrine by the gate named after her, the Porta Carmentalis.',history:"The feast fell on two days, 11 and 15 January. Ovid says that leather, as a thing taken from a dead animal, was not to be brought into her shrine, and the mothers of Rome kept her rites.\n\nThe second day had a story: when women were forbidden to ride in their carriages (carpenta), they refused to bear children until the right was restored, and a second feast was added in thanks. Plutarch and Ovid both tell versions of it; it is an aetiological tale, a story invented to explain a custom, but a telling one about whose festival this was.\n\nCarmenta was also honoured as two sisters or aspects, Porrima (or Antevorta) and Postverta, who looked forward and back. Gellius, quoting Varro, says they were invoked for births: one for a child coming head first, the other for a child coming feet first.",knowAndDont:{known:'The two dates, her flamen and her shrine by the Porta Carmentalis; the ban on leather; her link with childbirth and prophecy; the paired names Porrima and Postverta.',unknown:'What the women actually did in her rites. Whether the carriage story reflects any real event. How old her link with Evander really is.'},celebrate:['Honour the midwives, doulas, nurses and parents in your life, or give to a maternity charity.','Keep leather out of your shrine or altar space for the day, as her worshippers did.','Write or speak a blessing for a child, or for your own birth: what you hope has been sung over your life.','Practise a gentle form of divination: write your question, sit quietly, and listen for the first words that come.'],ritual:{title:'A song for the newborn',outline:['Clear a small space and remove anything made of leather from it.','Light a white candle safely and offer a little milk, honey or a small cake to Carmenta.','Speak the name of a child (or a new project) and ask her to sing it a kind fate.','Look back with Postverta: name what has led here. Look forward with Porrima: name what you hope for.','Write the blessing down and keep it, or give it to the family it was for.']},correspondences:{deities:['Carmenta','Juno'],herbs:['Bay','Lavender'],crystals:['Moonstone'],colors:['White Candle'],foods:['Milk and honey','Small cakes'],symbols:['Song','Carriage','Two faces looking forward and back']},sources:[n(1)+' (1.461\u2013636)','Aulus Gellius, Attic Nights 16.16',"Plutarch, Roman Questions 56: Plutarch, Roman Questions (Loeb, 1936), at LacusCurtius: https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Moralia/Roman_Questions*/home.html",'Varro, De Lingua Latina 6.12\u201326 (Latin text): https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/varro.ll6.html',o]},{ref:'roman-carmentalia-2',title:'Carmentalia (second day)',aka:['Carmentalia II'],tradition:t,summary:'The second day of Carmenta\u2019s feast, four days after the first. See the Carmentalia for the full story.',honours:'Carmenta again, especially as Porrima and Postverta, who look forward and back at a birth.',history:"Ovid and Plutarch say the second day was added after Roman women refused to bear children until their right to ride in carriages was given back. Whatever the truth of that story, the two days belonged together as one feast of Carmenta. The fuller page is on the Carmentalia, 11 January.",knowAndDont:{known:'That the calendars mark 15 January as a second Carmentalia.',unknown:'Why the feast had two days four days apart; the carriage story is a later explanation.'},celebrate:['Return to the blessing you made on the first day and read it aloud again.','Give a small thanks for a safe birth, your own or another\u2019s.'],ritual:{title:'See the Carmentalia',outline:['Light the candle again, offer milk or honey, and reread your blessing from the first day.']},correspondences:{deities:['Carmenta'],colors:['White Candle']},sources:[n(1)+' (1.617\u2013636)',o]},{ref:'roman-quirinalia',title:'Quirinalia',aka:['Feast of Quirinus','Stultorum feriae','Feast of Fools'],tradition:t,summary:'The feast of Quirinus, god of the Roman people as citizens, later identified with Romulus made divine; also the \u201cfeast of fools\u201d for anyone who had missed their own oven festival.',honours:'Quirinus, one of Rome\u2019s oldest gods, who with Jupiter and Mars formed the ancient triad and had one of the three major flamens. The Romans called themselves Quirites, citizens, and by the late Republic Quirinus was taken to be Romulus, the founder, carried up to the gods in a storm.',history:"On 17 February the flamen Quirinalis sacrificed to Quirinus at his temple on the Quirinal hill. Ovid tells the story of Romulus vanishing in a thunderstorm and appearing afterwards to Proculus Julius, asking to be worshipped as Quirinus.\n\nThe day was also the last chance to keep the Fornacalia, the festival of ovens, when each curia (the old voting divisions of the people) roasted spelt in honour of Fornax, the oven goddess. The curio maximus posted notices saying which curia kept it when; those who did not know their own curia, or missed the day, kept it on the Quirinalia, which is why it was nicknamed the feast of fools.",knowAndDont:{known:'The date, the flamen, the temple on the Quirinal; the link with the Fornacalia from Varro and Ovid; the late-Republican identification of Quirinus with Romulus.',unknown:'Who Quirinus was before he became Romulus, a question argued by scholars for a century (a god of the community, of the Sabines, or of peaceful farming are all proposed). What the Fornacalia rite looked like in detail.'},celebrate:['Bake bread, or roast grain in the oven, for the Fornacalia you may have \u201cmissed\u201d.','Do one act of good citizenship: vote, volunteer, write to a representative, help a neighbour.','Laugh at your own foolishness: make up a small thing you forgot or put off.','Read the story of Romulus\u2019s disappearance in Ovid\u2019s Fasti or Livy 1.16.'],ritual:{premade:'kitchen-prosperity-bread',title:'Bread for the oven goddess and the people'},correspondences:{deities:['Quirinus','Fornax','Mars','Jupiter'],herbs:['Bay','Oak'],colors:['Red Candle','Brown Candle'],foods:['Roasted spelt or farro','Bread'],symbols:['Oven','Spear','Storm']},sources:[n(2)+' (2.475\u2013532)',"Varro, De Lingua Latina 6.12\u201326 (Latin text): https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/varro.ll6.html (6.13)",'Livy 1.16',o]},{ref:'roman-caristia',title:'Caristia',aka:['Cara Cognatio','Day of Dear Kin'],tradition:t,summary:'The family feast after the days of the dead: the living kin sat down together, set aside their quarrels and gave a share of the meal to the household Lares.',honours:'The living family, and the Lares of the house who watch over it. It follows directly on the Parentalia (13\u201321 February), the days for honouring the family dead, as if to turn from the grave back to the table.',history:"Ovid describes the day: after visiting the tombs, the family turns to its living members. Those who had wronged their kin (a cruel brother, a grasping heir, a harsh stepmother, Ovid lists them) should stay away. Incense was offered to the family\u2019s gods, food was set out for the Lares in a dish, and at nightfall the family drank to one another and to the emperor.\n\nValerius Maximus says the feast was meant to settle family disputes, with no outsiders present so that any quarrel could be made up among kin. It is one of the most human of Roman festivals, and one of the easiest to keep now.",knowAndDont:{known:'The date and the shape of the day, from Ovid (Fasti 2.617\u2013638) and Valerius Maximus 2.1.8: a family meal, offerings to the Lares, reconciliation.',unknown:'How widely it was kept, and how old it is; it appears in the calendars but little else.'},celebrate:['Share a meal with your family, of blood or of choice. Set a small plate aside for the household spirits.','Reach out to someone you have fallen out with, if it is safe and kind to do so.','Raise a glass to each person at the table in turn, saying one good thing about them.','If you are estranged from family for good reasons, keep it with the people who are kin to you now.'],ritual:{premade:'kitchen-family-meal-blessing',title:'Blessing the family meal'},correspondences:{deities:['Lares','Concordia'],herbs:['Frankincense','Rosemary'],colors:['Pink Candle','White Candle'],foods:['A shared meal','Wine','Small cakes for the Lares'],symbols:['The family table','The dish for the Lares']},sources:[n(2)+' (2.617\u2013638)','Valerius Maximus, Memorable Doings and Sayings 2.1.8',o]},{ref:'roman-terminalia',title:'Terminalia',aka:['Feast of Terminus'],tradition:t,summary:'The feast of Terminus, god of boundary stones: neighbours met at the stone between their lands, garlanded it and shared a sacrifice and a feast.',honours:'Terminus, the god present in every boundary marker, who kept the peace between neighbours by staying exactly where he was put. When Jupiter\u2019s great temple was built on the Capitol, said the story, Terminus alone refused to move, so his stone stayed inside, under a hole in the roof, because he must see the sky.',history:"On 23 February the owners of neighbouring fields met at the boundary stone. Ovid describes it fondly: each garlanded the stone from his own side and brought a cake; an altar was built, the farmer\u2019s wife carried fire from the hearth, the old man cut wood, a son threw grain into the flames three times and a daughter offered honeycomb; wine was poured, a lamb or a suckling pig might be sacrificed, and the neighbours feasted together singing Terminus\u2019s praises.\n\nThere was also a public rite at the sixth milestone on the road to Laurentum, once Rome\u2019s boundary. Varro notes that the Terminalia had been the last day of the old year, when the year began in March. Roman law took boundaries seriously: moving a boundary stone was an ancient crime.",knowAndDont:{known:'The shape of the rural rite from Ovid\u2019s vivid account, the public rite at the sixth milestone, and the Capitol story (Livy, Ovid, Dionysius).',unknown:'Whether every farm kept it as fully as Ovid describes, or whether his account is idealised (it probably is, a little).'},celebrate:['Walk the edges of your home or garden, and mark or tidy a boundary: a fence post, a doorstep, the edge of a bed.','Share cakes, honey or a drink with a neighbour.','Take stock of your own boundaries, with people and with time, and name one you want to keep firmer.','Don\u2019t move anyone else\u2019s marker, literally or otherwise.'],ritual:{premade:'green-garden-boundary-planting',title:'A blessing for the boundary',outline:['Choose a boundary: a fence, a garden edge, a stone, or the threshold of your room.','Garland it with a ribbon, a sprig or a flower.','Offer a small cake and a little honey beside it, and pour a few drops of wine or juice.','Thank Terminus for keeping the peace between you and your neighbours, and ask for a good year on both sides of the line.','Share the rest of the cakes with a neighbour if you can.']},correspondences:{deities:['Terminus','Jupiter'],herbs:['Oak','Rosemary'],crystals:['Hematite','Smoky Quartz'],colors:['Brown Candle','White Candle'],foods:['Cakes','Honeycomb','Wine'],symbols:['Boundary stone','Garland','Hole in the temple roof']},sources:[n(2)+' (2.639\u2013684)',"Varro, De Lingua Latina 6.12\u201326 (Latin text): https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/varro.ll6.html (6.13)",'Livy 1.55',o]},{ref:'roman-regifugium',title:'Regifugium',aka:['Fugalia','Flight of the King'],tradition:t,summary:'The \u201cflight of the king\u201d: after a sacrifice in the Comitium, the rex sacrorum ran from the Forum. The Romans tied it to the expulsion of their last king; what it first meant is lost.',honours:'The god of the sacrifice is not recorded. The day remembers the end of the monarchy and the beginning of the Republic, and perhaps, more anciently, a turning of the year.',history:"Ovid connects the day to the flight of Tarquin the Proud, the last king of Rome, driven out around 509 BCE. Plutarch knew the rite: the rex sacrorum offered a sacrifice in the Comitium, the assembly place, and then fled from it at once.\n\nModern scholars doubt the link with Tarquin. The day falls straight after the Terminalia, at the end of the old year that began in March; some see a rite of the year\u2019s ending, others link it with the two days marked Q.R.C.F. (\u201cwhen the king has gone to the assembly, it is lawful\u201d) in March and May. No explanation has won out.",knowAndDont:{known:'The date and the bare rite: a sacrifice in the Comitium and the flight of the rex sacrorum (Plutarch, Roman Questions 63; Ovid, Fasti 2.685\u2013852).',unknown:'Its original meaning; the god honoured; whether it had anything to do with Tarquin.'},celebrate:['Reflect on power: who has it over you, who you have it over, and what you would want to flee or let go of.','Read the story of Lucretia and the founding of the Republic (Livy 1.57\u201360), with care: it is a story of violence.','Close something properly before the new season begins: finish, return, release.'],ritual:{title:'Leaving the old year behind',outline:['Write on a slip of paper something you are done with: a role, a habit, an old rule over you.','Light a candle safely and read it aloud once.','Tear the paper and put it in the bin or compost, then walk out of the room without looking back.','Come back after a few minutes, wash your hands and begin something new.']},correspondences:{deities:['Jupiter','Janus'],colors:['Purple Candle','White Candle'],symbols:['Running figure','Empty throne']},sources:[n(2)+' (2.685\u2013852)',"Plutarch, Roman Questions 63: Plutarch, Roman Questions (Loeb, 1936), at LacusCurtius: https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Moralia/Roman_Questions*/home.html",o,'A. K. Michels, The Calendar of the Roman Republic (Princeton, 1967)']},{ref:'roman-equirria',title:'Equirria',aka:['Equirria (February)','Horse races of Mars'],tradition:t,summary:'Horse races for Mars on the Campus Martius, the first of two (the second is 14 March), as the season of war drew near.',honours:'Mars, god of war and of the young men of the community, and in early Rome also a guardian of fields and boundaries. The Romans\u2019 year once began in his month, March.',history:"Ovid says Romulus himself founded the Equirria. The races were held on the Campus Martius, the \u201cfield of Mars\u201d outside the old city, or, when the Tiber flooded the field, on the Caelian hill. A second Equirria followed on 14 March.\n\nLike the other festivals of Mars in February and March (the dances of the Salii, the purifying of the trumpets, the October Horse at the season\u2019s end), the races seem to prepare horses and men for the year\u2019s campaigns. The details of the races themselves are not recorded.",knowAndDont:{known:'The dates, the place and the dedication to Mars, from the calendars, Ovid (Fasti 2.857\u2013862, 3.517\u2013522) and Varro.',unknown:'How the races were run, who rode, and what rites went with them.'},celebrate:['Get your body moving: run, ride, cycle, or go for a long brisk walk.','Prepare for something demanding ahead: plan, train, mend your gear.','Light a red candle for Mars and ask for courage and discipline in a struggle you face.'],ritual:{premade:'cosmic-tuesday-mars-courage',title:'A spark of courage for Mars'},correspondences:{deities:['Mars'],herbs:['Bay','Ginger','Black Pepper'],crystals:['Carnelian','Bloodstone','Red Jasper'],colors:['Red Candle'],symbols:['Horse','Spear','Racecourse']},sources:[n(2)+' (2.857\u2013862)',"Varro, De Lingua Latina 6.12\u201326 (Latin text): https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/varro.ll6.html (6.13)",o]},{ref:'roman-equirria-2',title:'Equirria (March)',aka:['Second Equirria'],tradition:t,summary:'The second horse races for Mars, two weeks into his own month, on the Campus Martius (or the Caelian when the Tiber flooded). See the February Equirria.',honours:'Mars, at the height of his month, as the campaigning season opened.',history:"Ovid places the second Equirria on 14 March and again sends the races to the Caelian if the Campus Martius was under water. It belongs with the other rites of Mars\u2019s month: the Salii dancing through the city with their sacred shields, the Quinquatrus and the Tubilustrium. The fuller page is on the Equirria, 27 February.",knowAndDont:{known:'The date and place (Ovid, Fasti 3.517\u2013522).',unknown:'As with the first Equirria, the races themselves are not described.'},celebrate:['Repeat what you began at the first Equirria: training, preparation, courage.','Notice the lengthening days and the spring coming in, the season Mars once opened.'],ritual:{premade:'cosmic-tuesday-mars-courage',title:'See the Equirria (February)'},correspondences:{deities:['Mars'],crystals:['Carnelian'],colors:['Red Candle'],symbols:['Horse']},sources:[n(3)+' (3.517\u2013522)',o]},{ref:'roman-liberalia',title:'Liberalia',aka:['Feast of Liber Pater'],tradition:t,summary:'The feast of Liber and Libera, gods of growth, wine and freedom: old women crowned with ivy sold honey cakes in the streets, and boys often put on the toga of manhood.',honours:'Liber Pater, an old Italian god of growth and of the free (liber), identified with the Greek Dionysus; his partner Libera; and Ceres, with whom they shared a temple on the Aventine, the temple of the plebs, from 493 BCE.',history:"Ovid describes old women, crowned with ivy, sitting in the streets with portable hearths, selling liba, cakes of flour, cheese and honey, and offering a piece of each to the god on behalf of the buyer. Varro says much the same. It was also the usual day for a Roman boy of about fifteen or sixteen to lay aside the purple-bordered toga of childhood and put on the plain white toga virilis, with a procession to the Capitol.\n\nAugustine, quoting Varro, describes a cruder country form of the feast at Lavinium, with a phallus carried through the fields for fertility; he was writing to condemn it, so his account should be read with care. In 186 BCE the Senate suppressed the Bacchanalia, the secret Bacchic societies, but the Liberalia itself carried on.",knowAndDont:{known:'The date, the honey cakes and the old women selling them (Ovid, Fasti 3.713\u2013790; Varro 6.14), and the coming-of-age custom.',unknown:'How Liber was worshipped before he merged with Dionysus; the details of the rural rites outside Augustine\u2019s hostile summary.'},celebrate:['Bake liba, simple honey-and-cheese cakes (Cato gives a recipe: cheese, flour and egg, baked on bay leaves; don\u2019t eat the leaves), and give a piece first to the god.','Mark a coming of age: a young person\u2019s milestone, or your own step into a new freedom.','Pour a small libation of wine or grape juice to Liber.','Wear or hang ivy (it is a decoration only: ivy is not for eating).'],ritual:{premade:'kitchen-honey-sweetening-cake',title:'Honey cakes for Liber'},correspondences:{deities:['Liber Pater','Bacchus','Libera','Ceres','Dionysus'],herbs:['Bay'],crystals:['Amethyst'],colors:['Purple Candle','Green Candle'],foods:['Liba (honey and cheese cakes)','Wine or grape juice','Honey'],symbols:['Ivy','Toga','Wine cup']},sources:[n(3)+' (3.713\u2013790)',"Varro, De Lingua Latina 6.12\u201326 (Latin text): https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/varro.ll6.html (6.14)",'Cato, De Agri Cultura 75 (libum)','Augustine, City of God 7.21',o]},{ref:'roman-quinquatrus',title:'Quinquatrus',aka:['Quinquatria','Greater Quinquatrus','Feast of Minerva'],tradition:t,summary:'Minerva\u2019s five-day festival of crafts, learning and healing (19\u201323 March), kept by artisans, teachers, doctors and students; once a single day for Mars.',honours:'Minerva, goddess of skill of hand and mind: of weaving and every craft, of teachers and their pupils, of doctors, poets and painters, and later of war strategy, identified with the Greek Athena. On the first day, Mars too was honoured by the dancing Salii.',history:"The name means \u201cthe fifth day after\u201d (the Ides), and Varro points out that it was really only one day, misread as five because of the name. Still, by Ovid\u2019s time it ran five days: the first, the anniversary of Minerva\u2019s temple on the Aventine, had no bloodshed; the next four had gladiatorial shows.\n\nOvid lists who should pray to Minerva at this feast: girls and boys learning, spinners and weavers, fullers and dyers, cobblers, carpenters, doctors, teachers, painters, sculptors and poets. Schoolchildren had holidays and brought their teachers a fee, the minerval. A \u201clesser Quinquatrus\u201d for the flute-players fell on 13 June.",knowAndDont:{known:'The dates, the link with Minerva\u2019s Aventine temple, the list of trades, the minerval, and Varro\u2019s note that it began as one day (Varro 6.14; Ovid, Fasti 3.809\u2013848).',unknown:'How Mars and Minerva came to share the day; what the earliest single-day rite involved.'},celebrate:['Bless your tools: a pen, a laptop, needles, brushes, a stethoscope, whatever you work with.','Thank a teacher, or give to a school, library or craft guild.','Learn or practise a skill for an hour with full attention.','Finish a piece of handwork you\u2019ve left aside.'],ritual:{premade:'cosmic-wednesday-mercury-study',title:'A desk and tool blessing for Minerva\u2019s feast'},correspondences:{deities:['Minerva','Mars','Athena'],herbs:['Rosemary','Sage','Bay'],crystals:['Fluorite','Lapis Lazuli','Sodalite'],colors:['Blue Candle','Yellow Candle'],foods:['Olives','Honey'],symbols:['Owl','Olive','Loom','Tools of a trade']},sources:[n(3)+' (3.809\u2013848)',"Varro, De Lingua Latina 6.12\u201326 (Latin text): https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/varro.ll6.html (6.14)",o,'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quinquatria']},{ref:'roman-tubilustrium',title:'Tubilustrium',aka:['Purification of the Trumpets'],tradition:t,summary:'The purifying of the sacred trumpets used in ritual, on the last day of the Quinquatrus; repeated on 23 May.',honours:'Mars, and perhaps Nerio, his consort; the trumpets (tubae) themselves, which called the people to sacrifices and to war.',history:"Varro explains the day in one line: the trumpets of the sacred rites were purified in the Hall of the Shoemakers (Atrium Sutorium). A ewe lamb was sacrificed. The trumpets, like the shields of the Salii and the weapons purified at the Armilustrium in October, had to be ritually clean before the season\u2019s work.\n\nOvid attaches the day to Minerva, as the close of her Quinquatrus, and to Mars. The same rite was repeated on 23 May.",knowAndDont:{known:'The dates, the place, the purification of ritual trumpets and the lamb, from Varro (6.14) and the calendars.',unknown:'The exact rite of purification and the god it was offered to, which the sources leave unclear.'},celebrate:['Clean and bless a musical instrument, a bell or anything you use to call or begin.','Clear the air of your home with sound: a bell, a drum, a singing bowl, or clapping in the corners.','Ring something to open the next season\u2019s work.'],ritual:{title:'Clearing with sound',outline:['Open a window.','Wipe down your instrument, bell or chime with clean water.','Sound it once in each corner of the room, moving clockwise.','Sound it at the centre and say what the coming season\u2019s work is for.','Close the window and put the instrument where you will see it.']},correspondences:{deities:['Mars','Minerva'],herbs:['Bay','Rosemary'],colors:['Red Candle','Gold Candle'],symbols:['Trumpet','Bell']},sources:["Varro, De Lingua Latina 6.12\u201326 (Latin text): https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/varro.ll6.html (6.14)",n(3)+' (3.849\u2013850)',o]},{ref:'roman-veneralia',title:'Veneralia',aka:['Festival of Venus Verticordia','Kalends of April'],tradition:t,summary:'The feast of Venus Verticordia, \u201cturner of hearts\u201d, and Fortuna Virilis on 1 April: women washed the goddess\u2019s statue, decked it with flowers, and bathed crowned with myrtle.',honours:'Venus, goddess of love, beauty and desire, here as Verticordia, who turns hearts towards virtue; and Fortuna Virilis, Fortune who smiles on women in their dealings with men. April, said the Romans, was her month.',history:"Ovid tells the women of Rome to take the golden necklaces off the goddess\u2019s statue, wash it, dry it, put the jewels back and give her fresh flowers and roses. Then they themselves bathed, crowned with myrtle, and offered incense to Fortuna Virilis, so that any flaw of the body would be hidden from men\u2019s eyes. They also drank a mix of poppy, milk and honey, as Venus did when she was first led to her husband.\n\nThe temple of Venus Verticordia was built in 114 BCE after a scandal involving three Vestal Virgins, when the Sibylline books advised it. Whether married women and poorer women kept the feast in different ways (one bathing in the men\u2019s baths, as one source has it) is debated.",knowAndDont:{known:'The shape of the rite, from Ovid (Fasti 4.133\u2013164), and the founding of Verticordia\u2019s temple (Valerius Maximus 8.15.12).',unknown:'Exactly which groups of women did what; the history of Fortuna Virilis and how she came to share the day.'},celebrate:['Wash and dress a statue, image or candle of Venus, and give her fresh flowers.','Take a slow, cleansing bath with rose petals and a sprig of myrtle or rosemary.','Spend time on care of your body that is for you, not for anyone\u2019s eyes.','Ask Venus to turn your heart toward what is good for it.'],ritual:{premade:'cosmic-friday-venus-love',title:'A rose bath for Venus Verticordia'},correspondences:{deities:['Venus','Fortuna Virilis'],herbs:['Rose','Jasmine','Vanilla'],crystals:['Rose Quartz','Emerald'],colors:['Pink Candle','Green Candle'],foods:['Milk and honey'],symbols:['Myrtle crown','Mirror','Dove']},sources:[n(4)+' (4.133\u2013164)','Valerius Maximus 8.15.12',o]},{ref:'roman-megalesia',title:'Megalesia',aka:['Ludi Megalenses','Games of the Great Mother','Megalensia'],tradition:t,summary:'Seven days of games (4\u201310 April) for the Magna Mater, Cybele, brought to Rome from Phrygia in 204 BCE: processions to drum and cymbal, plays in front of her temple, and dinner parties among the leading families.',honours:'The Magna Mater, the Great Mother of the gods, the Phrygian Cybele of Mount Ida, mistress of wild beasts and of mountain places, drawn in a chariot by lions. Her consort Attis came with her worship.',history:"In 204 BCE, during the war with Hannibal, the Sibylline books and the oracle at Delphi told Rome to bring the Mother of the gods from Asia Minor. Her sacred black stone was shipped from Pessinus by way of Pergamon. Ovid tells how the ship stuck in the Tiber mud until the noblewoman Claudia Quinta, whose chastity had been doubted, drew it free with her girdle. Her temple on the Palatine was dedicated in 191 BCE.\n\nHer priests, the galli, were eunuchs who castrated themselves in devotion, and Roman citizens were at first forbidden to join them. They processed through the streets begging alms, with frenzied music. The Roman elite, meanwhile, held the games in her honour with plays (several of Terence\u2019s comedies were first performed at them) and gave each other dinners, the mutitationes. Ovid says moretum, a pounded dish of herbs and cheese, was set before the goddess because the earliest people ate the earth\u2019s herbs.\n\nThe later great spring festival of Cybele and Attis, with mourning and rejoicing in late March, belongs to the Empire and is a separate thing.",knowAndDont:{known:'The arrival of the goddess in 204 BCE (Livy 29.10\u201314), the temple, the games and plays, the dinners, and the galli, from Livy, Ovid (Fasti 4.179\u2013372), Cicero and others.',unknown:'How the Phrygian rites were adapted for Rome in detail; how far the galli\u2019s practice was shared with or hidden from ordinary Roman worshippers.'},celebrate:['Host a dinner for friends, as the Roman families did, and return the invitation through the year.','Make moretum, a garlic, herb and cheese spread (Virgil\u2019s Moretum poem gives the idea), and offer the first spoonful to the Mother.','Play or listen to drumming and cymbals, music that moves the body.','See a play or read one aloud with friends.','Honour the wild places: the mountains, the untamed land, the lions of her chariot.'],ritual:{title:'A drum for the Great Mother',outline:['Set out an image of a lion, a mountain or a black stone for the Mother, with a candle.','Light the candle safely and offer a little moretum, honey or bread.','Drum, clap or shake a rattle for a few minutes, letting the rhythm build.','In the quiet after, speak your thanks for the earth that holds you up.','Snuff the candle and share the food with others.']},correspondences:{deities:['Magna Mater (Cybele)','Attis'],herbs:['Garlic','Rosemary','Myrrh'],crystals:['Obsidian','Jet','Garnet'],colors:['Red Candle','Purple Candle'],foods:['Moretum (herb and cheese)','Bread','Honey'],symbols:['Lions','Turreted crown','Drum and cymbals','Black stone','Pine']},sources:['Livy 29.10\u201314, 36.36',n(4)+' (4.179\u2013372)',o,h,'L. Roller, In Search of God the Mother: The Cult of Anatolian Cybele (California, 1999)']},{ref:'roman-cerialia',title:'Cerialia',aka:['Ludi Ceriales','Games of Ceres'],tradition:t,summary:'The games and festival of Ceres, goddess of grain and growing things (12\u201319 April), ending with the Cerialia proper, when foxes with burning torches were let loose in the Circus.',honours:'Ceres, goddess of the grain, of the growing crops and of the laws of the earth, mother of Proserpina; identified with the Greek Demeter. Her temple on the Aventine, shared with Liber and Libera, was the heart of the Roman plebs and held their archives.',history:"The games ran from 12 April, with the last and main day, the Cerialia, on the 19th, when there were races in the Circus Maximus. Ovid explains a strange custom of that day with a story from Carseoli: a boy wrapped a fox in straw and set it alight, and it ran through the crops and burned them; foxes were burned in the Circus in memory. Modern readers see a rite to protect the grain from blight or fire, but this is a guess.\n\nOvid also says Ceres is happy with little: a little spelt, a pinch of salt, grains of incense, and no ox, since the ox works the fields. People wore white for her. Her worship was closely tied to the plebs: the aediles of the plebs ran her temple, and offenders against the plebeian tribunes could be made forfeit to her.",knowAndDont:{known:'The dates, the games and races, the fox rite and the white clothing, from Ovid (Fasti 4.393\u2013620, 4.679\u2013712) and the calendars; the temple\u2019s plebeian role from Livy and others.',unknown:'The real meaning of the foxes. How much of the cult was originally Italian and how much Greek, since her priestesses were brought in from Greek cities.'},celebrate:['Wear white, and offer Ceres spelt or bread, a pinch of salt and a little incense.','Sow seeds, tend a garden or a windowsill pot, or support a local farmer.','Give to a food bank: Ceres\u2019s temple was the people\u2019s.','Bake bread from scratch, kneading slowly.','Keep the fox safe: protect, don\u2019t harm.'],ritual:{premade:'green-seed-blessing-before-sowing',title:'Seed blessing for Ceres'},correspondences:{deities:['Ceres','Liber Pater','Libera','Proserpina','Demeter','Persephone'],herbs:['Calendula','Meadowsweet','Frankincense'],crystals:['Citrine','Moss Agate'],colors:['White Candle','Gold Candle','Green Candle'],foods:['Spelt','Bread','Salt','Honey'],symbols:['Wheat ears','Torch','Poppy','Fox']},sources:[n(4)+' (4.393\u2013620, 4.679\u2013712)',"Varro, De Lingua Latina 6.12\u201326 (Latin text): https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/varro.ll6.html (6.15)",o,'B. Spaeth, The Roman Goddess Ceres (Texas, 1996)']},{ref:'roman-fordicidia',title:'Fordicidia',aka:['Hordicidia'],tradition:t,summary:'A pregnant cow was offered to Tellus, Mother Earth, in each of the thirty curiae and on the Capitol, for the fertility of fields and herds; the eldest Vestal burned the unborn calves and kept the ashes for the Parilia.',honours:'Tellus, the Earth, in April when she too is \u201cpregnant\u201d with the seed, as Ovid says.',history:"The name comes from forda, a cow in calf (Varro). Ovid gives the details: thirty-one cows, one for each curia and one on the Capitol; the unborn calves were taken by attendants and burned by the senior Vestal, and the ash was kept to be mixed into the suffimen, the purifying fumigation, used at the Parilia six days later. He also tells how King Numa learned the rite in a dream sent by Faunus during a famine.\n\nIt is one of the oldest and starkest Roman rites, and it ties three April festivals together: Fordicidia, Cerialia and Parilia. Modern Roman polytheists do not repeat the sacrifice and instead make bloodless offerings to the Earth.",knowAndDont:{known:'The rite as Ovid describes it (Fasti 4.629\u2013672), the name (Varro 6.15) and the use of the ash at the Parilia.',unknown:'What prayers were said; how the curiae shared the meat; how much Ovid\u2019s story of Numa reflects old tradition.'},celebrate:['Make a bloodless offering to the Earth: milk, grain, flowers or compost given to the soil.','Sit or lie on the ground for a while and feel what is growing beneath you.','Support farmers who treat their animals and land well.','Collect a little ash from a safe hearth or incense to keep for the Parilia.'],ritual:{premade:'green-land-spirit-garden-offering',title:'An offering to the Earth'},correspondences:{deities:['Tellus','Vesta','Faunus'],herbs:['Dandelion','Comfrey'],crystals:['Moss Agate','Smoky Quartz'],colors:['Green Candle','Brown Candle'],foods:['Milk','Grain'],symbols:['Cow','Ash','Furrow']},sources:[n(4)+' (4.629\u2013672)',"Varro, De Lingua Latina 6.12\u201326 (Latin text): https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/varro.ll6.html (6.15)",o]},{ref:'roman-parilia',title:'Parilia',aka:['Palilia','Natale di Roma','Birthday of Rome','Dies Natalis Romae'],tradition:t,summary:'The festival of Pales, protector of shepherds and flocks, and the birthday of Rome: sheepfolds were swept and smoked, and people and flocks leapt through bonfires of straw to be purified.',honours:'Pales, the guardian of shepherds and their flocks, whose gender even the Romans were unsure of; and Rome itself, founded, by tradition, on this day in 753 BCE.',history:"Ovid, who says he leapt the fires himself, gives a full account. People went to the Vestals for the suffimen, a mix of the ash of the calves burned at the Fordicidia, the blood of the October Horse and bean stalks. At dawn the shepherd sprinkled his sheep with water, swept the fold and decked it with branches and garlands, burned sulphur so the sheep bleated in the smoke, and offered millet cakes and milk to Pales. Facing east, he prayed four times, asking pardon for anything done unknowingly: grazing a sacred grove, disturbing a spring, sitting under a holy tree. Then he washed his hands in dew, drank milk and wine, and leapt through the burning straw.\n\nIt was also Rome\u2019s birthday, and it is still celebrated in Rome as the Natale di Roma, with parades of historical re-enactors on 21 April each year.",knowAndDont:{known:'The rural rite in detail from Ovid (Fasti 4.721\u2013862) and Propertius 4.4; the suffimen; the link with Rome\u2019s founding.',unknown:'Pales\u2019s gender and origin; how the shepherd rite became the city\u2019s birthday.'},celebrate:['Clean your home or the place where your animals live, and freshen it with branches and flowers.','Ask pardon for any harm done unknowingly to the land, its waters and its trees.','Bless your pets or any animals in your care with clean water and a kind word.','Don\u2019t leap a bonfire: walk past a candle flame or a small, contained fire pit instead, with water at hand.','Raise a glass of milk to Rome\u2019s birthday, or watch the Natale di Roma parade online.'],ritual:{title:'A purifying smoke for home and animals',outline:['Sweep and tidy the room or the animals\u2019 space, and decorate it with a green branch.','Light a little incense or a bundle of bay or rosemary in a fire-safe dish; keep it away from pets\u2019 noses and open a window.','Carry the smoke around the space, asking Pales to keep the household and its creatures healthy.','Offer a little milk and millet or plain biscuits on a dish.','Ask pardon for any harm done unknowingly to the land, then wash your hands in cool water.','Pass your hands over a candle flame once (not through it), and snuff the candle.']},correspondences:{deities:['Pales','Vesta','Romulus (Quirinus)'],herbs:['Bay','Rosemary','Juniper'],crystals:['Red Jasper','Carnelian'],colors:['Red Candle','White Candle','Green Candle'],foods:['Milk','Millet cakes','Wine'],symbols:['Straw bonfire','Sheepfold','She-wolf']},sources:[n(4)+' (4.721\u2013862)','Propertius 4.4.73\u201378',"Varro, De Lingua Latina 6.12\u201326 (Latin text): https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/varro.ll6.html (6.15)",o,'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parilia']},{ref:'roman-vinalia',title:'Vinalia Priora',aka:['First Vinalia','Vinalia Urbana'],tradition:t,summary:'The wine festival of Jupiter: last year\u2019s wine was opened and its first draught poured for him; Venus too was honoured, especially by the women of the streets at her temple by the Colline Gate.',honours:'Jupiter, to whom the first of the new wine belonged, and Venus, who in Ovid\u2019s version shares the day.',history:"Varro says plainly that the day belongs to Jupiter, not Venus, even though many think otherwise. The wine pressed the autumn before was tasted for the first time, and the first of it poured as a libation to Jupiter before anyone could sell or drink it.\n\nOvid tells two stories. One is of the Etruscan king Mezentius, who demanded the first of the Latins\u2019 wine, so that they vowed it to Jupiter instead and won. The other is of the temple of Venus Erycina outside the Colline Gate, dedicated in 181 BCE, where the prostitutes of Rome came with myrtle, mint, rushes and roses to pray for beauty and favour.",knowAndDont:{known:'The date, Jupiter\u2019s claim to the first wine (Varro 6.16), and the cult of Venus Erycina on the day (Ovid, Fasti 4.863\u2013900).',unknown:'The exact rite of the libation, and how Venus\u2019s share in the festival came about.'},celebrate:['Open a bottle you have been keeping, or a fresh juice, and pour the first of it out for Jupiter.','Give the first and best of something to the gods before you enjoy it yourself.','Offer Venus myrtle, mint and roses.','If you don\u2019t drink, use grape juice: the offering is the first share, not the alcohol.'],ritual:{title:'The first cup',outline:['Set out a cup, the bottle unopened, and a dish or patch of earth to pour into.','Wash your hands and light a candle safely.','Open the bottle and pour the first cup, all of it, for Jupiter, naming what you are thankful for from the past year.','Then pour a cup for yourself and drink.','Lay a sprig of mint or a rose for Venus beside the candle.']},correspondences:{deities:['Jupiter','Venus'],herbs:['Mint','Rose','Bay'],crystals:['Amethyst'],colors:['Purple Candle','Gold Candle'],foods:['Wine or grape juice'],symbols:['Wine jar','Myrtle','Thunderbolt']},sources:["Varro, De Lingua Latina 6.12\u201326 (Latin text): https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/varro.ll6.html (6.16)",n(4)+' (4.863\u2013900)','Pliny, Natural History 18.287',o]},{ref:'roman-robigalia',title:'Robigalia',aka:['Feast of Robigus'],tradition:t,summary:'Offerings to Robigus, the power of the red rust that blights grain, to keep it from the young wheat, at a grove on the Via Claudia.',honours:'Robigus (or Robigo), the spirit of wheat rust and mildew, worshipped not out of love but to be turned away: Romans honoured some powers so that they would keep their distance.',history:"Ovid says he met the procession on his way back from Nomentum: the flamen Quirinalis, in white, was going to the grove of Robigus to burn the entrails of a dog and a sheep with incense and wine. He gives the priest\u2019s prayer, which asks the harsh goddess to spare the crops and turn her rust to swords and iron instead. Varro says simply that sacrifice was made to Robigus so that rust would not seize the crops. There were also races for men and boys.\n\nRed dogs were sacrificed in other rites for the crops as well, perhaps because their colour matched the rust. The Christian Major Litany, with its procession for the crops on 25 April, may have taken over the day; scholars have long suggested so, though it cannot be proved.",knowAndDont:{known:'The date, the priest, the grove at the fifth milestone, the sacrifice and the prayer (Ovid, Fasti 4.901\u2013942; Varro 6.16).',unknown:'How old the cult was and whether the Christian rogation procession was its direct successor.'},celebrate:['Walk your garden or a nearby field, and check young plants for rust, mildew and pests.','Treat plant diseases gently and organically; clear away infected leaves.','Ask whatever might harm what you are growing, in the garden or in life, to pass you by.','Make a bloodless offering of incense and a little wine at the edge of the garden.'],ritual:{title:'Turning blight away',outline:['Go to the edge of your garden or the pots you are growing.','Light a little incense in a fire-safe holder.','Pour a few drops of wine or juice on the ground, outside the growing area.','Ask Robigus to stay away from the crops this year, and name what you want to keep safe.','Look over each plant carefully and tend what needs it.']},correspondences:{deities:['Robigus','Ceres','Quirinus'],herbs:['Frankincense','Garlic','Yarrow'],crystals:['Moss Agate','Green Aventurine'],colors:['White Candle','Green Candle'],foods:['Wine','Incense'],symbols:['Wheat with red rust','Grove','Dog']},sources:[n(4)+' (4.901\u2013942)',"Varro, De Lingua Latina 6.12\u201326 (Latin text): https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/varro.ll6.html (6.16)",'Pliny, Natural History 18.284\u2013285',o]},{ref:'roman-mercuralia',title:'Mercuralia',aka:['Feast of Mercury','Ides of May'],tradition:t,summary:'The festival of Mercury on the Ides of May, the birthday of his temple: merchants dipped laurel in water from his spring and sprinkled themselves and their goods for luck in trade.',honours:'Mercury, god of merchants, trade, travel and messages, identified with the Greek Hermes; and his mother Maia, after whom May was named, who also received a sacrifice on the day.',history:"Mercury\u2019s temple by the Circus Maximus was dedicated on 15 May 495 BCE, and the merchants\u2019 guild met there. Ovid describes a spring of Mercury near the Porta Capena: the merchant, belted, draws water in a jar, dips a laurel branch, sprinkles the goods he is selling and his own head, and prays, with a wink from the poet, that Mercury will wash away the lies he has told and the oaths he has broken, and bring him profit.\n\nThe Ides of May was also the day of the Argei, when the Vestals threw rush figures of men from the Pons Sublicius into the Tiber, a rite as puzzling to the Romans as it is to us.",knowAndDont:{known:'The temple date (Livy 2.21, 2.27), the water rite and the merchant\u2019s prayer (Ovid, Fasti 5.663\u2013692).',unknown:'Whether the water rite was a public ritual or a merchants\u2019 custom; much about the Argei on the same day.'},celebrate:['Bless your workplace, shop, tools or online business with water and a sprig of bay.','Promise honesty in your dealings for the coming month: the reverse of Ovid\u2019s merchant.','Send messages you owe, and pay small debts.','Plan or bless a journey.'],ritual:{title:'Mercury\u2019s water for honest trade',outline:['Fill a jar or bowl with fresh water, from a spring if you can.','Dip a bay sprig in it and sprinkle your wallet, tools, shop door or workspace.','Sprinkle a little on your own head.','Ask Mercury for fair dealing and good trade, and promise honesty in return.','Pour what\u2019s left on a plant.']},correspondences:{deities:['Mercury','Maia','Hermes'],herbs:['Bay','Lavender','Peppermint'],crystals:['Citrine','Green Aventurine','Fluorite'],colors:['Orange Candle','Yellow Candle'],foods:['Bread','Wine'],symbols:['Caduceus','Purse','Winged hat','Laurel sprinkler']},sources:[n(5)+' (5.663\u2013692)','Livy 2.21, 2.27',o]},{ref:'roman-matralia',title:'Matralia',aka:['Feast of Mater Matuta'],tradition:t,summary:'The festival of Mater Matuta, goddess of the dawn and of mothers, kept by women married only once, who prayed for their sisters\u2019 children before their own.',honours:'Mater Matuta, \u201cMother of the Morning\u201d, an old Italian goddess of the dawn, of growth and of children, identified by the Romans with the Greek Ino, who became the sea-goddess Leucothea.',history:"Her temple stood in the Forum Boarium, next to that of Fortuna. Only univirae, women in their first marriage, could garland her image. Ovid says they baked golden cakes in earthenware pots (Varro calls them testuacia) for her, and that a slave-woman was led into the temple and then driven out with blows, which Ovid explains by Ino\u2019s jealousy of a slave who was her husband\u2019s lover. The women prayed first for their sisters\u2019 children.\n\nPlutarch was puzzled by both customs, and modern scholars still argue about them. Some see the care of nieces and nephews as a trace of an older family structure; others as the bonds of sisters that held Roman families together.",knowAndDont:{known:'The temple, the univirae, the cakes, the expelled slave-woman and the prayers for sisters\u2019 children (Ovid, Fasti 6.473\u2013568; Plutarch, Roman Questions 16\u201317; Varro 5.106).',unknown:'The original meaning of the customs, and how Mater Matuta was understood before her identification with Ino.'},celebrate:['Get up for the dawn and watch the light come.','Do something for your nieces, nephews, godchildren or your siblings\u2019 families.','Bake small golden cakes and share them.','Thank the women who have mothered you, by blood or by care.'],ritual:{title:'A dawn prayer for the children of the family',outline:['Rise before sunrise and face east with a candle and a small cake.','Light the candle as the sky brightens.','Name the children of your siblings, friends or community, one by one, asking Mater Matuta to watch over them.','Then name your own, if you have them.','Offer the cake and snuff the candle once the sun is up.']},correspondences:{deities:['Mater Matuta','Leucothea','Fortuna'],herbs:['Calendula','Chamomile'],crystals:['Sunstone','Moonstone'],colors:['Yellow Candle','Pink Candle'],foods:['Golden cakes baked in earthenware'],symbols:['Dawn','Mother and child','Sea']},sources:[n(6)+' (6.473\u2013568)',"Plutarch, Roman Questions 16\u201317: Plutarch, Roman Questions (Loeb, 1936), at LacusCurtius: https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Moralia/Roman_Questions*/home.html",'Varro, De Lingua Latina 5.106',o]},{ref:'roman-fors-fortuna',title:'Fors Fortuna',aka:['Feast of Lucky Fortune'],tradition:t,summary:'The festival of Fors Fortuna, goddess of luck, at her temples across the Tiber: ordinary people and the enslaved floated down the river in garlanded boats to her shrines and drank on the way back.',honours:'Fors Fortuna, Fortune as pure chance and good luck, worshipped especially by the common people. Her temples were said to have been founded by King Servius Tullius, himself born, the story went, of an enslaved mother.',history:"Her temples stood on the right bank of the Tiber, at the first and sixth milestones of the road to Portus. Ovid tells the people to go there joyfully, some on foot, some by boat, and not to be ashamed to come home tipsy, garlanded young men drinking in the boats. Because Servius Tullius was of humble birth, the festival belonged especially to the plebs and to the enslaved.\n\nThe date, close to the summer solstice, is also noted by the farm writer Columella, who mentions the gardeners\u2019 joyful holiday on the day.",knowAndDont:{known:'The temples, the boats and the merrymaking (Ovid, Fasti 6.773\u2013786), its popularity with the poor and enslaved, and the link with Servius Tullius (Varro 6.17).',unknown:'What rites took place at the temples themselves.'},celebrate:['Take a boat trip, or walk by a river, and float a small biodegradable flower offering for luck.','Treat friends who have less, or give to a cause for workers\u2019 rights.','Try your luck in some small way that harms nobody.','Picnic outdoors near the solstice.'],ritual:{premade:'sea-river-flower-offering',title:'A river offering for Fortune'},correspondences:{deities:['Fors Fortuna','Fortuna'],herbs:['Basil','Mint','Calendula'],crystals:['Green Aventurine','Citrine','Tiger Eye'],colors:['Green Candle','Gold Candle'],foods:['Wine','Picnic food'],symbols:['Wheel','Rudder','Cornucopia','Garlanded boat']},sources:[n(6)+' (6.773\u2013786)',"Varro, De Lingua Latina 6.12\u201326 (Latin text): https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/varro.ll6.html (6.17)",'Columella, On Agriculture 10 (the gardeners\u2019 holiday of Fors Fortuna)',o]},{ref:'roman-poplifugia',title:'Poplifugia',aka:['Flight of the People'],tradition:t,summary:'The \u201cflight of the people\u201d, an old festival of Jupiter whose meaning the Romans themselves explained in different ways.',honours:'Jupiter, by the calendars. Nothing else certain is known of the rite.',history:"Varro says the people fled in sudden panic on that day, probably, he thinks, before an attack by neighbouring peoples after the Gauls had sacked Rome. Plutarch and Dionysius tell instead that it was the day Romulus vanished in a storm, and the people scattered in terror. Macrobius and others link it with the Nonae Caprotinae two days later, when, the story went, slave-women saved Rome from a Latin attack.\n\nThe confusion itself is the main fact: by the late Republic, the Romans no longer knew what their own rite meant. Modern scholars have suggested a ritual flight like the Regifugium, but nobody knows.",knowAndDont:{known:'That 5 July was marked Poplifugia, a feast of Jupiter, in the calendars; Varro\u2019s and Plutarch\u2019s explanations.',unknown:'What the rite was or meant. Almost everything.'},celebrate:['Sit with not knowing: a whole festival survived as a name only.','Think about crowds and fear, and how a community recovers from panic.','Make a small offering to Jupiter for steadiness and good order.'],ritual:{title:'A steadying for Jupiter',outline:['Light a candle safely and sit quietly with your feet on the floor.','Breathe slowly until any rush or panic in you settles.','Offer a little wine or water to Jupiter for order and safety in your community.','Name one thing you can do to be steady for others when panic spreads.']},correspondences:{deities:['Jupiter'],crystals:['Smoky Quartz','Hematite'],colors:['Blue Candle','White Candle'],symbols:['Crowd','Storm']},sources:["Varro, De Lingua Latina 6.12\u201326 (Latin text): https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/varro.ll6.html (6.18)",'Plutarch, Romulus 27, Camillus 33',"Macrobius, Saturnalia 1.7\u20131.16 (1.11.36\u201340)",o]},{ref:'roman-ludi-apollinares',title:'Ludi Apollinares',aka:['Games of Apollo'],tradition:t,summary:'Games for Apollo (6\u201313 July), vowed in 212 BCE during the war with Hannibal: plays in the theatre and, on the last day, races in the Circus, with the people watching crowned with laurel.',honours:'Apollo, god of healing, prophecy, music and the laurel, one of the few Greek gods the Romans took over with his own name; and Latona, his mother.',history:"Livy tells how, in 212 BCE, the prophecies of a seer named Marcius advised games for Apollo to drive the enemy from Italy. They were held with sacrifices in the Greek manner, the people watched wearing garlands, and married women prayed at the temples. In 208 BCE, during a plague, they were made yearly and given a fixed date.\n\nApollo\u2019s temple in Rome stood in the Campus Martius, built in 431 BCE after another plague, for Apollo Medicus, the healer. Later Augustus raised a magnificent temple to Apollo on the Palatine, next to his own house. The games came to last eight days, mostly theatrical, with the races in the Circus on the 13th.",knowAndDont:{known:'The founding and the vow (Livy 25.12, 27.23), the dates, the plays and races, the laurel crowns.',unknown:'Which plays were performed in most years, and how the rites at the temple were carried out.'},celebrate:['Go to the theatre, a concert or an open-air play; or read a play aloud.','Wear or hang a laurel (bay) crown.','Give to a cause for public health or medical care.','Make music for Apollo.'],ritual:{title:'Laurel and music for Apollo',outline:['Weave a small crown or bundle of bay leaves.','Wash your hands and light a candle safely.','Offer a leaf or two, a little honey or a cake, and ask Apollo for health and clarity.','Play, sing or read aloud a poem for him.','Wear the crown for a while, then hang it in your home.']},correspondences:{deities:['Apollo','Latona'],herbs:['Bay','Saffron','Calendula'],crystals:['Sunstone','Citrine','Amber'],colors:['Gold Candle','Yellow Candle'],foods:['Honey cakes','Wine'],symbols:['Laurel crown','Lyre','Theatre mask']},sources:['Livy 25.12, 27.23',o,h]},{ref:'roman-lucaria',title:'Lucaria',aka:['Festival of the Grove'],tradition:t,summary:'The festival of the grove (lucus), on 19 and 21 July, perhaps to win the favour of the spirits of a wood before clearing it; little else is known.',honours:'The numina, the divine powers, of sacred groves. Romans believed a grove had its own presiding god or goddess, even when they did not know its name.',history:"Festus, our main source, says the Lucaria was held in a great grove between the Via Salaria and the Tiber, where the Romans had taken refuge after their defeat by the Gauls at the Allia in 390 BCE. Plutarch connects the word lucar, the money paid to actors, with revenues from sacred groves.\n\nCato, writing about farming, gives the prayer a farmer should say before thinning a grove: a pig was offered to the god or goddess to whom the grove was sacred, asking for favour on the household. The Lucaria may have had a similar purpose, but no text says so.",knowAndDont:{known:'The two dates and the grove by the Via Salaria (Festus); Cato\u2019s grove-thinning prayer (De Agri Cultura 139).',unknown:'The god or goddess honoured, the rite, and its true purpose; it is one of the most obscure festivals in the calendar.'},celebrate:['Go into a wood or park and sit quietly under the trees.','If you need to cut back a tree or hedge, ask its spirit first and give an offering.','Plant a tree, or support a woodland trust.','Pick up litter in a park or forest.'],ritual:{premade:'green-tree-befriending-offering',title:'Befriending the grove'},correspondences:{deities:['Silvanus','Diana','Faunus'],herbs:['Oak','Birch','Willow'],crystals:['Moss Agate','Jade'],colors:['Green Candle','Brown Candle'],symbols:['Grove','Oak leaf']},sources:['Festus, De Verborum Significatu, s.v. Lucaria',"Plutarch, Roman Questions 88: Plutarch, Roman Questions (Loeb, 1936), at LacusCurtius: https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Moralia/Roman_Questions*/home.html",'Cato, De Agri Cultura 139',o]},{ref:'roman-lucaria-2',title:'Lucaria (second day)',aka:['Lucaria II'],tradition:t,summary:'The second day of the festival of the grove, two days after the first. See the Lucaria.',honours:'The spirits of sacred groves.',history:"The calendars mark both 19 and 21 July as Lucaria. As with other paired festivals, the reason for two days is not recorded. The fuller page is on the Lucaria, 19 July.",knowAndDont:{known:'That both days were marked.',unknown:'Why there were two, or how the second differed.'},celebrate:['Return to the wood or tree you visited on the first day and leave a little water at its roots.','Spend some time outdoors under leaves.'],ritual:{premade:'green-tree-befriending-offering',title:'See the Lucaria'},correspondences:{herbs:['Oak'],colors:['Green Candle'],symbols:['Grove']},sources:[o]},{ref:'roman-furrinalia',title:'Furrinalia',aka:['Feast of Furrina'],tradition:t,summary:'The feast of Furrina, an ancient goddess with a grove and spring on the Janiculum; by Varro\u2019s time hardly anyone remembered who she was, though she kept her own flamen.',honours:'Furrina, a goddess so old that she had one of the fifteen flamens, the priests of the oldest cults, yet so forgotten that Varro wrote her name was known to few.',history:"Varro says simply that the Furrinalia is named for Furrina, for whom the day is a public festival, and that the flamen Furrinalis kept her rites, though her name was now barely known. Her grove, the Lucus Furrinae, lay on the slopes of the Janiculum, across the Tiber; it was there that Gaius Gracchus died in 121 BCE. Cicero, guessing, linked her with the Furies.\n\nInscriptions from the site under the Empire speak of nymphs, Furrinae, which suggests a goddess, or goddesses, of a spring. The site later held a sanctuary of Syrian gods, whose ruins have been excavated.",knowAndDont:{known:'Her name, her flamen, her grove and its spring, from Varro (5.84, 6.19), Cicero, Plutarch and inscriptions.',unknown:'Who she was and what her worship involved. She is a lesson in how gods can be forgotten.'},celebrate:['Visit a spring, a fountain or a stream, and leave a flower.','Remember a forgotten god, ancestor or tradition: learn its name and say it aloud.','Write down something about your own practice that you would not want lost.'],ritual:{title:'For the forgotten goddess',outline:['Pour fresh water into a bowl and set a candle beside it.','Light the candle safely and say Furrina\u2019s name three times.','Admit honestly that we don\u2019t know who she was, and offer the water and a flower anyway.','Name any other forgotten powers or people you want remembered.','Pour the water on a plant or into the earth outside.']},correspondences:{deities:['Furrina','Nymphs'],herbs:['Willow','Mint'],crystals:['Aquamarine'],colors:['Blue Candle','Grey Candle'],symbols:['Spring','Grove']},sources:["Varro, De Lingua Latina 6.12\u201326 (Latin text): https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/varro.ll6.html (6.19)",'Cicero, On the Nature of the Gods 3.46','Plutarch, Gaius Gracchus 17',o,'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Furrina']},{ref:'roman-diana',title:'Ides of August: Diana',aka:['Nemoralia','Festival of Torches','Dies natalis of Diana on the Aventine','Servorum dies festus'],tradition:t,summary:'The birthday of Diana\u2019s temple on the Aventine and a holiday for the enslaved; women washed their hair, and at her grove by Lake Nemi worshippers came with torches.',honours:'Diana, goddess of the wild woods, of hunting, of the moon and of women in childbirth; in the Aventine temple a protector of the Latin peoples and of the lowly, since the temple was said to have been founded by Servius Tullius. At Nemi she was Diana Nemorensis, \u201cof the grove\u201d, with the water-nymph Egeria and the hero Virbius.',history:"Festus calls the Ides of August a holiday of slaves, because Servius Tullius, born of an enslaved mother, dedicated Diana\u2019s temple on the Aventine that day. Plutarch asks why women washed their hair on the Ides of August, and suggests it was for the same reason.\n\nAt Aricia, by the volcanic lake of Nemi in the Alban hills, Diana had a great sanctuary in a grove. Ovid and Propertius describe women coming there with torches, garlanded, to give thanks for prayers answered. The poet Statius, writing around 90 CE, says that on the Ides of August the whole grove shone with torches, hunting dogs were garlanded and rested, and the wild animals left in peace. Votive offerings from Nemi include many models of wombs, breasts and babies, showing her care for childbirth.\n\n\u201cNemoralia\u201d is a modern name for this festival, popular among pagans and witches today, who often keep it with candles and moon-lit vows, sometimes at the August full moon. The ancient evidence supports the torches, the rest and the holiday; the specific modern rituals are new.",knowAndDont:{known:'The Aventine temple\u2019s day and the slaves\u2019 holiday (Festus), the hair-washing (Plutarch, Roman Questions 100), the torch procession at Nemi (Ovid, Fasti 3.261\u2013272; Propertius 2.32; Statius, Silvae 3.1.52\u201360), and the votive offerings.',unknown:'Whether the torchlit pilgrimage was kept on this one day or at other times too. The name \u201cNemoralia\u201d for the day is modern, as are its present-day rites. The story of the \u201cKing of the Grove\u201d at Nemi, made famous by Frazer, is real but unrelated to this date.'},celebrate:['Give yourself, and anyone who works for you, a day off.','Wash your hair as an act of devotion, and dress it with flowers.','Light candles or lanterns at a window or by water at dusk; never leave them burning unattended.','Walk in a wood or by a lake, and let wild things be.','Give thanks for a prayer answered, or make a vow to Diana for a birth or a hope.'],ritual:{title:'Torches for Diana',outline:['At dusk, wash your hair or your face in cool water.','Set candles or lanterns safely by a bowl of water, or by a window facing the moon.','Light them one by one, each for something you want to thank Diana for or ask her for.','Tie a ribbon to a branch or to the bowl as your vow.','Sit in the quiet of the light for a while, then snuff the candles.']},correspondences:{deities:['Diana','Egeria','Virbius'],herbs:['Mugwort','Willow','Jasmine'],crystals:['Moonstone','Selenite','Labradorite'],colors:['Silver Candle','White Candle'],foods:['Apples','Honey cakes'],symbols:['Torch','Crescent moon','Hunting dog','Deer','Lake']},sources:['Festus, De Verborum Significatu, s.v. Servorum dies',"Plutarch, Roman Questions 100: Plutarch, Roman Questions (Loeb, 1936), at LacusCurtius: https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Moralia/Roman_Questions*/home.html",n(3)+' (3.261\u2013272)','Statius, Silvae 3.1.52\u201360','C. M. C. Green, Roman Religion and the Cult of Diana at Aricia (Cambridge, 2007)','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nemoralia']},{ref:'roman-portunalia',title:'Portunalia',aka:['Feast of Portunus'],tradition:t,summary:'The feast of Portunus, god of harbours, doors and keys, at his temple by the Tiber port.',honours:'Portunus, the god of the portus (harbour) and the porta (gate or door), shown holding a key; later identified with the Greek Palaemon, a sea god who helped sailors. He had his own flamen, so his worship was very old.',history:"Varro says the day is named for Portunus, whose temple was built in the Tiber port and whose festival was then established. The small, beautifully preserved Ionic temple in the Forum Boarium beside the river, long called the Temple of Fortuna Virilis, is now usually identified as his.\n\nFestus records that his flamen anointed the weapons of Quirinus with oil, a rite whose day and meaning are unclear. Many modern books say that on the Portunalia keys were thrown into a fire for luck; we have not found an ancient source for this, so treat it with caution.",knowAndDont:{known:'The date, the temple by the river port, the flamen, and Portunus as god of harbours and keys (Varro 6.19; Festus).',unknown:'What the rite involved. The often-repeated custom of throwing keys into the fire lacks a clear ancient source.'},celebrate:['Bless your keys and your front door.','Oil a stiff lock or hinge, as an act of care for your thresholds.','Visit a harbour, a river port or a canal, and give thanks for safe arrivals.','Bless a journey by water.'],ritual:{premade:'kitchen-threshold-salt-blessing',title:'A blessing for the door and its keys'},correspondences:{deities:['Portunus','Janus','Neptune'],herbs:['Rosemary','Bay'],crystals:['Aquamarine','Hematite'],colors:['Blue Candle','Silver Candle'],symbols:['Key','Harbour','Door']},sources:["Varro, De Lingua Latina 6.12\u201326 (Latin text): https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/varro.ll6.html (6.19)",'Festus, s.v. Portunus, persillum',o,'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portunus_(mythology)']},{ref:'roman-vinalia-rustica',title:'Vinalia Rustica',aka:['Country Vinalia','Second Vinalia'],tradition:t,summary:'The country wine festival: the priest of Jupiter opened the grape harvest by cutting the first grapes, praying for the vintage.',honours:'Jupiter, master of the weather that makes or ruins a vintage; and Venus, two of whose temples were dedicated on this day.',history:"Varro says the flamen Dialis, the priest of Jupiter, began the grape harvest: he sacrificed a ewe lamb and, between cutting the meat and offering it, picked the first grapes. Until this was done, no new grapes could be brought into the city. The rite asked Jupiter to protect the ripening vines from storms in the last weeks before harvest.\n\nTemples of Venus were dedicated on the day, among them Venus Obsequens in 295 BCE, and the gardeners kept it as a holiday. The April Vinalia opened last year\u2019s wine; this one opened the new harvest.",knowAndDont:{known:'The flamen Dialis\u2019s auspicatio vindemiae, the first picking of grapes (Varro 6.16), and the dedications to Venus.',unknown:'How Venus came to be linked with the Vinaliae, a question the Romans themselves discussed.'},celebrate:['Eat the first grapes, plums or blackberries of the season, and offer the first to Jupiter.','Pray for good weather for the harvest, and for farmers facing storms or drought.','Visit a vineyard, orchard or farm market.'],ritual:{title:'The first grapes',outline:['Set a bunch of grapes or the first ripe fruit of the season on a plate.','Wash your hands and light a candle safely.','Cut the first grape and set it aside for Jupiter, asking him to guard the harvest from storms.','Eat the rest with thanks, and share them.','Leave Jupiter\u2019s portion outside on the earth.']},correspondences:{deities:['Jupiter','Venus'],herbs:['Bay','Oak'],crystals:['Amethyst'],colors:['Purple Candle','Green Candle'],foods:['Grapes','Wine or grape juice'],symbols:['Vine','Pruning knife']},sources:["Varro, De Lingua Latina 6.12\u201326 (Latin text): https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/varro.ll6.html (6.16, 6.20)",'Pliny, Natural History 18.289',o]},{ref:'roman-consualia',title:'Consualia',aka:['Feast of Consus'],tradition:t,summary:'The festival of Consus, god of the stored harvest, whose altar under the Circus was uncovered today; horses and mules rested and were garlanded, and there were races. (Kept again on 15 December.)',honours:'Consus, god of the grain safely stored, his name probably from condere, \u201cto store away\u201d; closely linked with Ops, the goddess of plenty, whose own feast followed four days later.',history:"Consus\u2019s altar stood underground in the Circus Maximus and was uncovered only on his feast days. Livy says it was at the Consualia that Romulus invited the Sabines to games and then seized their daughters, a founding story for Roman marriage. Plutarch says horses and mules were garlanded and rested on the day, and there were races of horses and mules.\n\nTertullian, a Christian writer, says that on 21 August the flamen Quirinalis and the Vestal Virgins sacrificed at the underground altar. The second Consualia in December, at the end of the autumn sowing, may have blessed the seed put into the earth as this one blessed the grain put into store.",knowAndDont:{known:'The hidden altar, the races, the resting and garlanding of animals (Plutarch, Roman Questions 48), the Sabine story (Livy 1.9) and the priests (Tertullian, De Spectaculis 5).',unknown:'Why the altar was underground; whether Consus was always a god of storage, or also of counsel, as some Romans thought.'},celebrate:['Give working animals, pets and yourself a day of rest; garland a pet\u2019s collar with a flower.','Fill your pantry, put up preserves or freeze a harvest.','Uncover something you have kept hidden or stored and take stock of it.','Watch or ride in a horse event, or simply visit horses.'],ritual:{title:'Blessing the store cupboard',outline:['Clear and wipe out a cupboard or pantry shelf.','Light a candle safely beside it.','Set a little of each stored food back in, naming it and giving thanks.','Offer a handful of grain or a crust of bread to Consus and Ops.','Close the cupboard and snuff the candle.']},correspondences:{deities:['Consus','Ops'],herbs:['Bay','Meadowsweet'],crystals:['Citrine','Pyrite'],colors:['Brown Candle','Gold Candle'],foods:['Grain','Bread','Preserves'],symbols:['Underground altar','Granary','Garlanded horse or mule']},sources:['Livy 1.9',"Plutarch, Roman Questions 48: Plutarch, Roman Questions (Loeb, 1936), at LacusCurtius: https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Moralia/Roman_Questions*/home.html",'Tertullian, De Spectaculis 5',"Varro, De Lingua Latina 6.12\u201326 (Latin text): https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/varro.ll6.html (6.20)",o]},{ref:'roman-volcanalia',title:'Volcanalia',aka:['Feast of Vulcan'],tradition:t,summary:'The festival of Vulcan, god of fire, in the dry season when fires threatened the granaries; small fish were thrown into his fire as offerings.',honours:'Vulcan (Volcanus), god of fire, both destructive and useful, and of the forge, identified with the Greek Hephaestus. His oldest shrine, the Volcanal, stood at the foot of the Capitol.',history:"Varro says the people threw living creatures into the fire on their own behalf. Festus, writing of a fishermen\u2019s festival in June, says small fish from the Tiber were taken to the Volcanal and offered there in place of human souls; scholars usually take the creatures of the Volcanalia to be fish of the same kind, though no source says so outright. Coming two days after the Consualia, with the harvest just stored, the feast asked the god of fire to spare the grain.\n\nPliny the Younger says his uncle, the elder Pliny, began studying by lamplight from the Volcanalia onwards, as if this were the day work moved indoors to the lamps. After the great fire of Rome in 64 CE, Domitian built altars to Vulcan in each region of the city, against future fires.",knowAndDont:{known:'The date, the Volcanal, the offering of living creatures (Varro 6.20), and the lamp custom (Pliny, Letters 3.5).',unknown:'Exactly what creatures were offered (fish is likely but inferred), and why; the prayers spoken.'},celebrate:['Check your smoke alarms, fire extinguisher and escape route.','Clean the stove, the chimney or the grill.','Give thanks for the fire that cooks your food and warms your house.','Begin an evening study habit by lamplight, as Pliny did.'],ritual:{title:'Thanking and taming the fire',outline:['Have water or a fire blanket at hand before you begin.','Light a single candle in a deep, stable holder.','Thank Vulcan for the fire that serves you, and ask him to keep the house, the harvest and the land from wildfire.','Offer a little grain or a piece of fish to the earth outside, not into the flame.','Snuff the candle and test your smoke alarm.']},correspondences:{deities:['Vulcan','Vesta'],herbs:['Dragon\u2019s Blood','Cinnamon'],crystals:['Carnelian','Garnet','Obsidian'],colors:['Red Candle','Orange Candle'],foods:['Small fish','Grain'],symbols:['Forge','Hammer and tongs','Lamp']},sources:["Varro, De Lingua Latina 6.12\u201326 (Latin text): https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/varro.ll6.html (6.20)",'Festus, s.v. piscatorii ludi','Pliny the Younger, Letters 3.5',o]},{ref:'roman-opiconsivia',title:'Opiconsivia',aka:['Opeconsiva','Feast of Ops Consiva'],tradition:t,summary:'The festival of Ops Consiva, goddess of plenty and of the harvest stored away, in her tiny shrine in the Regia, entered only by the Vestals and the public priest.',honours:'Ops, goddess of abundance and the wealth of the earth, wife of Saturn in Roman theology; here as Consiva, \u201cwho stores\u201d, partner of Consus.',history:"Varro says her shrine in the Regia was so sacred that only the Vestal Virgins and the public priest could enter it, and the priest went veiled in white. The feast came four days after the Consualia, just as Ops\u2019s winter feast, the Opalia, came four days after the December Consualia, a pairing scholars have noticed for a long time.\n\nNothing is known of the rite inside the shrine.",knowAndDont:{known:'The shrine in the Regia and who could enter it (Varro 6.21); the pairing with Consus.',unknown:'The rite itself, kept hidden even in antiquity.'},celebrate:['Count your blessings in a literal way: list what you have stored, saved and grown this year.','Give some of your plenty away.','Keep a small, private act of devotion that no one else needs to see.'],ritual:{title:'A hidden shrine of plenty',outline:['Choose a small box, jar or cupboard as a private shrine.','Cover your head with a white cloth if you like, as the priest did.','Place in it a little grain, a coin and a written list of what you are thankful for.','Light a candle beside it safely and ask Ops to keep your household in plenty.','Close it and keep it closed until the Opalia in December.']},correspondences:{deities:['Ops','Consus','Saturn'],herbs:['Bay','Cinnamon'],crystals:['Citrine','Pyrite','Green Aventurine'],colors:['Gold Candle','Green Candle'],foods:['Grain','Honey'],symbols:['Granary','Cornucopia','White veil']},sources:["Varro, De Lingua Latina 6.12\u201326 (Latin text): https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/varro.ll6.html (6.21)",o,'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ops']},{ref:'roman-ludi-romani',title:'Ludi Romani',aka:['Roman Games','Ludi Magni','Epulum Iovis'],tradition:t,summary:'The Roman Games for Jupiter Best and Greatest, the city\u2019s oldest games, with the Epulum Iovis, a feast laid for the Capitoline gods, on the Ides of September.',honours:'Jupiter Optimus Maximus, the chief god of Rome, with Juno and Minerva, the Capitoline triad, whose great temple was dedicated, by tradition, on the Ides of September in the first year of the Republic.',history:"Tradition traced the games to the kings. By Cicero\u2019s time they ran from 5 to 19 September, and a day was added in honour of Julius Caesar after his death, so they began on the 4th. They opened with a grand procession (the pompa circensis) from the Capitol to the Circus, with the images of the gods carried in it, and included chariot races, boxing and, from 364 BCE, plays.\n\nOn the Ides the Epulum Iovis was held: the images of Jupiter, Juno and Minerva were set on couches as at a dinner party and served a feast, while the senators dined on the Capitol. On the same day the consul, in early times, drove a nail into the wall of the temple to count the years.",knowAndDont:{known:'The dates and growth of the games, the procession, the Epulum Iovis (Livy, Dionysius of Halicarnassus 7.72, Cicero), and the nail ceremony (Livy 7.3).',unknown:'The real age of the games; how the earliest ones looked before they were expanded.'},celebrate:['Host a dinner and set a place for the gods: a small plate on the altar or at the head of the table.','Watch or play a sport, and dedicate it to Jupiter.','Mark the year: drive a nail, plant a bulb or make a notch to count it.','Give generously to a public good.'],ritual:{premade:'cosmic-thursday-jupiter-prosperity',title:'An open hand for Jupiter, and a place set at the feast'},correspondences:{deities:['Jupiter','Juno','Minerva'],herbs:['Oak','Bay','Sage'],crystals:['Lapis Lazuli','Sapphire','Amethyst'],colors:['Purple Candle','Blue Candle','Gold Candle'],foods:['A feast','Wine','Bread'],symbols:['Eagle','Thunderbolt','Chariot','Banquet couch']},sources:['Livy 7.2\u20133','Dionysius of Halicarnassus, Roman Antiquities 7.72','Cicero, In Verrem 2.5.36',o,h]},{ref:'roman-meditrinalia',title:'Meditrinalia',aka:['Feast of the new wine'],tradition:t,summary:'The new wine was first tasted, mixed with old, with the words \u201cI drink new and old wine; I am healed of new and old illness.\u201d',honours:'Jupiter, as the calendars suggest, and the healing power of the wine. Later writers named a goddess Meditrina, \u201cthe healer\u201d, perhaps invented to explain the festival\u2019s name.',history:"Varro gives the day and the words, in Latin: \u201cNovum vetus vinum bibo: novo veteri morbo medeor\u201d, \u201cI drink new and old wine; I cure new and old illness.\u201d He says the day is named from medendo, healing, and that the custom was kept in Latium. The new wine of the harvest was poured first as a libation and then tasted, mixed with last year\u2019s.\n\nFestus names the goddess Meditrina. No temple or other rite of hers is known.",knowAndDont:{known:'The date and the formula, from Varro (6.21).',unknown:'Whether Meditrina was ever a goddess before the name was explained by her; how the rite went beyond the tasting.'},celebrate:['Pour a little of last year\u2019s and this year\u2019s wine, juice or cider together, say Varro\u2019s words, and drink.','Name an old hurt and a new one, and ask for healing for both.','Make a healing broth or tea for someone who is ill.','If you avoid alcohol, use fresh grape juice and last year\u2019s preserve or jam.'],ritual:{title:'New and old',outline:['Pour a little old wine or juice and a little new into one cup.','Pour the first few drops on the ground as an offering.','Say: \u201cI drink new and old wine; I am healed of new and old illness.\u201d','Drink slowly, thinking of one old and one new wound.','Write down one step toward healing each.']},correspondences:{deities:['Jupiter','Meditrina'],herbs:['Bay','Thyme','Chamomile'],crystals:['Amethyst','Bloodstone'],colors:['Purple Candle','Green Candle'],foods:['New wine or grape juice','Old wine'],symbols:['Two cups','Vine']},sources:["Varro, De Lingua Latina 6.12\u201326 (Latin text): https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/varro.ll6.html (6.21, the formula)",'Festus, s.v. Meditrinalia',o]},{ref:'roman-fontinalia',title:'Fontinalia',aka:['Fontanalia','Feast of Fons'],tradition:t,summary:'The festival of Fons, god of springs: garlands were thrown into springs and wells, and wellheads were crowned with flowers.',honours:'Fons (or Fontus), god of springs and fresh water, called a son of Janus; and all the springs that fed the city before and alongside its aqueducts.',history:"Varro says the Fontinalia is named for Fons, whose feast day it is, \u201cand so they throw garlands into springs and crown wells.\u201d Fons had an altar on the Janiculum and a temple near the Porta Fontinalis, the \u201cspring gate\u201d, vowed in 231 BCE.\n\nFresh water was sacred in Roman religion; every spring had its spirit, and many were also honoured by nymphs. The custom of dressing wells with flowers lived on in many places in Europe, though the English well-dressings of Derbyshire are a separate, later tradition.",knowAndDont:{known:'The date, the garlands and well-crowning (Varro 6.22), the temple (Cicero, On the Laws 2.56).',unknown:'Any further rite, and the origin of Fons\u2019s link with Janus.'},celebrate:['Lay a garland or a few flowers by a spring, a fountain or a well (natural, biodegradable ones only).','Clean up a stream bank or support clean-water work.','Drink a glass of fresh water slowly, with thanks.','Find out where your tap water comes from.'],ritual:{title:'Crowning the well',outline:['Make a small circlet of flowers and greenery.','Go to a spring, fountain, stream or your kitchen tap.','Thank Fons for the water that keeps you alive.','Lay the garland beside the water, or float a single flower if it\u2019s natural water.','Drink a cup of water there, and pour the last of it back.']},correspondences:{deities:['Fons','Janus','Nymphs'],herbs:['Mint','Willow','Meadowsweet'],crystals:['Aquamarine','Blue Lace Agate','Clear Quartz'],colors:['Blue Candle','Silver Candle'],foods:['Fresh spring water'],symbols:['Garland','Well','Spring']},sources:["Varro, De Lingua Latina 6.12\u201326 (Latin text): https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/varro.ll6.html (6.22)",'Cicero, On the Laws 2.56',o,'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fontinalia']},{ref:'roman-october-horse',title:'October Horse',aka:['Equus October','Ides of October'],tradition:t,summary:'A chariot race for Mars on the Ides of October; the right-hand horse of the winning team was sacrificed to him, at the end of the season of war.',honours:'Mars, as the campaigning year closed. The rite also served the city\u2019s purification: the horse\u2019s blood was kept for the Parilia in April.',history:"On the Campus Martius two-horse chariots raced, and the right-hand horse of the winning team was killed with a spear at the altar of Mars. Its head was cut off and decorated with loaves of bread, and two neighbourhoods, the people of the Sacred Way and the people of the Subura, fought for it: if the Sacred Way won, it was hung on the Regia; if the Subura, on the Mamilian tower. The tail was carried at a run to the Regia so that its blood dripped on the hearth, and the blood was kept by the Vestals for the Parilia suffimen.\n\nThe sources (Festus, Plutarch, Polybius) are brief and puzzling. Ancient writers linked the horse with Troy; modern scholars have compared horse sacrifices in other Indo-European cultures. It is one of the strangest rites in Roman religion.",knowAndDont:{known:'The outline of the rite from Festus, Plutarch (Roman Questions 97) and Polybius (12.4b), including the fight over the head and the blood kept for the Parilia.',unknown:'Its meaning: a harvest rite, a war rite, a royal rite, or all three.'},celebrate:['Close a season of struggle: put away tools, kit or work you no longer need.','Give thanks to Mars for strength through the year, and ask for rest.','Bake bread and share it, recalling the loaves that crowned the horse.','Make a donation to a horse sanctuary or animal welfare charity.'],ritual:{title:'Closing the campaign',outline:['Gather objects that stand for the year\u2019s hard work: a planner, a uniform, tools.','Light a red candle safely.','Thank Mars for the strength you had this year, and name what you won and lost.','Offer a small loaf or a piece of bread.','Put the objects away, snuff the candle, and rest for the evening.']},correspondences:{deities:['Mars'],herbs:['Bay','Oak'],crystals:['Bloodstone','Red Jasper'],colors:['Red Candle'],foods:['Bread loaves'],symbols:['Horse','Spear','Chariot']},sources:['Festus, s.v. October equus, Panibus',"Plutarch, Roman Questions 97: Plutarch, Roman Questions (Loeb, 1936), at LacusCurtius: https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Moralia/Roman_Questions*/home.html",'Polybius 12.4b',o,'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/October_Horse']},{ref:'roman-armilustrium',title:'Armilustrium',aka:['Purification of Arms'],tradition:t,summary:'The purifying of arms on the Aventine, as the campaigning season closed and the Salii danced for Mars.',honours:'Mars, and perhaps Minerva; the weapons and the men who had carried them.',history:"Varro explains the name from the place, the Armilustrium on the Aventine, where men under arms performed rites, and from the purifying (lustrare) of arms. The Salii, the leaping priests of Mars who had danced through the city with their sacred shields in March, danced again, closing the season they had opened. Trumpets may have sounded, as at the Tubilustrium.\n\nAs the Quinquatrus and Tubilustrium in March prepared the arms of war, the October Horse and the Armilustrium put them away clean.",knowAndDont:{known:'The name, place and purpose (Varro 5.153, 6.22); the Salii\u2019s role.',unknown:'The details of the purification itself.'},celebrate:['Clean and put away tools, gear or sports equipment for the winter.','Lay down an argument or a fight that has gone on long enough.','Remember those who served in war, and those harmed by it.'],ritual:{title:'Cleansing the tools',outline:['Gather tools or gear you have used hard this year.','Make khernips-style water: plunge a lit match or bay sprig into a bowl of water (keep it away from anything flammable).','Wipe each tool with the water, thanking it for its work.','Oil or sharpen what needs it, and put it away.','Pour the rest of the water outside.']},correspondences:{deities:['Mars','Minerva'],herbs:['Bay','Rosemary','Hyssop'],crystals:['Hematite','Black Tourmaline'],colors:['Red Candle','White Candle'],symbols:['Shield','Spear','Dancing priests']},sources:["Varro, De Lingua Latina 6.12\u201326 (Latin text): https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/varro.ll6.html (6.22)",'Varro, De Lingua Latina 5.153','Plutarch, Romulus 23',o]},{ref:'roman-ludi-plebeii',title:'Ludi Plebeii',aka:['Plebeian Games','Epulum Iovis (November)'],tradition:t,summary:'The Plebeian Games (4\u201317 November), with plays and races, and the Epulum Iovis, the feast of Jupiter on the Capitol, on the Ides.',honours:'Jupiter Optimus Maximus, and the Roman plebs, the common people, for whom the games were held.',history:"The Plebeian Games were established by the late third century BCE, and probably held in the Circus Flaminius. Like the Roman Games in September, they had a great feast for Jupiter on the Ides (13 November) and, by the late Republic, ran for about two weeks, most days given to plays. Plautus\u2019s comedy Stichus was first performed at them in 200 BCE.\n\nThey were organised by the plebeian aediles, the officials of the common people, and were part of the long political struggle in which the plebs won their own institutions alongside the patricians\u2019.",knowAndDont:{known:'The games, the Epulum Iovis and the performance of plays (Livy; the production notice of Plautus\u2019s Stichus).',unknown:'Their exact founding date and early form.'},celebrate:['See a comedy or a show with friends, and laugh.','Share a meal and set a place for Jupiter.','Support community theatre or free public events.'],ritual:{title:'A place at the table for Jupiter',outline:['Lay the table for a meal with one extra small place.','Light a candle safely there.','Serve the first portion of the food and drink to Jupiter\u2019s place.','Eat together and tell stories or jokes.','Afterwards, give the god\u2019s portion to the earth.']},correspondences:{deities:['Jupiter','Juno','Minerva'],herbs:['Oak','Sage'],crystals:['Lapis Lazuli'],colors:['Purple Candle','Blue Candle'],foods:['A shared feast'],symbols:['Theatre mask','Banquet couch']},sources:['Livy 23.30, 25.2',o,h]},{ref:'roman-faunalia',title:'Faunalia',aka:['Rustic Faunalia','Nones of December'],tradition:t,summary:'Country people honoured Faunus, god of woods and flocks, with wine, a kid and dancing in the fields.',honours:'Faunus, the wild god of woods, pasture and flocks, who spoke prophecy in the voices of the forest and was identified with the Greek Pan.',history:"Horace\u2019s ode to Faunus (Odes 3.18) is the main witness. Faunus is asked to pass gently through the poet\u2019s fields and be kind to the young of the flock, for a kid falls to him at the year\u2019s end and wine is plentiful. When the Nones of December come round, \u201ccum tibi Nonae redeunt Decembres\u201d, the whole village takes its holiday in the meadows with the oxen at rest; the wolf wanders among unafraid lambs, the woods scatter their leaves for him, and the labourer delights to stamp the hated earth in a three-beat dance.\n\nFaunus also had a temple on the Tiber island, dedicated on the Ides of February, and was involved in the Lupercalia. The rural Faunalia of December was a country feast, not a state festival.",knowAndDont:{known:'The country feast as Horace describes it, around 23 BCE.',unknown:'How widely it was kept beyond Horace\u2019s own Sabine district; the rite in detail.'},celebrate:['Take a walk in the fields or woods and leave a little wine or milk for Faunus.','Give working animals, and yourself, a holiday.','Dance, even badly, on the cold ground.','Listen to the woods, and notice what they say.'],ritual:{premade:'hedge-hearing-the-land-walk',title:'Hearing the land for Faunus'},correspondences:{deities:['Faunus','Silvanus'],herbs:['Oak','Holly','Juniper'],crystals:['Moss Agate','Tiger Eye'],colors:['Green Candle','Brown Candle'],foods:['Wine','Milk'],symbols:['Goat','Pan pipes','Fallen leaves']},sources:['Horace, Odes 3.18 (Latin text): https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/horace/carm3.shtml',o]},{ref:'roman-consualia-2',title:'Consualia (December)',aka:['Second Consualia'],tradition:t,summary:'The second festival of Consus, god of the stored harvest, as the autumn sowing ended. See the Consualia of 21 August.',honours:'Consus, god of what is stored away, and probably the seed put into the earth.',history:"Consus was honoured twice, in August when the harvest was stored and in December when the sowing was done; four days later, on each occasion, came a feast of Ops, his partner. The December rite is even less described than the August one. The fuller page is on the Consualia, 21 August.",knowAndDont:{known:'The date and the pairing with the Opalia.',unknown:'The December rite itself.'},celebrate:['Check your winter stores and seeds.','Give working animals a rest, as on the August feast.'],ritual:{title:'See the Consualia',outline:['Bless your store cupboard or seed box with a candle and a handful of grain for Consus.']},correspondences:{deities:['Consus','Ops'],colors:['Brown Candle'],foods:['Grain']},sources:[o]},{ref:'roman-opalia',title:'Opalia',aka:['Feast of Ops'],tradition:t,summary:'The winter festival of Ops, goddess of plenty and the earth\u2019s riches, during the Saturnalia.',honours:'Ops, goddess of abundance and of the earth, wife of Saturn, honoured in the middle of his Saturnalia.',history:"Macrobius says Ops was the wife of Saturn and stood for the earth, which brings forth every good thing, and that her worshippers prayed to her sitting, touching the earth. Her festival fell on 19 December, in the middle of the Saturnalia, four days after the December Consualia.\n\nVery little else is known; her feast was overshadowed by her husband\u2019s.",knowAndDont:{known:'The date, the link with Saturn, and the custom of praying seated on the ground (Macrobius, Saturnalia 1.10.20\u201322).',unknown:'The rest of the rite.'},celebrate:['Sit on the ground, palms on the earth or the floor, and give thanks for plenty.','Share food during the winter festivities, especially with those who have little.','Open the box of thanks you set up at the Opiconsivia, if you did.'],ritual:{title:'Hands on the earth',outline:['Sit on the ground or floor with a candle and a bowl of grain, nuts or bread.','Light the candle safely.','Put both palms flat on the earth and thank Ops for what the year has given.','Offer a handful of the food on the earth outside, and eat some.','Give something away before the year ends.']},correspondences:{deities:['Ops','Saturn'],herbs:['Cinnamon','Clove'],crystals:['Pyrite','Citrine','Garnet'],colors:['Gold Candle','Green Candle'],foods:['Nuts','Honey','Bread'],symbols:['Cornucopia','Earth']},sources:["Macrobius, Saturnalia 1.7\u20131.16 (1.10.18\u201322)",o]},{ref:'roman-divalia',title:'Divalia',aka:['Angeronalia','Feast of Angerona'],tradition:t,summary:'The festival of Angerona, a goddess shown with her finger to her sealed lips, at the winter solstice; offerings were made in the shrine of Volupia. She was said to guard the secret name of Rome.',honours:'Angerona, a goddess of silence, and perhaps of the turning year, whose statue showed her with her mouth bound and sealed. Pliny says Rome had a secret name that was never to be spoken, and Angerona kept it.',history:"Varro says the Angeronalia was named for Angerona, to whom sacrifice was made in the Curia Acculeia. Macrobius adds that the pontiffs sacrificed to her in the shrine of Volupia, goddess of pleasure, and that her statue there had its mouth bound and sealed. Ancient writers offered explanations: that she cured quinsy (angina), a throat disease; that she drove away anguish (angor); or that her silence protected Rome\u2019s secret name, which Pliny says a tribune was put to death for revealing.\n\nComing on 21 December, close to the solstice, the feast has been read by modern writers as a rite of the sun\u2019s turning, when the days stop shortening, but no ancient source says so.",knowAndDont:{known:'The date, the sealed mouth of her statue, the places of sacrifice (Varro 6.23; Macrobius 1.10.7\u20139) and the secret name of Rome (Pliny, Natural History 3.65).',unknown:'Her original nature; whether the solstice had anything to do with her.'},celebrate:['Keep an hour or an evening of silence.','Write down something you will keep secret and safe, and seal it.','Mind your words: speak less, listen more, keep confidences.','Sit in the dark of the solstice and let it be quiet.'],ritual:{title:'A sealed silence',outline:['Light a single candle safely in a dark room.','Place a finger to your lips and sit in silence for ten minutes.','Write on paper something that should be kept: a vow, a hope, a name.','Fold it, seal it with wax or tape, and put it away until the year turns.','Snuff the candle without speaking, and stay quiet for a while.']},correspondences:{deities:['Angerona','Volupia'],herbs:['Myrrh','Lavender','Mugwort'],crystals:['Obsidian','Labradorite','Amethyst'],colors:['Black Candle','Indigo Candle'],symbols:['Finger to lips','Seal','Solstice']},sources:["Varro, De Lingua Latina 6.12\u201326 (Latin text): https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/varro.ll6.html (6.23)","Macrobius, Saturnalia 1.7\u20131.16 (1.10.7\u20139)",'Pliny, Natural History 3.65',o]},{ref:'roman-larentalia',title:'Larentalia',aka:['Larentinalia','Feast of Acca Larentia'],tradition:t,summary:'Offerings for the dead at the tomb of Acca Larentia, foster-mother of Romulus and Remus.',honours:'Acca Larentia, in one story the shepherd\u2019s wife who nursed the twins Romulus and Remus, and mother of the first Arval Brothers; in another a wealthy woman who left her estate to the Roman people. Both stories honour a mother of Rome.',history:"Varro says that the priests made a funeral offering (parentatio) at her tomb in the Velabrum, near the foot of the Palatine. Gellius and Macrobius add that the flamen Quirinalis was present. It was also a feast of Jupiter.\n\nPlutarch tells both stories of Larentia: the nurse of the twins, sometimes called lupa (which can mean she-wolf or prostitute, a pun the Romans enjoyed), and the courtesan who, after spending a night in Hercules\u2019 temple, married a rich man and left all her wealth to Rome. Whichever she was, Rome honoured her among its dead.",knowAndDont:{known:'The offering at her tomb in the Velabrum and the priests involved (Varro 6.23\u201324; Gellius 7.7; Macrobius 1.10.11\u201317); the two legends (Plutarch, Romulus 4\u20135).',unknown:'Who she was, if anyone; whether she was once a goddess of the dead or of the Lares, as her name might suggest.'},celebrate:['Honour those who raised you, especially those who were not your birth parents.','Visit a grave and leave flowers, or light a candle for the family dead.','Give to a children\u2019s or foster-care charity.','Retell the story of the she-wolf and the twins.'],ritual:{premade:'hedge-ancestor-candle-evening',title:'An evening for the ones who raised us'},correspondences:{deities:['Acca Larentia','Lares','Jupiter'],herbs:['Rosemary','Cypress','Myrrh'],crystals:['Jet','Smoky Quartz'],colors:['Black Candle','White Candle'],foods:['Milk','Bread','Wine'],symbols:['She-wolf','Twins','Tomb']},sources:["Varro, De Lingua Latina 6.12\u201326 (Latin text): https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/varro.ll6.html (6.23\u201324)",'Aulus Gellius, Attic Nights 7.7',"Macrobius, Saturnalia 1.7\u20131.16 (1.10.11\u201317)",'Plutarch, Romulus 4\u20135',o]},{ref:'attic-athena',title:'Athena\u2019s day (Tritomenis)',aka:['Trit\u0113 histamenou','The Third','Tritogeneia\u2019s birthday'],tradition:'Hellenic',summary:'The third day of every Attic month, kept as Athena\u2019s monthly birthday: she was called Tritogeneia, \u201cthird-born\u201d, and the Athenians honoured her on the third.',honours:'Athena, goddess of Athens, of wisdom, crafts, weaving and the defence of the city, born fully armed from the head of Zeus. In Athens she was Polias, guardian of the city, and Parthenos, the maiden of the Parthenon.',history:"The Greeks gave several of their gods a monthly birthday, a day in every month that was theirs. Athena\u2019s was the third. Ancient scholars explained her old title Tritogeneia as \u201cborn on the third\u201d, though it may first have meant \u201cborn by the waters of Triton\u201d; either way the Athenians took the third as her day. Her greatest festival, the Panathenaia, fell on the 28th of Hekatombaion, which, counting the last days of the month backwards as the Athenians did, was also a \u201cthird\u201d, the third of the waning month.\n\nThese monthly days were not usually great public festivals. They were days when a god was especially near, and when private offerings, household prayers and small gatherings made sense. Modern Hellenic polytheists keep them with simple offerings at home.",knowAndDont:{known:'That the third was Athena\u2019s day, from ancient scholars and lexicographers, gathered by Mikalson; the Panathenaia\u2019s date.',unknown:'How ordinary Athenians kept the monthly day, if at all, beyond the great festivals that fell on it; the original meaning of Tritogeneia.'},celebrate:['Make khernips, wash your hands, and pour a libation of water, olive oil or wine for Athena.','Offer her an olive, a little oil in a lamp or a small cake.','Do a piece of careful, skilled work: weave, write, study, mend.','Ask for good counsel in a decision you are facing.'],ritual:{premade:'folk-khernips-libation',title:'Khernips and a libation for Athena'},correspondences:{deities:['Athena','Hestia'],herbs:['Frankincense','Rosemary'],crystals:['Lapis Lazuli','Sodalite'],colors:['Blue Candle','White Candle'],foods:['Olives','Olive oil','Honey cakes'],symbols:['Owl','Olive tree','Aegis','Loom']},sources:['J. D. Mikalson, The Sacred and Festival Calendar of Athens (Princeton, 1975)','R. Parker, Polytheism and Society at Athens (Oxford, 2005)','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Attic_calendar','https://www.theoi.com/Olympios/Athena.html']},{ref:'attic-tetras',title:'The Fourth (Tetras)',aka:['Tetart\u0113 histamenou','Day of Hermes and Aphrodite'],tradition:'Hellenic',summary:'The fourth of every Attic month, sacred to Hermes, Aphrodite, Herakles and Eros. Hermes and Herakles were said to be born on a fourth; Hesiod calls it a good day to bring home a bride.',honours:'Hermes, the messenger, guide of travellers and souls, god of luck, trade and herms at the door; Aphrodite, goddess of love and beauty; Eros; and Herakles, the hero who laboured for others.',history:"The Homeric Hymn to Hermes says he was born on the fourth day of the month, at dawn, and by evening had stolen Apollo\u2019s cattle. Herakles too was said to be born on a fourth, and a proverb about someone \u201cborn on the fourth\u201d meant a person who toils for others\u2019 benefit, like him. Aphrodite and Eros shared the day.\n\nHesiod advises: \u201cOn the fourth of the month bring home your bride, but choose the omens which are best for this business\u201d (Works and Days 800, tr. Evelyn-White, 1914). Theophrastus, in his sketch of the superstitious man, has him on the fourth and the seventh of every month send his household to mull wine while he buys myrtle and frankincense and spends the day garlanding images of the gods at home: a joke at his expense, but proof that ordinary people kept these days. There were also clubs called Tetradistai, \u201cFourth-day people\u201d, who met on the fourth.",knowAndDont:{known:'The day\u2019s gods, from the Homeric Hymn to Hermes, Hesiod, Theophrastus and later scholia; the existence of Fourth-day clubs.',unknown:'What the Tetradistai did when they met; how Aphrodite and Herakles came to share the day.'},celebrate:['Leave an offering at your door or at a boundary for Hermes: a coin, a little honey, a stone added to a cairn.','Do something kind for someone you love, or plan a date.','Take on a job for someone else\u2019s benefit, as Herakles did.','Lay myrtle, roses or frankincense on your shrine, as Theophrastus\u2019s man did.'],ritual:{title:'For Hermes and Aphrodite on the fourth',outline:['Wash your hands in khernips or clean water.','Light a candle or lamp and greet Hestia first.','At your front door, offer Hermes a coin or a little honey, and ask for good journeys and good luck.','At your shrine, offer Aphrodite a flower or a little frankincense, and ask for love in your home.','Pour a short libation for Herakles and Eros, then greet Hestia last.']},correspondences:{deities:['Hermes','Aphrodite','Eros','Herakles','Hestia'],herbs:['Frankincense','Rose','Lavender'],crystals:['Rose Quartz','Citrine'],colors:['Pink Candle','Yellow Candle'],foods:['Honey','Wine','Cakes'],symbols:['Herm','Caduceus','Myrtle','Dove','Club']},sources:['Homeric Hymn to Hermes 19 (tr. Evelyn-White): https://www.theoi.com/Text/HomericHymns1.html','Hesiod, Works and Days 800 (tr. Evelyn-White, 1914): https://www.theoi.com/Text/HesiodWorksDays.html','Theophrastus, Characters 16 (The Superstitious Man): https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/58242','J. D. Mikalson, The Sacred and Festival Calendar of Athens (Princeton, 1975)']},{ref:'attic-artemis',title:'Artemis\u2019s day',aka:['Hekt\u0113 histamenou','The Sixth'],tradition:'Hellenic',summary:'The sixth of the month, the birthday of Artemis and sacred to her; several of her annual festivals fell on a sixth.',honours:'Artemis, the huntress, mistress of wild animals and wild places, guardian of girls and of women in childbirth, twin sister of Apollo.',history:"Diogenes Laertius says Socrates was born on the sixth of Thargelion, the day on which the Athenians purified the city and the Delians said Artemis was born. Her monthly birthday followed. Several of her Athenian festivals fell on a sixth: the Elaphebolia, for Artemis the deer-shooter, on 6 Elaphebolion; and on 6 Boedromion the great yearly thank-offering of goats to Artemis Agrotera, the huntress, vowed before the battle of Marathon.\n\nLike the other monthly birthdays, it was a day when her presence was felt rather than a great civic event every month.",knowAndDont:{known:'Her birthday on the sixth (Diogenes Laertius 2.44), the Elaphebolia and the Marathon offering (Plutarch, On the Malice of Herodotus 26; Xenophon, Anabasis 3.2.12).',unknown:'Exactly what households did on the monthly sixth.'},celebrate:['Go walking in wild country, or at least under trees, and keep your eyes open for animals.','Offer Artemis water, honey cakes or a sprig of something wild, left outside.','Support wildlife conservation, or girls\u2019 education and sport.','Honour your own independence for a day.'],ritual:{title:'An offering in the wild for Artemis',outline:['Wash your hands in clean water.','Go to a wild or green place, or a window looking out on one.','Greet Artemis, huntress and protector, and pour a little water or leave a honey cake.','Sit quietly and watch for birds or animals; don\u2019t chase them.','Ask her protection for someone young or vulnerable, and leave nothing behind but the offering.']},correspondences:{deities:['Artemis','Apollo'],herbs:['Mugwort','Wormwood','Cypress'],crystals:['Moonstone','Selenite'],colors:['Silver Candle','White Candle','Green Candle'],foods:['Honey cakes','Water'],symbols:['Bow','Deer','Crescent moon','Torch']},sources:['Diogenes Laertius, Lives 2.44','Plutarch, On the Malice of Herodotus 26','J. D. Mikalson, The Sacred and Festival Calendar of Athens (Princeton, 1975)','https://www.theoi.com/Olympios/Artemis.html']},{ref:'attic-apollo',title:'Apollo\u2019s day (Hebdom\u0113)',aka:['Hebdom\u0113 histamenou','The Holy Seventh'],tradition:'Hellenic',summary:'The seventh of the month, the birthday of Apollo, holy to him; Hesiod calls it a holy day \u201con which Leto bare Apollo with the blade of gold\u201d.',honours:'Apollo, god of light, prophecy, music, healing and purification, whose oracle was at Delphi; called Hebdomeios and Hebdomagetes, \u201cof the seventh\u201d.',history:"Hesiod lists the first, the fourth and the seventh as holy days: the seventh, \u201con which Leto bare Apollo with the blade of gold\u201d (Works and Days 770\u2013771, tr. Evelyn-White, 1914). Callimachus tells that swans circled Delos seven times as he was born. Many of his Athenian festivals fell on a seventh: the Thargelia on 7 Thargelion, the Pyanepsia on 7 Pyanepsion and the Boedromia on 7 Boedromion. At Delphi the oracle was said to have spoken at first only once a year, on his birthday, the seventh of the month Bysios.",knowAndDont:{known:'The seventh as Apollo\u2019s holy day, from Hesiod, the festival calendar and Plutarch.',unknown:'How it was kept by ordinary households each month; Theophrastus\u2019s superstitious man suggests garlands and incense at home.'},celebrate:['Purify yourself with khernips and offer Apollo bay leaves, frankincense or a little honey.','Play, sing or listen to music with full attention.','Do something for your health: a walk in the sun, a check-up you have put off.','Practise a little divination and listen carefully to the answer.'],ritual:{title:'Bay and song for Apollo',outline:['Make khernips by quenching a lit bay leaf or match in a bowl of water, and wash your hands.','Light a lamp or candle and greet Hestia first.','Burn a pinch of frankincense or lay a bay leaf on the shrine for Apollo (burn only in a fire-safe dish).','Sing, play or read aloud a hymn or poem for him.','Ask for clarity or healing, then pour a libation and greet Hestia last.']},correspondences:{deities:['Apollo','Leto','Artemis'],herbs:['Bay','Frankincense','Saffron'],crystals:['Sunstone','Citrine','Amber'],colors:['Gold Candle','Yellow Candle','White Candle'],foods:['Honey cakes','Wine'],symbols:['Lyre','Laurel','Swan','Bow']},sources:['Hesiod, Works and Days 765\u2013771 (tr. Evelyn-White, 1914): https://www.theoi.com/Text/HesiodWorksDays.html','Callimachus, Hymn to Delos 249\u2013255','Plutarch, Greek Questions 9','J. D. Mikalson, The Sacred and Festival Calendar of Athens (Princeton, 1975)']},{ref:'attic-poseidon-theseus',title:'Poseidon and Theseus',aka:['Ogdo\u0113 histamenou','The Eighth'],tradition:'Hellenic',summary:'The eighth of the month, sacred to Poseidon and to his son Theseus, the hero of Athens.',honours:'Poseidon, god of the sea, of earthquakes and of horses, called Asphaleios, \u201cthe steadfast\u201d, and Gaieochos, \u201cearth-holder\u201d; and Theseus, the hero who killed the Minotaur and united Attica, Poseidon\u2019s son in one version of his story.',history:"Plutarch explains it in his life of Theseus. The Athenians held their chief sacrifice to Theseus, the Theseia, on 8 Pyanepsion, the day he came back from Crete; they honoured him on the eighth of other months too, either because he first came to Athens on 8 Hekatombaion, or because they also honoured Poseidon on the eighth. Eight, Plutarch says, is the first cube of an even number and double the first square, so it stands for the steadiness and immovability of the god who holds the earth firm.\n\nHesiod also counts the eighth among the good days of the waxing month for work.",knowAndDont:{known:'The eighth as the day of Theseus and Poseidon, and the reasons Plutarch gives (Theseus 36).',unknown:'How Poseidon was honoured on the monthly eighth in practice.'},celebrate:['Pour a libation of water with a pinch of salt for Poseidon, at the sea or a basin.','Ask for steadiness: a firm footing in a shaky time.','Read the story of Theseus and think about courage that serves a city, not only oneself.','Thank the sea for what it gives, and give to a coastal clean-up or lifeboat charity.'],ritual:{title:'Salt water for the Earth-Holder',outline:['Wash your hands in khernips or clean water.','Light a lamp and greet Hestia first.','Stir a pinch of sea salt into a bowl of water and offer it to Poseidon, asking him to hold the earth steady beneath you.','Pour a little wine or juice for Theseus, and name a place where you need courage.','Pour the salt water away outside, not on plants, and greet Hestia last.']},correspondences:{deities:['Poseidon','Theseus','Hestia'],herbs:['Juniper'],crystals:['Aquamarine','Hematite'],colors:['Blue Candle','Indigo Candle'],foods:['Sea salt','Wine','Fish'],symbols:['Trident','Horse','Bull','Labyrinth']},sources:['Plutarch, Theseus 36 (Loeb, 1914): https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Theseus*.html','Hesiod, Works and Days 772\u2013773','J. D. Mikalson, The Sacred and Festival Calendar of Athens (Princeton, 1975)']},{ref:'icelandic-skerpla',title:'First day of Skerpla',aka:['Skerpla','Eggt\xed\xf0','Stekkt\xed\xf0'],tradition:'Old Icelandic calendar',summary:'The second month of summer begins, on a Saturday in mid-May: the time of lambing, eggs and the first spring work in the home fields.',honours:'The season itself: the land waking after winter, the lambs and the birds\u2019 eggs. No god is attached to the month in the sources.',history:"In the list of months in Snorri\u2019s Edda (13th century) this month is called eggt\xed\xf0 or stekkt\xed\xf0, \u201cegg time\u201d or \u201clambing-pen time\u201d, when birds\u2019 eggs were gathered from the cliffs and ewes and lambs were kept in small pens (stekkir). The name Skerpla appears later and nobody knows what it means; some have guessed it was the name of a forgotten goddess, like Harpa before it, but that is only a guess.\n\nThe month\u2019s work was practical: lambing, mending walls, spreading manure on the home field, and gathering eggs and down.",knowAndDont:{known:'Its place in the calendar, its old names in Snorri\u2019s Edda, and the farming work of the season.',unknown:'The meaning of the name Skerpla, and whether any rite marked the day.'},celebrate:['Eat eggs, or decorate a few, as a nod to egg time.','Do a piece of spring work outside: weed, mend a fence, plant out seedlings.','Look for birds nesting and give them space.'],ritual:{title:'Spring work blessing',outline:['Go out to your garden, balcony or a nearby green space.','Pour a little milk or water on the ground for the land spirits (landv\xe6ttir).','Do one real job: plant, weed, mend.','Thank the land for the season\u2019s growth, and go in.']},correspondences:{deities:['Freyr','Landv\xe6ttir'],herbs:['Birch','Dandelion'],crystals:['Moss Agate'],colors:['Green Candle'],foods:['Eggs','Skyr','Milk'],symbols:['Egg','Lamb','Lambing pen']},sources:['Snorri Sturluson, Edda, Sk\xe1ldskaparm\xe1l (list of the months)','\xc1rni Bj\xf6rnsson, Saga daganna (Reykjav\xedk, 1993)','Almanak H\xe1sk\xf3la \xcdslands: https://almanak.hi.is/rim.html','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Icelandic_calendar']},{ref:'icelandic-solmanudur',title:'First day of S\xf3lm\xe1nu\xf0ur',aka:['S\xf3lm\xe1nu\xf0ur','Selm\xe1nu\xf0ur','Sun month'],tradition:'Old Icelandic calendar',summary:'The sun month begins, on a Monday in mid-June, near midsummer, when in Iceland the sun barely sets.',honours:'The sun, at its height. In this month falls J\xf3nsmessa, Midsummer (24 June), with some of Iceland\u2019s best-loved folk beliefs.',history:"Snorri\u2019s Edda calls this month s\xf3lm\xe1nu\xf0ur, \u201csun month\u201d, or selm\xe1nu\xf0ur, \u201cshieling month\u201d, when animals were taken up to summer pastures and their milk made into butter and skyr at the shieling huts (sel).\n\nMidsummer night, J\xf3nsmessun\xf3tt, is one of the four magic nights of the Icelandic year in folklore. Rolling naked in the dew was said to heal and protect, magic stones could be found in the hills, and cows could speak. These beliefs are recorded in the 19th-century folklore collections of J\xf3n \xc1rnason; many Icelanders still like to walk out on that night.",knowAndDont:{known:'The month\u2019s place and old names (Snorri) and the J\xf3nsmessa folklore (J\xf3n \xc1rnason, 19th century).',unknown:'How old the Midsummer beliefs are, and whether any pre-Christian rite marked the month.'},celebrate:['Watch the sunset and sunrise around midsummer, and stay up for the short night.','Walk barefoot in the morning dew (bathing in it is optional).','Make butter or skyr, or eat the dairy foods of the shieling.','Spend the longest day outside.'],ritual:{title:'Dew and sun',outline:['Go out early on a midsummer morning.','Wash your face, hands and feet in the dew on the grass.','Face the sun and thank it for the light.','Look for a smooth stone to keep as a reminder of the night.']},correspondences:{deities:['S\xf3l','Freyr','Baldur'],herbs:['St John\u2019s Wort','Meadowsweet','Yarrow'],crystals:['Sunstone','Amber'],colors:['Gold Candle','Yellow Candle'],foods:['Skyr','Butter','Milk'],symbols:['Sun','Dew','Shieling hut']},sources:['Snorri Sturluson, Edda, Sk\xe1ldskaparm\xe1l (list of the months)','J\xf3n \xc1rnason, \xcdslenzkar \xfej\xf3\xf0s\xf6gur og \xe6fint\xfdri (1862\u20131864)','\xc1rni Bj\xf6rnsson, Saga daganna (Reykjav\xedk, 1993)','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Icelandic_calendar']},{ref:'icelandic-aukanaetur',title:'Aukan\xe6tur',aka:['The extra nights','Aukan\xe6tur of midsummer'],tradition:'Old Icelandic calendar',summary:'The four extra nights of midsummer, between S\xf3lm\xe1nu\xf0ur and Heyannir, that make the year 52 whole weeks.',honours:'Time itself: the reckoning that let a farming people keep their year in step with the sun without writing.',history:"Twelve months of thirty days make 360 days. The old Icelanders added four nights (they counted in nights, not days) at midsummer to make 364, exactly fifty-two weeks, so that every month began on the same weekday every year. \xcdslendingab\xf3k, written by Ari the Learned around 1125, says that the first settlers counted 364 days in the year.\n\nAukan\xe6tur belong to no month. They come between the third and fourth months of summer, just before the hay harvest.",knowAndDont:{known:'The rule and its purpose, from \xcdslendingab\xf3k and the almanac.',unknown:'Whether the days were ever marked by any custom; they appear to have been purely a matter of reckoning.'},celebrate:['Take four days, or four evenings, as \u201ctime between\u201d: don\u2019t schedule anything you don\u2019t have to.','Learn how your own calendar works, and why it has leap days.','Prepare tools and plans for the busy harvest month ahead.'],ritual:{title:'Four nights outside time',outline:['On each of the four evenings, light a candle safely at dusk.','Sit for a few minutes without a clock or screen.','Note one thing you noticed that day, in a notebook kept just for these nights.','On the fourth night, read the four notes together and snuff the candle.']},correspondences:{deities:['S\xf3l','M\xe1ni'],crystals:['Clear Quartz'],colors:['White Candle'],symbols:['Week','Wheel of the year','Midsummer night']},sources:['Ari \xdeorgilsson, \xcdslendingab\xf3k ch. 4','Almanak H\xe1sk\xf3la \xcdslands: https://almanak.hi.is/rim.html','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Icelandic_calendar']},{ref:'icelandic-sumarauki',title:'Sumarauki',aka:['The summer increase','Leap week'],tradition:'Old Icelandic calendar',summary:'The \u201csummer increase\u201d: a leap week added in some years before Heyannir, so the calendar keeps pace with the sun.',honours:'\xdeorsteinn surtr, the Icelander remembered for proposing it, and the long human effort to make our counting match the sky.',history:"\xcdslendingab\xf3k, written by Ari the Learned in the early 12th century, tells the story. The early settlers\u2019 364-day year was slipping: people noticed that summer was moving back into spring. A man named \xdeorsteinn surtr, who had dreamed he was at the Law Rock, proposed at the Althing that a week be added every seventh summer to see how it worked. The proposal was accepted, around 955 by most estimates, and Ari says it was the best of improvements.\n\nSeven days every seven years was still not quite right, and the rule was changed over the centuries; after Iceland took the Gregorian calendar in 1700 the almanac fixed the modern rule. The last sumarauki was in 2023; the next is in 2029.",knowAndDont:{known:'Its origin in \xdeorsteinn surtr\u2019s proposal (\xcdslendingab\xf3k ch. 4) and the modern almanac rule.',unknown:'The exact year of the reform, and how consistently the early rule was applied.'},celebrate:['Use the extra week for something you never have time for.','Give thanks for the people who keep calendars, maps and records accurate.','Notice where your own life has drifted out of step with its seasons, and add the time it needs.'],ritual:{title:'A week added to summer',outline:['At the start of the week, write down one thing you will use the gift of time for.','Light a candle safely and say \xdeorsteinn surtr\u2019s name in thanks.','Each day of the week, do a little of the thing you named.','At the end, note what you did with the extra week.']},correspondences:{deities:['S\xf3l'],crystals:['Sunstone','Clear Quartz'],colors:['Gold Candle','Green Candle'],symbols:['Seven days','Law Rock']},sources:['Ari \xdeorgilsson, \xcdslendingab\xf3k ch. 4','\xdeorsteinn S\xe6mundsson & Gunnlaugur Bj\xf6rnsson, \u201cHva\xf0 er sumarauki og \xe1 hva\xf0a \xe1rum er hann?\u201d, V\xedsindavefurinn: https://www.visindavefur.is/svar.php?id=69821','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%8Dslendingab%C3%B3k']},{ref:'icelandic-heyannir',title:'First day of Heyannir',aka:['Heyannir','Hay month'],tradition:'Old Icelandic calendar',summary:'Hay-making month begins, on a Sunday in late July: the busiest time of the farming year, when the winter\u2019s fodder was cut and dried.',honours:'The hay, the weather, and the workers. In a land with little grain, the hay decided whether the animals, and so the people, would survive the winter.',history:"Heyannir means \u201chay work\u201d. The whole household and hired workers mowed the home field (t\xfan) first, whose best hay was called ta\xf0a, and then the outlying meadows, working long hours in the light summer nights. When the home-field hay was all in, a feast called t\xf6\xf0ugj\xf6ld was given to the workers, with better food than usual: a custom recorded well into the 20th century.\n\nThe month begins after the extra nights of midsummer, so it is the fourteenth week of summer, or the fifteenth in a year with sumarauki.",knowAndDont:{known:'The month\u2019s name and place, and the farm customs of haymaking and t\xf6\xf0ugj\xf6ld, recorded by Icelandic ethnographers.',unknown:'Whether any ritual, beyond the harvest feast, marked the start of the month.'},celebrate:['Gather and dry herbs from your garden or the wild, ethically.','Throw a small feast for the people who have worked hard with you this year.','Pray, in whatever way you do, for good weather for those bringing in the harvest.'],ritual:{premade:'green-drying-hanging-herb-bundles',title:'Drying the harvest'},correspondences:{deities:['Freyr','Sif','Thor'],herbs:['Meadowsweet','Yarrow','Thyme'],crystals:['Citrine','Tiger Eye'],colors:['Gold Candle','Green Candle'],foods:['Bread and butter','Pancakes (p\xf6nnuk\xf6kur)','Coffee'],symbols:['Scythe','Haystack']},sources:['\xc1rni Bj\xf6rnsson, Saga daganna (Reykjav\xedk, 1993)','Almanak H\xe1sk\xf3la \xcdslands: https://almanak.hi.is/rim.html','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Icelandic_calendar']},{ref:'icelandic-tvimanudur',title:'First day of Tv\xedm\xe1nu\xf0ur',aka:['Tv\xedm\xe1nu\xf0ur','Kornskur\xf0arm\xe1nu\xf0ur'],tradition:'Old Icelandic calendar',summary:'Tv\xedm\xe1nu\xf0ur begins, on a Tuesday in late August, in the late summer harvest.',honours:'The late harvest: grain where it grew, berries on the heaths, and the last of the hay.',history:"Snorri\u2019s Edda calls this month kornskur\xf0arm\xe1nu\xf0ur, \u201cgrain-cutting month\u201d, which suits medieval Iceland, where barley was grown in the south until the climate cooled. The later name Tv\xedm\xe1nu\xf0ur, \u201ctwo-month\u201d, is usually explained as the month when two months of summer remain, or as the second month of the harvest.\n\nIt is still the season for picking crowberries and bilberries on the heaths, an outing many Icelandic families keep.",knowAndDont:{known:'The month\u2019s place and names; its farm work.',unknown:'The exact meaning of \u201ctwo-month\u201d.'},celebrate:['Go berry-picking, and make jam or a simple dessert with skyr.','Harvest the last herbs before autumn, with thanks.','Count the weeks of summer left and plan how to spend them.'],ritual:{premade:'green-harvesting-herbs-with-thanks',title:'Harvesting with thanks'},correspondences:{deities:['Freyr','Sif'],herbs:['Yarrow','Juniper'],crystals:['Garnet','Citrine'],colors:['Orange Candle','Purple Candle'],foods:['Berries','Skyr','Barley bread'],symbols:['Sickle','Berry basket']},sources:['Snorri Sturluson, Edda, Sk\xe1ldskaparm\xe1l (list of the months)','\xc1rni Bj\xf6rnsson, Saga daganna (Reykjav\xedk, 1993)','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Icelandic_calendar']},{ref:'icelandic-haustmanudur',title:'First day of Haustm\xe1nu\xf0ur',aka:['Haustm\xe1nu\xf0ur','Autumn month'],tradition:'Old Icelandic calendar',summary:'Autumn month, the last of summer, begins on a Thursday in late September: the sheep are gathered from the highlands (g\xf6ngur and r\xe9ttir).',honours:'The flocks and the people who bring them home; the turning of the year toward winter.',history:"In September Icelanders go up into the highlands on foot and horseback to gather the sheep that have grazed freely all summer (g\xf6ngur), and drive them down to the sorting pens (r\xe9ttir), where each farmer finds their own by the ear-mark. The r\xe9ttir are still a great social event, with singing and drink, and many people come home from the towns to help.\n\nThe month leads to Winter Nights and the first day of winter, when the old sources speak of feasts and of sacrifices for a good year.",knowAndDont:{known:'The month\u2019s place, the round-up customs (still kept), and the medieval sagas\u2019 references to autumn feasts.',unknown:'Exactly what pre-Christian rites, if any, were held at the start of this month rather than at Winter Nights.'},celebrate:['Gather in what is yours: tidy, bring things home, call the family together.','Make lamb soup (kj\xf6ts\xfapa) or a hearty vegetable stew to share.','Hold a small bl\xf3t of thanks for the summer\u2019s end.'],ritual:{premade:'folk-simple-blot',title:'A simple bl\xf3t for the summer\u2019s end'},correspondences:{deities:['Freyr','Odin','Frigg'],herbs:['Juniper','Birch'],crystals:['Smoky Quartz','Tiger Eye'],colors:['Brown Candle','Orange Candle'],foods:['Lamb soup','Rye bread'],symbols:['Sheep','Sorting pen','Shepherd\u2019s crook']},sources:['\xc1rni Bj\xf6rnsson, Saga daganna (Reykjav\xedk, 1993)','Almanak H\xe1sk\xf3la \xcdslands: https://almanak.hi.is/rim.html','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Icelandic_calendar']},{ref:'icelandic-ylir',title:'First day of \xddlir',aka:['\xddlir','Frerm\xe1nu\xf0ur'],tradition:'Old Icelandic calendar',summary:'The second month of winter begins, on a Monday in late November, as the dark closes in toward Yule.',honours:'The deepening winter. \xddlir\u2019s name may be linked with Yule (j\xf3l); one of Odin\u2019s many names in the Eddas is J\xf3lnir, \u201cthe Yule one\u201d.',history:"Snorri\u2019s Edda calls this month frerm\xe1nu\xf0ur, \u201cfrost month\u201d. The name \xddlir is of uncertain origin; it is often linked with j\xf3l, Yule, the midwinter feast, but the connection is not certain.\n\nIn \xddlir, by modern custom, the Icelandic Yule Lads (j\xf3lasveinar) begin coming down from the mountains, one each night from 12 December, leaving small gifts in children\u2019s shoes on the windowsill. They are folklore figures, once frightening, softened into gift-bringers in the 20th century.",knowAndDont:{known:'The month\u2019s place and its name in Snorri; the Yule Lads tradition, recorded since the 17th century and reshaped in the 20th.',unknown:'What \xddlir means.'},celebrate:['Prepare for midwinter: plan gifts, light the first candles at the window.','Read the H\xe1vam\xe1l on a dark evening.','Leave a shoe on the windowsill for the Yule Lads if you have children in the house.'],ritual:{premade:'folk-havamal-reflection',title:'H\xe1vam\xe1l on a dark evening'},correspondences:{deities:['Odin','Frigg'],herbs:['Juniper','Holly'],crystals:['Smoky Quartz','Jet'],colors:['Indigo Candle','White Candle'],foods:['Laufabrau\xf0 (leaf bread)','Hot chocolate'],symbols:['Frost','Shoe on the windowsill']},sources:['Snorri Sturluson, Edda, Sk\xe1ldskaparm\xe1l (list of the months)','\xc1rni Bj\xf6rnsson, Saga daganna (Reykjav\xedk, 1993)','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Icelandic_calendar']},{ref:'icelandic-morsugur',title:'First day of M\xf6rsugur',aka:['M\xf6rsugur','J\xf3lm\xe1nu\xf0ur','Hr\xfatm\xe1nu\xf0ur'],tradition:'Old Icelandic calendar',summary:'M\xf6rsugur, the midwinter month, begins on a Wednesday (20\u201327 December), around the solstice.',honours:'Midwinter and the returning light; the household gathered at Yule; and, in folk belief, the elves and hidden people, who move house at New Year.',history:"\u201cM\xf6rsugur\u201d means \u201cfat-sucker\u201d, from the rich, fatty foods eaten at midwinter. It was also called j\xf3lm\xe1nu\xf0ur, Yule month; Snorri\u2019s Edda calls it hr\xfatm\xe1nu\xf0ur, \u201cram month\u201d, when the rams were put to the ewes.\n\nThe month holds Iceland\u2019s whole Christmas season: \xdeorl\xe1ksmessa on 23 December, when many still eat fermented skate; Christmas Eve at six o\u2019clock; New Year\u2019s Eve, one of the magic nights when elves move house and cows speak; and \xderett\xe1ndinn, Twelfth Night on 6 January, with bonfires and elf dances as the Yule Lads go home. In the pre-Christian period, j\xf3l was a midwinter feast with drinking and sacrifice, as the sagas record, but its exact form is unclear.",knowAndDont:{known:'The month\u2019s names and its place; the modern and folk customs of the Icelandic Christmas season.',unknown:'The form of the heathen j\xf3l in Iceland; the sagas describe it in general terms and were written by Christians centuries later.'},celebrate:['Leave a little food out for the house spirit or the hidden people on New Year\u2019s Eve.','Light candles through the darkest days (safely).','Share a rich, warming meal with others.','Clean the house before the year turns, as was done for the elves\u2019 visit.'],ritual:{premade:'hedge-house-spirit-offering',title:'An offering to the house spirit at midwinter'},correspondences:{deities:['Odin','Frigg','Freyr','Baldur'],herbs:['Juniper','Holly','Mistletoe'],crystals:['Garnet','Sunstone'],colors:['Red Candle','Gold Candle','White Candle'],foods:['Smoked lamb (hangikj\xf6t)','Leaf bread','Rice pudding'],symbols:['Candle','Bonfire','Elves']},sources:['Snorri Sturluson, Edda, Sk\xe1ldskaparm\xe1l (list of the months)','J\xf3n \xc1rnason, \xcdslenzkar \xfej\xf3\xf0s\xf6gur og \xe6fint\xfdri (1862\u20131864)','\xc1rni Bj\xf6rnsson, Saga daganna (Reykjav\xedk, 1993)']},{ref:'icelandic-konudagur',title:'Konudagur',aka:['First day of G\xf3a','Woman\u2019s Day','Wife\u2019s Day'],tradition:'Old Icelandic calendar',summary:'The first day of G\xf3a, \u201cwife\u2019s day\u201d, a Sunday in late February: in folk custom the woman of the house welcomed G\xf3a in, and today wives and partners are given flowers and treats.',honours:'G\xf3a, the late winter month, imagined as a woman; and the women of the household. The month pairs with \xdeorri before it, whose first day, b\xf3ndadagur, is the husband\u2019s day.',history:"A medieval story (in Orkneyinga saga and the related tale \u201cHow Norway was settled\u201d) makes \xdeorri a king of the far north whose daughter G\xf3i vanished; a month after his midwinter feast, the \xdeorrabl\xf3t, he held another sacrifice, the G\xf3ibl\xf3t, to find her. Whatever its age, the story shows that the months were already imagined as people in the Middle Ages.\n\nFolk custom recorded in the 19th century said the housewife should rise first on the morning of G\xf3a and welcome her into the house, as the husband welcomed \xdeorri a month before. The earliest references to the day as \u201ckonudagur\u201d come from the mid-19th century. Giving flowers and treats to wives and partners is a 20th-century custom that has grown with the help of florists and bakers, and it is now kept all over Iceland.",knowAndDont:{known:'The story of G\xf3i and the G\xf3ibl\xf3t (Orkneyinga saga ch. 1\u20132), the 19th-century folk custom of welcoming G\xf3a, and the modern customs.',unknown:'Whether the G\xf3ibl\xf3t was ever a real feast in Iceland or Norway, or only a story.'},celebrate:['Give flowers, a cake or a kind word to the women in your life.','Welcome the late-winter month at your door with a small greeting in the morning.','Enjoy a Sunday treat: pancakes with cream, or a cream bun.','Notice the lengthening light.'],ritual:{title:'Welcoming G\xf3a',outline:['Rise early, before the others in the house if you can.','Open the front door and welcome G\xf3a in, by name, with a few words of your own.','Light a candle safely and set out a flower and a small treat.','Give thanks for the women of your household and your family line.','Share the treat with the household later in the day.']},correspondences:{deities:['Frigg','Freyja'],herbs:['Rose','Birch'],crystals:['Rose Quartz','Moonstone'],colors:['Pink Candle','White Candle'],foods:['Cream cake','Pancakes','Coffee'],symbols:['Flowers','Open door']},sources:['Orkneyinga saga ch. 1\u20132 (Fundinn Noregr)','\xc1rni Bj\xf6rnsson, Saga daganna (Reykjav\xedk, 1993)','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Konudagur','https://islensktalmanak.is/dagar/konudagur/']},{ref:'icelandic-einmanudur',title:'First day of Einm\xe1nu\xf0ur',aka:['Einm\xe1nu\xf0ur','Yngismannadagur','Lads\u2019 Day'],tradition:'Old Icelandic calendar',summary:'The last month of winter begins, on a Tuesday in late March; traditionally the young men\u2019s day (yngismannadagur).',honours:'The last of winter and the waiting for summer; the young men of the household, as Harpa\u2019s first day was the young women\u2019s.',history:"Einm\xe1nu\xf0ur, \u201cone month\u201d or \u201csingle month\u201d, is the last month of winter, probably named because one month remained before summer. Folk custom, as described by \xc1rni Bj\xf6rnsson from older records, made its first day yngismannadagur, the young men\u2019s day, when the young women of the household were to look after them, returning the favour the young men had shown on the first day of summer, yngismeyjadagur, the maidens\u2019 day.\n\nLike b\xf3ndadagur and konudagur, these were half-joking customs of the farm household, and they never became public holidays.",knowAndDont:{known:'The month\u2019s place and name; the folk custom of the young men\u2019s day, reported in Icelandic ethnography.',unknown:'How widely or how long the young men\u2019s day was kept.'},celebrate:['Do something kind for the young people in your life.','Start clearing for spring: look over seeds, tools and plans.','Count down the weeks to the first day of summer.'],ritual:{title:'One month to summer',outline:['Light a candle safely at the window.','Write a list of what you will do when summer comes.','Do one small thing today to get ready for it.','Give a small gift or a treat to a young person in your household.']},correspondences:{deities:['Freyr','Baldur'],herbs:['Birch','Willow'],crystals:['Green Aventurine','Clear Quartz'],colors:['Green Candle','Yellow Candle'],foods:['Pancakes','Hot soup'],symbols:['Thaw','Lengthening light']},sources:['\xc1rni Bj\xf6rnsson, Saga daganna (Reykjav\xedk, 1993)','\xc1rni Bj\xf6rnsson, High Days and Holidays in Iceland (Reykjav\xedk, 1995)','Almanak H\xe1sk\xf3la \xcdslands: https://almanak.hi.is/rim.html']}]},2503,[]);
__d(function(e,a,t,o,n,r,s){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(r,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(r,"HOLIDAY_PAGES",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return i}});const i=[{ref:'wheel-imbolc',title:'Imbolc',aka:['Imbolg','Oimelc','Candlemas','Brigid\u2019s Day','L\xe1 Fh\xe9ile Br\xedde'],tradition:'Wheel of the Year',summary:'The first stirring of spring on 1 February, when the light is visibly longer and the ewes come into milk. On the modern Wheel it is the sabbat of Brigid, the hearth and holy wells: cleaning, candles and new beginnings.',honours:'Brigid above all, the Irish goddess of poetry, healing and smithcraft and the much-loved saint of Kildare who carries many of her images. It also honours the hearth fire, the young lambs and the first green shoots: the household turning, very quietly, toward the light.',history:'Imbolc is genuinely old as a date. Early Irish literature names it as one of the four quarter days of the Gaelic year, with Bealtaine, Lughnasadh and Samhain. Its meaning was already a puzzle in the Middle Ages: a tenth-century Irish glossary explained the variant \u201coimelc\u201d as \u201cewe\u2019s milk\u201d, and many scholars now prefer \u201ci mbolg\u201d, \u201cin the belly\u201d, for the pregnant ewes. Both point to lambing season.\n\nWhat we know of how the day was kept comes almost entirely from the feast of St Brigid, recorded by folklorists in nineteenth- and twentieth-century Ireland, Scotland and Man. Rush crosses were woven, a cloth or ribbon was left outside overnight to be blessed as Brigid passed, a doll of her (the Br\xeddeog) was carried from door to door, and in the Hebrides a bed was made for her by the hearth so that she might be welcomed in.\n\nIn the 1950s Gerald Gardner\u2019s Wicca took the four Gaelic quarter days as its \u201cgreater sabbats\u201d, and Imbolc, sometimes called Candlemas in early Wiccan writing, became the first sabbat of spring on the modern Wheel of the Year.',knowAndDont:{known:'Imbolc is attested as a quarter day in early Irish texts. Brigid as a goddess is named in a medieval Irish glossary as a poet and the daughter of the Dagda, with sisters of healing and smithcraft. The saint\u2019s folk customs are richly documented, and the Kildare fire tended by nuns was described by Gerald of Wales in the 1180s.',unknown:'We have no description of a pre-Christian Imbolc rite. Whether the saint \u201cis\u201d the goddess, or simply shares her name and some of her qualities, cannot be settled from the evidence, and both Christian and pagan Irish people hold strong, sincere views. The modern sabbat\u2019s focus on candles and purification owes as much to Candlemas and Wicca as to anything Gaelic.'},celebrate:['Give the house a real clean and open the windows for a few minutes, letting winter out.','Weave a Brigid\u2019s cross from rushes, straw or paper and hang it over the door until next year.','Leave a strip of cloth (a brat Br\xedde) on a sill or branch overnight, and keep it as a blessing for healing.','Light a candle in every room after dark, or one candle for the year\u2019s hopes, and never leave it unattended.','Walk somewhere you can look for snowdrops, catkins or lambs, and write a few lines of poetry for Brigid.'],ritual:{premade:'wicca-imbolc-brigids-bed'},correspondences:{deities:['Brigid'],herbs:['Rowan','Bay','Chamomile'],crystals:['Amethyst','Garnet','Moonstone'],colors:['White','Red','Green'],foods:['Butter and buttermilk','Oat bannocks','Colcannon','Milk with honey'],symbols:['Brigid\u2019s cross','Brat Br\xedde (Brigid\u2019s cloth)','Candles','Snowdrops','Holy wells','The ewe and lamb']},sources:['Kuno Meyer (tr.), The Wooing of Emer (Tochmarc Emire), CELT: https://celt.ucc.ie/published/T301021/index.html','Alexander Carmichael, Carmina Gadelica vol. 1 (1900): https://archive.org/details/carminagadelicah01carm','Catholic Encyclopedia (1907), \u201cSt. Brigid of Ireland\u201d: https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/02784b.htm','Ronald Hutton, The Stations of the Sun: A History of the Ritual Year in Britain (Oxford, 1996), ch. 13.','J. A. MacCulloch, The Religion of the Ancient Celts (1911): https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/14672']},{ref:'wheel-ostara',title:'Ostara',aka:['Spring Equinox','Vernal Equinox','Eostre','Alban Eilir'],tradition:'Wheel of the Year',summary:'The spring equinox, around 20 March, when day and night stand equal and then the light pulls ahead. A modern sabbat of seeds, eggs, hares and greening earth.',honours:'The returning sun and the waking land: buds, nesting birds, the hare in the field. Many witches also honour Eostre, a spring goddess named by the Anglo-Saxon monk Bede, or simply the balance of light and dark at the hinge of the year.',history:'The equinox itself has been watched for thousands of years, and the date of Easter is still reckoned from it. But there is no record of a pagan festival called Ostara. The name comes from two sources. In 725 Bede wrote that the English called April Eosturmonath after a goddess, Eostre, in whose honour feasts had once been held; he says nothing more about her. In 1835 Jacob Grimm, reasoning from Bede and from the German word Ostern, proposed a German goddess \u201cOstara\u201d.\n\nThe modern sabbat name was chosen by the American Pagan Aidan Kelly in 1974, when he gave names to the solstices and equinoxes, which early Wicca had simply called by their seasons. It spread through the Pagan magazine Green Egg and the books of the 1980s.\n\nThe hare and the egg belong to European spring folklore rather than to Eostre. The Easter hare that brings eggs is first described in Germany in 1682, and decorated eggs were made across Christian Europe because eggs, given up for Lent, came back at Easter.',knowAndDont:{known:'Bede records a month named for Eostre and feasts in her honour. Linguists accept that the name is related to old words for dawn and the east, and inscriptions to matron goddesses called the Austriahenae have been found in Germany. The equinox, eggs and hares are real spring traditions.',unknown:'Everything else about Eostre is unknown: her stories, her rites, even whether Bede had it right. The often-repeated tale of Eostre turning a bird into an egg-laying hare is a modern invention, and \u201cOstara\u201d as a sabbat dates from 1974. That does not make the day less worth keeping; it just means we are building, not recovering.'},celebrate:['Sow seeds in a pot or the garden, saying what you hope will grow in you this season.','Dye or paint eggs with onion skins, beetroot or turmeric, and give them away.','Spend a moment at sunrise or sunset noticing the balance, then name one thing to bring back into balance in your life.','Clear a little space outdoors for wildflowers, or put out water for birds and bees.','Eat something fresh and green: the first salad leaves, sprouted seeds, spring herbs.'],ritual:{premade:'wicca-ostara-seed-and-egg'},correspondences:{deities:['Eostre'],herbs:['Lemon Balm','Mint','Dandelion','Calendula'],crystals:['Rose Quartz','Green Aventurine','Moss Agate','Aquamarine'],colors:['Green','Yellow','Pink','White'],foods:['Eggs','Hot cross buns or seed bread','Honey','Spring greens and sprouts'],symbols:['Eggs','The hare','Seeds','Spring flowers','The balanced scale']},sources:['Bede, De temporum ratione, ch. 15 (725); tr. Faith Wallis, Bede: The Reckoning of Time (Liverpool, 1999).','Jacob Grimm, Teutonic Mythology, tr. J. S. Stallybrass (1882), vol. 1, ch. 13.','Ronald Hutton, The Stations of the Sun (Oxford, 1996), ch. 18\u201319.','Ronald Hutton, The Triumph of the Moon: A History of Modern Pagan Witchcraft (Oxford, 1999).']},{ref:'wheel-beltane',title:'Beltane',aka:['Bealtaine','May Day','May Eve','Calan Mai','Walpurgisnacht'],tradition:'Wheel of the Year',summary:'May Day, the start of summer in the old Gaelic year and the flowering height of spring. A fire festival of fertility, joy and protection, with bonfires, hawthorn blossom and dew.',honours:'Life at its most abundant: the land in blossom, the herds going out to pasture, love and desire. Modern witches honour the union of the Goddess and the God; older Gaelic custom asked the fire for protection over people and cattle for the summer ahead.',history:'Bealtaine is one of the four quarter days named in early Irish literature. A tenth-century Irish glossary says that on this day druids made two fires and drove cattle between them as a protection against disease, and later Irish and Scottish custom kept the same idea: hearth fires were put out and relit from a communal fire, and cattle and people passed by the flames.\n\nFolk custom recorded in the eighteenth to twentieth centuries adds much more: washing the face in May morning dew for beauty, bringing in blossom or a May bush, hanging rowan over the door, and guarding the dairy, since butter and luck were thought easy to steal on May morning. The maypole and the May Queen belong to the English and continental May Day, not to the Gaelic Bealtaine; both are now part of the modern sabbat.\n\nWicca took Beltane as a greater sabbat in the 1950s, and gave it its modern focus on the sacred marriage. Today it is also kept publicly, as at Edinburgh\u2019s Beltane Fire Festival, revived in 1988, and the bonfire at the Hill of Uisneach in Ireland.',knowAndDont:{known:'The name and date are early medieval; the glossary account of two fires and cattle is a real medieval source; and the protective fire, dew and rowan customs are well documented by folklorists.',unknown:'The glossary was written by Christian scholars centuries after druids disappeared, so its picture of druid ritual is uncertain. There is no evidence of an ancient \u201csacred marriage\u201d rite at Beltane: that is Wiccan theology, and a meaningful one, but modern.'},celebrate:['Rise early and wash your face in the morning dew, or in water left out overnight under the sky.','Bring in flowering branches or make a flower crown, but cut hawthorn only from the hedge, never a lone tree, and with thanks.','Light a fire safely outdoors with friends, or a candle at home, and leap or step over a small flame only if it is truly safe.','Hang a sprig of rowan tied with red thread over the door for protection through summer.','Do something joyful with your body: dance, walk, make love, swim.'],ritual:{premade:'wicca-beltane-flower-crown'},correspondences:{deities:['Freyr','Aphrodite','Cernunnos','Rhiannon'],herbs:['Hawthorn','Rowan','Rose','Meadowsweet','Birch'],crystals:['Carnelian','Rose Quartz','Emerald','Garnet'],colors:['Green','Red','White','Pink'],foods:['Oatcakes or Beltane bannock','Honey cakes','Dairy','May wine (with sweet woodruff, in moderation)'],symbols:['Bonfire','Maypole','Hawthorn blossom','Ribbons','Morning dew','Flower crown']},sources:['J. A. MacCulloch, The Religion of the Ancient Celts (1911), ch. 18: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/14672','Kuno Meyer (tr.), The Wooing of Emer (Tochmarc Emire), CELT: https://celt.ucc.ie/published/T301021/index.html','Ronald Hutton, The Stations of the Sun (Oxford, 1996), ch. 21\u201324.','Kevin Danaher, The Year in Ireland (Cork, 1972).']},{ref:'wheel-litha',title:'Litha',aka:['Midsummer','Summer Solstice','St John\u2019s Eve','Alban Hefin'],tradition:'Wheel of the Year',summary:'The summer solstice, around 21 June, the longest day, when the sun stands at the height of its power. A sabbat of fire, herbs and celebration, and of the quiet turn toward the waning year.',honours:'The sun at its strongest and the green world at its fullest. Many witches honour the God at his peak, or sun deities such as Apollo or Lugh, and the herbs that were gathered at midsummer for their power.',history:'The solstice has been marked for a very long time: monuments such as Stonehenge are aligned to the solstices, though their builders left no word of what they did there. In Christian Europe midsummer was kept on St John\u2019s Eve, 23 June, with bonfires, rolling burning wheels, gathering herbs such as St John\u2019s wort, and divination for love. These customs are recorded from the Middle Ages to living memory, from Ireland to Russia.\n\n\u201cLitha\u201d comes from Bede, who in 725 listed the old English month names: the months around midsummer were \xc6rra Li\xfea and \xc6fterra Li\xfea, \u201cbefore Litha\u201d and \u201cafter Litha\u201d. Bede tells us nothing of a festival. The American Pagan Aidan Kelly took the name for the solstice in 1974, possibly helped by Tolkien, whose hobbits keep \u201cLithe\u201d at midsummer.\n\nToday crowds gather at Stonehenge for the sunrise, and Druids call the day Alban Hefin, a name from the eighteenth-century Welsh writer Iolo Morganwg.',knowAndDont:{known:'Midsummer fires and herb-gathering are among the best-documented folk customs in Europe, mostly tied to St John\u2019s Eve. Bede records the month names \xc6rra and \xc6fterra Li\xfea.',unknown:'We do not know what, if anything, the pagan English did at midsummer, or what Stonehenge\u2019s builders believed. The sabbat name is a 1970s choice, and the Oak King and Holly King battle often told at Litha is a modern story from the late twentieth century.'},celebrate:['Watch the sunrise or sunset, and spend some of the long day outdoors.','Gather herbs at their peak, with thanks, to dry for the year; label anything you won\u2019t eat.','Light a fire or candles at dusk, safely and lawfully, and let go of something into the flames.','Make sun water or a midsummer garland of flowers for the door.','Share food outside with friends: strawberries, honey, bread, something grilled.'],ritual:{premade:'wicca-litha-noon-herb-gathering'},correspondences:{deities:['Apollo','Lugh','Baldur','Ra'],herbs:['St John\u2019s Wort','Yarrow','Lavender','Calendula','Oak','Elder'],crystals:['Sunstone','Citrine','Tiger Eye','Carnelian'],colors:['Gold','Yellow','Orange','Green'],foods:['Strawberries','Honey','Fresh bread','Elderflower cordial','Summer salads'],symbols:['The sun wheel','Bonfire','Oak leaves','Herb garland','Sunflowers']},sources:['Bede, De temporum ratione, ch. 15 (725); tr. Faith Wallis, Bede: The Reckoning of Time (Liverpool, 1999).','Ronald Hutton, The Stations of the Sun (Oxford, 1996), ch. 30\u201331.','James George Frazer, The Golden Bough (abridged 1922), ch. 62: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/3623','Ronald Hutton, The Triumph of the Moon (Oxford, 1999).']},{ref:'wheel-lammas',title:'Lammas',aka:['Lughnasadh','Loaf Mass','First Harvest','G\u0175yl Awst'],tradition:'Wheel of the Year',summary:'The first harvest, on 1 August, when the grain is cut and the first loaf baked. A sabbat of gratitude, bread and sharing, named for both the English Loaf Mass and the Irish festival of Lugh.',honours:'The ripened grain and the work that brings it in. Many witches honour Lugh, the many-skilled Irish god said to have founded the festival for his foster-mother Tailtiu, and the spirit of the grain that is cut so that people may eat.',history:'The modern sabbat combines two real festivals. In Ireland, Lughnasadh was one of the four quarter days: medieval tales say Lugh founded games at Tailtiu in memory of his foster-mother, who died clearing the plains for farming. In English, \u201chlafm\xe6sse\u201d, the loaf-mass, appears in the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle for 921: bread made from the first ripe wheat was brought to church to be blessed.\n\nIrish folk custom kept Lughnasadh alive as Garland Sunday or Bilberry Sunday, the last Sunday of July or first of August, when people climbed hills, picked bilberries, held fairs and matched couples. The folklorist M\xe1ire MacNeill recorded the custom at nearly two hundred sites in her 1962 study.\n\nWicca took the date as a greater sabbat, often under both names. The story of John Barleycorn, the grain who is cut down and rises again as bread and ale, comes from an English folk ballad and is widely sung at modern Lammas rites.',knowAndDont:{known:'Lughnasadh is an attested Irish quarter day tied in the literature to Lugh and Tailtiu. Lammas is an attested English church festival of the first loaf. The hill-top gatherings are well documented in Ireland.',unknown:'We do not know what pre-Christian rites, if any, were held, and the medieval story of Lugh and Tailtiu was written by Christian authors. The idea of a sacrificed grain-god or \u201ccorn king\u201d owes a great deal to J. G. Frazer\u2019s nineteenth-century theories, which scholars have largely set aside.'},celebrate:['Bake a loaf (or buy a good one), and share the first piece with someone or set it out for the birds.','Make a simple corn dolly or a plait of wheat or grass to hang in the kitchen until spring.','Climb a hill or walk to a high place, and look out over the land that feeds you.','Pick blackberries or bilberries where it is permitted, and leave plenty for wildlife.','Write down the first harvest of your own year: what has ripened since spring.'],ritual:{premade:'wicca-lammas-bread-offering'},correspondences:{deities:['Lugh','Ceres','Demeter','Freyr'],herbs:['Calendula','Meadowsweet','Basil','Rosemary'],crystals:['Citrine','Peridot','Tiger Eye','Carnelian'],colors:['Gold','Orange','Yellow','Brown'],foods:['Bread','Bilberries and blackberries','New potatoes','Ale or cider','Oats and barley'],symbols:['The first loaf','Wheat sheaf','Corn dolly','The sickle','Hilltop']},sources:['M\xe1ire MacNeill, The Festival of Lughnasa (Oxford, 1962).','The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle, entry for 921 (\u201chlafm\xe6sse\u201d); tr. J. A. Giles (1847), Project Gutenberg: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/657','Ronald Hutton, The Stations of the Sun (Oxford, 1996), ch. 32\u201333.','J. A. MacCulloch, The Religion of the Ancient Celts (1911): https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/14672']},{ref:'wheel-mabon',title:'Mabon',aka:['Autumn Equinox','Harvest Home','Second Harvest','Alban Elfed'],tradition:'Wheel of the Year',summary:'The autumn equinox, around 22 September, when day and night are equal before the dark pulls ahead. A modern sabbat of the second harvest: gratitude, sharing and getting ready for winter.',honours:'The fruits, nuts and late grain of the year, and the turn toward the dark. The name honours Mabon ap Modron, \u201cson of the Mother\u201d, a mysterious figure of Welsh legend who was taken from his mother as a baby and later freed; some witches also honour Persephone\u2019s descent at this season.',history:'There was no ancient Celtic festival at the autumn equinox that we know of. In Britain the end of harvest was marked by Harvest Home, a supper for the workers when the last load came in, whenever that happened to be. Churches added a Harvest Festival service from 1843, when the Cornish vicar Robert Hawker held one in his parish, and it quickly spread. Nearby, Michaelmas (29 September) was a quarter day for rents and hiring.\n\nThe name Mabon was given by Aidan Kelly in 1974. He was looking for a story like Persephone\u2019s, of a child taken into the underworld, and found Mabon in the Welsh tale Culhwch and Olwen, where he is a prisoner who must be released to win the hero\u2019s quest. The name has become very widely loved, although historians point out that it has no link with the season in the old tales.\n\nDruids call the day Alban Elfed, and many Pagans keep it simply as the Autumn Equinox.',knowAndDont:{known:'Harvest suppers, the last sheaf customs (the \u201cneck\u201d or \u201cmare\u201d), and the church Harvest Festival are well documented in Britain and Ireland. Mabon ap Modron appears in medieval Welsh literature.',unknown:'There is no evidence of any pre-Christian equinox festival in Britain or Ireland, and nothing links Mabon to it beyond the 1974 naming. It is a modern sabbat, and none the worse for being honest about it.'},celebrate:['Make a gratitude list of the year\u2019s harvest, the good and the hard-won.','Cook with the season: apples, squash, nuts, late plums, and share the meal.','Give to a food bank or share surplus produce with neighbours.','Walk at dusk and notice the earlier dark; decide what you want to bring with you into winter.','Tidy your altar and home for the dark half of the year, and put away summer things with thanks.'],ritual:{premade:'wicca-mabon-thanks-and-balance'},correspondences:{deities:['Persephone','Demeter','Ceres'],herbs:['Sage','Rosemary','Cinnamon','Clove','Oak'],crystals:['Smoky Quartz','Tiger Eye','Red Jasper','Amber'],colors:['Orange','Brown','Gold','Red'],foods:['Apples','Blackberries','Squash','Nuts','Cider','Bread'],symbols:['The balanced scale','Apples','Cornucopia','Acorns','Falling leaves']},sources:['Ronald Hutton, The Stations of the Sun (Oxford, 1996), ch. 34\u201335 (harvest customs and Harvest Festival).','Culhwch and Olwen, in The Mabinogion, tr. Lady Charlotte Guest (1849): https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/5160','Aidan Kelly\u2019s own account of naming the sabbats (2017), summarised in religion journalism and Pagan histories; see also Hutton, The Triumph of the Moon (Oxford, 1999).']},{ref:'wheel-samhain',title:'Samhain',aka:['Halloween','All Hallows\u2019 Eve','O\xedche Shamhna','Calan Gaeaf','Witches\u2019 New Year'],tradition:'Wheel of the Year',summary:'From sunset on 31 October: summer\u2019s end and, for many witches, the new year. A night to honour the ancestors and the beloved dead, to look into the year ahead, and to rest as the dark half begins.',honours:'The dead of our families and lines, those we loved and those we never met, and the turning of the year into darkness. Many witches also honour gods of death and the dark, such as Hekate, the Morrigan or Hel, and the Otherworld that the old tales say comes close tonight.',history:'Samhain is the best-attested of the Gaelic quarter days. Medieval Irish tales place great assemblies and feasts at Samhain, and many of their strangest events happen on that night: the mounds of the Otherworld open, the dead and the s\xedde walk abroad, and heroes cross between worlds.\n\nThe Church set All Saints\u2019 Day on 1 November in the eighth and ninth centuries and All Souls\u2019 on the 2nd soon after, and Halloween grew from both. Folklorists recorded bonfires, guising in masks, turnip lanterns, apple and nut games to divine a future spouse, and food left for the dead in Ireland, Scotland, Wales and Man. Irish and Scottish emigrants carried Halloween to North America, which gave it pumpkins and trick-or-treating.\n\nThe idea that Samhain was \u201cthe Celtic New Year\u201d comes from nineteenth-century scholars and is debated; it became central in modern Wicca, where Samhain is the most important sabbat of all.',knowAndDont:{known:'Samhain is attested in the earliest Irish literature as a quarter day of assemblies and feasting, closely linked with the Otherworld. The Halloween customs of guising, divination, lanterns and feeding the dead are well documented from the eighteenth century onward.',unknown:'We have no description of pagan religious rites at Samhain, and claims that druids made sacrifices on this night, or that it was a festival of a \u201cLord of the Dead\u201d, are not supported by the sources. Whether it began the year is uncertain. The dumb supper and much modern Samhain liturgy are recent, and beautiful.'},celebrate:['Set a photo or the names of your beloved dead on a small altar with a candle, and tell their stories.','Lay a place at the table or set out a plate of food for the ancestors, and give it to the earth the next day.','Carve a turnip or pumpkin lantern and set it at the door to light the way home.','Try an old divination: apple peel thrown over the shoulder, or a few cards for the year ahead.','Rest. Go to bed early, and let the dark be the start of something rather than an ending.'],ritual:{premade:'wicca-samhain-dumb-supper'},correspondences:{deities:['Hekate','The Morrigan','Cerridwen','The Horned God','Hel','Hades','Persephone'],herbs:['Mugwort','Rosemary','Sage','Yew','Wormwood','Rowan'],crystals:['Obsidian','Jet','Smoky Quartz','Black Tourmaline','Onyx'],colors:['Black','Orange','Purple','Silver'],foods:['Apples','Nuts','Barmbrack or soul cakes','Colcannon','Pumpkin and squash'],symbols:['Turnip or pumpkin lantern','Apples','Candles in the window','Masks','The ancestors\u2019 plate']},sources:['J. A. MacCulloch, The Religion of the Ancient Celts (1911), ch. 18: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/14672','Ronald Hutton, The Stations of the Sun (Oxford, 1996), ch. 36\u201337.','Catholic Encyclopedia (1907), \u201cAll Saints\u2019 Day\u201d: https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/01315a.htm','Kevin Danaher, The Year in Ireland (Cork, 1972).']},{ref:'wheel-yule',title:'Yule',aka:['Winter Solstice','Midwinter','J\xf3l','Alban Arthan','Longest Night'],tradition:'Wheel of the Year',summary:'The winter solstice, around 21 December, the longest night. The sun stands still, then begins to return: a sabbat of candles, evergreens, feasting and hope.',honours:'The reborn sun and the light that comes back after the darkest night; the evergreen life that holds on through winter; the household and its ancestors gathered at the fire. Many witches honour the Goddess giving birth to the Sun Child, or sun and winter gods of the old north.',history:'People have marked the midwinter sun since the Stone Age: the passage tomb at Newgrange in Ireland, built around 3200 BCE, lets the rising sun into its inner chamber on the mornings around the solstice. What its builders believed, we cannot say.\n\nThe name Yule is old. Bede says the pagan English called the months of December and January \u201cGiuli\u201d, and that their year began on what is now Christmas Eve, which they called Modranecht, \u201cMothers\u2019 Night\u201d. In Norway, Snorri Sturluson wrote that King Hakon the Good (tenth century) moved the heathen Yule feast to coincide with Christmas. Through the Middle Ages, \u201cYule\u201d simply meant Christmas in English and the Scandinavian languages, and still does.\n\nThe Yule log, evergreens, wassailing and feasting are folk customs of the Christian centuries, recorded from the 1600s on. Modern Pagans returned the name Yule to the solstice itself and made it one of the eight sabbats.',knowAndDont:{known:'Newgrange and other monuments are aligned to midwinter. The word Yule and a pagan midwinter feast are attested by Bede and the sagas, and Christmas absorbed and kept the name and many customs.',unknown:'Almost nothing is known of what pagan Yule rites involved beyond feasting, drinking and sacrifice \u201cfor a good year\u201d. Claims that the Christmas tree, Yule log or holly are ancient pagan survivals are usually unproven. The Holly King and Oak King story is modern.'},celebrate:['Keep vigil through the longest night, or wake for the sunrise, and greet the returning sun.','Bring in evergreens: holly, ivy, pine or a living tree, and give the birds some suet or seed.','Burn a Yule log in a safe fireplace, or decorate a log with candles and never leave it burning unattended.','Feast with the people you love, and drink a toast to those who have gone before.','Give gifts made by hand, or give to someone in need.'],ritual:{premade:'wicca-yule-log-sun-vigil'},correspondences:{deities:['Frigg','Odin','Baldur','Ra','Dazhbog','The Horned God','Aengus \xd3g','Boann'],herbs:['Holly','Mistletoe','Juniper','Cedar','Cinnamon','Frankincense'],crystals:['Garnet','Ruby','Bloodstone','Clear Quartz','Sunstone'],colors:['Red','Green','Gold','White'],foods:['Spiced cider or mulled wine','Gingerbread','Roast vegetables','Oranges with cloves','Yule log cake'],symbols:['Yule log','Evergreens','Candles','The sun wheel','Holly and ivy','Wreath']},sources:['Bede, De temporum ratione, ch. 15 (725); tr. Faith Wallis, Bede: The Reckoning of Time (Liverpool, 1999).','Snorri Sturluson, Heimskringla, Saga of Hakon the Good, ch. 15\u201316, tr. Samuel Laing (1844): https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/598','Ronald Hutton, The Stations of the Sun (Oxford, 1996), ch. 1\u201310.','Office of Public Works, Br\xfa na B\xf3inne (Newgrange) visitor information: https://heritageireland.ie/places-to-visit/bru-na-boinne-visitor-centre-newgrange-and-knowth/']},{ref:'celtic-la_fheile_bride',title:'Imbolc \xb7 L\xe1 Fh\xe9ile Br\xedde',aka:['Imbolc','St Brigid\u2019s Day','L\xe0 Fh\xe8ill Br\xecghde','G\u0175yl Ffraid','Laa\u2019l Breeshey'],tradition:'Celtic',summary:'The first day of spring in the Gaelic year, 1 February, and the feast of Brigid. Crosses are woven, a cloth is left out for her blessing, and she is welcomed into the house.',honours:'Brigid: the goddess remembered in medieval Irish learning as poet, healer and smith, and the saint of Kildare, patron of Ireland with Patrick and Columba, of dairymaids, midwives, poets and travellers. The day also honours the turning of the farming year: lambs, milk and the first ploughing.',history:'Imbolc is named in early Irish literature as one of the four quarter days. A medieval Irish glossary describes a goddess Brigid, daughter of the Dagda, \u201ca goddess whom poets worshipped\u201d, with two sisters of the same name for healing and smithcraft. The saint, Brigid of Kildare, died around 525; her early Lives are full of miracles of milk, butter, fire and hospitality, and in the 1180s Gerald of Wales described a fire at Kildare kept burning by nineteen nuns in turn, with Brigid herself tending it on the twentieth night.\n\nThe customs of her eve were recorded across Ireland, Scotland and Man into the twentieth century. Crosses of rushes or straw were woven and hung above doors and in byres. A strip of cloth, the brat Br\xedde, was left outside to be touched by the saint as she passed, and kept for curing headaches and sickness. Girls carried a Br\xeddeog, a doll of Brigid, from house to house; in the Hebrides she was laid in a basket bed by the hearth with a white wand, and the ashes checked in the morning for her footprint.\n\nIn 2023 Ireland made the first Monday of February (or 1 February when it falls on a Friday) a public holiday in her honour, and the Brigid\u2019s flame was relit in Kildare in 1993 by the Brigidine Sisters, who still tend it.',knowAndDont:{known:'The date and name are attested in early medieval Irish texts. The goddess is described in a medieval glossary; the saint\u2019s Lives, Gerald\u2019s account of the Kildare fire, and the nineteenth- and twentieth-century folk customs are all well recorded.',unknown:'We do not know what, if any, pre-Christian rite was held on 1 February, or how far the saint\u2019s customs preserve worship of the goddess. People hold this question in different ways, and the honest answer is that it cannot be proved either way.'},celebrate:['On the eve, weave a Brigid\u2019s cross from rushes, straw or paper and hang it above the door.','Put a strip of cloth or ribbon outside overnight, and keep it through the year for comfort and healing.','Make up a little bed for Brigid by the hearth or stove, and invite her in with a candle and a word of welcome.','Visit a holy well or spring, or bless a bowl of water at home.','Write or read a poem, mend something, or care for someone who is unwell, in Brigid\u2019s three arts.'],ritual:{premade:'folk-brigid-cross-well'},correspondences:{deities:['Brigid','The Dagda'],herbs:['Rowan','Angelica','Chamomile'],crystals:['Amethyst','Garnet','Bloodstone'],colors:['White','Red','Green'],foods:['Butter','Buttermilk','Oatcakes and bannocks','Colcannon','Barmbrack'],symbols:['Brigid\u2019s cross','Brat Br\xedde','Br\xeddeog doll','Perpetual flame','Holy well']},respect:'St Brigid\u2019s Day is a living Christian feast in Ireland and the Irish diaspora, and Brigid is also honoured by Irish Pagans and polytheists. If you go to a holy well or to Kildare, behave as you would in a church: be quiet, leave nothing synthetic, and follow local custom. The Brigidine Sisters at Solas Bhr\xedde in Kildare welcome visitors of any faith.',sources:['Kuno Meyer (tr.), The Wooing of Emer, CELT: https://celt.ucc.ie/published/T301021/index.html','Alexander Carmichael, Carmina Gadelica vol. 1 (1900): https://archive.org/details/carminagadelicah01carm','Catholic Encyclopedia (1907), \u201cSt. Brigid of Ireland\u201d: https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/02784b.htm','Kevin Danaher, The Year in Ireland (Cork, 1972), \u201cThe Feast of St Brigid\u201d.','Solas Bhr\xedde Centre, Kildare: https://solasbhride.ie/']},{ref:'celtic-bealtaine',title:'Bealtaine',aka:['Beltane','L\xe1 Bealtaine','May Day','Calan Mai','Latha Bealltainn','Shenn Laa Boaldyn'],tradition:'Celtic',summary:'The first day of summer in the Gaelic year, 1 May. Fires were lit and cattle driven past them for protection before they went up to summer pastures; dew, blossom and rowan guarded house and herd.',honours:'The land coming into summer, the herds, the dairy and the household\u2019s luck. The fire itself was the great protector. In Wales, Calan Mai is the start of summer too, and the night before is one of the \u201cspirit nights\u201d when the Otherworld is close.',history:'Bealtaine is one of the four quarter days of the early Irish texts. A tenth-century glossary says its name meant \u201clucky fire\u201d and describes two fires made by druids with incantations, between which cattle were driven against disease each year. Later writers placed a great Bealtaine assembly at the Hill of Uisneach, the symbolic centre of Ireland.\n\nFolk custom kept the spirit of protection strong. In Ireland and Scotland the household fire was put out and relit from a bonfire; cattle were driven past the flames or singed with a burning bush; rowan and primroses were placed on doors and byres; the first water drawn from the well and the May dew were prized, and it was unlucky to give away fire, milk or salt on May Day. In the Highlands, a Bealltainn bannock was baked and shared, and one piece marked for whoever must leap the fire.\n\nIn recent years a Bealtaine fire has again been lit at Uisneach as a public festival, and in Wales the old May carols (carolau Mai) are sung again in some places.',knowAndDont:{known:'The quarter day, the protective fires, and the dew, rowan, bannock and dairy customs are well recorded from the medieval period to living memory.',unknown:'The glossary\u2019s druids are seen through Christian eyes centuries later. We don\u2019t know which gods, if any, were honoured; claims that Bealtaine means \u201cfire of Bel\u201d or honours a god Belenus are guesses that most scholars doubt. The maypole and the May Queen are English customs, not Gaelic ones.'},celebrate:['Rise early on May morning and wash your face in the dew.','Put primroses, marigolds or a sprig of rowan on your doorstep or windowsill.','Light a fire safely outdoors with others, or a candle at home, and walk around it sunwise for protection through summer.','Bake oatcakes or a bannock, and share it out.','Bless your animals and garden: walk them round, speak a blessing, and check fences and gates.'],ritual:{premade:'folk-bealtaine-dew'},correspondences:{deities:['The Dagda','Brigid','Rhiannon'],herbs:['Rowan','Hawthorn','Birch','Meadowsweet','Elder'],crystals:['Carnelian','Emerald','Red Jasper'],colors:['Green','Yellow','Red'],foods:['Oatcakes and bannocks','Butter and cream','Milk','Eggs'],symbols:['Twin fires','Cattle','May bush','Rowan and red thread','May dew','Primroses']},sources:['J. A. MacCulloch, The Religion of the Ancient Celts (1911), ch. 18: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/14672','Kevin Danaher, The Year in Ireland (Cork, 1972), \u201cMay Day\u201d.','Ronald Hutton, The Stations of the Sun (Oxford, 1996), ch. 21.','Kuno Meyer (tr.), The Wooing of Emer, CELT: https://celt.ucc.ie/published/T301021/index.html']},{ref:'celtic-lughnasadh',title:'Lughnasadh',aka:['L\xfanasa','Lammas','Garland Sunday','Bilberry Sunday','Reek Sunday','G\u0175yl Awst','Lughnasa'],tradition:'Celtic',summary:'The harvest festival of the god Lugh, on 1 August: the start of autumn in the Gaelic year. Assemblies, games, matchmaking, hill-climbing and the first fruits of the harvest.',honours:'Lugh, the bright, many-skilled god of the Irish tales, and Tailtiu, his foster-mother, who died after clearing the plains of Ireland for farming. It also honours the harvest itself, eaten for the first time, and the end of the \u201chungry month\u201d of July.',history:'Medieval Irish texts say that Lugh founded the \xd3enach Tailten, the assembly at Tailtiu (Teltown, County Meath), as funeral games for his foster-mother. Assemblies, horse races, trading, contests and marriages took place there at the start of August, and similar gatherings were held at other royal sites.\n\nIn folk custom the festival lived on as Garland Sunday, Bilberry Sunday or Height Sunday, at the end of July or start of August. People climbed hills and mountains, picked bilberries, danced and courted, and in many places brought the first new potatoes or a sheaf to the table. M\xe1ire MacNeill\u2019s The Festival of Lughnasa (1962) traced these gatherings at nearly two hundred sites and found stories of Lugh defeating a dark figure, Crom Dubh, to win the harvest. The pilgrimage up Croagh Patrick on Reek Sunday and the Puck Fair at Killorglin in August are among its living descendants.\n\nIn Wales the day is G\u0175yl Awst, the August feast, and in England it is Lammas.',knowAndDont:{known:'The quarter day, the link with Lugh and Tailtiu in medieval texts, and the hill-top and bilberry customs of later centuries are all well documented.',unknown:'The medieval accounts are literary and written by Christians. MacNeill\u2019s reconstruction of a myth of Lugh and Crom Dubh is careful but interpretive. We do not know the pre-Christian rites.'},celebrate:['Climb a hill or walk somewhere high, and leave a small offering of flowers or a little bread at the top (biodegradable only).','Gather bilberries or blackberries where it is allowed, and make a crumble or jam to share.','Eat the first new potatoes, or bake a loaf from the new season\u2019s flour.','Hold games: a race, a contest of skill, a craft swap, in Lugh\u2019s honour as master of all arts.','Make or renew a promise or a friendship; this was a time for bargains, handfasts and agreements.'],ritual:{title:'A hill-top first fruits for Lugh',outline:['Choose a safe hill, high place or simply your garden, and bring water, a small loaf or some berries, and a ribbon or flower.','At the top, stand and look over the land. Say who you are and that you have come to honour Lugh and Tailtiu at the harvest\u2019s start.','Break the bread or share the berries. Leave a small portion on the earth for Tailtiu and the land, and eat the rest.','Name one skill you want to grow this coming season, and ask Lugh, master of every art, to help you learn it.','Tie the ribbon to your bag or a garden plant (not a wild tree), and take it home as a reminder.','Walk down with thanks, and take all your rubbish with you.']},correspondences:{deities:['Lugh','Lleu Llaw Gyffes','The Dagda'],herbs:['Meadowsweet','Calendula','Yarrow','Oak'],crystals:['Citrine','Carnelian','Peridot'],colors:['Gold','Orange','Green'],foods:['Bilberries','New potatoes','Bread from new flour','Blackberries','Ale or cider'],symbols:['The hill','Bilberries','First sheaf','Spear of Lugh','Games and races']},sources:['M\xe1ire MacNeill, The Festival of Lughnasa (Oxford, 1962).','J. A. MacCulloch, The Religion of the Ancient Celts (1911), ch. 18: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/14672','Kevin Danaher, The Year in Ireland (Cork, 1972), \u201cL\xfanasa\u201d.','Kuno Meyer (tr.), The Wooing of Emer, CELT: https://celt.ucc.ie/published/T301021/index.html']},{ref:'celtic-samhain_gaelic',title:'Samhain (O\xedche Shamhna)',aka:['Samhain','Samhuinn','Halloween','O\xedche Shamhna','Calan Gaeaf','Hop-tu-Naa'],tradition:'Celtic',summary:'\u201cSummer\u2019s end\u201d, kept from sunset on 31 October: the start of winter and, in many tellings, of the Gaelic year. The Otherworld draws near, fires are lit, guisers go door to door, and food is set out for the dead.',honours:'The dead of the household and the community, and the beings of the s\xedde, the Otherworld hills, who move about on this night. It also marks the herds brought down from summer pastures and the end of the farming year.',history:'In the earliest Irish literature Samhain is a time of assemblies and feasting, and of crossing between worlds. In one tale the hero Nera goes out on Samhain night and finds his way into the s\xedd of Cr\xfaachan; in others, Otherworld women and warriors appear, and kings meet their deaths. The great feast of Tara was said to be held at Samhain.\n\nIrish, Scottish and Manx folklore kept the night as one for fires and protection, for guising and mischief, and for fortune-telling about love and death: nuts set to burn on the hearth, apples bobbed or peeled, cabbages pulled blindfold. A place might be left at the table and the door unlatched for the dead. In Wales the eve of Calan Gaeaf was an ysbrydnos, a spirit night, when it was wise to hurry home before the tailless black sow, the hwch ddu gwta, caught you. On the Isle of Man the night is Hop-tu-Naa, with turnip lanterns and songs still today.\n\nThe church feasts of All Saints and All Souls were placed beside it, and Halloween as the world knows it grew from both.',knowAndDont:{known:'Samhain as an Otherworld-haunted assembly time is well established in medieval texts. The folk customs of fires, guising, divination and the dead are richly recorded in the eighteenth to twentieth centuries.',unknown:'There is no reliable description of pagan Samhain worship. Stories of druid sacrifices or of a god of death called Samhain are modern errors. Whether it began the year is likely but not proven; the idea comes partly from nineteenth-century scholars.'},celebrate:['Light a candle in the window to guide the dead home, and say their names.','Set a plate of food and a cup aside for them at dinner, and give it to the earth in the morning.','Carve a turnip lantern (the older way) and put it by the door.','Play the old divination games with apples and nuts, with friends, for fun and for meaning.','Visit the graves of your people, or a local graveyard, and tidy one neglected stone.'],ritual:{premade:'hedge-ancestor-candle-evening'},correspondences:{deities:['The Morrigan','The Dagda','The Cailleach','Aengus \xd3g','Cerridwen','Manann\xe1n mac Lir'],herbs:['Rowan','Yew','Mugwort','Elder'],crystals:['Jet','Obsidian','Smoky Quartz'],colors:['Black','Orange','White'],foods:['Barmbrack','Colcannon','Apples and nuts','Champ','Oatcakes'],symbols:['Turnip lantern','Bonfire','Masks and guisers','Apples','The s\xedd mound','Unlatched door']},sources:['J. A. MacCulloch, The Religion of the Ancient Celts (1911), ch. 18: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/14672','Kevin Danaher, The Year in Ireland (Cork, 1972), \u201cSamhain\u201d.','Ronald Hutton, The Stations of the Sun (Oxford, 1996), ch. 36\u201337.','Trefor M. Owen, Welsh Folk Customs (Cardiff, 1959).']},{ref:'norse-thorrablot',title:'\xdeorrabl\xf3t',aka:['Thorrablot','B\xf3ndadagur','Husbands\u2019 Day','Midwinter feast'],tradition:'Norse & Heathen',summary:'The feast at the start of \xdeorri, the old Icelandic midwinter month, beginning on a Friday in late January. Icelanders gather to eat traditional preserved foods, sing and drink toasts through the worst of the winter.',honours:'\xdeorri, the personification of deep winter, and the endurance of the people who got through it. Modern heathens also honour the gods, the land spirits and the ancestors who kept the old food and the old calendar alive.',history:'\xdeorri is the fourth month of winter in the old Icelandic calendar, which counted the year in weeks of summer and winter rather than by the Roman months. A medieval story (in Orkneyinga saga and the related tale \u201cHow Norway was settled\u201d) makes \xdeorri a king who held a sacrifice at midwinter, called the \xfeorrabl\xf3t, and whose daughter G\xf3i vanished and was searched for until the next month, which bears her name.\n\nIn later Icelandic folk custom the first day of \xdeorri was B\xf3ndadagur, the farmer\u2019s or husbands\u2019 day: the man of the house was said to go out in his shirt with one trouser leg on, hopping round the house to welcome \xdeorri in. The feast itself was revived in the nineteenth century by Icelandic students and societies, beginning in 1873, as part of the national awakening. In 1958 the Reykjav\xedk restaurant Nausti\xf0 began serving \u201c\xfeorramatur\u201d in wooden troughs, and the modern feast took shape.\n\nToday \xdeorrabl\xf3t is kept all over Iceland and by Icelandic communities abroad, and the \xc1satr\xfaarf\xe9lagi\xf0 (Iceland\u2019s modern heathen fellowship) holds its own bl\xf3t at this season.',knowAndDont:{known:'The month of \xdeorri and the custom of B\xf3ndadagur are genuinely old Icelandic tradition. The name \xfeorrabl\xf3t appears in a medieval story, and the modern feast and its food date from 1873 and 1958.',unknown:'We do not know if a real midwinter sacrifice called \xfeorrabl\xf3t was ever held in heathen times; the medieval story may be a learned tale explaining month names. \xdeorri may have been a winter spirit or a by-name of Thor, but that is speculation.'},celebrate:['Share a winter feast of preserved foods: smoked or cured meat, dried fish, pickles, rye bread, butter.','Toast the gods, the land and the ancestors with ale, mead or a non-alcoholic drink, and pass the cup round.','On the first morning, bring breakfast or a small gift to the husband, partner or farmer of the household.','Sing, tell winter stories, or read a saga aloud by candlelight.','Check on neighbours and elders who may struggle in the cold.'],ritual:{premade:'folk-simple-blot'},correspondences:{deities:['Thor','Odin','Skadi'],herbs:['Juniper','Birch','Yarrow'],crystals:['Hematite','Smoky Quartz','Clear Quartz'],colors:['White','Red','Black'],foods:['Hangikj\xf6t (smoked lamb)','Har\xf0fiskur (dried fish)','R\xfagbrau\xf0 (rye bread)','Butter','Pickled foods','Brenniv\xedn or ale'],symbols:['Wooden trough (trog)','Drinking horn','Snow','The hearth fire']},sources:['\xc1rni Bj\xf6rnsson, Icelandic Feasts and Holidays (Reykjav\xedk, 1980).','Orkneyinga saga, ch. 1\u20133, tr. Jon A. Hjaltalin and Gilbert Goudie (1873).','Icelandic Canadian Club of Toronto, \u201cHistory of Thorrablot\u201d: https://www.icct.info/history-of-thorrablot','L\xf6gberg-Heimskringla, 17 February 1995, p. 8 (on Nausti\xf0 and \xfeorramatur): https://timarit.is/page/2239925']},{ref:'norse-disablot',title:'D\xedsabl\xf3t',aka:['Disablot','D\xedsting','Disting','Bl\xf3t to the d\xedsir'],tradition:'Norse & Heathen',summary:'A sacrifice to the d\xedsir, the female guardian spirits and ancestral mothers of a family. Many modern heathens keep it in late winter around the February full moon, after the Swedish D\xedsting held at Uppsala.',honours:'The d\xedsir: protective female powers who watch over a family line, who may be ancestral mothers, goddesses, or something in between. Freyja is called Vanad\xeds, \u201cd\xeds of the Vanir\u201d, and Frigg is often honoured too. It is also a time to remember the women of your own line.',history:'Several sagas mention a d\xedsabl\xf3t. In Ynglinga saga, the Swedish king A\xf0ils rides around the hall at Uppsala during the d\xedsabl\xf3t, falls from his horse and dies. In Egils saga a d\xedsabl\xf3t is held at a farm in Norway in autumn, with heavy drinking. In V\xedga-Gl\xfams saga a feast for the d\xedsir is held at the Winter Nights in mid-October. So in the sagas the date varies.\n\nThe late-winter date comes from Sweden. Snorri Sturluson wrote that in heathen times the chief sacrifice of the Swedes was held at Uppsala in the month of G\xf3i (February to March), with a great assembly and market that lasted a week. After conversion the market was moved to Candlemas; Swedish sources call it D\xedsting, the \u201cThing of the d\xedsir\u201d, and a Disting market is still held in Uppsala each February.\n\nModern heathens therefore keep D\xedsabl\xf3t either at the Winter Nights or in late winter, many at the full moon of February.',knowAndDont:{known:'D\xedsabl\xf3t is named in several sagas; the Uppsala assembly in late winter is described by Snorri; the Disting market is historical and still runs.',unknown:'Who the d\xedsir were is not clear: ancestors, fate-women, valkyrie-like beings or minor goddesses. The sagas were written down by Christians two or three centuries after conversion. Whether the Uppsala assembly was itself a d\xedsabl\xf3t, rather than a general sacrifice, is debated.'},celebrate:['Set a candle by photos or names of the women of your family, your mothers\u2019 line, or women who mothered you.','Pour a little mead, milk or juice to the d\xedsir and ask for their protection over your household.','Cook a dish from your grandmothers\u2019 kitchens, or one from the region your people came from.','Write a short letter of thanks to a woman, living or dead, who shaped you.','If family history is painful, honour the protective spirits of your chosen family instead; that is fully in keeping.'],ritual:{premade:'folk-simple-blot',title:'A bl\xf3t for the d\xedsir',outline:['At dusk, set a cloth with a candle, a bowl, and a horn or cup of mead, milk or juice. Add names or photos of the women of your line.','Light the candle and say you are holding bl\xf3t for the d\xedsir who guard your family.','Hallow the drink by raising it and asking the d\xedsir to receive it. Drink a little.','Speak to them: thanks for protection, and one thing you ask for the household this year.','Pour the rest into the bowl, and later onto the earth outside.','Sit a while in quiet before putting out the candle.']},correspondences:{deities:['Freyja','Frigg'],herbs:['Mugwort','Yarrow','Birch','Juniper'],crystals:['Moonstone','Amber','Garnet'],colors:['Red','White','Silver'],foods:['Mead or milk','Porridge with butter','Bread','Family recipes'],symbols:['Drinking horn','Spindle','Keys of the household','The full moon']},sources:['Snorri Sturluson, Heimskringla, Saga of Olaf Haraldson, ch. 76\u201377, tr. Samuel Laing (1844): https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/598','Snorri Sturluson, Ynglinga saga, ch. 29 (A\xf0ils at the d\xedsabl\xf3t).','Egils saga Skalla-Gr\xedmssonar, ch. 44; V\xedga-Gl\xfams saga, ch. 6.','Rudolf Simek, Dictionary of Northern Mythology (Cambridge, 1993), \u201cd\xedsir\u201d, \u201cd\xedsabl\xf3t\u201d.']},{ref:'norse-sigrblot',title:'Sigrbl\xf3t \xb7 Sumardagurinn fyrsti',aka:['Sigrbl\xf3t','Sigurbl\xf3t','First Day of Summer','Sumardagurinn fyrsti','Summer Finding'],tradition:'Norse & Heathen',summary:'The first day of summer in the old Icelandic year, the first Thursday after 18 April. The sagas speak of a sacrifice at summer\u2019s start \u201cfor victory\u201d, for the season of voyages and undertakings ahead.',honours:'Odin, giver of victory, and the gods and land spirits who favour a good season. In Iceland the day honours summer itself, with gifts for children and gladness after the long winter.',history:'Snorri Sturluson writes in Ynglinga saga that Odin set three great sacrifices: one at the beginning of winter for a good year, one at midwinter for growth, and a third at the beginning of summer, for victory. Summer was the season for raiding, trading and travel, so the third bl\xf3t asked for success in what lay ahead.\n\nThe old Icelandic calendar divides the year into summer and winter, and the first day of summer still falls on a Thursday between 19 and 25 April. Sumardagurinn fyrsti is a public holiday in Iceland, kept with parades, scouts, sports and summer gifts (sumargjafir), a custom older than Christmas presents there. Folk belief held that if summer and winter \u201cfroze together\u201d the night before, with frost, it would be a good summer.\n\nMany modern heathens keep Sigrbl\xf3t on this day or around the spring equinox.',knowAndDont:{known:'Snorri records the three seasonal sacrifices and names the summer one as for victory. The Icelandic first day of summer and its gift custom are long-standing and still kept.',unknown:'Snorri wrote in the thirteenth century, as a Christian, and presented Odin as a human king; we can\u2019t be sure how the bl\xf3t was really held or whether it was kept everywhere.'},celebrate:['Give a small summer gift to a child or friend.','Hold a bl\xf3t for success: name one undertaking for the summer and ask Odin or your gods for victory in it.','Get outside: plan the first long walk, sail, journey or project of the season.','Watch for frost the night before, and see if summer and winter \u201cfreeze together\u201d.','Clean and ready your tools, bike, boots or boat for the months ahead.'],ritual:{premade:'folk-simple-blot'},correspondences:{deities:['Odin','Thor','Tyr','Freyr'],herbs:['Birch','Yarrow','Oak'],crystals:['Tiger Eye','Carnelian','Bloodstone'],colors:['Red','Gold','Green'],foods:['Pancakes','Mead or ale','Fresh bread','Smoked fish'],symbols:['The ship','Spear','First flowers','Sun']},sources:['Snorri Sturluson, Ynglinga saga, ch. 8; in Heimskringla, tr. Lee M. Hollander (Austin, 1964).','\xc1rni Bj\xf6rnsson, Icelandic Feasts and Holidays (Reykjav\xedk, 1980).']},{ref:'norse-midsummer',title:'Midsummer (Mi\xf0sumar)',aka:['Midsommar','Mi\xf0sumar','J\xf3nsmessa','Sankthans','Summer Solstice'],tradition:'Norse & Heathen',summary:'The height of the sun\u2019s power at the summer solstice: bonfires, flowers, feasting and gathering, the season when the old assemblies met.',honours:'The sun and the full green earth; Baldur, the shining god whose death many modern heathens link with the turning of the light; Freyr and Freyja for fertility and plenty; and the community gathered together.',history:'There is no clear record of a heathen midsummer bl\xf3t in the sagas; Snorri\u2019s three great sacrifices are at winter, midwinter and summer\u2019s start. But midsummer was a time of gathering: Iceland\u2019s Althing, the national assembly founded in 930, met at \xdeingvellir for two weeks in June, with law, trade, marriages and feuds.\n\nIn the Christian centuries Scandinavians kept St John\u2019s Eve (J\xf3nsmessa in Iceland, Sankthans in Norway and Denmark) with bonfires and herbs. In Iceland, it was said that cows could speak, seals take human form, and the dew of J\xf3nsmessun\xf3tt had healing power if you rolled in it naked. In Sweden, midsommar is the great national festival: a flower-decked pole raised and danced around, wreaths, herring, new potatoes and strawberries, and girls putting seven flowers under their pillows to dream of a husband. The Swedish midsummer pole was probably borrowed from the German maypole in the late Middle Ages.\n\nModern heathens keep the solstice as one of the high points of the year.',knowAndDont:{known:'The Althing\u2019s June assembly is historical. Scandinavian midsummer bonfires, dew, herb and flower customs are well recorded in Christian times.',unknown:'Whether there was a specific midsummer sacrifice in heathen Scandinavia is uncertain; the sources are silent. The link of Baldur with midsummer is a modern interpretation, influenced by Frazer.'},celebrate:['Gather wildflowers for a wreath (pick only common flowers, a few of each).','Hold a bonfire or a candle-lit supper outdoors, safely, and stay up for the light night.','Eat the Swedish way: new potatoes, dill, pickled herring or smoked fish, strawberries and cream.','Put seven different flowers under your pillow and see what you dream.','Meet with your community: a gathering, a picnic, a group decision or a promise renewed.'],ritual:{premade:'green-harvesting-herbs-with-thanks'},correspondences:{deities:['Baldur','Freyr','Freyja','Thor'],herbs:['St John\u2019s Wort','Birch','Yarrow','Meadowsweet','Oak'],crystals:['Sunstone','Amber','Citrine'],colors:['Gold','Yellow','Green','Blue'],foods:['New potatoes with dill','Pickled herring','Strawberries and cream','Mead or ale'],symbols:['Bonfire','Midsummer pole','Flower wreath','Sun wheel','Dew']},sources:['\xc1rni Bj\xf6rnsson, Icelandic Feasts and Holidays (Reykjav\xedk, 1980), \u201cJ\xf3nsmessa\u201d.','Jesse Byock, Viking Age Iceland (London, 2001), on the Althing.','\xdeingvellir National Park, history of the Althing: https://www.thingvellir.is/en/education/history/','James George Frazer, The Golden Bough (abridged 1922), ch. 62\u201365: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/3623']},{ref:'norse-haustblot',title:'Haustbl\xf3t',aka:['Autumn bl\xf3t','Harvest bl\xf3t','Autumn Equinox'],tradition:'Norse & Heathen',summary:'An autumn harvest bl\xf3t kept by many modern heathens at the equinox, giving thanks to the gods and the land for the year\u2019s harvest.',honours:'Freyr, lord of harvest, peace and plenty; Sif of the golden hair, often linked by modern heathens with ripe grain; Thor who blesses the fields with rain; and the land spirits (landv\xe6ttir) who share the land with us.',history:'The sagas speak of a sacrifice at the start of winter, in mid-October, \u201cfor a good year\u201d, and of toasts at feasts to Njord and Freyr \u201cfor good seasons and peace\u201d. A separate autumn equinox bl\xf3t is not recorded; Haustbl\xf3t, \u201cautumn sacrifice\u201d, is a modern name for a modern observance, though the thanksgiving it expresses is very old.\n\nIn Scandinavian folk custom the end of harvest had its own rites. The last sheaf was left for Odin\u2019s horse or the birds, or made into a figure; a harvest feast was held for the workers. In Norway and Sweden a sheaf of grain, the julenek, was later set out for the birds at Christmas.\n\nMany heathen groups now keep an autumn bl\xf3t at the equinox, and some move it to the Winter Nights.',knowAndDont:{known:'Seasonal sacrifices for a good year and toasts for harvest and peace are attested in the sagas; harvest-end customs are recorded in Scandinavian folklore.',unknown:'There is no evidence of a heathen equinox festival. Sif\u2019s link with grain is a modern reading of her golden hair, not something the sources state.'},celebrate:['Bake bread or cook with the harvest and set the first portion aside for the land spirits.','Hold a bl\xf3t of thanks, naming everything the year has given you.','Leave a sheaf, seed head or handful of grain out for the birds.','Put your garden to bed, saving seed for next year.','Share food with someone who needs it.'],ritual:{premade:'folk-gratitude-ritual',title:'Harvest thanks to Freyr and the land',outline:['Set out bread, apples or produce from your area, a cup of mead, cider or juice, and a bowl.','Hallow the space by walking round it with a lit candle, and say that you have come to give thanks.','Raise the cup to Freyr for the harvest and to the landv\xe6ttir for sharing the land, and drink.','Name aloud what the year has brought, good and hard.','Pour the rest of the cup into the bowl, and take it and some of the food outside for the land.','Share the remaining food with your household.']},correspondences:{deities:['Freyr','Freyja','Thor'],herbs:['Oak','Birch','Rowan','Yarrow'],crystals:['Amber','Tiger Eye','Moss Agate'],colors:['Gold','Brown','Green'],foods:['Bread','Apples','Mead or cider','Barley','Root vegetables'],symbols:['Last sheaf','Boar','Drinking horn','Golden grain']},sources:['Snorri Sturluson, Heimskringla, Saga of Hakon the Good, ch. 16, tr. Samuel Laing (1844): https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/598','Rudolf Simek, Dictionary of Northern Mythology (Cambridge, 1993), \u201cbl\xf3t\u201d.','Hilda Ellis Davidson, Myths and Symbols in Pagan Europe (Manchester, 1988).']},{ref:'norse-vetrnaetur',title:'Winter Nights (Vetrn\xe6tur)',aka:['Vetrn\xe6tur','Fyrsti vetrardagur','\xc1lfabl\xf3t','D\xedsabl\xf3t','First Day of Winter'],tradition:'Norse & Heathen',summary:'The start of winter in the Icelandic year, a Saturday in late October. The sagas tell of a bl\xf3t \u201cfor a good year\u201d, and it is a time to honour the \xe1lfar (elves) and the d\xedsir.',honours:'The gods, for a good year; the \xe1lfar, spirits of the land and of the mound-dwelling dead; the d\xedsir, protective ancestral mothers; and the household itself, as the herds come in and winter closes round.',history:'In the old Icelandic calendar winter begins on a Saturday between 21 and 27 October, and the two \u201cwinter nights\u201d before it were a time of feasting. Snorri\u2019s list of Odin\u2019s sacrifices puts the first at the beginning of winter, for a good year. In G\xedsla saga a Winter Nights feast is held and \xdeorgr\xedmr sacrifices to Freyr; in V\xedga-Gl\xfams saga there is a feast for the d\xedsir at the Winter Nights.\n\nThe Icelandic skald Sigvatr \xde\xf3r\xf0arson, travelling through Sweden around 1019, wrote verses about being turned away from farm after farm because the people inside were holding \xe1lfabl\xf3t, a sacrifice to the elves, and would not let a stranger in. It is one of the few eyewitness glimpses of a private heathen rite.\n\nToday Fyrsti vetrardagur is still marked in Iceland, and many heathens keep the Winter Nights with bl\xf3t to the \xe1lfar, d\xedsir and ancestors, much as others keep Samhain.',knowAndDont:{known:'The date is part of the old Icelandic calendar. Winter Nights feasts and sacrifices appear in several sagas, and Sigvatr\u2019s \xe1lfabl\xf3t verses are a contemporary eleventh-century source.',unknown:'Sigvatr does not say what the \xe1lfabl\xf3t involved, only that it was private and closed to strangers. Who the \xe1lfar were exactly, and how they relate to the dead, is debated.'},celebrate:['Close the house for the evening: just family or close friends, as in the old \xe1lfabl\xf3t.','Set out an offering of food and drink for the \xe1lfar and the land spirits, outside or at the threshold.','Honour the d\xedsir and ancestors with a candle and their names.','Bring the garden in, store food, and check your winter supplies.','Drink to a good winter and a good year.'],ritual:{premade:'hedge-ancestor-candle-evening'},correspondences:{deities:['Freyr','Freyja','Odin','Frigg'],herbs:['Juniper','Yew','Rowan','Mugwort'],crystals:['Jet','Smoky Quartz','Amber'],colors:['Black','Red','Brown'],foods:['Roast meat or stew','Porridge with butter','Mead or ale','Bread'],symbols:['Closed door','Burial mound','Drinking horn','First frost']},sources:['Sigvatr \xde\xf3r\xf0arson, Austrfararv\xedsur (c. 1019), in Heimskringla, Saga of Olaf Haraldson.','G\xedsla saga S\xfarssonar, ch. 15; V\xedga-Gl\xfams saga, ch. 6.','Snorri Sturluson, Ynglinga saga, ch. 8.','Rudolf Simek, Dictionary of Northern Mythology (Cambridge, 1993), \u201c\xe1lfabl\xf3t\u201d, \u201cvetrn\xe6tur\u201d.']},{ref:'norse-jol',title:'Yule (J\xf3l)',aka:['J\xf3l','Jul','J\xf3labl\xf3t','Midwinter','Yuletide'],tradition:'Norse & Heathen',summary:'The great midwinter feast, traditionally kept for twelve nights: drinking to the gods and the ancestors for a good year and peace, and welcoming back the sun.',honours:'Odin, the Yule-father (J\xf3lnir is one of his names); Njord and Freyr for a good year and peace; Thor; the dead, who were remembered with a memorial cup; and the household gathered round the fire.',history:'J\xf3l is the oldest name we have for a Germanic midwinter feast. Snorri Sturluson says that before King Hakon the Good, heathen Yule began on \u201cslaughter night\u201d at midwinter and was kept for three nights, and that Hakon made a law that it should begin at the same time as the Christian feast, with every household brewing ale for it. He also describes how a sacrificial feast was held: blood sprinkled with twigs, meat boiled for everyone, and toasts passed over the fire, first to Odin for victory and power, then to Njord and Freyr for good seasons and peace, then the bragafull, and a memorial cup for kinsmen who had died.\n\nYule merged with Christmas, and \u201cjul\u201d still means Christmas in Scandinavia. Folk custom kept many old elements: Yule ale, the Yule goat, the sheaf for the birds, food left out for the house spirit (nisse or tomte), and the belief that the dead and the Wild Hunt are abroad.\n\nMost modern heathens keep J\xf3l from the solstice for twelve nights, with the first night as Mother Night after Bede.',knowAndDont:{known:'Snorri\u2019s account of the Yule law and of how toasts were made is a key source. The word j\xf3l, the Yule ale, and the folk customs of the house spirit and the Yule goat are well documented.',unknown:'Snorri wrote about two centuries after Hakon. The exact date of heathen Yule (a full moon, the solstice, or mid-January) is debated, and the \u201ctwelve nights\u201d is a Christian period that modern heathens have taken over.'},celebrate:['Brew or buy a Yule ale, mead or cider, and drink toasts over the fire: to the gods, to a good year and peace, and to your dead.','Leave porridge with butter out for the house spirit on Yule night.','Put out a sheaf of grain or seed for the birds.','Keep the twelve nights with a small candle or toast each night, and some quiet reflection.','Make an oath for the coming year over the cup, and keep it short and realistic.'],ritual:{premade:'folk-simple-blot',title:'Yule toasts (full)',outline:['Light the hearth or candles and gather your household with a horn or cup of ale, mead or juice.','First toast, to Odin: for wisdom and strength in the year ahead.','Second toast, to Njord and Freyr: til \xe1rs ok fri\xf0ar, \u201cfor a good year and peace\u201d.','A bragafull: anyone may make a promise or a boast for the coming year.','The minni: name and drink to those who have died.','Pour the last of the cup outside for the land and the dead.']},correspondences:{deities:['Odin','Frigg','Freyr','Njord','Thor','Baldur'],herbs:['Juniper','Holly','Mistletoe','Birch','Cinnamon'],crystals:['Garnet','Amber','Bloodstone','Clear Quartz'],colors:['Red','Green','Gold','White'],foods:['Roast pork or ham','Yule ale or mead','Rice porridge','Gingerbread','Spiced bread'],symbols:['Yule goat','Drinking horn','Sun wheel','Evergreens','Sheaf for the birds','Wild Hunt']},sources:['Snorri Sturluson, Heimskringla, Saga of Hakon the Good, ch. 15\u201316, tr. Samuel Laing (1844): https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/598','Bede, De temporum ratione, ch. 15 (725).','Ronald Hutton, The Stations of the Sun (Oxford, 1996), ch. 1.','Rudolf Simek, Dictionary of Northern Mythology (Cambridge, 1993), \u201cj\xf3l\u201d.']},{ref:'hellenic-kronia',title:'Kronia',aka:['Cronia','Festival of Kronos'],tradition:'Hellenic',summary:'The festival of Kronos on 12 Hekatombaion, in high summer, when masters and the enslaved feasted at the same table in memory of the Golden Age.',honours:'Kronos, the Titan who ruled before Zeus, and his consort Rhea. In his reign, Hesiod says, people lived like gods, without toil or grief, and the earth gave its fruit freely. The Kronia remembers that lost age of equality and plenty.',history:'The Kronia was one of the older festivals of Athens, held in the first month of the year, after the harvest. Demosthenes mentions it as a day when the Council did not sit. The Roman writer Macrobius, quoting the Athenian historian Philochorus and the poet Accius, says that at the Kronia heads of households ate with their slaves and served them, and that the Greeks honoured Kronos with this custom long before the Romans kept the Saturnalia.\n\nKronos and Rhea had a sanctuary in Athens near the Olympieion. The festival was a reversal of the everyday order, a glimpse of the world as it was imagined before hierarchy, labour and law.\n\nModern Hellenic polytheists keep the Kronia as a day of shared food and equality, and some use it to reflect on justice and labour today.',knowAndDont:{known:'The date, the feast of masters and slaves together, and the link with the Golden Age are attested by Philochorus (via Macrobius), Accius, and Athenian inscriptions and speeches.',unknown:'We know little about any public sacrifice or temple rite. The festival\u2019s meaning was already being explained by ancient writers in different ways.'},celebrate:['Cook a meal and serve the people who usually serve you: housemates, carers, staff, parents.','Eat together at one table with no head and no ranks.','Give a day\u2019s pay, or a day of work, to a cause that supports workers or people in poverty.','Read Hesiod\u2019s Golden Age passage in Works and Days (lines 109\u2013126) and talk about what a fair world would look like.','Pour a libation to Kronos and Rhea for abundance shared.'],ritual:{premade:'folk-khernips-libation',title:'A Golden Age table',outline:['Wash your hands in khernips, then light a candle for Hestia.','Lay one table for everyone, with the best food you can manage, and seat people at random.','Pour a little wine, juice or water to Kronos and Rhea, asking for a world with enough for all.','Let the person who usually cooks or serves be served first.','At the end, each person names one thing they will do to share more fairly in the coming year.']},correspondences:{deities:['Kronos','Rhea','Demeter'],herbs:['Bay','Basil','Thyme'],crystals:['Citrine','Smoky Quartz','Onyx'],colors:['Gold','Black'],foods:['Barley bread','Figs','Honey cakes','Wine mixed with water','Summer fruit'],symbols:['The sickle','The shared table','Golden Age']},sources:['Macrobius, Saturnalia 1.7.37 and 1.10.22 (quoting Accius and Philochorus): https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Macrobius/Saturnalia/home.html','Hesiod, Works and Days 109\u2013126, tr. H. G. Evelyn-White (1914): https://www.theoi.com/Text/HesiodWorksDays.html','H. W. Parke, Festivals of the Athenians (London, 1977), Hekatombaion.','Theoi Project, \u201cKronos\u201d: https://www.theoi.com/Titan/TitanKronos.html']},{ref:'hellenic-panathenaia',title:'Panathenaia',aka:['Panathenaea','Great Panathenaia','Athena\u2019s birthday'],tradition:'Hellenic',summary:'Athena\u2019s birthday festival, on 28 Hekatombaion. A great procession brings her ancient statue a newly woven robe, the peplos, with a hecatomb sacrifice, games and contests.',honours:'Athena Polias, guardian of the city, goddess of wisdom, weaving and crafts, of the olive and of disciplined courage. The festival honoured her as the heart of Athens, and the city honoured itself through her.',history:'The Panathenaia was the greatest festival of Athens. Every year there was a sacrifice and procession; every fourth year, from 566 BCE, the Great Panathenaia added athletic, musical and equestrian contests over several days. Prizes were large painted amphorae of oil from Athena\u2019s sacred olive trees.\n\nThe heart of the festival was the peplos, a saffron-yellow robe woven by Athenian women and girls over nine months, decorated with the battle of the gods and giants. It was carried in the procession, which went from the Dipylon gate through the Agora to the Acropolis, where it was given to the old olive-wood statue of Athena Polias. A night-long celebration (pannychis) with torch races and dancing came before the procession, and a hecatomb of cattle was sacrificed, with meat shared among the citizens.\n\nThe frieze of the Parthenon, now mostly in the British Museum, is often read as a picture of this procession.',knowAndDont:{known:'The procession, the peplos, the sacrifice and the contests are described in inscriptions, ancient writers and vase paintings; prize amphorae survive in their hundreds.',unknown:'Whether the Parthenon frieze really shows the Panathenaic procession is debated. Details of the order of events changed over time, and some aspects are reconstructed from scattered evidence.'},celebrate:['Weave, sew, knit or mend something as a gift to Athena, or finish a craft you started.','Pour olive oil as an offering, and cook with good oil in her honour.','Walk in procession, alone or with friends, to a high point in your town.','Hold a friendly contest: a race, a reading, a quiz, a song.','Do a civic good deed: volunteer, write to a representative, care for a public space.'],ritual:{premade:'folk-khernips-libation',title:'A peplos for Athena',outline:['Prepare a small piece of cloth or ribbon you have woven, sewn or embroidered over the past weeks, or a new scarf.','Wash your hands in khernips and light a lamp for Hestia.','Carry the cloth in a short procession through your home to your altar or a high window.','Offer it to Athena, with a pinch of incense and a few drops of olive oil.','Ask her for wisdom in your work and courage in your city.','Keep the cloth on her image or altar through the year.']},correspondences:{deities:['Athena','Hephaestus','Erechtheus'],herbs:['Bay','Frankincense','Thyme'],crystals:['Lapis Lazuli','Clear Quartz','Sodalite'],colors:['Yellow','Blue','Gold'],foods:['Olives and olive oil','Barley cakes','Honey','Figs'],symbols:['The owl','The olive tree','The peplos','Panathenaic amphora','Torch race']},sources:['Smith\u2019s Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities (1875), \u201cPanathenaea\u201d: https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Panathenaea.html','Jenifer Neils (ed.), Goddess and Polis: The Panathenaic Festival in Ancient Athens (Princeton, 1992).','H. W. Parke, Festivals of the Athenians (London, 1977).','Theoi Project, \u201cAthena\u201d: https://www.theoi.com/Olympios/Athena.html']},{ref:'hellenic-eleusinia',title:'Eleusinian Mysteries',aka:['Greater Mysteries','Eleusinia','Mysteria'],tradition:'Hellenic',summary:'The Greater Mysteries of Demeter and Persephone at Eleusis, 15\u201323 Boedromion: the most revered initiation of the ancient world, about loss, return and the promise of a better lot after death.',honours:'Demeter, mother of grain, and her daughter Persephone (Kore), taken by Hades and returned to her for part of each year. Also Iakchos, who led the procession, and the gods of the underworld. The Mysteries offered initiates a blessed afterlife.',history:'For nearly two thousand years, people came from all over the Greek and later Roman world to be initiated at Eleusis, near Athens. Men and women, free and enslaved, could take part if they spoke Greek and were free of blood-guilt. The Homeric Hymn to Demeter tells the founding story: Demeter, grieving for her stolen daughter, came to Eleusis disguised as an old woman, and when Persephone was restored, she taught the rites to the people there. The hymn says: \u201cHappy is he among men upon earth who has seen these mysteries; but he who is uninitiate and who has no part in them, never has lot of like good things once he is dead, down in the darkness and gloom.\u201d\n\nThe outline of the festival is known. On the first day the initiates gathered; on the second they bathed in the sea, each with a piglet to sacrifice; then came sacrifices and a day for latecomers; on the 19th a great procession walked the Sacred Way to Eleusis, crying out to Iakchos; then the initiates fasted, drank the kykeon (a barley drink), and entered the Telesterion at night to see and hear what was shown. They returned to Athens on the 23rd.\n\nThe Mysteries ended in the late fourth century CE, under imperial bans on pagan worship and the sack of Eleusis by Alaric\u2019s Goths.',knowAndDont:{known:'The calendar of the festival, the procession, the fasting and kykeon, the founding myth in the Hymn, and the huge prestige of the Mysteries are well attested. The site at Eleusis survives.',unknown:'The secret was kept. We do not know what was shown inside the Telesterion. Christian writers later claimed it was an ear of grain reaped in silence; theories of a psychedelic kykeon are speculation. Any modern \u201cEleusinian\u201d rite is a respectful reimagining, not the real thing.'},celebrate:['Read the Homeric Hymn to Demeter slowly, perhaps over several evenings.','Fast lightly during the day if your health allows, and break the fast at nightfall with a simple barley drink or porridge.','Walk a meaningful route in silence, as the initiates walked the Sacred Way.','Honour grief and return: a candle for someone lost, and one for what has come back.','Eat a pomegranate, thinking of Persephone between two worlds.'],ritual:{title:'A night for Demeter and Kore',outline:['After dark, purify with khernips and light a lamp for Hestia, then a torch-candle for Demeter searching.','Read the Hymn\u2019s account of Demeter\u2019s grief and Persephone\u2019s return aloud.','Drink a simple kykeon of barley water flavoured with fresh mint. Never use pennyroyal, the ancient herb: it is toxic.','Hold an ear of wheat or a handful of grain in silence for a while.','Name what you have lost and what you hope will return.','Pour a libation of barley water to Demeter and Persephone, and keep the grain to sow in spring.']},correspondences:{deities:['Demeter','Persephone','Hades','Hekate'],herbs:['Mint','Myrrh','Cypress'],crystals:['Garnet','Obsidian','Moonstone'],colors:['Gold','Black','Red'],foods:['Barley','Pomegranate','Wheat','Honey'],symbols:['Ear of wheat','Torches','Pomegranate','Piglet','Kykeon cup']},sources:['Homeric Hymn to Demeter, tr. H. G. Evelyn-White (1914): https://www.theoi.com/Text/HomericHymns1.html','Smith\u2019s Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities (1875), \u201cEleusinia\u201d: https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Eleusinia.html','Walter Burkert, Ancient Mystery Cults (Harvard, 1987).','George E. Mylonas, Eleusis and the Eleusinian Mysteries (Princeton, 1961).']},{ref:'hellenic-pyanepsia',title:'Pyanepsia',aka:['Pyanopsia','Feast of Beans','Eiresione'],tradition:'Hellenic',summary:'Apollo\u2019s autumn festival on 7 Pyanepsion: a pot of mixed beans is boiled and shared, and the eiresione, an olive branch hung with wool and fruits, is carried home and hung by the door for a year\u2019s blessing.',honours:'Apollo, god of light, healing and order, at the harvest; and Helios and the Horai, the seasons, in some accounts. The day also remembers Theseus and the young Athenians he brought home safe from Crete.',history:'Plutarch tells the story: when Theseus returned from Crete, he paid his vows to Apollo on the seventh of Pyanepsion. The young people who came back with him put together the last of their provisions, \u201cboiled it in one common pot, feasted upon it, and ate it all up together.\u201d So Athenians boiled beans and pulses on this day.\n\nThey also carried the eiresione, \u201ca bough of olive wreathed with wool, such as Theseus used at the time of his supplication, and laden with all sorts of fruit-offerings,\u201d and as they went they sang:\n\u201cEiresione for us brings figs and bread of the richest,\nBrings us honey in pots and oil to rub off from the body,\nStrong wine too in a beaker, that one may go to bed mellow.\u201d\nBoys carried such branches from house to house, and each family hung one over its door until it was replaced the next year.\n\nThe Oschophoria, a festival of grape-vine branches and races, was held around the same time.',knowAndDont:{known:'The date, the bean pot and the eiresione with its song are well attested, chiefly in Plutarch\u2019s Life of Theseus and in later lexicographers.',unknown:'The festival\u2019s meaning was explained in different ways in antiquity; some linked it to the Heracleidae. We know little about the public sacrifices.'},celebrate:['Cook a pot of mixed beans and pulses, and share it with everyone in the house.','Make an eiresione: an olive or other branch wound with wool and hung with dried figs, small breads and a little pot of honey.','Hang it over your door until next year\u2019s Pyanepsia, then burn or compost the old one.','Sing or say the eiresione song as you hang it.','Give thanks to Apollo for health, healing and safe returns.'],ritual:{premade:'folk-khernips-libation',title:'Hanging the eiresione',outline:['Make your branch: wind white and red wool around it, and tie on figs, a small bun, nuts or fruit.','Put a pot of mixed beans to simmer.','Wash your hands in khernips and pour a libation to Hestia and then to Apollo.','Carry the branch to the front door, singing or speaking the eiresione song.','Hang it over the door, asking Apollo to keep the house well-fed and healthy for the year.','Eat the beans together, leaving none.']},correspondences:{deities:['Apollo','Helios'],herbs:['Bay','Rosemary','Thyme'],crystals:['Sunstone','Citrine','Carnelian'],colors:['Gold','White','Red'],foods:['Mixed beans and pulses','Figs','Honey','Bread','Olive oil','Wine'],symbols:['The eiresione branch','Wool','Bean pot','Laurel']},sources:['Plutarch, Life of Theseus 22, tr. Bernadotte Perrin (1914): https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Theseus*.html','Smith\u2019s Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities (1875), \u201cPyanepsia\u201d: https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Pyanepsia.html','H. W. Parke, Festivals of the Athenians (London, 1977), Pyanepsion.']},{ref:'hellenic-thesmophoria',title:'Thesmophoria',aka:['Thesmophoria of Demeter'],tradition:'Hellenic',summary:'The women\u2019s festival of Demeter Thesmophoros and Persephone, 11\u201313 Pyanepsion, at the autumn sowing: for the fertility of the fields and of families.',honours:'Demeter Thesmophoros, \u201cbringer of law\u201d or \u201cof the things laid down\u201d, and her daughter Persephone. Also the women of the city, who held the festival alone, and the seed about to be sown.',history:'The Thesmophoria was the most widespread festival of Demeter in the Greek world, and one of the few times citizen women met as a body on their own. In Athens it lasted three days. On the Anodos, the \u201cgoing up\u201d, the women climbed to the Thesmophorion and set up camp. On the Nesteia they fasted, sitting on the ground on mats of plant stalks in memory of Demeter\u2019s grief, and traded the ribald jokes (aischrologia) that recalled Iambe, the woman whose jokes made the grieving goddess laugh. The final day was the Kalligeneia, \u201cfair birth\u201d, a feast.\n\nAn ancient commentary on Lucian adds the most famous detail: piglets had been thrown into underground chambers (megara) earlier in the year, and their rotted remains were brought up by women called \u201cbailers\u201d and placed on the altars, to be mixed with seed for a good harvest.\n\nMen were strictly excluded. Aristophanes\u2019 comedy Women at the Thesmophoria (411 BCE) imagines a man sneaking in disguise, and getting caught.',knowAndDont:{known:'The three days, the fasting, the joking and the exclusion of men are attested in Aristophanes, ancient commentaries and inscriptions.',unknown:'The secret rites themselves are mostly unknown; the piglet account comes from a late commentary and its timing is debated. Modern groups differ on whether to keep the festival women-only.'},celebrate:['Gather with women friends, or with a group of your choosing, for food, laughter and frank talk.','Fast for part of the second day if it is safe for you, then share a generous meal.','Bless seed for autumn sowing, or plant bulbs.','Honour the bodies, labour and lives of women: mothers, midwives, carers.','Tell bawdy jokes and laugh until it hurts, as Iambe did for Demeter.'],ritual:{title:'Sowing with Demeter Thesmophoros',outline:['Gather seed, bulbs or a pot of compost, a candle, a bowl of khernips, and some bread or cake.','Purify with khernips and light the candle for Hestia, then for Demeter.','Sit on the ground for a few minutes in silence, remembering Demeter\u2019s grief for Persephone.','Share a joke or story that makes everyone laugh, as Iambe did.','Bless the seed in Demeter\u2019s name and plant it.','Break bread together for the Kalligeneia, the fair birth.']},correspondences:{deities:['Demeter','Persephone'],herbs:['Mint','Willow','Garlic'],crystals:['Moss Agate','Garnet','Red Jasper'],colors:['Gold','Red','Green'],foods:['Pomegranate','Sesame and honey cakes','Barley bread','Pork'],symbols:['Seed','Piglet','Torch','Pine branch','Underground chamber']},sources:['Smith\u2019s Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities (1875), \u201cThesmophoria\u201d: https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Thesmophoria.html','Aristophanes, Thesmophoriazusae (411 BCE).','Scholion on Lucian, Dialogues of the Courtesans 2.1.','H. W. Parke, Festivals of the Athenians (London, 1977), Pyanepsion.']},{ref:'hellenic-haloa',title:'Haloa',aka:['Haloea','Festival of the threshing floor'],tradition:'Hellenic',summary:'A winter festival of Demeter, Kore and Dionysus around 26 Poseideon, kept mainly by women at Eleusis with feasting, joking and sexual symbols, to quicken the young crops.',honours:'Demeter and Kore, for the sprouting grain; Dionysus, for the vines being pruned. Its name comes from the halos, the threshing floor, and it was a festival of fertility in the dead of winter.',history:'The Haloa was held at Eleusis in midwinter. An ancient commentary on Lucian describes it: the women alone went in, with jests and mockery, carrying models of genitals; wine and rich foods were set out, but not those forbidden at the Mysteries, such as pomegranates, eggs and certain fish. The archons laid the tables and then left the women to themselves. There were also cakes shaped like sexual organs.\n\nAn Athenian court speech tells of a hierophant who was condemned for making a sacrifice at the Haloa on behalf of a courtesan, on the altar in the courtyard at Eleusis, when on that day sacrifices were the priestess\u2019s business.\n\nThe festival\u2019s mixture of Demeter and Dionysus, and of joking and feasting, shows how much fertility was a matter of laughter as well as solemnity.',knowAndDont:{known:'The date, the place at Eleusis, the women\u2019s feast and the ribald symbols are described by the scholiast on Lucian and the speech Against Neaira.',unknown:'The scholion is late and brief. We do not know how the festival related to the threshing it was named for, since there is no threshing in winter, or exactly what rites the women performed.'},celebrate:['Hold a feast with friends, with wine or juice and plenty of rich food, and leave out pomegranates for the day.','Bake playful cakes or biscuits in suggestive shapes, and don\u2019t be shy about laughing.','Prune or care for vines, fruit trees or houseplants.','Honour your own body and pleasure without shame.','Pour a libation to Demeter and Dionysus for growth in the dark.'],ritual:{premade:'folk-khernips-libation',title:'A Haloa feast',outline:['Lay a table with bread, cakes, cheese, wine or juice, and anything that delights you.','Purify with khernips and light a lamp for Hestia.','Pour libations to Demeter and Kore, then to Dionysus.','Feast, jest and tell stories. Laughter is the offering.','Before you finish, name one thing you want to grow in the coming spring.']},correspondences:{deities:['Demeter','Persephone','Dionysus'],herbs:['Mint','Thyme'],crystals:['Carnelian','Garnet'],colors:['Green','Red','Gold'],foods:['Bread and cakes','Wine','Cheese','Figs'],symbols:['Threshing floor','Vine','Young wheat']},sources:['Scholion on Lucian, Dialogues of the Courtesans 7.4.','[Demosthenes] 59, Against Neaira 116.','H. W. Parke, Festivals of the Athenians (London, 1977), Poseideon.','Theoi Project, \u201cDemeter\u201d: https://www.theoi.com/Olympios/Demeter.html']},{ref:'hellenic-lenaia',title:'Lenaia',aka:['Lenaea','Dionysus Lenaios'],tradition:'Hellenic',summary:'The winter festival of Dionysus Lenaios, around 12 Gamelion, with dramatic contests of comedy and tragedy for the people of Athens.',honours:'Dionysus of the wine-press (lenos) or of the lenai, his ecstatic women followers. The god of wine, theatre, masks and release, honoured in midwinter when the new wine was settling.',history:'The Lenaia was held in the month of Gamelion (January\u2013February), when sailing was over and few foreigners were in Athens, so it was a more local festival than the City Dionysia. There was a procession and sacrifice, and from about 440 BCE official contests in comedy, later also tragedy.\n\nMany of Aristophanes\u2019 comedies were first performed at the Lenaia: Acharnians, Knights, and Frogs among them. In Acharnians he jokes that \u201cwe are by ourselves now, at the Lenaia\u201d, so he can say what he likes about the city.\n\nA group of Athenian vases shows women ladling wine before a masked pillar-image of Dionysus; some scholars connect these with the Lenaia, though this is debated.',knowAndDont:{known:'The date, the dramatic contests, and the plays first performed there are well recorded.',unknown:'The religious rites are less clear, and the meaning of \u201cLenaios\u201d (wine-press or maenads) is debated.'},celebrate:['Watch, read or stage a comedy, ancient or modern.','Make or wear a mask, and play.','Open a bottle of wine or grape juice and pour the first cup to Dionysus.','Laugh at power: political satire was the Lenaia\u2019s speciality.','Invite friends to a winter party with music.'],ritual:{premade:'folk-khernips-libation',title:'A cup for Dionysus Lenaios',outline:['Purify with khernips and light a lamp for Hestia.','Set a mask, an ivy sprig or an image of Dionysus on your altar.','Pour the first of the wine or grape juice to Dionysus, asking for joy and freedom from care.','Read aloud a few lines from a comedy, or perform a scene with friends.','Finish with a toast and a song.']},correspondences:{deities:['Dionysus'],herbs:['Rosemary','Cinnamon'],crystals:['Amethyst','Garnet'],colors:['Purple','Red','Green'],foods:['Wine or grape juice','Grapes','Figs','Bread'],symbols:['Comic and tragic masks','Ivy','Thyrsus','Wine cup']},sources:['Arthur Pickard-Cambridge, The Dramatic Festivals of Athens (2nd ed., Oxford, 1968).','Aristophanes, Acharnians 504\u2013508.','Theoi Project, \u201cDionysus\u201d: https://www.theoi.com/Olympios/Dionysos.html','H. W. Parke, Festivals of the Athenians (London, 1977), Gamelion.']},{ref:'hellenic-anthesteria',title:'Anthesteria',aka:['Festival of Flowers','Pithoigia','Choes','Chytroi'],tradition:'Hellenic',summary:'Dionysus\u2019s three-day festival of flowers and the new wine, 11\u201313 Anthesterion. On the last day the spirits of the dead walk, and are sent away at its close.',honours:'Dionysus, whose new wine is opened; Hermes Chthonios, guide of souls; and the dead of the city, who return to wander the streets for a day.',history:'The Anthesteria was one of the oldest festivals of Athens. On the first day, Pithoigia, \u201cjar-opening\u201d, the new wine was opened at the sanctuary of Dionysus in the Marshes and offered to the god. On the second, Choes, \u201cjugs\u201d, everyone, including slaves and children of three or so, drank from their own jug in a drinking contest held in silence, each at a separate table, in memory of the polluted Orestes who was once served this way. Children received small jugs and garlands of flowers; many of these little choes survive. On this day the wife of the archon basileus was ceremonially married to Dionysus.\n\nThe Choes was also a day of pollution: the dead were abroad. People chewed buckthorn leaves in the morning and smeared their doors with pitch to keep spirits away. On the third day, Chytroi, \u201cpots\u201d, a pot of mixed grains was cooked for Hermes Chthonios and the dead, not for the gods above. At the end, the dead were sent off with the words recorded by ancient lexicographers: \u201cOut of doors, Keres! It is no longer Anthesteria.\u201d',knowAndDont:{known:'The three days, the jugs and garlands, the silent drinking, the sacred marriage, the pitch and buckthorn, and the pots for Hermes and the dead are attested by ancient writers and by the thousands of small choes jugs found in children\u2019s graves and houses.',unknown:'Whether the Keres sent away were ghosts or another kind of spirit (and whether the phrase was originally \u201cKares\u201d) is debated. The details of the sacred marriage were secret.'},celebrate:['Open a new bottle or jar of something: wine, juice, preserves, and offer the first of it to Dionysus.','Give children (or yourself) a garland of spring flowers and a little cup of their own.','Remember your dead on the third day with a pot of mixed grains or a simple porridge.','Mark the doorway at dusk with a dab of oil or pitch (or a sprig of hawthorn), and close the festival by saying the words to send the spirits away.','Spend some of the evening in quiet, without talking.'],ritual:{title:'Opening, drinking and sending home',outline:['Day one: open the new jar or bottle, pour the first of it to Dionysus, and share.','Day two: drink a cup in silence at your own place, then crown yourself with flowers. Mark the door against wandering spirits.','Day three: cook a pot of mixed grains or pulses and offer a portion to Hermes Chthonios and the dead outside or at the threshold. Do not eat that portion.','At nightfall, open the door and say: \u201cOut of doors, Keres! It is no longer Anthesteria.\u201d','Close the door, wash your hands, and light a fresh lamp for Hestia.']},correspondences:{deities:['Dionysus','Hermes','Hades'],herbs:['Hawthorn','Rosemary'],crystals:['Amethyst','Obsidian'],colors:['Purple','Black','White'],foods:['New wine or grape juice','Mixed grain pot (panspermia)','Honey'],symbols:['Choes jug','Flower garland','Pitch on the door','Wine jar']},sources:['Walter Burkert, Homo Necans (California, 1983), ch. 4; and Greek Religion (Harvard, 1985).','H. W. Parke, Festivals of the Athenians (London, 1977), Anthesterion.','Theoi Project, \u201cDionysus\u201d: https://www.theoi.com/Olympios/Dionysos.html']},{ref:'hellenic-dionysia',title:'City Dionysia',aka:['Great Dionysia','Dionysia','Dionysia en astei'],tradition:'Hellenic',summary:'Athens\u2019 great spring festival of Dionysus, beginning around 10 Elaphebolion, where the tragedies and comedies we still read were first performed.',honours:'Dionysus Eleuthereus, \u201cof Eleutherai\u201d, whose image was brought to Athens from the border town of that name. God of wine, ecstasy, masks and theatre, who frees and transforms.',history:'The City Dionysia was held in spring when the sea was open and visitors filled Athens. The statue of Dionysus was taken out of the city and brought back in torchlight procession, re-enacting his first arrival. A great procession followed, with bulls for sacrifice, phallic poles, and citizens and foreigners in fine clothes.\n\nThen came the contests: dithyrambs sung by choruses of men and boys, and three days of tragedy, each poet presenting three tragedies and a satyr play. Comedies were added in 486 BCE. Thespis is said to have won the first tragic contest around 534 BCE. Aeschylus, Sophocles and Euripides all competed here; Sophocles won some eighteen times.\n\nBefore the plays, the city showed off: tribute from allies was displayed, and orphans of war, raised at public expense, were paraded in armour.',knowAndDont:{known:'The procession, the contests, and many winning plays and poets are recorded in inscriptions and ancient writers; we still have plays first staged here.',unknown:'The exact sequence of days changed over the centuries, and the origins of tragedy in Dionysian ritual are much debated.'},celebrate:['Read or watch a Greek tragedy, or see any play.','Write or perform something of your own: a scene, a song, a poem.','Pour wine or grape juice to Dionysus before the performance.','Bring a spring branch or ivy into the house.','Let yourself be moved; tragedy was worship too.'],ritual:{premade:'folk-khernips-libation',title:'Theatre for Dionysus',outline:['Purify with khernips and light a lamp for Hestia.','Bring an image of Dionysus or an ivy wreath in procession to where you will watch or perform.','Pour a libation of wine or grape juice to Dionysus Eleuthereus.','Watch, read or perform a play, giving it your full attention.','Afterwards, talk with others about what moved you, and pour a last libation in thanks.']},correspondences:{deities:['Dionysus','Apollo'],herbs:['Rosemary','Bay'],crystals:['Amethyst','Garnet','Carnelian'],colors:['Purple','Red','Gold'],foods:['Wine or grape juice','Bread','Figs','Olives'],symbols:['Theatre masks','Ivy','Thyrsus','Bull','Panther']},sources:['Smith\u2019s Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities (1875), \u201cDionysia\u201d: https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Dionysia.html','Arthur Pickard-Cambridge, The Dramatic Festivals of Athens (2nd ed., Oxford, 1968).','Simon Goldhill, \u201cThe Great Dionysia and Civic Ideology\u201d, Journal of Hellenic Studies 107 (1987).']},{ref:'hellenic-mounichia',title:'Mounichia',aka:['Munychia','Festival of Artemis Mounichia'],tradition:'Hellenic',summary:'The festival of Artemis Mounichia on 16 Mounichion, at the full moon of spring, with round cakes ringed by little torches (amphiphontes), shining like the moon.',honours:'Artemis Mounichia, worshipped at her harbour sanctuary on the hill of Mounichia in Piraeus: protector of girls growing up, of sailors and of Athens in war. In later times the festival also gave thanks for the naval victory at Salamis.',history:'The Mounichia was held on 16 Mounichion, near the full moon. Worshippers brought amphiphontes, \u201cshining all round\u201d, cakes set with small torches around the edge, to Artemis. The goat was her sacrifice, and later the ephebes (young men in training) held boat races in her honour.\n\nPlutarch says the Athenians dedicated the sixteenth of Mounichion to Artemis because on that day the goddess shone with a full moon on the Greeks as they won at Salamis. Like Brauron, the sanctuary at Mounichia was a place where young girls served Artemis as \u201clittle bears\u201d, and a story was told there of a bear killed in the sanctuary, and a girl sacrificed or replaced by a goat.',knowAndDont:{known:'The date, the amphiphontes, the ephebic boat races, and the sanctuary on Mounichia hill are attested by inscriptions, lexicographers and Plutarch; the sanctuary has been excavated.',unknown:'The Salamis link may be a later explanation of an older festival. The bear stories survive in confusing versions.'},celebrate:['Bake a round cake and set it with small candles round the edge, lit safely on a fireproof plate.','Go out under the full moon and greet Artemis.','Honour girls and young women in your life: celebrate a milestone, give a gift.','If you are near the sea, walk the shore; Artemis Mounichia protected harbours.','Give to a charity that supports girls\u2019 education or wildlife.'],ritual:{premade:'folk-khernips-libation',title:'Cakes that shine all round',outline:['Bake or buy a round cake. Set small birthday candles around its rim, on a fireproof plate.','Purify with khernips and light a lamp for Hestia.','Light the candles and carry the cake to your altar or outside under the moon.','Pray to Artemis Mounichia for protection of the young and of all who travel by water.','Let the candles burn a minute, blow them out, and share the cake, leaving one piece for Artemis outdoors.']},correspondences:{deities:['Artemis','Selene'],herbs:['Mugwort','Willow','Cypress'],crystals:['Moonstone','Selenite','Clear Quartz'],colors:['Silver','White'],foods:['Round cakes','Honey','Cheese','Goat\u2019s milk'],symbols:['Amphiphontes cake','Full moon','Bear','Bow','Torches','Boat']},sources:['Smith\u2019s Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities (1875), \u201cMunychia\u201d: https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Munychia.html','Plutarch, On the Glory of the Athenians 349F.','Theoi Project, \u201cArtemis\u201d: https://www.theoi.com/Olympios/Artemis.html','H. W. Parke, Festivals of the Athenians (London, 1977), Mounichion.']},{ref:'hellenic-thargelia',title:'Thargelia',aka:['Thargelia of Apollo','Birthday of Apollo and Artemis'],tradition:'Hellenic',summary:'Festival of Apollo and Artemis on their birthdays, 6\u20137 Thargelion, with first fruits of the harvest and the purification of the city.',honours:'Apollo, born on the 7th, and Artemis, born on the 6th, according to Athenian tradition. Apollo as purifier and healer, and as god of the first ripened grain.',history:'On the first day, the 6th, Athens was purified. Two people, the pharmakoi, were led out of the city, wearing strings of figs, and driven out as scapegoats, carrying the city\u2019s pollution with them. Ancient sources disagree on how harsh this was; it was probably expulsion, not death, in classical Athens.\n\nOn the 7th came Apollo\u2019s feast. The thargelos was carried: a pot of the first grain, cooked, or a first loaf. Choruses of men and boys competed in song, and the victors dedicated tripods. Plato and Socrates were said to have been born at the Thargelia, and Plato\u2019s birthday was kept by his followers on the 7th.\n\nThe Thargelia connected cleansing with harvest: before the first fruits could be enjoyed, the community had to be set right.',knowAndDont:{known:'The dates, the pharmakoi, the thargelos, and the choral contests are attested in lexicographers, inscriptions and ancient poets.',unknown:'Exactly how the pharmakoi were chosen and treated is uncertain. Earlier claims that they were killed rely on doubtful sources.'},celebrate:['Clean your home thoroughly on the first day, and take rubbish and old things out of the house.','On the second day, cook the first grain or a first loaf of the season, and offer some to Apollo.','Sing, alone or with others.','Celebrate Artemis on the 6th and Apollo on the 7th with candles and libations.','Release one burden you have carried for others; set it down outside the door.'],ritual:{premade:'folk-khernips-libation',title:'Cleansing and first fruits',outline:['Day one: clean and air the house. Write on a fig leaf or paper what you want to send out, and take it outside to compost or bin.','Purify with khernips and light a lamp for Hestia; pour a libation to Artemis for her birthday.','Day two: cook a pot of grain or bake a small loaf.','Offer the first spoonful or slice to Apollo with a hymn or song.','Share the rest, and keep a bay leaf from the day on your altar.']},correspondences:{deities:['Apollo','Artemis'],herbs:['Bay','Hyssop','Rosemary'],crystals:['Sunstone','Moonstone','Clear Quartz'],colors:['Gold','Silver','White'],foods:['First grain or loaf','Figs','Honey','Fruit'],symbols:['Tripod','Laurel','Lyre','Figs on a string','Swan']},sources:['Smith\u2019s Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities (1875), \u201cThargelia\u201d: https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Thargelia.html','Jan Bremmer, \u201cScapegoat Rituals in Ancient Greece\u201d, Harvard Studies in Classical Philology 87 (1983).','Theoi Project, \u201cApollo\u201d: https://www.theoi.com/Olympios/Apollon.html']},{ref:'hellenic-plynteria',title:'Plynteria',aka:['Plunteria','Washing of Athena','Kallynteria'],tradition:'Hellenic',summary:'On 25 Thargelion, Athena\u2019s ancient statue was undressed, taken to be washed and her robes cleaned. It was a solemn and unlucky day of cleansing and renewal.',honours:'Athena Polias, whose olive-wood image was the city\u2019s most sacred object; and Aglauros, the daughter of the first king, linked with Athena\u2019s cult. The day honoured her presence by keeping her image pure.',history:'The Plynteria was the job of the Praxiergidai, a priestly family. They removed the robe and ornaments from Athena\u2019s ancient image, covered it, and the temple was roped off. The image was then washed, perhaps at the sea at Phaleron, and a procession carrying a cake of figs (the hegeteria) went with it. The Kallynteria, a day of sweeping and decorating the temple, came just before.\n\nBecause the goddess was \u201caway\u201d, the Plynteria was counted among the most unlucky days of the year: no public business was done. Plutarch and Xenophon note that Alcibiades returned to Athens on the day of the Plynteria in 407 BCE, and people saw it as a bad omen.',knowAndDont:{known:'The day, the Praxiergidai, the covering of the statue and the ill-omened nature of the day are attested by Plutarch, Xenophon and inscriptions.',unknown:'Whether the statue was carried to the sea to be washed or washed in the city is debated, as are the exact day and the order of the Kallynteria and Plynteria.'},celebrate:['Clean your altar and wash any images, statues or altar cloths gently.','Keep the day quiet, without starting new ventures.','Sweep and air your home or workplace.','Mend and launder clothes, especially special ones.','Re-dress your altar at the end of the day, fresh and bright.'],ritual:{title:'Washing the image',outline:['Purify with khernips, then cover your altar or the image of your god with a clean cloth.','Take everything off the altar and wash it: images, bowls, cloths, the surface itself.','Use plain water, and a little salt water for objects it won\u2019t harm.','Let everything dry, and keep the day quiet in the meantime.','In the evening, re-dress the altar, uncover the image, and offer olive oil and a fig to Athena.']},correspondences:{deities:['Athena'],herbs:['Bay','Hyssop','Thyme'],crystals:['Clear Quartz','Selenite'],colors:['White','Blue'],foods:['Figs','Olive oil'],symbols:['Veiled statue','Washing bowl','The peplos','The sea']},sources:['Smith\u2019s Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities (1875), \u201cPlynteria\u201d: https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Plynteria.html','Plutarch, Life of Alcibiades 34, tr. Bernadotte Perrin (1916): https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Alcibiades*.html','Xenophon, Hellenica 1.4.12.','H. W. Parke, Festivals of the Athenians (London, 1977), Thargelion.']},{ref:'hellenic-skira',title:'Skira',aka:['Skirophoria','Scira'],tradition:'Hellenic',summary:'A midsummer women\u2019s festival of Demeter and Persephone on 12 Skirophorion, ahead of the threshing, at the very end of the Athenian year.',honours:'Demeter and Kore; and in the public procession, Athena, Poseidon-Erechtheus and Helios. It honours the end of the year and the summer\u2019s grain.',history:'The Skira gave its name to the last month of the Athenian year. A procession left the Acropolis for a place called Skiron on the road to Eleusis: the priestess of Athena, the priest of Poseidon-Erechtheus and the priest of Helios walked under a great white parasol (skiron). There was a sacrifice there, and a festival held by women.\n\nWe hear that women at the Skira ate garlic so as to keep their husbands at a distance, and Aristophanes\u2019 comedy Assemblywomen imagines women plotting at the Skira to take over the government. A late ancient commentary seems to connect the Skira with piglets placed in the megara (underground chambers) to be brought up at the Thesmophoria.\n\nThe Skira was a festival of separation and readiness, at the hottest point of the year before the threshing and the new year.',knowAndDont:{known:'The date, the parasol procession, the women\u2019s gathering, and the garlic are attested by ancient writers and lexicographers.',unknown:'The Skira is one of the less understood Athenian festivals. Its connection to the Thesmophoria and the meaning of the parasol are debated.'},celebrate:['Gather with women friends, or anyone you choose, under shade, for food and talk.','Eat something with plenty of garlic.','Take stock of the year that is ending, as the Athenian year closed.','Give thanks for the grain and prepare for the new year\u2019s first month.','Walk a stretch of road at midday under a sunshade or parasol, and drink plenty of water.'],ritual:{premade:'folk-khernips-libation',title:'Under the white parasol',outline:['Purify with khernips and light a lamp for Hestia.','Set up a white parasol or sheet for shade outside, or a white cloth over your altar.','Pour libations to Demeter and Kore, and to Athena.','Eat a simple garlic-rich meal, and talk with others about what this year has been.','Name one thing to leave behind with the old year before the new moon of Hekatombaion.']},correspondences:{deities:['Demeter','Persephone','Athena','Poseidon'],herbs:['Garlic','Mint','Thyme'],crystals:['Citrine','Moss Agate'],colors:['White','Gold'],foods:['Garlic','Barley','Figs','Bread'],symbols:['White parasol','Grain','Piglets']},sources:['H. W. Parke, Festivals of the Athenians (London, 1977), Skirophorion.','Aristophanes, Ecclesiazusae 18 and 59.','Walter Burkert, Homo Necans (California, 1983).','Theoi Project, \u201cDemeter\u201d: https://www.theoi.com/Olympios/Demeter.html']},{ref:'hellenic_monthly-noumenia',title:'Noumenia',aka:['New Moon day','Neomenia','First of the month'],tradition:'Hellenic',summary:'The first day of the lunar month, when the first sliver of the new crescent appears: offerings to the household gods for a good month.',honours:'The household gods: Hestia of the hearth, Zeus Ktesios guardian of the stores, Apollo Noumenios, Hermes and Hekate at the door, and the Agathos Daimon. It is the holiest day of the month, when no other festival was held.',history:'The Athenian month began when the new crescent was first seen at sunset. Ancient writers called the Noumenia the most sacred of days, and in Athens no other festival was scheduled on it. Offerings were small and household-centred: incense, honey cakes, garlands for the household images.\n\nThe philosopher Porphyry tells a story, borrowed from Theopompus, of a poor man of Methydrion in Arcadia, Klearchos, who was judged by the oracle at Delphi to be more pious than a rich man who made splendid sacrifices. Klearchos simply crowned and polished his images of Hermes and Hekate every new moon, and offered them incense, barley cakes and small cakes. That story still guides how many Hellenists keep the day: small, regular and sincere.',knowAndDont:{known:'The Noumenia was the first day of the lunar month, sacred and kept free of other festivals in Athens. Small household offerings on this day are well attested.',unknown:'Exactly which gods each household honoured varied, and much of the modern monthly cycle (Deipnon, Noumenia, Agathos Daimon) is assembled from scattered sources.'},celebrate:['Look for the new crescent at sunset, and greet it.','Clean and garland your household shrine, as Klearchos did.','Offer incense and a little honey or a barley cake to your household gods.','Set one intention for the month.','Make a fresh start in one small thing: a tidy drawer, a new notebook.'],ritual:{premade:'folk-noumenia-blessing'},correspondences:{deities:['Hestia','Hermes','Hekate','Apollo','Selene'],herbs:['Frankincense','Bay','Myrrh'],crystals:['Moonstone','Selenite','Clear Quartz'],colors:['Silver','White'],foods:['Honey cakes','Barley cakes','Fresh water','Incense'],symbols:['New crescent','Garlands','Household shrine']},sources:['Porphyry, On Abstinence from Animal Food 2.16, tr. Thomas Taylor (1823).','Jon D. Mikalson, The Sacred and Civil Calendar of the Athenian Year (Princeton, 1975).','Hellenion, \u201cNoumenia\u201d: https://hellenion.org/']},{ref:'hellenic_monthly-agathos_daimon',title:'Agathos Daimon',aka:['Agathodaimon','The Good Spirit','Second of the month'],tradition:'Hellenic',summary:'The second day of the month, honouring the Good Spirit of the household with a libation of wine.',honours:'The Agathos Daimon, the \u201cgood spirit\u201d or \u201cgood god\u201d of the house and its fortunes, often shown as a snake, and often paired with Agathe Tyche, Good Fortune. He guards the vines, the grain and the household\u2019s luck.',history:'Greeks drank a small cup of unmixed wine to the Agathos Daimon at the end of a meal, before the main drinking began; Aristophanes\u2019 characters refer to it as a familiar custom. Snakes in the house were treated kindly as forms of the house\u2019s good spirit. In Hellenistic Alexandria, the Agathos Daimon became a protector of the whole city.\n\nThe second day of the month as his day appears in later ancient lists of the days of the month and their gods, and in modern scholarship of the Athenian calendar, where the first eight days each had a patron. Modern Hellenists keep it as a quiet, domestic day, with a libation and thanks for the household\u2019s luck.',knowAndDont:{known:'The after-dinner libation of unmixed wine to the Agathos Daimon is well attested. His snake form and link with Agathe Tyche are known from art and inscriptions.',unknown:'The evidence that the second of every month was sacred to him is thinner than for the Noumenia, and how widely it was kept is uncertain.'},celebrate:['After dinner, pour a little wine or juice for the Agathos Daimon before you leave the table.','Thank the spirit of your home for its luck and shelter.','Tend something that brings the household luck: plants, the pantry, the savings jar.','Light a small lamp beside an image of a snake, or simply your household shrine.'],ritual:{title:'A cup for the Good Spirit',outline:['After the evening meal, wash your hands.','Pour a small cup of unmixed wine, or grape juice, or water.','Lift it and say: \u201cTo the Agathos Daimon, good spirit of this house, thank you for our luck and our bread.\u201d','Pour a few drops on the hearth, a bowl or the ground outside, and drink a sip.','Leave the rest by the household shrine overnight, then pour it out.']},correspondences:{deities:['Agathos Daimon','Agathe Tyche','Hestia'],herbs:['Basil','Bay','Thyme'],crystals:['Green Aventurine','Citrine','Jade'],colors:['Green','Gold'],foods:['Wine or grape juice','Bread','Honey'],symbols:['The house snake','Cornucopia','Wine cup']},sources:['Aristophanes, Knights 85\u2013106; Wasps 525.','Jon D. Mikalson, The Sacred and Civil Calendar of the Athenian Year (Princeton, 1975).','Hellenion, \u201cAgathos Daimon\u201d: https://hellenion.org/?p=1514','Walter Burkert, Greek Religion (Harvard, 1985).']},{ref:'hellenic_monthly-deipnon',title:'Hekate\u2019s Deipnon',aka:['Deipnon','Hekate\u2019s Supper','Hekataia','Dark moon'],tradition:'Hellenic',summary:'Hekate\u2019s supper on the last day of the lunar month: cleansing the house and leaving food for Hekate at a crossroads before the new month begins.',honours:'Hekate, goddess of thresholds, crossroads and the night, and the restless dead who travel with her. The house is purified and the month closed so that the new month can begin clean.',history:'On the last day of the month, Athenians left food at the crossroads for Hekate, often at a small shrine (hekataion) where three roads met. Aristophanes jokes about it in Wealth: ask Hekate, a character says, whether wealth or poverty is better, for the rich send her a supper every month and the poor snatch it before it is even set down. So the poor did eat these suppers, and the custom was known to everyone.\n\nThe same day was a time of purification. Sweepings from the house and the remains of purification rites were taken to the crossroads too. Foods mentioned for Hekate include cakes, eggs, cheese, small fish such as red mullet, and garlic.\n\nModern Hellenists and many witches keep the Deipnon each dark moon, cleaning the house and leaving a meal for Hekate.',knowAndDont:{known:'Suppers for Hekate at crossroads at the month\u2019s end, and the link with household purification, are attested in Aristophanes and other ancient writers.',unknown:'Exact rites varied, and much of what is said about the Deipnon today comes from modern practice rather than ancient sources.'},celebrate:['Clean your home: sweep, dust, and take the sweepings and rubbish out.','Leave a small supper for Hekate at a crossroads near home or your doorstep, and walk away without looking back.','Use pet-safe foods if anything can reach it: no chocolate, grapes, raisins, onion or garlic outdoors.','Reflect on the month: settle debts, apologise, close out what is unfinished.','Rest. Tomorrow is the Noumenia.'],ritual:{premade:'folk-hekate-deipnon'},correspondences:{deities:['Hekate'],herbs:['Garlic','Mugwort','Myrrh','Cypress'],crystals:['Obsidian','Black Tourmaline','Jet'],colors:['Black','Silver'],foods:['Bread','Eggs','Cheese','Honey cakes','Garlic (indoors only)'],symbols:['Crossroads','Torches','Keys','Dogs','The broom']},sources:['Aristophanes, Wealth (Plutus) 594\u2013597: https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0040:line=594','Theoi Project, \u201cHekate\u201d: https://www.theoi.com/Khthonios/Hekate.html','Sarah Iles Johnston, Restless Dead: Encounters between the Living and the Dead in Ancient Greece (California, 1999).']},{ref:'roman-parentalia',title:'Parentalia',aka:['Dies Parentales','Feralia','Caristia'],tradition:'Roman',summary:'Nine days, 13\u201321 February, honouring the family\u2019s ancestors with gifts of flowers, grain, salt and bread soaked in wine at their tombs. It ends with the Feralia on the 21st and the family reconciliation of the Caristia on the 22nd.',honours:'The di parentes, the family dead, especially parents and grandparents. Unlike the Lemuria\u2019s restless ghosts, these are the loved and honoured dead, given what is due to them.',history:'The Parentalia opened at noon on 13 February, when a Vestal Virgin made offering for the dead; the Calendar of 354 still marks the day \u201cVirgo Vesta parentat\u201d. For nine days, temples were closed, magistrates laid aside their insignia, no weddings were held, and families went to the tombs outside the city.\n\nOvid says the dead ask little: a tile wreathed with garlands, a scattering of grain, a few grains of salt, bread softened in wine, and loose violets. In his words: \u201ctegula porrectis satis est velata coronis / et sparsae fruges parcaque mica salis, / inque mero mollita Ceres violaeque solutae.\u201d He tells of a time when the rites were neglected and the dead came out of their tombs, howling through the streets.\n\nOn the 21st, the Feralia, the public offering to the dead closed the festival. The next day, the Caristia, families met for a meal to make peace, with incense and food offered to the Lares.',knowAndDont:{known:'The dates and customs are described by Ovid\u2019s Fasti and confirmed by Roman calendars. The rites were simple and domestic.',unknown:'We know less about how ordinary people kept every one of the nine days, or how strictly the closures were observed in later centuries.'},celebrate:['Visit a family grave and leave violets or other flowers, or set flowers by a photo at home.','Offer a little grain, salt and bread dipped in wine at your shrine for your parents and ancestors.','Say the names of your dead aloud.','On the 22nd, the Caristia, share a meal with family and mend a quarrel if you can.','If family is complicated, honour the dead who loved you, or the ancestors of your craft or place.'],ritual:{premade:'folk-parentalia'},correspondences:{deities:['Vesta','Janus','Mercury'],herbs:['Rosemary','Cypress','Myrrh'],crystals:['Jet','Amethyst','Smoky Quartz'],colors:['Purple','Black','White'],foods:['Bread soaked in wine','Salt','Spelt or grain','Violets (as offering)'],symbols:['Violets','Garlanded tile','Tombs','Lamps']},sources:['Ovid, Fasti 2.533\u2013638 (Latin): https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/ovid/ovid.fasti2.shtml','Calendar of Filocalus, Chronography of 354, February: https://www.tertullian.org/fathers/chronography_of_354_06_calendar.htm','H. H. Scullard, Festivals and Ceremonies of the Roman Republic (London, 1981).']},{ref:'roman-lupercalia',title:'Lupercalia',aka:['Lupercal','Februa'],tradition:'Roman',summary:'An ancient festival of purification and fertility on 15 February, honouring Faunus and the she-wolf who nursed Romulus and Remus, at the cave of the Lupercal below the Palatine.',honours:'Faunus (or Inuus), god of the wild and of herds; the she-wolf who saved Rome\u2019s founders; and the fertility of people and flocks. The name probably joins lupus, \u201cwolf\u201d, and the cave of the Lupercal.',history:'At the Lupercal cave, the priests called Luperci sacrificed goats and a dog. Two young men were touched on the forehead with the bloody knife, then the blood was wiped off with wool soaked in milk, and they had to laugh. The Luperci, naked but for goatskins, then ran around the Palatine, striking those they met with strips of goatskin called februa, purifiers. Women held out their hands to be struck, believing it helped fertility and childbirth.\n\nThe name of February comes from these februa. Mark Antony, as a Lupercus, famously offered Julius Caesar a crown at the Lupercalia of 44 BCE. The festival lasted into Christian times; in the 490s Pope Gelasius wrote against Christians who still kept it.\n\nThere is no evidence that the Lupercalia is the origin of Valentine\u2019s Day, a link often made but never shown.',knowAndDont:{known:'The sacrifice, the run and the februa are described by Plutarch, Ovid, Varro and others. The festival lasted until the late fifth century.',unknown:'The original meaning was already debated in antiquity, and the identity of the god honoured is unclear. Modern stories of a \u201clove lottery\u201d are not ancient.'},celebrate:['Purify your home: open windows, clean, and sprinkle salt water at the doorways.','Run or walk briskly around your home, block or park for vitality.','Pour milk as an offering for Faunus and the she-wolf.','Support a wolf or wildlife conservation group.','Laugh: the young men had to.'],ritual:{title:'A februa purification',outline:['Make februa: a few strips of leather or cloth, or a bundle of twigs.','Pour a little milk into a bowl, and dip a wool tuft or cloth in it.','Walk round your home or space, lightly tapping the walls and doorways with the strips to drive out stale energy. Never strike a person or animal.','Touch the milk-soaked wool to your forehead and laugh out loud.','Pour the milk outside for Faunus and the she-wolf, and wash your hands.']},correspondences:{deities:['Faunus','Juno','Mars'],herbs:['Rosemary','Hyssop','Bay'],crystals:['Red Jasper','Garnet','Bloodstone'],colors:['Red','White'],foods:['Milk','Salted cakes (mola salsa)','Goat\u2019s cheese'],symbols:['She-wolf','Goatskin strips','The cave','Twins']},sources:['Plutarch, Life of Romulus 21, tr. Bernadotte Perrin (1914): https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Plutarch/Lives/Romulus*.html','Smith\u2019s Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities (1875), \u201cLupercalia\u201d: https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Lupercalia.html','Ovid, Fasti 2.267\u2013474 (Latin): https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/ovid/ovid.fasti2.shtml','Mary Beard, John North and Simon Price, Religions of Rome (Cambridge, 1998).']},{ref:'roman-matronalia',title:'Matronalia',aka:['Matronales Feriae','Women\u2019s Kalends'],tradition:'Roman',summary:'Festival of Juno Lucina, goddess of childbirth, on 1 March, the old Roman new year: mothers and wives are honoured and given gifts.',honours:'Juno Lucina, \u201cshe who brings to light\u201d, who helps women in labour; and the matronae, the married women of Rome. Mars, whose month it begins, is honoured as Juno\u2019s son and as the father of Rome.',history:'The temple of Juno Lucina on the Esquiline was dedicated on 1 March, traditionally in 375 BCE. On that day the married women of Rome went to the temple with flowers, prayed for their families, and received gifts from husbands and daughters.\n\nOvid asks Mars why women keep his festival, and is told it remembers the Sabine women, who made peace between their Roman husbands and their Sabine fathers. He also says Juno Lucina is honoured because the year begins with life, as March, the first month of the old Roman year, begins the spring. According to Macrobius, Roman women also served their slaves a meal on this day, as men did at the Saturnalia.',knowAndDont:{known:'The date, the temple dedication, and the custom of gifts and prayers for mothers are described by Ovid and other Roman writers.',unknown:'Details of the temple rite are thin. The festival\u2019s link to the Sabine women is one of several ancient explanations.'},celebrate:['Give flowers and a gift to the mothers and mother-figures in your life.','Pray for or support anyone expecting a baby or recovering from birth.','Cook a meal for the person who usually cooks.','Make peace between family members if you can, as the Sabine women did.','Light a candle for Juno Lucina for all who labour in childbirth.'],ritual:{premade:'folk-lararium-offering',title:'Flowers for Juno Lucina',outline:['Gather fresh flowers, a candle and a small cake or bread.','At your shrine, light the candle and offer the flowers to Juno Lucina.','Name the mothers, midwives and birth-givers you wish to honour, living or dead.','Ask Juno to bring safe births and protect families.','Give the cake to a mother or share it in her honour.']},correspondences:{deities:['Juno','Mars'],herbs:['Rose','Calendula','Jasmine'],crystals:['Rose Quartz','Moonstone','Garnet'],colors:['White','Pink','Gold'],foods:['Honey cakes','Fresh flowers','Milk'],symbols:['Peacock','Flowers','Lamp','Pomegranate']},sources:['Ovid, Fasti 3.167\u2013258 (Latin): https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/ovid/ovid.fasti3.shtml','Smith\u2019s Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities (1875), \u201cMatronalia\u201d: https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Matronalia.html','Macrobius, Saturnalia 1.12.7: https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Macrobius/Saturnalia/home.html']},{ref:'roman-floralia',title:'Floralia',aka:['Ludi Florales','Festival of Flora'],tradition:'Roman',summary:'Festival of Flora, goddess of flowers and springtime, from 28 April to 3 May, with games, theatre, bright clothing and joyful licence.',honours:'Flora, goddess of everything that blooms: flowers, the blossom of fruit trees and grain, and the sweetness of youth and pleasure. She was an old Italian goddess with her own priest (flamen Floralis).',history:'Flora\u2019s temple near the Circus Maximus was founded in 241 or 238 BCE, after the Sibylline books were consulted during a drought. The games became annual in 173 BCE, when crops were damaged, and grew to six days.\n\nThe Floralia was famous for its merriment. People wore bright colours instead of white; mimes and farces were performed, and actresses performed naked at the crowd\u2019s request. Hares and goats were let loose in the Circus, and beans, lupins and vetch were scattered among the crowd as symbols of fertility. Ovid has Flora herself explain her cult, and she says that she loves flowers and fun, and that her festival is free and easy because life itself should be enjoyed while it blooms.',knowAndDont:{known:'The dates, the temple, the games, the bright clothes and theatrical licence are described by Ovid, Pliny and other Roman writers.',unknown:'We know little about the religious rites beyond the games. Some ancient stories about Flora were invented later.'},celebrate:['Wear bright colours, and put flowers in your hair or on your table.','Plant flowers or scatter wildflower seed where it is welcome.','Go to the theatre, watch a comedy, or put on something playful with friends.','Scatter a handful of beans or seeds for the birds or in the garden.','Enjoy pleasure without guilt, kindly and safely.'],ritual:{premade:'green-hawthorn-may-blossom-blessing'},correspondences:{deities:['Flora','Venus','Ceres'],herbs:['Rose','Calendula','Jasmine','Hawthorn'],crystals:['Rose Quartz','Peridot','Rhodochrosite'],colors:['Pink','Yellow','Green','Rainbow'],foods:['Honey','Milk','Beans and lupins','Spring greens'],symbols:['Flowers','Hares','Garlands','Bright clothes','Theatre masks']},sources:['Ovid, Fasti 5.183\u2013378 (Latin): https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/ovid/ovid.fasti5.shtml','Smith\u2019s Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities (1875), \u201cFloralia\u201d: https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Floralia.html','H. H. Scullard, Festivals and Ceremonies of the Roman Republic (London, 1981).']},{ref:'roman-lemuria',title:'Lemuria',aka:['Lemuralia','Remuria'],tradition:'Roman',summary:'Rites to appease the restless dead (lemures) on 9, 11 and 13 May, when the head of the household sent the ghosts out of the house at midnight with black beans.',honours:'The lemures, unquiet or hungry spirits of the dead, especially those without kin to care for them. Not worship so much as a careful, respectful sending-away, so the house could be at peace.',history:'Ovid gives the rite in detail. At midnight, when dogs and birds are silent, the father of the house rises barefoot, makes a sign with his thumb between his fingers to ward off ghosts, and washes his hands in spring water. He takes up black beans, turns away, and throws them behind him, saying nine times without looking back: \u201chaec ego mitto; his redimo meque meosque fabis\u201d, \u201cThese I send; with these beans I redeem me and mine.\u201d The ghost is thought to gather the beans and follow. He washes again, clashes bronze, and nine times asks the shades to leave: \u201cManes exite paterni\u201d, \u201cGhosts of my fathers, go out!\u201d Then he looks back, and the rite is done.\n\nOvid links the festival to Remus, whose ghost appeared after his death, and says Remuria became Lemuria. Temples were closed on these days and it was unlucky to marry in May.',knowAndDont:{known:'Ovid\u2019s Fasti describes the household rite step by step, and the dates are in the Roman calendars.',unknown:'Ovid\u2019s link with Remus is a poet\u2019s etymology. How widely the rite was kept in his day, and what lemures were believed to be, is not clear.'},celebrate:['At midnight, perform Ovid\u2019s rite, or a gentle version of it, to clear your home of heaviness.','Don\u2019t make big commitments or move house in these days if you can help it.','Remember the forgotten dead: those with no one to mourn them.','Clean and air the house the next morning.','If a home feels troubled by grief, speak with someone you trust as well as doing the rite.'],ritual:{premade:'hedge-letting-go-of-the-dead',title:'Ovid\u2019s midnight rite',outline:['At midnight, rise barefoot and in silence. Wash your hands in fresh water.','Take nine black beans (dried, uncooked) in your hand.','Turn your back and throw the beans behind you, saying nine times: \u201cThese I send; with these beans I redeem me and mine.\u201d Do not look back.','Wash your hands again, and strike a pot or bell.','Say nine times: \u201cGhosts of my fathers, go out.\u201d','Then turn, look back, and sweep the beans out of the house in the morning.']},correspondences:{deities:['Mercury','Janus'],herbs:['Rosemary','Rue','Bay'],crystals:['Obsidian','Black Tourmaline','Jet'],colors:['Black','White'],foods:['Black beans','Spring water'],symbols:['Black beans','Bronze bell or pot','Bare feet','Midnight']},sources:['Ovid, Fasti 5.419\u2013492 (Latin): https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/ovid/ovid.fasti5.shtml','Smith\u2019s Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities (1875), \u201cLemuralia\u201d: https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Lemuralia.html','H. H. Scullard, Festivals and Ceremonies of the Roman Republic (London, 1981).']},{ref:'roman-vestalia',title:'Vestalia',aka:['Festival of Vesta'],tradition:'Roman',summary:'Festival of Vesta, goddess of the hearth, on 9 June, when her storehouse was opened to the women of Rome and bakers and their donkeys took a holiday.',honours:'Vesta, the sacred fire at the heart of Rome and of every Roman home, tended by the Vestal Virgins; and the bakers, millers and donkeys who made the bread.',history:'Vesta\u2019s round temple in the Forum held the eternal flame and the secret sacred objects of Rome. From 7 to 15 June, the innermost store (penus) was opened, and matrons came barefoot to bring food offerings. On 9 June, the Vestalia, bakers and millers rested, and the donkeys that turned the millstones were garlanded with violets and strings of small loaves.\n\nThe Vestals made the mola salsa, salted spelt flour used in sacrifices, during these days. On the 15th, the temple was swept and the refuse taken to the Tiber; until that was done, the day was unlucky. Ovid describes the scene, and reminds us that Vesta has no image, for she is living flame.',knowAndDont:{known:'The dates, the opening of the penus, the bakers\u2019 holiday and the garlanded donkeys are described by Ovid and Roman calendars.',unknown:'What was kept in the penus was secret. Details of the matrons\u2019 offerings are few.'},celebrate:['Clean your kitchen or hearth thoroughly.','Bake bread or a simple salted loaf, and offer a piece to Vesta.','Give a rest day to anyone who usually cooks, or to a working animal.','Light a candle on the stove or table for Vesta, and keep watch over it.','Take old cooking oil, crumbs and kitchen rubbish out on the 15th.'],ritual:{premade:'folk-vestalia-hearth'},correspondences:{deities:['Vesta','Hestia'],herbs:['Bay','Rosemary','Basil'],crystals:['Carnelian','Garnet','Sunstone'],colors:['White','Red','Orange'],foods:['Salted bread','Mola salsa (salted flour)','Spelt','Water'],symbols:['Eternal flame','Round temple','Garlanded donkey','Violets']},sources:['Ovid, Fasti 6.249\u2013468 (Latin): https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/ovid/ovid.fasti6.shtml','Calendar of Filocalus, Chronography of 354, June: https://www.tertullian.org/fathers/chronography_of_354_06_calendar.htm','Smith\u2019s Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities (1875), \u201cVestales\u201d: https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Vestales.html']},{ref:'roman-neptunalia',title:'Neptunalia',aka:['Festival of Neptune'],tradition:'Roman',summary:'Festival of Neptune on 23 July, in the hottest, driest part of summer, with shelters of leafy branches.',honours:'Neptune, an old Italian god of fresh water, springs and rivers before he became identified with the Greek Poseidon and the sea. At the height of summer drought, he was honoured to keep water flowing.',history:'Very little is known about the Neptunalia. The ancient writer Festus says that people built umbrae, shelters of leafy branches, to keep off the sun during the festival, and feasted in them. It was a day for gathering and drinking, perhaps by springs and rivers.\n\nNeptune had a temple in the Campus Martius. The Furrinalia, a festival of a mysterious spring goddess, Furrina, followed two days later, also linked with water.',knowAndDont:{known:'The date and the leafy shelters are recorded.',unknown:'The rites, offerings and original meaning of the festival are mostly unknown.'},celebrate:['Make a shady shelter outdoors with branches or a sheet, and picnic in it.','Visit a spring, river or the sea, and leave an offering of flowers (no plastic).','Save water: fix a leak or water the garden at dusk.','Pour a libation of fresh water for Neptune.','Give to a charity that brings clean water to people.'],ritual:{premade:'sea-safe-passage-blessing',title:'Shade and water for Neptune',outline:['Build a simple shelter of leafy branches or a sheet over chairs outdoors.','Fill a bowl with fresh water.','Sit in the shade and pour a little water for Neptune, asking for wells, springs and rivers to stay full.','Share cool drinks and food.','Water a thirsty plant or tree with what is left.']},correspondences:{deities:['Neptune','Poseidon','Manann\xe1n mac Lir'],herbs:['Willow','Mint','Lemon Balm'],crystals:['Aquamarine','Blue Lace Agate','Turquoise'],colors:['Blue','Green'],foods:['Cool water','Fish','Fresh fruit','Watered wine'],symbols:['Trident','Leafy shelter','Springs','Horses']},sources:['Smith\u2019s Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities (1875), \u201cNeptunalia\u201d: https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Neptunalia.html','Calendar of Filocalus, Chronography of 354, July: https://www.tertullian.org/fathers/chronography_of_354_06_calendar.htm','H. H. Scullard, Festivals and Ceremonies of the Roman Republic (London, 1981).']},{ref:'roman-saturnalia',title:'Saturnalia',aka:['Io Saturnalia','Festival of Saturn'],tradition:'Roman',summary:'Festival of Saturn from 17 December: feasting, candles and gift-giving, when ordinary rules were set aside and roles turned upside down.',honours:'Saturn, god of sowing and of the Golden Age, when he ruled Italy and all were equal. His statue\u2019s woollen bonds were loosened for the festival, setting him free for a while.',history:'The Saturnalia began with a sacrifice at the temple of Saturn in the Forum and a public banquet, with the cry \u201cIo Saturnalia!\u201d For several days (it grew from one to seven), Romans wore casual clothes and the felt cap of freed slaves, gambled openly, ate and drank, and gave gifts: wax candles (cerei) and small pottery figures (sigillaria). Slaves were served by their masters or ate with them, and a mock king was chosen to give silly orders.\n\nCatullus called it \u201cSaturnalibus, optimo dierum\u201d, \u201cthe Saturnalia, best of days\u201d. Seneca grumbled that the whole city went mad, and Pliny the Younger hid in a soundproof room to work. Macrobius set his long dialogue Saturnalia during the festival.\n\nMany Christmas and New Year customs, such as gift-giving, candles, feasting and the Lord of Misrule, echo the Saturnalia, though direct descent is often hard to prove.',knowAndDont:{known:'The customs are well described by Roman writers, including Macrobius, Seneca, Catullus, Martial and Lucian.',unknown:'How far masters truly served slaves, and how these customs passed into Christmas, is debated.'},celebrate:['Give small, playful gifts: candles, little figures, sweets.','Serve those who usually serve you, and share a table with no head.','Wear something comfortable and festive, and play games of chance (for buttons, not money).','Light many candles and greet friends with \u201cIo Saturnalia!\u201d','Choose a mock king or queen for the evening.'],ritual:{premade:'folk-lararium-offering',title:'Loosening Saturn\u2019s bonds',outline:['Tie a woollen thread around an image or symbol of Saturn (a sickle, a sheaf, or a candle).','Light candles, and offer wine or juice and a little grain to Saturn.','Untie the thread, and say: \u201cIo Saturnalia! Be free among us.\u201d','Exchange small gifts, and feast together without ranks.','At the end of the festival, retie the thread and put the image back.']},correspondences:{deities:['Saturn','Janus','Ceres'],herbs:['Holly','Bay','Cinnamon','Juniper'],crystals:['Garnet','Onyx','Smoky Quartz'],colors:['Red','Gold','Green'],foods:['Roast pork','Wine','Nuts','Honey cakes','Figs and dates'],symbols:['Candles (cerei)','Felt cap (pilleus)','Sigillaria figurines','Dice','Sickle']},sources:['Macrobius, Saturnalia, book 1: https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Macrobius/Saturnalia/home.html','Catullus 14.15 (Latin): https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/catullus.shtml','Smith\u2019s Dictionary of Greek and Roman Antiquities (1875), \u201cSaturnalia\u201d: https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/secondary/SMIGRA*/Saturnalia.html','Seneca, Letters 18.']},{ref:'roman-sol_invictus',title:'Dies Natalis Solis Invicti',aka:['Birthday of the Unconquered Sun','Sol Invictus','Natalis Invicti'],tradition:'Roman',summary:'The birthday of the Unconquered Sun, 25 December, celebrating the sun\u2019s return after the winter solstice.',honours:'Sol Invictus, the unconquered sun, a god especially honoured by the later Roman emperors as protector of the empire, and the reborn light of midwinter.',history:'Sol was an old Roman god, but the emperor Aurelian made Sol Invictus a major state god and built him a temple in 274 CE. The Calendar of 354, made for a Christian Roman, marks 25 December as \u201cN INVICTI CM XXX\u201d, the birthday of the Unconquered, with thirty chariot races. On the same date the Christian list in that document says Christ was born in Bethlehem.\n\nThe emperor Julian, in his Hymn to King Helios (362), says that games for the sun were held after the Saturnalia. Some scholars think Christians chose 25 December to replace the sun festival; others argue the Christian date came from calculations of Jesus\u2019s conception and is independent. The evidence is not decisive.\n\nThe idea that Mithras was born on 25 December is often repeated, but no ancient source says so.',knowAndDont:{known:'Aurelian\u2019s temple, the Calendar of 354 entry, and Julian\u2019s reference to games for the sun are firm evidence.',unknown:'Whether the festival existed before Christmas or alongside it, and how they influenced each other, is debated. Claims about Mithras are unsupported.'},celebrate:['Watch the sunrise and greet the returning sun.','Light a gold or yellow candle at dawn.','Spend time outdoors in daylight, however brief.','Make a sun symbol for your door or window.','Count the minutes of daylight each day until they visibly grow.'],ritual:{title:'Greeting the unconquered sun',outline:['Rise before dawn and find a place with a view east, or a window.','Light a gold or yellow candle.','As the sun rises, raise your hands and greet it: \u201cHail, Sol Invictus, unconquered sun, born again.\u201d','Offer incense or a little wine, and name what you want to grow with the light.','Let the candle burn while you have breakfast, then put it out.']},correspondences:{deities:['Sol','Apollo','Ra','Dazhbog'],herbs:['Frankincense','Bay','Cinnamon','Saffron'],crystals:['Sunstone','Citrine','Tiger Eye','Amber'],colors:['Gold','Yellow','Orange'],foods:['Bread','Oranges','Honey','Wine'],symbols:['Radiate crown','Chariot','Sun disc','Dawn']},sources:['Calendar of Filocalus, Chronography of 354, December: https://www.tertullian.org/fathers/chronography_of_354_06_calendar.htm','Julian, Hymn to King Helios 156B\u2013C.','Steven Hijmans, Sol: The Sun in the Art and Religions of Rome (Groningen, 2009).','Thomas J. Talley, The Origins of the Liturgical Year (New York, 1986).']},{ref:'slavic-marzanna',title:'Drowning of Marzanna',aka:['Topienie Marzanny','Vyn\xe1\u0161en\xed Morany','Morana','Smrtn\xe1 ned\u011ble','Death Sunday'],tradition:'Slavic',summary:'An effigy of Marzanna (Morana), the figure of winter and death, is burned or drowned to send winter away and welcome spring. Traditionally on a Sunday in mid-Lent; many now keep it on the first day of spring or at the equinox.',honours:'Winter itself, sent off with respect and relief; spring, brought into the village with a green branch; and, for those who see her as a goddess, Morana, lady of winter and death, who must leave so that life can return.',history:'In Poland, Czechia, Slovakia and neighbouring lands, children and young people made a straw figure dressed in women\u2019s clothes, carried it through the village singing, and threw it into a river or burned it, often without looking back. Then a green branch or small tree decorated with ribbons and eggs (the gaik or nov\xe9 l\xe9to, \u201cnew summer\u201d) was carried back into the village. The custom was kept on the fourth or fifth Sunday of Lent, sometimes called Death Sunday.\n\nThe name Marzanna first appears in the fifteenth century, when the chronicler Jan D\u0142ugosz listed her among Polish gods and likened her to Ceres. Church synods of the same period already disapproved of carrying and drowning \u201cdeath\u201d. Scholars debate whether Marzanna was a real pre-Christian goddess or a folk personification of winter and death.\n\nToday schoolchildren in Poland often make and drown or burn a Marzanna on 21 March, the first day of spring, and Rodnovers (followers of Slavic Native Faith) keep it at the equinox.',knowAndDont:{known:'The custom is well documented from the fifteenth century to today, across West Slavic lands, with the effigy, the songs, the water and the green branch.',unknown:'Whether Marzanna was ever worshipped as a goddess is uncertain; D\u0142ugosz\u2019s list of Polish gods is learned and partly guesswork. The rite may have been a folk custom of the Christian centuries from the start.'},celebrate:['Make a small Marzanna from straw, twigs, paper and natural cloth, with no plastic or wire.','Carry her out singing, and burn her safely in a fire pit, or let her go into running water only if she is fully biodegradable and local rules allow it.','Walk home without looking back.','Bring home a green branch tied with ribbons to welcome spring.','Spring-clean, and put away heavy winter things.'],ritual:{title:'Sending winter away',outline:['Make a small figure of straw or twigs, dressed in a scrap of natural cloth.','Tell her, aloud or on a slip of paper tucked inside, what winter has meant and what you are ready to leave behind.','Carry her outside in procession, singing or drumming.','Burn her in a safe fire pit or metal bowl, with water nearby, or bury her in the garden.','Turn and walk away without looking back.','Bring a green branch or spring flowers inside and decorate it with ribbons.']},correspondences:{deities:['Morana','Lada'],herbs:['Willow','Birch','Rowan'],crystals:['Obsidian','Moss Agate','Clear Quartz'],colors:['White','Black','Green'],foods:['Eggs','Rye bread','First greens'],symbols:['Straw effigy','River','Green branch with ribbons','Painted eggs']},respect:'Marzanna is above all a living folk custom of Poland, Czechia and Slovakia, kept by Catholic and secular families and schools as well as by Rodnovers. If you take part, follow local customs and environmental rules: many towns now ask that Marzannas be burned or made without plastic, not thrown into rivers.',sources:['Jan D\u0142ugosz, Annales seu cronicae incliti Regni Poloniae, book 1 (15th century).','Ronald Hutton, The Stations of the Sun (Oxford, 1996), on \u201ccarrying out Death\u201d.','James George Frazer, The Golden Bough (abridged 1922), ch. 28: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/3623','Aleksander Gieysztor, Mitologia S\u0142owian (Warsaw, 1982).']},{ref:'slavic-kupala',title:'Kupala Night',aka:['Noc Kupa\u0142y','Ivan Kupala','Kupalle','Sob\xf3tka','St John\u2019s Eve','Kupalnocka'],tradition:'Slavic',summary:'Midsummer night of fire and water: jumping over bonfires, floating flower wreaths on rivers, and searching for the magical fern flower. Kept on St John\u2019s Eve, 23\u201324 June; in Orthodox lands by the Old Style, 6\u20137 July.',honours:'The sun at its height, water and fire together, young love, and the healing herbs of midsummer. In Christian terms it is the feast of St John the Baptist (Ivan); Rodnovers honour Kupala as a figure of midsummer, and some honour Dazhbog or Lada.',history:'Midsummer customs among the Slavs are recorded from the Middle Ages, often in church complaints about \u201cdevilish games\u201d. A seventeenth-century Ukrainian chronicle names Kupalo as a god of the earth\u2019s fruits, though scholars now think Kupala was more likely a name for the festival itself, from a root meaning \u201cto bathe\u201d, joined with the Baptist\u2019s feast.\n\nThe customs are vivid and widespread. Young people light bonfires on river banks and jump over them in pairs. Girls weave wreaths of flowers and herbs and set them floating with candles; if a boy catches a girl\u2019s wreath, they will marry. People bathe in rivers and roll in the dew. The night\u2019s greatest secret is the fern flower, which blooms only at midnight and brings luck and the ability to understand animals and find treasure; of course ferns do not flower, which is part of the magic.\n\nIn Poland the festival is called Sob\xf3tka, and Jan Kochanowski wrote about it in 1586. In Belarus Kupalle is a national celebration, and in Ukraine and Russia it is kept in early July.',knowAndDont:{known:'The midsummer customs of fire, water, wreaths, herbs and the fern flower are richly documented from the sixteenth century to today.',unknown:'Whether there was a god called Kupala is doubtful; the name is probably the festival\u2019s. The pre-Christian form of the festival is not directly recorded.'},celebrate:['Weave a wreath of wild flowers and herbs and float it on a river or lake (natural materials only).','Light a bonfire safely and lawfully, and jump only a small, safe flame, or simply walk round it.','Gather midsummer herbs with thanks, to dry for the year.','Bathe in a river, lake or the sea if it is safe, or walk barefoot in the dew.','Stay up late and search the woods for the fern flower, with friends and a torch.'],ritual:{premade:'folk-kupala-wreath'},correspondences:{deities:['Dazhbog','Lada','Perun'],herbs:['St John\u2019s Wort','Mugwort','Yarrow','Birch','Elder'],crystals:['Sunstone','Moonstone','Aquamarine'],colors:['Red','Gold','Green','White'],foods:['Bread and honey','Kvass or mead','Cheese','Strawberries'],symbols:['Flower wreath','Bonfire','Fern flower','River','Dew']},respect:'Kupala is a living folk festival in Belarus, Ukraine, Poland, Russia and the Baltic lands, kept by Orthodox, Catholic and secular communities and by Rodnovers. If you join a public celebration, ask about local customs, keep the rivers clean, and remember that for many people from these countries it carries strong feelings of home and identity, especially in time of war.',sources:['Linda J. Ivanits, Russian Folk Belief (Armonk, 1989).','Jan Kochanowski, Pie\u015b\u0144 \u015bwi\u0119toja\u0144ska o Sob\xf3tce (1586).','W. R. S. Ralston, The Songs of the Russian People (London, 1872).','Aleksander Gieysztor, Mitologia S\u0142owian (Warsaw, 1982).']},{ref:'slavic-dziady',title:'Dziady',aka:['Forefathers\u2019 Eve','Dzyady','Radunitsa','Zaduszki','Dmitrov Saturday'],tradition:'Slavic',summary:'Forefathers\u2019 Eve, when the spirits of the ancestors are welcomed home and fed. Kept several times a year in Belarus; the autumn Dziady falls before St Dmitri\u2019s day in early November, and in Poland it has merged with All Saints\u2019 and All Souls\u2019.',honours:'The dziady, \u201cgrandfathers\u201d: all the family dead, who return home on these nights to be fed and remembered. Also the souls with no one to remember them.',history:'In Belarus, Ukraine, Lithuania and eastern Poland, families kept several days for the dead each year: in spring (Radunitsa, after Easter), at Trinity, and in autumn (Dzmitrava Saturday, before St Dmitri\u2019s day). On the autumn Dziady the house was cleaned, a special supper prepared, and the door or window opened. Food was set out for the dead, and a share was left on the table overnight; sometimes the first spoonful of each dish was put aside for them. People spoke little and avoided sharp knives, so as not to hurt the souls.\n\nThe Polish poet Adam Mickiewicz made the custom famous in his verse drama Dziady (1823\u20131860), in which a village ritual summons the dead. In Poland the customs largely merged with All Saints\u2019 Day and All Souls\u2019 (Zaduszki), with candles on every grave. In Belarus, Dziady is still widely kept and has become a day of national remembrance.',knowAndDont:{known:'The customs of feeding the dead on set days are well documented in nineteenth- and twentieth-century folklore, and the Orthodox and Catholic memorial days are part of church life.',unknown:'The pre-Christian origins are assumed but not directly recorded. Practice varies a great deal by region.'},celebrate:['Clean the house and prepare a simple supper with traditional foods.','Open a door or window, and invite your dead to come.','Set a place for them, and leave a share of the food overnight.','Visit graves and light candles, especially on forgotten ones.','Read Mickiewicz\u2019s Dziady, or tell stories about your family dead.'],ritual:{premade:'folk-dziady-meal'},correspondences:{deities:['Veles','Mokosh','Rod'],herbs:['Rosemary','Juniper','Birch'],crystals:['Jet','Smoky Quartz','Amber'],colors:['White','Black','Red'],foods:['Kutia (wheat with honey and poppy seed)','Pancakes (bliny)','Bread','Kissel','Vodka or kvass'],symbols:['Open door','Empty place at the table','Candles on graves','Towel (rushnyk)']},respect:'Dziady and the days of the dead are kept by Orthodox and Catholic Christians and secular families in Belarus, Poland, Ukraine and Lithuania, and by Rodnovers. In Belarus it also carries political meaning as a day of remembrance for victims of repression. If you visit a cemetery, behave quietly, don\u2019t touch other families\u2019 graves except to light a candle on a neglected one, and follow local customs.',sources:['Adam Mickiewicz, Dziady (Forefathers\u2019 Eve), parts II and IV (1823).','Linda J. Ivanits, Russian Folk Belief (Armonk, 1989).','Aleksander Gieysztor, Mitologia S\u0142owian (Warsaw, 1982).']},{ref:'slavic-koliada',title:'Koliada',aka:['Kolyada','Koleda','Kol\u0119dowanie','Svyat Vechir','Carolling'],tradition:'Slavic',summary:'The season of carolling: koliadki sung from house to house for blessings in the year ahead, from Christmas Eve to Epiphany. Keeping it at the solstice as a festival of the newborn sun is a modern Rodnover reconstruction.',honours:'The newborn Christ, for most who keep it; the household and its prosperity for the year; the ancestors, who are fed at the Holy Supper; and, for Rodnovers, the reborn sun.',history:'The name comes from the Latin calendae, the first day of the month, especially the New Year\u2019s Kalends, borrowed into Slavic languages in early Christian times. Carollers went from house to house through the Christmas season, often with a star on a pole, a goat costume (koza), and a puppet show (vertep). Their songs, koliadki and shchedrivky, wished the household health, good harvests and marriages, and they were given food and coins in return.\n\nOn Christmas Eve families shared the Holy Supper of twelve meatless dishes, beginning with kutia, wheat with honey and poppy seeds, and set a spoonful aside for the dead. A sheaf of wheat, the didukh, stood in the corner. The Ukrainian carol Shchedryk, arranged by Mykola Leontovych, became the English \u201cCarol of the Bells\u201d.\n\nNo pre-Christian Koliada festival is recorded. Rodnovers have reconstructed Koliada as a solstice festival of the newborn sun, and keep it in that form today.',knowAndDont:{known:'The carolling customs, songs, Holy Supper and didukh are richly documented and still very much alive.',unknown:'The pre-Christian midwinter customs of the Slavs are not directly recorded, and the idea of a sun god Koliada is a modern reconstruction.'},celebrate:['Go carolling with friends, or learn a koliadka or Shchedryk.','Cook kutia, or a meatless supper, and set a spoon aside for the ancestors.','Stand a sheaf of wheat or a bundle of grasses in the corner of the room.','Make a star on a pole and carry it with your carollers.','Give to carollers or to people in need.'],ritual:{title:'A Holy Supper and carol',outline:['Place a little hay or straw under the tablecloth and a sheaf in the corner.','Light a candle in the window and wait for the first star to appear.','Share a supper of simple, meatless dishes, beginning with kutia.','Set aside a portion for the ancestors.','Sing a carol or koliadka, and bless the house for the year.']},correspondences:{deities:['Dazhbog','Svarog','Veles'],herbs:['Juniper','Birch','Holly'],crystals:['Garnet','Amber','Clear Quartz'],colors:['Red','Gold','White'],foods:['Kutia','Pierogi or varenyky','Borscht','Uzvar (dried fruit drink)','Poppy-seed cake'],symbols:['Carollers\u2019 star','Didukh (wheat sheaf)','Goat mask','Candle in the window']},respect:'Koliada is above all the Christmas season of Orthodox and Catholic Slavs, kept by families and churches in Ukraine, Belarus, Poland, Russia, Serbia, Slovakia and beyond. Rodnovers keep their own solstice form. If you join carolling or a Holy Supper, honour the Christian meaning for those who hold it, and the strong ties to home and family it carries, especially for Ukrainians today.',sources:['Linda J. Ivanits, Russian Folk Belief (Armonk, 1989).','Mykola Leontovych, Shchedryk (arranged 1916).','Ronald Hutton, The Stations of the Sun (Oxford, 1996), on calends and carolling.']},{ref:'christian-epiphany',title:'Epiphany',aka:['Twelfth Night','Three Kings\u2019 Day','Theophany','Little Christmas','D\xeda de Reyes'],tradition:'Christian',summary:'On 6 January the Magi come to the Christ child, and Twelfth Night ends the Christmas season. Doors are chalked with the year and the Magi\u2019s initials to bless the house for the year ahead.',honours:'Christ revealed to the world: to the Magi from the east in the Western churches, and at his baptism in the Jordan in the Eastern churches. The Magi, later named Caspar, Melchior and Balthasar, are honoured as the first pilgrims.',history:'Epiphany, \u201cmanifestation\u201d, is one of the oldest Christian feasts, kept in the East from the third or fourth century. In the West it came to focus on the visit of the Magi in Matthew\u2019s Gospel; in the East, as Theophany, on Christ\u2019s baptism, with great blessings of water in rivers and the sea.\n\nFolk customs abound. The house blessing with chalk, writing the year and C+M+B above the door (for the Magi\u2019s names, or Christus mansionem benedicat, \u201cMay Christ bless this house\u201d), is kept in Central Europe, and children go from house to house as star singers collecting for charity. In Spain and Latin America the Three Kings bring the gifts; in Italy La Befana does. A king cake or Twelfth cake with a hidden bean or figure chooses a king for the night. In England, Twelfth Night was a night of feasting and misrule, and the Christmas greenery came down.',knowAndDont:{known:'The feast is ancient. The chalking, star singers, king cake and water blessings are long-established and still widely kept.',unknown:'The Gospel does not say how many Magi there were or that they were kings; the names and the number three come from later tradition.'},celebrate:['Chalk the year and C+M+B above your door, with a short prayer for the house.','Bake or buy a king cake with a hidden bean or figure; whoever finds it is king or queen for the night.','Take down Christmas decorations and greenery, and compost or burn it safely.','Bless water, or attend a church water blessing if you are near an Orthodox community.','Give a gift to someone in need, as the Magi did.'],ritual:{premade:'folk-epiphany-chalk'},correspondences:{herbs:['Frankincense','Myrrh'],crystals:['Citrine','Clear Quartz'],colors:['Gold','White'],foods:['King cake or Twelfth cake','Galette des rois','Rosca de Reyes','Spiced wine'],symbols:['Star','Chalk','The three gifts','Crowns','Water']},respect:'Epiphany is a Christian feast, kept as a holy day in many churches. Chalking the door is a Catholic and Lutheran house blessing; you are welcome to keep it, but it is a prayer to Christ, not a spell. If you attend a church service or a water blessing, come quietly, follow what others do, and ask before photographing.',sources:['Catholic Encyclopedia (1909), \u201cEpiphany\u201d: https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/05504c.htm','Matthew 2:1\u201312.','Ronald Hutton, The Stations of the Sun (Oxford, 1996), ch. 2.']},{ref:'christian-st_brigid',title:'St Brigid\u2019s Day',aka:['L\xe1 Fh\xe9ile Br\xedde','Feast of St Brigid','St Bride\u2019s Day','Imbolc'],tradition:'Christian',summary:'Feast of Brigid of Kildare on 1 February, saint of hearth, healing and holy wells. Rush crosses are woven and hung over doors for protection, and a cloth is left out overnight to take her blessing.',honours:'St Brigid of Kildare (died c. 525), abbess and founder of a double monastery, patron of Ireland with Patrick and Columba, of dairy workers, midwives, newborns and the poor. She is remembered for her generosity: giving away her father\u2019s goods, her mother\u2019s butter, and anything else to the needy.',history:'Brigid\u2019s early Lives, from the seventh century on, are full of miracles of food and abundance: butter multiplied, a cow giving milk three times, ale made from bath water for visiting clergy. Her monastery at Kildare became one of the most important in Ireland, and Gerald of Wales described a fire there tended by her nuns that never went out.\n\nHer feast on 1 February, the first day of spring in Ireland, is kept with many customs: weaving a cross from rushes, often while telling the story of Brigid explaining the cross to a dying chieftain; leaving out a cloth (brat Br\xedde) to be blessed; making a doll (Br\xeddeog) carried by children; and visiting holy wells.\n\nThe Brigidine Sisters relit her flame in Kildare in 1993, and in 2023 Ireland made her feast a public holiday.',knowAndDont:{known:'Brigid\u2019s cult, her Lives, the Kildare monastery, and the feast customs are well documented.',unknown:'Very little is known for certain about the historical Brigid. The relation between the saint and the goddess of the same name is debated by scholars and believers alike.'},celebrate:['Weave a Brigid\u2019s cross from rushes, straw or paper, and hang it inside the door.','Leave a strip of cloth outside overnight for Brigid\u2019s blessing.','Visit a holy well, or bless a bowl of water at home.','Give food or money to someone in need, as Brigid did.','Pray the traditional blessing or a prayer of your own for the home.'],ritual:{premade:'folk-brigid-cross-well'},correspondences:{herbs:['Rowan','Chamomile'],crystals:['Amethyst','Clear Quartz'],colors:['White','Green','Red'],foods:['Butter','Bread','Buttermilk','Colcannon'],symbols:['Brigid\u2019s cross','Brat Br\xedde','Holy well','Flame','Cow']},respect:'St Brigid\u2019s Day is a living Christian feast, and Brigid is a beloved Catholic, Anglican and Orthodox saint. Many Irish people keep both her Christian and folk customs. If you visit Kildare or a holy well, treat it as a holy place: quietly, without leaving litter or non-biodegradable rags.',sources:['Catholic Encyclopedia (1907), \u201cSt. Brigid of Ireland\u201d: https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/02784b.htm','Kevin Danaher, The Year in Ireland (Cork, 1972).','Solas Bhr\xedde Centre, Kildare: https://solasbhride.ie/']},{ref:'christian-candlemas',title:'Candlemas',aka:['Presentation of the Lord','Purification of Mary','Feast of Lights','Hypapante'],tradition:'Christian',summary:'The Presentation of Christ in the Temple on 2 February, forty days after Christmas: the light of the world. Candles for the year are blessed today, and the weather is watched for what is left of winter.',honours:'The infant Christ presented in the Temple; Mary, who came for purification after childbirth; and the old Simeon and Anna, who recognised him. Simeon\u2019s song calls Christ \u201ca light to lighten the Gentiles\u201d.',history:'The pilgrim Egeria described the feast in Jerusalem in the late fourth century. In Rome, by the seventh century, it was kept with a procession of candles, and the blessing of candles became central: churches and households had their candles for the year blessed at Candlemas.\n\nFolk weather lore says that if Candlemas is bright and fair, winter will have another flight; if it is dark and wet, winter is gone. In German-speaking America, this became Groundhog Day. In France, cr\xeapes are made at the Chandeleur. In some places the Christmas greenery was kept until Candlemas, and then removed.',knowAndDont:{known:'The feast, its candle procession and its weather lore are well documented.',unknown:'Claims that Candlemas replaced the Roman Lupercalia or Imbolc are not supported by evidence; it falls forty days after Christmas.'},celebrate:['Gather the candles you will use this year, and have them blessed at church or bless them yourself with a prayer.','Light a candle in each window at dusk.','Make pancakes or cr\xeapes.','Watch the weather, and note what it says about spring.','Take down the last of the Christmas greenery.'],ritual:{premade:'folk-candlemas-candles'},correspondences:{herbs:['Bay','Rosemary','Frankincense'],crystals:['Clear Quartz','Selenite'],colors:['White','Gold'],foods:['Cr\xeapes or pancakes','Bread','Milk'],symbols:['Candles','Two turtle doves','The Temple','Light']},respect:'Candlemas is a Christian feast still kept in Catholic, Orthodox, Anglican and Lutheran churches. Many Christians welcome visitors at the candle blessing. If you bless candles at home, you can use Simeon\u2019s song (Luke 2:29\u201332) in its Christian meaning.',sources:['Catholic Encyclopedia (1908), \u201cCandlemas\u201d: https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/03245b.htm','Luke 2:22\u201340.','Ronald Hutton, The Stations of the Sun (Oxford, 1996), ch. 14.']},{ref:'christian-ash_wednesday',title:'Ash Wednesday',aka:['Day of Ashes','Start of Lent'],tradition:'Christian',summary:'The first day of Lent, marked with ashes: \u201cremember that you are dust.\u201d Forty days of prayer, fasting and letting go begin.',honours:'God\u2019s mercy to those who turn back; the season of Lent, in which Christians follow Christ\u2019s forty days in the wilderness and prepare for Easter.',history:'Ashes as a sign of repentance are found in the Hebrew Bible. By the tenth century, Christians in Western Europe received ashes on their foreheads at the start of Lent, and the custom became universal in the Western church. The ashes are traditionally made by burning the palms from the previous year\u2019s Palm Sunday.\n\nThe priest or minister marks a cross on the forehead and says words from Genesis, in the King James version: \u201cfor dust thou art, and unto dust shalt thou return\u201d, or \u201cRepent, and believe the Gospel.\u201d It is a day of fasting for many Christians. The day before is Shrove Tuesday, Mardi Gras or Carnival, a feast to use up rich food.',knowAndDont:{known:'The custom of ashes at Lent is medieval, and the words are biblical.',unknown:'Exactly when Lent took its present form is debated, and practice varies among churches.'},celebrate:['Attend an Ash Wednesday service if you wish, or simply reflect on your mortality.','Choose something to give up or take up for Lent, gently and realistically.','Give to charity what you save by fasting.','Burn last year\u2019s palm crosses or a dried bundle of herbs to make your own ashes, safely.'],ritual:{title:'A quiet beginning to Lent',outline:['Sit with a candle and a small dish of ashes.','Read Genesis 3:19 or Psalm 51 slowly.','Touch a little ash to your forehead or the back of your hand, if you wish, and remember that you are dust.','Name one thing to let go of, and one practice to take up, for the forty days.','Put out the candle and keep the evening simple.']},correspondences:{herbs:['Hyssop','Rosemary','Myrrh'],crystals:['Smoky Quartz','Amethyst'],colors:['Purple','Grey'],foods:['Plain bread','Simple soup','Water'],symbols:['Ashes','Cross','Desert','Palm']},respect:'Ash Wednesday is a Christian day of penitence. Anyone may attend a service, and many churches welcome non-Christians to receive ashes; ask the minister. Keep any private observance respectful of its meaning.',sources:['Catholic Encyclopedia (1907), \u201cAsh Wednesday\u201d: https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/01775b.htm','Genesis 3:19 (King James Version).','Church of England, The Church\u2019s Year: https://www.churchofengland.org/prayer-and-worship/worship-texts-and-resources/common-worship/churchs-year']},{ref:'christian-lady_day',title:'Lady Day (Annunciation)',aka:['The Annunciation','Lady Day','Feast of the Incarnation'],tradition:'Christian',summary:'Gabriel\u2019s message to Mary on 25 March, nine months before Christmas. A feast of Mary, of yes-saying and new life, and an old quarter day when the year turned.',honours:'Mary, who said yes to God; the archangel Gabriel; and the conception of Christ.',history:'The Annunciation is told in Luke\u2019s Gospel. The feast was kept by the fifth century, and in the West its date, 25 March, was linked with the spring equinox and the creation of the world. In England, Lady Day was the legal start of the year until 1752, and one of the quarter days when rents were due and contracts began. That is why the British tax year still begins on 6 April, Lady Day adjusted for the calendar change.\n\nFolk customs of the day include seed sowing and blessings for the farm. In Orthodox lands, birds were released and it was considered a great feast when no work was done.',knowAndDont:{known:'The feast, its date, and its role as an English quarter day and New Year are well recorded.',unknown:'Many folk customs tied to Lady Day are local and variable.'},celebrate:['Sow seeds or plant something with a prayer for new life.','Read Luke 1:26\u201338, or the Magnificat.','Set a vase of spring flowers by an image of Mary.','Take stock of the year as the old Lady Day new year: what are you saying yes to?'],ritual:{premade:'green-seed-blessing-before-sowing'},correspondences:{herbs:['Rose','Lavender','Calendula'],crystals:['Moonstone','Rose Quartz','Lapis Lazuli'],colors:['Blue','White'],foods:['Waffles (V\xe5ffeldagen in Sweden)','Spring greens','Honey'],symbols:['Lily','Dove','Angel','Seed']},respect:'The Annunciation is a Christian feast of Mary, solemnly kept in Catholic, Orthodox and Anglican churches. If you honour Mary in your own way, respect that she is not a goddess in Christian belief, and keep devotion separate from spellwork.',sources:['Catholic Encyclopedia (1907), \u201cThe Feast of the Annunciation\u201d: https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/01542a.htm','Luke 1:26\u201338.','Ronald Hutton, The Stations of the Sun (Oxford, 1996).']},{ref:'christian-palm_sunday',title:'Palm Sunday',aka:['Passion Sunday','Willow Sunday','Yew Sunday','Flower Sunday'],tradition:'Christian',summary:'Christ\u2019s entry into Jerusalem, a week before Easter; Holy Week begins. Blessed palms are kept, often folded into crosses, as protection for the home through the year.',honours:'Christ entering Jerusalem as king, greeted by crowds with palm branches; and the beginning of the week of his passion.',history:'The pilgrim Egeria described the procession with branches in late fourth-century Jerusalem. The custom spread to the West, where branches were blessed and carried. In northern countries without palms, people used willow, yew, box or other evergreens; in Slavic lands it is Willow Sunday, and in Poland elaborate \u201cpalms\u201d of dried flowers and grasses are made.\n\nBlessed palms or branches were kept in the home, tucked behind pictures or crosses, and believed to protect against lightning, fire and illness. In many places they were burned the following year to make the Ash Wednesday ashes.',knowAndDont:{known:'The feast, the procession, and the custom of keeping blessed branches are long-established.',unknown:'The protective folk beliefs vary greatly from place to place.'},celebrate:['Attend a Palm Sunday service and take home a blessed palm or branch.','Fold a palm or willow into a cross and keep it in your home.','Bring in pussy willow or spring branches for the house.','Read the Gospel account of the entry into Jerusalem.'],ritual:{title:'A branch for the house',outline:['Collect a palm from church or cut a few sprigs of willow or yew with thanks.','Read Matthew 21:1\u201311.','Fold or tie the branches into a cross.','Place it behind a picture, over the door or by a crucifix, with a prayer for the house.','Keep it until next Lent, then burn it safely or return it to church.']},correspondences:{herbs:['Willow','Yew','Bay'],crystals:['Green Aventurine','Clear Quartz'],colors:['Red','Green'],foods:['Bread','Figs'],symbols:['Palm','Willow','Donkey','Cross']},respect:'Palm Sunday begins the holiest week of the Christian year. Visitors are welcome at most services; join the procession if invited. Keep blessed palms with respect; many Christians return old ones to church to be burned.',sources:['Catholic Encyclopedia (1911), \u201cPalm Sunday\u201d: https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/11432b.htm','Matthew 21:1\u201311.','Ronald Hutton, The Stations of the Sun (Oxford, 1996).']},{ref:'christian-good_friday',title:'Good Friday',aka:['Holy Friday','Great Friday'],tradition:'Christian',summary:'The crucifixion, the most solemn day of the Christian year: a day of fasting, silence and keeping vigil. Bread baked today was kept for healing.',honours:'Christ\u2019s passion and death on the cross, and all who suffer.',history:'Christians have marked the day of the crucifixion from the earliest centuries. Its liturgy includes the reading of the Passion, the veneration of the cross, and prayers for the whole world; no Mass is said in the Catholic church. In many countries it is kept with processions, Stations of the Cross, and silence.\n\nFolk customs include hot cross buns in England, which were believed not to go mouldy and were kept for healing; eggs laid on Good Friday were also kept. In some places, no iron was put in the ground and no nails hammered.',knowAndDont:{known:'The day\u2019s liturgy and many of its folk customs are well documented.',unknown:'Hot cross buns are first clearly recorded in the eighteenth century; claims of a pagan origin are unproven.'},celebrate:['Keep quiet for part of the day, especially between noon and three.','Walk the Stations of the Cross, or attend a service.','Eat simply, and fast only if your health allows.','Bake or buy hot cross buns, and keep one in the kitchen.','Pray or light a candle for those who suffer.'],ritual:{title:'Three hours of quiet',outline:['Clear the afternoon, from noon to three if you can.','Light a single candle by a cross or plain cloth.','Read one of the Passion accounts, such as John 18\u201319.','Sit in silence, and pray for the suffering of the world.','At three o\u2019clock, put out the candle and eat a simple meal.']},correspondences:{herbs:['Hyssop','Myrrh','Rosemary'],crystals:['Smoky Quartz','Onyx'],colors:['Black','Red','Purple'],foods:['Hot cross buns','Fish','Plain bread','Water'],symbols:['Cross','Crown of thorns','Nails','Candle']},respect:'Good Friday is a holy day of mourning for Christians. If you attend, dress soberly and keep the silence. This page offers devotion and reflection rather than magic, in keeping with the day.',sources:['Catholic Encyclopedia (1909), \u201cGood Friday\u201d: https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/06643a.htm','John 18\u201319.','Ronald Hutton, The Stations of the Sun (Oxford, 1996), ch. 19.']},{ref:'christian-easter',title:'Easter',aka:['Pascha','Resurrection Sunday','Ostern'],tradition:'Christian',summary:'The resurrection, the heart of the Christian year: new life out of death, kept with sunrise services, eggs, lilies and blessed water.',honours:'The risen Christ, and the promise of new life; the women at the tomb, who were the first witnesses.',history:'Easter is the oldest Christian feast, kept from the first century in connection with the Jewish Passover. Its date, the first Sunday after the first full moon on or after 21 March, was set by the fourth century. The Easter Vigil begins in darkness: a new fire is lit, the Paschal candle is carried into the church, and the cry goes up, \u201cChrist is risen!\u201d\n\nThe English name may come from Eostre, a goddess mentioned by Bede, though some scholars doubt this. Eggs, given up during Lent, return at Easter, and are dyed red in Orthodox lands, decorated as pisanki in Poland and Ukraine, or hunted for in Germany and beyond. Water drawn from a stream before sunrise on Easter morning was thought to have healing power.',knowAndDont:{known:'Easter\u2019s Christian origins, liturgy, date and folk customs are well documented.',unknown:'The derivation of \u201cEaster\u201d from Eostre rests on Bede alone. Claims that eggs or hares come from Eostre are unproven.'},celebrate:['Attend a sunrise service or the Easter Vigil.','Dye or decorate eggs, and share them.','Draw water from a stream in silence before dawn, and keep it.','Bring spring flowers or lilies into the house.','Share a feast with family and friends.'],ritual:{title:'Easter morning',outline:['Rise before dawn, and if you can, go out to watch the sunrise.','Draw a little water from a clean stream or tap, in silence, and bring it home.','Light a candle and say, \u201cChrist is risen.\u201d','Bless the house and family with the water.','Break your Lenten fast with eggs and bread.']},correspondences:{herbs:['Rosemary','Lavender','Hyssop'],crystals:['Clear Quartz','Rose Quartz'],colors:['White','Gold','Yellow'],foods:['Eggs','Lamb','Hot cross buns','Paska or kulich','Simnel cake'],symbols:['Empty tomb','Paschal candle','Eggs','Lilies','Lamb']},respect:'Easter is the most important Christian feast. Visitors are welcome at most services, especially sunrise services. Orthodox Easter often falls on a different date. Treat its symbols with respect even if you also keep the spring equinox.',sources:['Catholic Encyclopedia (1909), \u201cEaster\u201d: https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/05224d.htm','Bede, De temporum ratione, ch. 15 (725).','Ronald Hutton, The Stations of the Sun (Oxford, 1996), ch. 19\u201320.']},{ref:'christian-pentecost',title:'Pentecost',aka:['Whitsun','Whit Sunday','Feast of the Holy Spirit'],tradition:'Christian',summary:'The Holy Spirit comes as wind and tongues of fire, fifty days after Easter. A feast of gifts, inspiration and speaking with power.',honours:'The Holy Spirit, who came upon the apostles as they prayed together in Jerusalem, and the Church, which Christians say was born that day.',history:'Acts of the Apostles tells how, at the Jewish feast of Shavuot (Pentecost), the apostles heard a rushing wind and saw tongues of fire on each other\u2019s heads, then spoke in languages that people of many nations understood. Christians have kept the feast since at least the second century.\n\nIn England it was called Whitsun, perhaps from the white clothes of the newly baptised. Whitsun was a time of church ales, Morris dancing and processions, and Whit walks are still held in parts of northern England. In some churches rose petals are dropped from the ceiling to represent the tongues of fire.',knowAndDont:{known:'The feast is ancient and the account is in Acts 2.',unknown:'The origin of the name Whitsun is debated.'},celebrate:['Wear red, and attend a Pentecost service.','Fly a kite or sit in the wind, thinking of the Spirit.','Learn a phrase in a new language, or speak to someone from another culture.','Name your gifts and how you will use them this year.'],ritual:{title:'Wind and fire',outline:['Light a red candle in a safe place, ideally by an open window.','Read Acts 2:1\u201313.','Feel the air moving, and pray for the gifts you need.','Write each gift on a slip of paper and keep it on your altar or in your Bible.','Put out the candle and go out into the wind.']},correspondences:{herbs:['Bay','Frankincense','Rose'],crystals:['Carnelian','Ruby'],colors:['Red','White'],foods:['Bread','Honey','Strawberries'],symbols:['Dove','Flames','Wind','Rose petals']},respect:'Pentecost is a major Christian feast. Visitors are welcome at services. If you honour the Spirit in your own way, respect what the feast means to those who keep it.',sources:['Catholic Encyclopedia (1912), \u201cPentecost\u201d: https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/15614b.htm','Acts 2:1\u201313.','Ronald Hutton, The Stations of the Sun (Oxford, 1996), ch. 25\u201326.']},{ref:'christian-st_john',title:'St John\u2019s Eve and Day',aka:['Midsummer','Nativity of St John the Baptist','Sankthans','Juhannus','Noc \u015awi\u0119toja\u0144ska'],tradition:'Christian',summary:'The birth of John the Baptist at midsummer, 24 June. Bonfires are lit on the eve, and St John\u2019s wort, gathered now, is hung for protection.',honours:'John the Baptist, the forerunner of Christ, whose birth was six months before Jesus\u2019s; and the light of midsummer, which, as John said, \u201cmust decrease\u201d after him.',history:'The feast of John\u2019s birth was kept by the fourth century. St Augustine noted that after John\u2019s birth the days begin to shorten, and after Christ\u2019s they lengthen, echoing John\u2019s words, \u201cHe must increase, but I must decrease.\u201d\n\nMidsummer bonfires on St John\u2019s Eve are recorded across Europe from the Middle Ages, with leaping through the flames, rolling burning wheels, and gathering herbs. St John\u2019s wort, which blooms around this time, was hung over doors and windows to keep away evil and lightning. In Scandinavia, Finland and the Baltic lands, St John\u2019s Eve is still one of the great festivals of the year.',knowAndDont:{known:'The feast date, the bonfires and herb customs are well recorded.',unknown:'Whether the midsummer fires were originally pagan is debated; they are documented mainly in Christian times.'},celebrate:['Light a bonfire or candles on the eve, safely and lawfully.','Gather St John\u2019s wort and other midsummer herbs, and hang a bunch over the door (do not take it internally; it interacts with many medicines and can cause sun sensitivity).','Read the birth of John in Luke 1, or the Benedictus.','Stay up for the short night with friends.'],ritual:{premade:'green-harvesting-herbs-with-thanks'},correspondences:{herbs:['St John\u2019s Wort','Yarrow','Elder','Mugwort','Vervain'],crystals:['Sunstone','Citrine'],colors:['Gold','Red','Green'],foods:['Strawberries','New potatoes','Honey','Bread'],symbols:['Bonfire','St John\u2019s wort','Lamb','Shell']},respect:'St John\u2019s Day is a Christian feast and, in many countries, a national holiday with deep folk roots. Join public bonfires as a guest, follow local fire rules, and honour the Christian meaning for those who keep it.',sources:['Catholic Encyclopedia (1910), \u201cSt. John the Baptist\u201d: https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/08486b.htm','John 3:30; Luke 1:57\u201380.','Ronald Hutton, The Stations of the Sun (Oxford, 1996), ch. 30\u201331.']},{ref:'christian-lammas',title:'Lammas (Loaf Mass)',aka:['Loaf Mass','Hlafm\xe6sse','Lammas Day','Gule of August'],tradition:'Christian',summary:'On 1 August, the first loaf from the new wheat is baked and blessed in thanks for the harvest. Pieces were kept to bless the barn and hearth.',honours:'God as giver of the harvest, and the work of farmers and bakers. In the Western church, 1 August was also the feast of St Peter in Chains.',history:'The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle mentions \u201chlafm\xe6sse\u201d in 921. A loaf made from the first wheat was brought to church and blessed. An Anglo-Saxon charm recommends crumbling the blessed loaf in the four corners of the barn to protect the harvest.\n\nLammas was a quarter day in Scotland and a date for fairs and rents in England. The custom faded after the Reformation but has been revived in many Anglican parishes, where a loaf is offered at the altar.',knowAndDont:{known:'The name and the loaf blessing are recorded in Anglo-Saxon sources, and the date was a quarter day.',unknown:'How widely the loaf blessing was kept in medieval England is uncertain.'},celebrate:['Bake a loaf from new flour, and give thanks before cutting it.','Take bread to church to be blessed, if your parish keeps Lammas.','Crumble a little blessed bread in the corners of your kitchen or garden for protection.','Thank a farmer or baker.'],ritual:{premade:'kitchen-prosperity-bread'},correspondences:{herbs:['Calendula','Basil'],crystals:['Citrine','Tiger Eye'],colors:['Gold','Yellow'],foods:['New bread','Honey','Ale','Apples'],symbols:['Loaf','Wheat sheaf','Sickle']},respect:'Lammas is a Christian harvest custom, kept especially in Anglican churches, and shares a date and theme with the Wheel of the Year\u2019s Lammas. Both can be honoured; respect the church\u2019s blessing if you attend.',sources:['The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle, entry for 921; tr. J. A. Giles (1847), Project Gutenberg: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/657','Ronald Hutton, The Stations of the Sun (Oxford, 1996), ch. 33.']},{ref:'christian-assumption',title:'The Assumption of Mary',aka:['Assumption','Dormition of the Theotokos','Matka Boska Zielna','Ferragosto','Kr\xe4uterweihe'],tradition:'Christian',summary:'On 15 August, Mary taken up into heaven. In many places bundles of healing herbs are blessed today and hung in the house through the year.',honours:'Mary, the mother of Jesus, taken body and soul into heaven at the end of her life (in Catholic teaching), or falling asleep and taken up by Christ (the Orthodox Dormition).',history:'Stories of Mary\u2019s death and assumption circulated from the fifth century, and the feast was kept in the East by the sixth century and in Rome by the seventh. Pope Pius XII declared the Assumption a dogma in 1950.\n\nA legend tells that when Mary\u2019s tomb was opened, it was found full of flowers and sweet herbs. In Germany, Austria and Poland, bundles of herbs and grain (Kr\xe4uterbuschen, or bouquets for Matka Boska Zielna, \u201cOur Lady of the Herbs\u201d) are blessed at church and hung in the house or barn to protect against illness and storms. In Italy the day is Ferragosto, the summer holiday.',knowAndDont:{known:'The feast, the dogma, and the herb blessing customs are well documented.',unknown:'The historical end of Mary\u2019s life is unknown; the feast rests on tradition.'},celebrate:['Gather a bundle of herbs, flowers and grain and have it blessed at church, or bless it with a prayer.','Hang it in your kitchen or by the door for the year.','Visit a church or shrine of Mary.','Enjoy a summer feast with family.'],ritual:{title:'An Assumption herb bundle',outline:['Gather seven or nine kinds of herbs and flowers: yarrow, mint, tansy (do not eat), chamomile, wheat or oats, and others from your garden.','Tie them with a ribbon.','Take them to an Assumption Mass to be blessed, or pray the Hail Mary or Magnificat over them at home.','Hang the bundle in the kitchen or over the door for the year.','Burn a little of it in a fireproof dish during storms, safely, as was the old custom.']},correspondences:{herbs:['Yarrow','Mint','Chamomile','Calendula','Rose'],crystals:['Moonstone','Lapis Lazuli','Rose Quartz'],colors:['Blue','White','Gold'],foods:['Summer fruit','Bread','Honey'],symbols:['Herb bundle','Lily','Crown of stars']},respect:'The Assumption is a solemn feast of Mary for Catholics and Orthodox Christians and a public holiday in many countries. If you bless herbs, remember that in Christian belief Mary is honoured, not worshipped, and her feast is not a pagan harvest festival in disguise.',sources:['Catholic Encyclopedia (1907), \u201cAssumption of Mary\u201d: https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/02006b.htm','Pius XII, Munificentissimus Deus (1950).']},{ref:'christian-michaelmas',title:'Michaelmas',aka:['Feast of St Michael and All Angels','Michaelmas Day','Feast of the Archangels'],tradition:'Christian',summary:'Feast of St Michael the Archangel on 29 September, protector against evil, as autumn darkens. Blackberries were not picked after today, and protection is asked for the winter.',honours:'St Michael, the archangel who fights the dragon in the Book of Revelation, and all angels. Since 1969 the Catholic Church honours Gabriel and Raphael on the same day.',history:'A church to St Michael was dedicated near Rome on 29 September in the fifth century, and the feast spread across Europe. In England, Michaelmas was a quarter day for rents, hiring fairs and the start of the legal and university terms.\n\nFolk tradition said that when Michael threw the devil out of heaven, he fell into a bramble patch, and every year he spits or worse on the blackberries, so they should not be picked after Michaelmas (or Old Michaelmas, 10\u201311 October). A goose was eaten for good luck and money, and Michaelmas daisies bloom at this time.',knowAndDont:{known:'The feast, its date and its role as a quarter day are well recorded.',unknown:'The blackberry legend is folklore with many local versions.'},celebrate:['Pick the last blackberries before the day, and make a crumble.','Pray Psalm 91 or the prayer to St Michael for protection.','Check the house is ready for winter.','Settle debts or begin new work, as on the old quarter day.'],ritual:{premade:'folk-psalm-91-protection'},correspondences:{herbs:['Angelica','Bay','Rosemary','Frankincense'],crystals:['Black Tourmaline','Sapphire','Lapis Lazuli'],colors:['Blue','Red','Gold'],foods:['Roast goose','Blackberry crumble','St Michael\u2019s bannock','Apples'],symbols:['Sword','Scales','Dragon','Michaelmas daisy']},respect:'Michaelmas is a Christian feast of the archangels. If you seek Michael\u2019s protection, do so through prayer rather than spellwork, honouring how the Church understands him.',sources:['Catholic Encyclopedia (1911), \u201cSt. Michael the Archangel\u201d: https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/10275b.htm','Revelation 12:7\u20139.','Ronald Hutton, The Stations of the Sun (Oxford, 1996), ch. 35.']},{ref:'christian-all_hallows_eve',title:'All Hallows\u2019 Eve',aka:['Halloween','Hallowe\u2019en','Eve of All Saints'],tradition:'Christian',summary:'The eve of All Saints, 31 October, when the boundary with the dead feels thin: candles in windows, prayers for the departed, and the souls remembered.',honours:'All the saints, and all the faithful departed; the dead of our families.',history:'Halloween is the eve of the feast of All Saints. In medieval England, bells were rung for the souls in purgatory, candles lit, and soul cakes given to the poor who prayed for the dead; in later centuries children went souling, singing for cakes.\n\nThe customs of guising, lanterns and divination came from Gaelic Samhain traditions in Ireland and Scotland, and the two merged. Irish and Scottish emigrants brought Halloween to North America, which gave it trick-or-treating and the pumpkin lantern.',knowAndDont:{known:'The eve of All Saints and its medieval customs are well documented, as are the Gaelic folk elements.',unknown:'How much of Halloween is Christian and how much Gaelic is a lively debate.'},celebrate:['Light a candle in the window for the souls of the dead.','Bake soul cakes and give them away.','Carve a lantern and welcome children at the door.','Pray for the dead, or name them at your table.'],ritual:{premade:'folk-apple-candle-halloween'},correspondences:{herbs:['Rosemary','Sage','Rowan'],crystals:['Jet','Obsidian'],colors:['Black','Orange','White'],foods:['Soul cakes','Apples','Nuts','Pumpkin'],symbols:['Candle in the window','Lantern','Soul cakes','Bells']},respect:'All Hallows\u2019 Eve is the vigil of a Christian feast, and many churches hold services for it. Respect those who keep it as a holy night, and those who prefer not to celebrate Halloween at all.',sources:['Catholic Encyclopedia (1907), \u201cAll Saints\u2019 Day\u201d: https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/01315a.htm','Ronald Hutton, The Stations of the Sun (Oxford, 1996), ch. 36\u201337.']},{ref:'christian-all_saints',title:'All Saints\u2019 Day',aka:['All Hallows','Hallowmas','Toussaint','Wszystkich \u015awi\u0119tych','Todos los Santos'],tradition:'Christian',summary:'Honouring all the saints on 1 November, known and unknown: the great company of the faithful who have gone before.',honours:'All the saints: those named in the calendar, and the countless faithful whose names nobody remembers. Christians speak of the \u201ccommunion of saints\u201d, the bond between the living and the holy dead, and this is its feast.',history:'The Eastern churches kept a feast of all martyrs from the fourth century, and still keep All Saints on the Sunday after Pentecost. In Rome, the Pantheon was rededicated to Mary and all martyrs on 13 May 609. Pope Gregory III (731\u2013741) dedicated a chapel in St Peter\u2019s to all the saints, and in 835 Gregory IV extended a 1 November feast to the whole Western Church. Some historians think 1 November was already kept in England and Ireland before Rome adopted it, which would place it beside Samhain, though this is not proven.\n\nIn medieval England the day was Hallowmas, \u201cthe mass of the hallows (saints)\u201d, and its eve gave us Halloween. In Poland, France, Spain, the Philippines and much of Catholic Europe and Latin America, families visit cemeteries on 1 November, cleaning graves, laying chrysanthemums and lighting candles in coloured glass lanterns, so that graveyards glow through the night. In Mexico, 1 November begins D\xeda de Muertos, with the day for children who have died.',knowAndDont:{known:'The dates of Gregory III\u2019s chapel and Gregory IV\u2019s extension of the feast, and the customs of visiting graves, are well documented.',unknown:'Why 1 November was chosen is uncertain. Theories that it was set to replace Samhain are possible but rest on thin evidence.'},celebrate:['Visit and tend family graves, lay flowers and light a candle in a lantern.','Read the life of a saint you admire, or tell the story of a holy, good person from your own life.','Attend an All Saints\u2019 service; many churches read the names of the year\u2019s dead.','Give thanks for the ordinary good people who shaped you, and write one of them a letter if they are living.','Leave a candle on a neglected grave.'],ritual:{title:'Candles for the company of saints',outline:['At dusk, set out a candle in a glass holder for each saint or holy person you want to honour.','Light each and say the name aloud, with one thing they gave to the world.','Light one more for all whose names no one remembers.','Read Revelation 7:9\u201312, the great multitude, or sit in silence with them.','If you can, visit a cemetery and light a candle on a neglected grave.','Put out the indoor candles before you go to bed.']},correspondences:{herbs:['Rosemary','Frankincense','Bay'],crystals:['Clear Quartz','Amethyst'],colors:['White','Gold'],foods:['Bread','Seasonal fruit','Huesos de santo (Spain)','Panellets (Catalonia)'],symbols:['Candles on graves','Halo','Chrysanthemums','Great multitude in white']},respect:'All Saints\u2019 Day is a Christian holy day, a day of obligation for Catholics and a public holiday in many countries. Cemeteries are crowded and very personal on this day: dress soberly, be quiet, and don\u2019t photograph mourners. If you light a candle on a stranger\u2019s grave, do it simply and leave it.',sources:['Catholic Encyclopedia (1907), \u201cAll Saints\u2019 Day\u201d: https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/01315a.htm','Ronald Hutton, The Stations of the Sun (Oxford, 1996), ch. 36.','Revelation 7:9\u201312.']},{ref:'christian-all_souls',title:'All Souls\u2019 Day',aka:['Commemoration of All the Faithful Departed','Zaduszki','D\xeda de los Fieles Difuntos','Soulmas'],tradition:'Christian',summary:'Prayers for all the faithful departed on 2 November. Graves are visited and tended, candles lit, and the beloved dead named and remembered.',honours:'All who have died: family, friends, strangers, and especially those with no one to pray for them. In Catholic teaching, the prayers of the living help the dead on their way to God.',history:'Around 998, Odilo, abbot of the great monastery of Cluny, ordered that all the monasteries under Cluny keep 2 November as a day of prayer for all the dead, the day after All Saints. The custom spread through the Western Church. In 1915 Pope Benedict XV allowed every priest to say three Masses on this day because of the dead of the First World War.\n\nFolk customs grew richly around it. In England children and the poor went \u201csouling\u201d on All Souls\u2019 Eve, singing for soul cakes, small spiced buns, and promising prayers in return. Bells were tolled for the dead. In many Catholic lands people believed the souls returned home on this night: a fire was kept in, the table left laid, and water left for them. In Poland the evening of Zaduszki was associated with Dziady customs. Protestant churches abolished the feast at the Reformation, but many Anglican churches have restored it.',knowAndDont:{known:'The Cluny origin, the spread of the feast and the souling, bell and grave customs are well documented.',unknown:'Folk beliefs about returning souls vary enormously, and how far they predate the Christian feast is unclear.'},celebrate:['Light a candle for your dead and say their names.','Visit graves, tidy them and leave flowers or a candle.','Attend a requiem or All Souls\u2019 service where names of the dead are read.','Bake soul cakes and give them away, or share food with the hungry in memory of the dead.','If grief is raw, be gentle with yourself, and reach out to a friend or bereavement service.'],ritual:{premade:'hedge-ancestor-candle-evening'},correspondences:{herbs:['Rosemary','Cypress','Yew'],crystals:['Jet','Amethyst','Smoky Quartz'],colors:['Black','Purple','White'],foods:['Soul cakes','Bread','Pan de muerto','Beans and grain'],symbols:['Candles','Graves','Tolling bells','Chrysanthemums','Soul cakes']},respect:'All Souls\u2019 is a Christian day of prayer for the dead, and for many families it is deeply personal. If you visit a cemetery, be quiet and respectful. D\xeda de Muertos is its own living Mexican tradition with Indigenous and Catholic roots; learn from it and support Mexican communities, but don\u2019t borrow its altars or face-paint as a costume.',sources:['Catholic Encyclopedia (1907), \u201cAll Souls\u2019 Day\u201d: https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/01315b.htm','Ronald Hutton, The Stations of the Sun (Oxford, 1996), ch. 36\u201337.']},{ref:'christian-advent',title:'Advent Sunday',aka:['First Sunday of Advent','Advent','Advent Wreath'],tradition:'Christian',summary:'The Christian year begins in waiting for the light. The first candle of the Advent wreath is lit, and one more each Sunday until Christmas.',honours:'Christ who is coming: at Christmas, and at the end of time. Advent also honours those who waited for him: the prophets, John the Baptist, and Mary.',history:'Advent, from the Latin adventus, \u201ccoming\u201d, has been kept as a season of preparation since about the fifth and sixth centuries; in Gaul it was once a fast of forty days from St Martin\u2019s Day. Rome settled on four Sundays, and Advent Sunday, the fourth Sunday before Christmas, became the start of the Church\u2019s year. The season has two moods: penitence, with purple vestments, and joyful expectation, with the pink candle of Gaudete Sunday.\n\nThe Advent wreath is newer than it looks. In 1839 Johann Hinrich Wichern, a Lutheran pastor running a home for poor boys in Hamburg, made a cartwheel with candles, a small one for each weekday and a large one for each Sunday, so the children could count the days to Christmas. Evergreens were added, the wheel shrank to four candles, and the custom spread across the world in the twentieth century. Advent calendars began in nineteenth-century Germany too. The great \u201cO Antiphons\u201d sung from 17 December, which gave us \u201cO Come, O Come, Emmanuel\u201d, are much older, going back to at least the eighth century.',knowAndDont:{known:'The season\u2019s history, the start of the church year, Wichern\u2019s wreath and the O Antiphons are well documented.',unknown:'The earliest forms of Advent varied by region and are only partly known. Claims that the Advent wreath is a pagan Yule wheel in disguise are not supported; its origin in 1839 is well recorded.'},celebrate:['Make an Advent wreath of evergreens and four candles, and light the first at dusk.','Start an Advent calendar, perhaps with a small act of kindness for each day.','Slow down: keep a few minutes of quiet each evening in the run-up to Christmas.','Give to a food bank or shelter each week of Advent.','Sing or listen to \u201cO Come, O Come, Emmanuel\u201d.'],ritual:{title:'Lighting the first candle',outline:['Make a wreath of evergreens with four candles (often three purple and one pink) on a fireproof plate, away from curtains.','At dusk, turn off the lights and sit for a moment in the dark.','Light the first candle, the candle of hope.','Read Isaiah 9:2, \u201cThe people that walked in darkness have seen a great light.\u201d','Sit in the light and pray or reflect on what you are waiting for.','Put out the candle before leaving the room; light it again each evening this week.']},correspondences:{herbs:['Holly','Juniper','Cedar','Rosemary'],crystals:['Amethyst','Clear Quartz','Rose Quartz'],colors:['Purple','Pink','Green','Blue'],foods:['Stollen','Gingerbread','Spiced biscuits','Clementines'],symbols:['Advent wreath','Four candles','Star','Advent calendar']},respect:'Advent is the start of the Christian year and a season of prayer. You are welcome to keep a wreath or calendar; if you do, honour its meaning as waiting for Christ, and know that many churches welcome visitors at Advent carol services.',sources:['Catholic Encyclopedia (1907), \u201cAdvent\u201d: https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/01165a.htm','Church of England, The Church\u2019s Year: https://www.churchofengland.org/prayer-and-worship/worship-texts-and-resources/common-worship/churchs-year','Isaiah 9:2 (King James Version).']},{ref:'christian-st_lucy',title:'St Lucy\u2019s Day',aka:['Santa Lucia','Luciadagen','Lucy','Lussinatta','Lucia'],tradition:'Christian',summary:'Lucy, whose name means light, kept on 13 December, what was once the longest night: candles, crowns of light and saffron buns carry the light into midwinter.',honours:'St Lucy (Lucia) of Syracuse, a young Christian woman martyred in the persecution of Diocletian around 304, patron of the blind, of eyes and of light.',history:'Lucy\u2019s feast is one of the oldest in the Roman calendar, and her name is in the Canon of the Mass. Her legend says she gave her dowry to the poor, was denounced by a disappointed suitor, and could not be moved even by a team of oxen when the authorities tried to drag her away. Later stories, and many paintings, show her with her eyes on a dish.\n\nUnder the old Julian calendar her day fell near the winter solstice, which is why John Donne, in 1633, called it \u201cthe year\u2019s midnight, and it is the day\u2019s, / Lucy\u2019s, who scarce seven hours herself unmasks.\u201d In Scandinavia, Lucy night was feared as the longest and most dangerous of the year, when the Lussi and other spirits were abroad.\n\nThe Swedish Lucia procession, girls in white with a crown of candles and a red sash, singing the Neapolitan song Santa Lucia, spread from western Swedish country houses in the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries and became a national custom after a Stockholm newspaper crowned a Lucia in 1927. Saffron buns (lussekatter) and gingersnaps are served at dawn. In parts of Italy, Lucy brings gifts to children.',knowAndDont:{known:'Lucy\u2019s martyrdom and her early cult are well recorded; Donne\u2019s poem and the modern Swedish customs are documented.',unknown:'The details of her life come from later legend. The Scandinavian Lussi folklore and the procession grew separately, and how they joined is not entirely clear.'},celebrate:['Rise early and bring breakfast with saffron buns to someone, by candlelight.','Watch or join a Lucia procession; many Swedish churches abroad hold one.','Light candles in your windows in the darkest weeks.','Give to a charity for blind or partially sighted people.','Read Donne\u2019s poem and sit with the long night for a while.'],ritual:{title:'Carrying the light',outline:['Rise before dawn. Use a battery candle crown, or carry a single candle in a sturdy holder.','Bring a tray of saffron buns, gingersnaps and coffee or juice to someone in the house.','Sing or say a verse about light in darkness, such as John 1:5.','Pray for those whose sight or hope is dim.','Set the candle in a window as the day begins.']},correspondences:{herbs:['Saffron','Cinnamon','Clove'],crystals:['Clear Quartz','Citrine','Selenite'],colors:['White','Red','Gold'],foods:['Saffron buns (lussekatter)','Gingersnaps (pepparkakor)','Coffee or gl\xf6gg','Cucc\xeca (Sicilian wheat pudding)'],symbols:['Crown of candles','White dress','Red sash','Eyes on a dish','Palm of martyrdom']},respect:'St Lucy is a Christian saint, and her feast is beloved in Sweden, Norway, Finland and Italy, especially Sicily. Join public celebrations as a guest. The Lucia procession is often a children\u2019s church event, so ask before photographing.',sources:['Catholic Encyclopedia (1910), \u201cSt. Lucy\u201d: https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/09414a.htm','John Donne, \u201cA Nocturnal upon St Lucy\u2019s Day, Being the Shortest Day\u201d (1633).','Ronald Hutton, The Stations of the Sun (Oxford, 1996).']},{ref:'christian-christmas_eve',title:'Christmas Eve',aka:['Wigilia','Heiligabend','Holy Night','Nochebuena','Svyat Vechir','Badnji dan'],tradition:'Christian',summary:'The holy night of waiting on 24 December, kept with candles, midnight services, and the old belief that animals speak at midnight.',honours:'The coming birth of Christ; Mary and Joseph seeking shelter in Bethlehem; and, in many homes, the dead and the stranger, who are given a place at the table.',history:'Christmas Eve is the vigil of the Nativity. The midnight Mass began in Rome by the fifth century at the church of Santa Maria Maggiore, and became the \u201cMass of the angels\u201d, celebrated in the dark when Christ was believed to have been born.\n\nIn much of Central and Eastern Europe, Christmas Eve is the main family celebration. In Poland, the Wigilia supper begins when the first star appears: hay is placed under the white tablecloth, an empty place is set for an unexpected guest or the absent dead, and the op\u0142atek wafer is broken and shared with wishes before the meatless meal. Ukrainians share Sviata Vecheria with twelve dishes; Serbs bring in the badnjak, an oak branch, and lay it on the fire. In Germany and Scandinavia, gifts are opened on the Eve.\n\nFolk belief across Europe held that at midnight animals kneel in their stalls and can speak, water turns to wine, and bees hum the Hundredth Psalm. Thomas Hardy\u2019s poem \u201cThe Oxen\u201d (1915) remembers the belief. \u201cSilent Night\u201d was first sung on Christmas Eve 1818 in Oberndorf, Austria.',knowAndDont:{known:'The vigil, the midnight Mass and the regional customs of the Christmas Eve supper are long established and well recorded.',unknown:'Folk beliefs about animals and midnight vary from place to place; their origins are obscure.'},celebrate:['Set a candle in the window to welcome the holy family and any traveller.','Share a supper with an empty place laid for the stranger or the absent.','Break bread or a wafer together, offering each person a wish for the year.','Attend Midnight Mass or a carol service.','At midnight, step outside, look at the stars, and listen.'],ritual:{title:'A holy night vigil',outline:['Put a little hay or straw under the tablecloth and a candle in the window at dusk.','Wait for the first star before sitting down to eat.','Break bread or a wafer and share it, each person offering a wish to the others.','Leave one place empty for the stranger or the absent dead.','Read Luke 2:1\u201320, then sing a carol.','At midnight, step outside in quiet, then put out the candle before bed.']},correspondences:{herbs:['Frankincense','Myrrh','Holly','Cinnamon'],crystals:['Clear Quartz','Garnet','Citrine'],colors:['Red','Gold','White'],foods:['Wigilia dishes: barszcz, pierogi, carp','Kutia','Mince pies','Mulled wine or cider'],symbols:['Candle in the window','First star','Hay under the cloth','Empty place','Manger']},respect:'Christmas Eve is one of the holiest nights of the Christian year, and for many families the most important meal of the year. Visitors are welcome at most services; arrive early, as churches fill. If you are invited to a Wigilia or Holy Supper, follow your hosts\u2019 customs: wait for the first star and accept the wafer graciously.',sources:['Catholic Encyclopedia (1908), \u201cChristmas\u201d: https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/03724b.htm','Luke 2:1\u201320.','Ronald Hutton, The Stations of the Sun (Oxford, 1996), ch. 1\u201310.']},{ref:'christian-christmas',title:'Christmas',aka:['The Nativity','Noel','Yule','Christ\u2019s Mass','Natale','Weihnachten'],tradition:'Christian',summary:'The birth of Christ on 25 December, the light coming into the world at midwinter. The twelve days of Christmas run from today to Epiphany.',honours:'The birth of Jesus in Bethlehem; Mary and Joseph; the shepherds who came from the fields and the angels who sang to them; and the belief that God came to share human life.',history:'The earliest firm record of Christmas on 25 December is in the Chronography of 354, whose list of martyrs (written in 336) begins: \u201cVIII kal. Ian. natus Christus in Betleem Iudeae\u201d, \u201ceight days before the Kalends of January, Christ was born in Bethlehem of Judaea.\u201d The Eastern churches kept 6 January; most of them later accepted 25 December, though those using the Julian calendar now keep it on 7 January.\n\nWhy 25 December? One theory says the Church chose it to replace the Roman feast of the Unconquered Sun; another says it was calculated from a belief that Jesus was conceived on the same day as he died, 25 March, nine months earlier. Both views have serious defenders.\n\nOver the centuries Christmas gathered customs: the twelve days of feasting, carols, the crib popularised by St Francis in 1223, the Yule log, evergreens, mumming and misrule, the German Christmas tree, and Santa Claus, grown from St Nicholas and the English Father Christmas. Puritans banned it in England in the 1640s and 50s; the Victorians reinvented much of it, with Dickens\u2019s A Christmas Carol (1843) at the centre.',knowAndDont:{known:'The date is attested in Rome by 336, and the history of the customs is well documented.',unknown:'The Gospels do not give a date for Jesus\u2019s birth, and the original reason for 25 December is still debated.'},celebrate:['Attend a Christmas service, or listen to carols from a cathedral.','Share a feast with family or friends, and invite someone who would be alone.','Give gifts, and give to those in need.','Set up a crib or nativity scene, and add the Magi on Epiphany.','Keep the twelve days: something small and festive each day until 6 January.'],ritual:{title:'Christmas morning',outline:['Light a candle at breakfast, or by the crib or tree.','Read Luke 2:1\u201320 or John 1:1\u201314.','Give thanks for the light coming into the world, and for the people around you.','Sing a carol together.','Share gifts and food, and set something aside for someone in need.']},correspondences:{herbs:['Frankincense','Myrrh','Holly','Cinnamon','Clove'],crystals:['Garnet','Clear Quartz','Ruby'],colors:['Red','Green','Gold','White'],foods:['Christmas pudding','Roast dinner','Mince pies','Gingerbread','Panettone or stollen'],symbols:['Star','Manger and crib','Evergreens','Candles','Angels']},respect:'Christmas is one of the two greatest Christian feasts. Many people also keep it as a cultural holiday, and many witches keep Yule beside it. Respect its meaning for Christians, and be gentle with those for whom it is a hard time of year.',sources:['Catholic Encyclopedia (1908), \u201cChristmas\u201d: https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/03724b.htm','Chronography of 354, Depositio Martyrum: https://www.tertullian.org/fathers/chronography_of_354_12_depositions_martyrs.htm','Thomas J. Talley, The Origins of the Liturgical Year (New York, 1986).','Ronald Hutton, The Stations of the Sun (Oxford, 1996), ch. 1\u201310.']}]},2504,[]);
